window.docsSearch = [{"Title":"Introduction","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"overview.html","Text":"Labena is a batteries-included Go framework delivered as an application template. Build a modular monolith with explicit module registration, HTTP APIs, background workers and shared capabilities for authentication, persistence, mail, notifications, storage and observability. You own the copied application and its dependencies. This documentation follows the shipped code: ordinary Go constructors, typed handlers and clear ownership of resources, data and business operations."},{"Title":"Introduction","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Start building","Parent":"","URL":"overview.html#start-building","Text":"New to Labena? Follow this short path. Each guide leaves you with something you can run or change. Create an application. Choose your Go module and display name; the installer prepares your source and configuration. Run the application. Start PostgreSQL and Redis, apply migrations and check your first HTTP response. Create your first module. Register a small Catalog endpoint and test it entirely in process, without a database. Maintain your application. Keep template provenance and incorporate upstream fixes into your customized source. Already running? Use configuration for settings and local development for a persistent development stack."},{"Title":"Introduction","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Find your next task","Parent":"","URL":"overview.html#find-your-next-task","Text":"I want to… Read… Add an API endpoint Routing, handlers, requests and validation Save data safely Entities and queries, transactions, migrations Sign users in Authentication and sessions Run work in the background Queues and tasks, events, schedules Send email or a notification Mail, notifications, push Upload and serve files File storage, media assets Verify a change Testing basics, HTTP and modules Run in production Deployment, logging, observability"},{"Title":"Introduction","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Understand the design","Parent":"","URL":"overview.html#understand-the-design","Text":"Business modules own their use cases and persistence. App constructs dependencies and selects the processes to run. Platform capabilities support modules, while pkg holds technical packages that can be reused independently. Start with the directory structure, then follow the application lifecycle and module registration. The project conventions are the architecture contract for changes. Labena uses PostgreSQL for application data, Asynq for Redis-backed tasks and Watermill for process-local events. These have different guarantees: a local event is best-effort, queue acceptance is not a SQL commit, and notification intent can be committed with a business transaction. Each guide explains its boundary."},{"Title":"Introduction","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Learn through a complete recipe","Parent":"","URL":"overview.html#learn-through-a-complete-recipe","Text":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation: follow the same use case through two transports, a service and a repository. SQL and notification intent: commit a profile change and its notification intent together. Account onboarding reactions: connect registration events, background tasks, notifications and mail."},{"Title":"Introduction","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Keep the reference close","Parent":"","URL":"overview.html#keep-the-reference-close","Text":"The configuration reference lists settings and defaults. The API reference explains how to inspect the running application's OpenAPI schema and Go packages. See support and compatibility before adopting or updating the template. Use the topic groups to browse, Search to find a guide, section or API symbol, and On this page to move within a guide. Every page has a Markdown download; direct links work on ordinary static hosting and with JavaScript disabled."},{"Title":"Create an application","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"creating-applications.html","Text":"Use the standalone labena installer to create your own application. It copies a versioned template, changes the Go module and imports, and configures your name. The resulting repository owns its source and can evolve independently."},{"Title":"Create an application","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Install the command","Parent":"","URL":"creating-applications.html#install-the-command","Text":"Install the released CLI: go install github.com/wisalgroup/labena/cmd/labena@latest For repeatable team setup, replace @latest with @v0.2.0. Go installs the binary into GOBIN, or $(go env GOPATH)/bin when GOBIN is unset. Put that directory on your shell's PATH, then check labena --version. Use the Go version declared in the release's go.mod. If your shell reports command not found, add the installation directory to the current session: go_bin=\"$(go env GOBIN)\" export PATH=\"${go_bin:-$(go env GOPATH)/bin}:$PATH\" labena --version Add the resolved directory to your shell configuration (for example, ~/.zshrc) to keep it available in future terminals. While the repository is private, configure Git authentication for GitHub and add github.com/wisalgroup/labena to your existing GOPRIVATE patterns. For a shell without other private-module patterns: export GOPRIVATE=github.com/wisalgroup/labena go install github.com/wisalgroup/labena/cmd/labena@latest Keep GOPRIVATE set when running the installer: it downloads the template too. This setting bypasses the public proxy and checksum database for this module; it does not grant repository access. Git still needs your credential helper or SSH configuration. Never put access tokens in a command or committed file."},{"Title":"Create an application","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Create a project","Parent":"","URL":"creating-applications.html#create-a-project","Text":"Run from the parent directory where the new project should live: labena new myapp \\ --module github.com/your-org/myapp \\ --name MyApp Replace github.com/your-org/myapp with your application's Go module path. myapp must not already exist, and its parent must exist. The module path identifies the application's Go source. MyApp is its display name; these are separate choices. The command creates: Application source, tests, examples, deployment files and documentation. A new go.mod module declaration and matching Go imports, including tests and examples. An APP_NAME default and example profiles using MyApp, plus initial Compose and Kubernetes branding. A private local .env copied from the quickstart example, with optional capabilities disabled. .labena.json, recording the source version and checksum, plus its commit when available. Commit the generated .env.example and .env.*.example profiles with your application. They contain example settings with your application name. The generated local .env remains ignored by Git; the template's local secrets are never copied. It runs go mod tidy, regenerates the documentation, and builds the application commands and examples. These checks need access to Go dependencies. It does not start services, apply migrations, initialize Git, or create a GitHub repository. On failure, it removes the new incomplete directory; an existing destination is never overwritten. Use --skip-checks to generate source without those checks, then run them yourself before using it. The installer itself is not copied into your application. labena creates projects; your application's go run ./cmd/cli manages its routes, migrations and other commands. Application CLI help still constructs the configured application. Installer help does not load application configuration or connect to infrastructure."},{"Title":"Create an application","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run your application","Parent":"","URL":"creating-applications.html#run-your-application","Text":"cd myapp Follow installation to start the tutorial PostgreSQL and Redis containers. Then run: go run ./cmd/cli migrate up go run ./cmd/app The generated .env already selects the tutorial settings. Replace example credentials and configure the features you need before deployment. Database names, JWT issuers/audiences, Redis namespaces, queues, image names and Kubernetes namespaces remain explicit operational settings. Review those when provisioning an independent environment; changing APP_NAME never changes them automatically. Likewise, update observability dashboards' service-name filters for your deployment. Start your own Git history when ready, preserving .labena.json and the supplied license. Upstream fixes are reviewed and ported into your copy; see adoption and updates."},{"Title":"Create an application","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Try an unpublished checkout","Parent":"","URL":"creating-applications.html#try-an-unpublished-checkout","Text":"From a Labena checkout, install the current CLI locally: go install ./cmd/labena labena new ../myapp \\ --module github.com/your-org/myapp \\ --name MyApp \\ --source . If you are in another directory, --source . points there rather than to Labena. Pass the absolute checkout path instead. For example, from your chosen projects directory, replace the source path below with your actual checkout: labena new myapp \\ --module github.com/your-org/myapp \\ --name MyApp \\ --source /absolute/path/to/labena The checkout folder may have a different name, such as go-modular; what matters is that it contains Labena's go.mod, source and Git metadata. The destination directory must be new; the installer never overwrites an existing application. --source must point to the root of a Labena Git checkout. It includes tracked and non-ignored source changes, excludes local environment files, runtime data, build artifacts and Git history, and rejects copied symlinks. Provenance marks this as a local snapshot and records whether the checkout was modified. Treat local source as trusted code: verification runs its Go documentation builder and compiles it. Use the default release path for reproducible adoption. A released CLI downloads its own exact template version through the Go module system. To select another compatible release explicitly, use --template-version vX.Y.Z; branches and latest are not accepted as template versions. Incompatible templates are rejected. Prefer installing the CLI from the desired release instead of mixing versions."},{"Title":"Create an application","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Publish the installer","Parent":"","URL":"creating-applications.html#publish-the-installer","Text":"Maintainers commit cmd/labena with the matching template and verification results, then publish a new immutable module release tag. Tags made before this command was added cannot install it; do not move an existing tag to add it. No second repository, package manager, or installer service is required. go install fetches the command from that version, and labena new uses the same version as its application template."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"index.html","Text":"Start the application with PostgreSQL and Redis, confirm that it is ready, then choose your next feature. You do not need a cloud account, Kubernetes, or email credentials for this first run. Want to understand a module first? Run go test ./tests/modules -run TestFirstModule and follow your first module. That example runs entirely in process. This tutorial runs the template as supplied. To make it your own application, use the installer to set its name and module path. Then follow adoption and updates to incorporate future security fixes."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Requirements","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#requirements","Text":"The Go version declared in go.mod (go version shows your installed version). Docker for the two disposable services below. Two terminals, opened at the repository root. The installed application uses PostgreSQL, Redis-backed Asynq tasks, and process-local Watermill events. Redis persists tasks; local events do not persist."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Start local dependencies","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#start-local-dependencies","Text":"These containers use dedicated host ports and disposable data. The credentials below are tutorial values only: docker run --rm -d --name go-modular-quickstart-postgres \\ -e POSTGRES_USER=demo -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=local-demo \\ -e POSTGRES_DB=go_modular -p 127.0.0.1:55432:5432 postgres:17-alpine docker run --rm -d --name go-modular-quickstart-redis \\ -p 127.0.0.1:56379:6379 redis:7-alpine Check both services before starting the application: docker exec go-modular-quickstart-postgres pg_isready -U demo -d go_modular docker exec go-modular-quickstart-redis redis-cli ping Expect accepting connections and PONG. PostgreSQL may need a few seconds; repeat the readiness check if it is still starting. For retained data, see local development."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Configure and migrate","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#configure-and-migrate","Text":"Select the supplied tutorial profile in the first terminal: export APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/cli migrate up This profile points to the two containers above. It disables authentication, cache, storage, push, scheduling and telemetry. Mail is always constructed, but SMTP connects only when sending: this tutorial sends no email. migrate up applies the registered Goose migrations and returns to your prompt. Migrations are explicit; starting the server never changes the schema. Confirm that there are no pending migrations: go run ./cmd/cli migrate status Existing shell variables override the profile. An unexpected database, credential error or exporter usually means an inherited override; review configuration precedence."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Start the application","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#start-the-application","Text":"In the same terminal: go run ./cmd/app The app main explicitly starts HTTP, queue workers, local event subscribers and the notification dispatcher. Run cmd/scheduler separately when recurring maintenance is needed. Leave this terminal running."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Check the app and enable signup","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#check-the-app-and-enable-signup","Text":"In the second terminal: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz Both return 200 after successful startup. /healthz checks the HTTP listener; /readyz checks application startup/shutdown state. Dependency probes are a separate readiness option. Open the running API documentation, or inspect its current schema: curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/openapi.json These routes reflect the registered application immediately. No OpenAPI generation command is required after editing a route. The quickstart has authentication disabled, so protected routes intentionally reject requests. To try signup/sign-in, copy the tutorial profile into your own local file, configure SMTP and Identity keys, and follow Identity setup. Do not use .env.example without filling its enabled capabilities' credentials. Ready to try… Continue with… An endpoint you can change Your first module Email OTP and social sign-in Identity A stored account through HTTP and CLI Persisted operation walkthrough A background task Queues The full container stack Local development"},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run a console command","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#run-a-console-command","Text":"Select the same profile in the second terminal: export APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/cli help go run ./cmd/cli route list Help lists nested command paths. route list prints methods, paths and operation names without opening another HTTP listener. Ordinary CLI commands construct and connect the application but do not run workers, subscribers or schedules. See console commands for the complete execution model."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Follow the request through the code","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#follow-the-request-through-the-code","Text":"When reading the supplied Identity module, follow this sequence: Responsibility Location Register an operation and its schema internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/routes.go Decode input and map service arguments internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/requests.go Authorize and call the operation internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/*_handler.go Apply business rules internal/modules/identity/service Execute queries internal/modules/identity/repository Define the user and its invariants internal/modules/shared/entities Construct and connect the module internal/modules/identity/register.go, internal/app/modules.go The directory map and dependency guide explain why these pieces are separate and where shared capabilities fit."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Stop and verify","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#stop-and-verify","Text":"Press Ctrl+C in the application terminal. The application drains admitted work within its shutdown budget, then closes owned resources. Remove the tutorial containers when finished; this deletes their disposable data: docker rm -f go-modular-quickstart-postgres go-modular-quickstart-redis For a quick source check: go test ./tests/modules -run TestFirstModule -count=1 go vet ./... Use the testing guide for the full suite and disposable integration services. Tests that skip because their service settings are absent have not verified those integrations."},{"Title":"Installation","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"If the first run fails","Parent":"","URL":"index.html#if-the-first-run-fails","Text":"Symptom First check Docker reports a name already in use A previous tutorial container is still running Database connection refused PostgreSQL readiness and host port 55432 Redis connection refused Redis readiness and host port 56379 Credentials or unexpected services are required APP_ENV_FILE and inherited process variables HTTP address is already in use Another app, Compose or Tilt process owns port 8080 Protected endpoint returns an error Authentication is intentionally off in this tutorial Continue with configuration, then your first module. More detailed symptoms are covered in troubleshooting."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html","Text":"Load settings once, validate them, then pass typed values into application construction. Business services receive the values and clients they need; they do not read environment variables during an operation."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Start with a profile","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#start-with-a-profile","Text":"For your first run, use the profile and services in installation: export APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/cli migrate up go run ./cmd/app For local edits, copy that profile to an ignored .env file if you do not already have one, then unset the selector: cp -n .env.quickstart.example .env unset APP_ENV_FILE cp -n preserves an existing .env. Edit it deliberately instead of overwriting working credentials. Never commit real credentials to an example file. File Use it for .env.quickstart.example First success with tutorial PostgreSQL/Redis; optional features off .env.example Full implemented feature set: fill provider credentials before use .env.postgres.example Expanded local settings, including MinIO; check its endpoints against your services .env.asynq.example Component-oriented settings; supply a database URL before serving installed modules deployments/development/kubernetes/app-config.yaml Public Kubernetes feature switches and cluster endpoints Examples deliberately differ. The full example enables Redis cache, MinIO, Identity/Google, Expo, queue tooling and observability. It is not a credential-free quickstart. Mail is always present and opens SMTP only when sending."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Set the application name","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#set-the-application-name","Text":"Set APP_NAME=MyApp for the API title, mail defaults, log viewer and telemetry service identity. Mail may override the sender and brand with MAIL_FROM_NAME and MAIL_BRAND_NAME; leave them blank to inherit the app name. A logo is optional. This setting does not change Go imports, database names, JWT issuers or deployment resources. Create an application handles the initial module rename and branding together."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Understand precedence","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#understand-precedence","Text":"The loader takes one isolated snapshot: Read the selected dotenv file, if any. Overlay process environment variables, including explicitly empty values. Decode typed settings and apply envDefault values. Validate the result without opening connections. Selector Behavior APP_ENV_FILE unset Read .env if it exists APP_ENV_FILE=.env.local Require this exact file APP_ENV_FILE='' Read no dotenv file Loading does not modify the process environment. An empty process variable wins over a dotenv value, but a field with envDefault can still receive its default. Empty strings are not a universal off switch. Override one value for a run: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example HTTP_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:8081 go run ./cmd/app The server now listens on port 8081. Other settings still come from the selected profile and Go defaults."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Keep routine settings at their defaults","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#keep-routine-settings-at-their-defaults","Text":"Environment files should express deployment decisions: enabled features, connections, credentials, public origins and deliberate tuning overrides. They do not need to repeat every timeout and pool size. APP_NAME=orders APP_ENV=production DATABASE_DRIVER=postgres DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASSWORD@DB_HOST:5432/DB_NAME?sslmode=require QUEUE_REDIS_URL=redis://REDIS_HOST:6379/1 EVENTS_DRIVER=gochannel CACHE_DRIVER=disabled FILESYSTEM_DISK=disabled This is a fragment, not a complete production profile. Set the remaining settings required by enabled features. APP_NAME supplies telemetry's standard service.name; its Go field is App.AppName. Find all supported keys, exact defaults and validation constraints in the configuration reference."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Enable capabilities deliberately","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#enable-capabilities-deliberately","Text":"Capability Settings to consider What else must exist Authentication AUTH_ENABLED, verification/access keys PostgreSQL, SMTP, rate-limit Redis Redis cache CACHE_DRIVER=redis, CACHE_REDIS_URL A separate Redis database from queues and rate limits Object storage FILESYSTEM_DISK, selected disk credentials Bucket and its intended access policy Push PUSH_ENABLED, Expo project/access settings Valid Expo registrations and worker delivery Traces/log export Tracing switch, LOG_CHANNELS, OTLP endpoints An OTLP collector Metrics METRICS_ENABLED, METRICS_ADDRESS A free listener address and a scraper Queue dashboard QUEUE_DASHBOARD_ENABLED, username/password HTTP role; queue CLI needs no enable flag Recurring work app.Processes{Scheduler: true} One designated scheduler and workers for its tasks Queue Redis defaults to database 1, cache to 0, and rate limits to 2. Validation rejects active stores configured with the same endpoint and database. These are logical namespaces within Redis, not separate failure domains. Generate each Identity key independently with openssl rand -base64 32; keep keys stable across restarts and consistent across replicas. Provider secrets belong in local ignored files or deployment secret storage, not ConfigMaps. See Identity and deployment."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Find the setting to change","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#find-the-setting-to-change","Text":"The typed schema lives under internal/app/config: File Responsibility config.go, load.go, validate.go Schema composition, snapshot loading and cross-capability validation app.go, http.go Application identity, lifetime budgets and HTTP listener database.go, cache.go Connections and cache policy queues.go, events.go Tasks, queue routing and local events filesystems.go, media.go Named disks, visibility, URL policy and Media settings mail.go, notifications.go SMTP, branding, Expo and delivery dispatch auth.go, ratelimit.go, cors.go Authentication policy and request admission observability.go Logging channels, tracing and metrics Channel formats, file retention and levels are defined in Go beside observability settings. See single or daily files."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Select process roles","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#select-process-roles","Text":"Each executable supplies explicit app.Processes flags in main.go. cmd/app and cli serve select HTTP and workers; cmd/worker selects workers; cmd/scheduler selects the scheduler. HTTP and workers include their local event consumers. Environment variables configure connections and features, not execution choices. Run one scheduler per schedule set. See entry points before splitting processes."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Supply settings from Go","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#supply-settings-from-go","Text":"config.Load() reads the environment. Use config.FromEnvironment for an isolated validated snapshot, or config.Defaults() as an editable baseline: cfg := config.Defaults() cfg.App.ShutdownTimeout = time.Minute cfg.Filesystem.Default = \"local\" cfg.Filesystem.Disks[\"local\"].Root = \"storage/app\" This fragment imports time and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/app/config. Set the required connection details before calling app.Serve(ctx, cfg, app.Processes{Workers: true}). Defaults performs no environment reads and does not validate the resulting baseline; Serve validates programmatic settings before acquisition. Maps and slices are immutable after assembly. Configuration has no live reload: restart the application after changes."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Add a setting","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#add-a-setting","Text":"Add a typed field beside its owning capability's settings: ExportTimeout time.Duration `env:\"EXPORT_TIMEOUT\" envDefault:\"30s\"` Then validate the range, translate it to owner options if needed, and pass it explicitly to the consumer. Update the configuration reference and any profile that needs a deployment-specific value. HTTP, CORS and events use complete environment names on their fields rather than inherited prefixes. Keep behavioral callbacks, such as queue retry classification, in Go. Do not add runtime environment reads to a service as a shortcut around construction."},{"Title":"Configuration","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Diagnose a configuration failure","Parent":"","URL":"configuration.html#diagnose-a-configuration-failure","Text":"Failure Resolution cannot load environment file Check the explicit path and working directory A default replaces an empty value Use the documented off switch or a nonempty override CLI help fails before printing It validates and constructs the full configured app Redis isolation validation fails Give queue, cache and active rate limits different databases or servers Changing a file has no effect Remove the process override and restart Continue with local development, dependency injection, or the complete configuration reference."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html","Text":"Create a greeting endpoint in one file, register it in the app, and call it. Then learn where services, repositories and additional transports fit as the module grows. Start small: the greeting needs no database or worker of its own."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Create the module","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#create-the-module","Text":"The supplied Catalog starter is compiled by the normal test suite. Try it without configuration or infrastructure: go test ./tests/modules -run TestFirstModule -count=1 The test registers the module with an in-process HTTP server and verifies Hello, Ada. Copy its source to begin an application module: mkdir -p internal/modules/catalog cp examples/module/catalog/register.go internal/modules/catalog/register.go Complete internal/modules/catalog/register.go: package catalog import ( \"context\" \"github.com/danielgtaylor/huma/v2\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api/response\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/dependencies\" ) type GreetingRequest struct { Name string `path:\"name\" minLength:\"1\"` } type GreetingResponse struct { Message string `json:\"message\"` } // Register installs the greeting route without opening resources. func Register(_ dependencies.Resources, targets dependencies.Targets) error { if targets.HTTP == nil { return nil } huma.Register(targets.HTTP, huma.Operation{Method: \"GET\", Path: \"/catalog/hello/{name}\", Summary: \"Greet a visitor\"}, func(ctx context.Context, input *GreetingRequest) (*response.JSON[GreetingResponse], error) { return response.OK(GreetingResponse{Message: \"Hello, \" + input.Name}), nil }) return nil } The github.com/wisalgroup/labena import prefix matches go.mod; change imports if you rename the Go module."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Understand registration","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#understand-registration","Text":"Piece Responsibility Register(resources, targets) Construct collaborators and attach routes, commands, tasks, events and migrations GreetingRequest Describe a path input and its Huma validation GreetingResponse Select the public output fields response.OK(...) Return the shared success envelope The unused Resources argument leaves room for this module's collaborators. An absent HTTP target skips HTTP registration. Register performs no I/O and starts no background work. There is no module instance to retain or initialize."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Register it with the app","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#register-it-with-the-app","Text":"In internal/app/modules.go, add the import: \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/catalog\" In the application's registerModules method, add one call after Identity's registration: if err := catalog.Register(a.resources, a.targets); err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"register catalog: %w\", err) } Future routes, commands and migrations belong in Catalog's Register function; they need no other application list. Pass business services and settings as explicit arguments only when this module needs them. Do not import app config or add business services to Resources."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run your route","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#run-your-route","Text":"Start the tutorial database and Redis, apply migrations, and restart the full app: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/app In another terminal: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/catalog/hello/Ada Expect 200 and an envelope like this; field order is not significant: { \"data\": {\"message\": \"Hello, Ada\"} } Open the running API docs to see the new operation and its schema. Registration drives both routing and OpenAPI. No separate annotation or generation step is required. The isolated starter test needs no infrastructure; the full installed application still constructs its configured PostgreSQL and Redis dependencies."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Add business behavior when needed","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#add-business-behavior-when-needed","Text":"Move an operation into service when it has business rules or several callers. Construct module-local services once inside Register and pass them to HTTP, CLI and task handlers. Services needed before registration are constructed by app and passed explicitly, as with Identity authentication: internal/modules/catalog/ register.go service/ service.go Public business interface product.go Private implementation and constructor repository/ repository.go Persistence interface product.go Queries, implementation and constructor delivery/ http/ Requests, responses, handlers and registration This is a growth map, not a scaffold to create in advance. Business entities belong in internal/modules/shared/entities; module-owned migrations define their schema. See the persisted operation for a real service shared by HTTP and CLI."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Keep the pattern as it grows","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#keep-the-pattern-as-it-grows","Text":"Need Add Register Business rules reused by several callers One cohesive service Its transport handlers Persistent state Shared entity, repository and module migration SQL through Targets.Migrations Deferred work jobs definition and delivery/jobs handler Handler through Targets.TaskRouter Business reaction Shared event contract and consuming delivery/events handler Handler through event targets A console operation Native Cobra constructor in delivery/cli Group through Targets.RootCommand Notification content A named constructor in notifications Reuse the existing notifier Recurring work An existing task or command factory Central timing in app/schedules.go Scheduling is the intentional centralized exception. Modules own executable handlers; app owns when recurring work runs. Details are in module registration and scheduling."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Keep large use cases on the same service","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#keep-large-use-cases-on-the-same-service","Text":"A substantial method can move to a separate file without creating a new service: service/ service.go ProductService interface product.go productService and NewProductService import.go Import method on *productService Constructors stay beside implementations. Contracts stay with their owners in service/service.go and repository/repository.go. Consumers import those contracts rather than redefining them or using a global contracts directory. Services receive narrow dependencies; only module assembly receives the complete Resources bundle. See dependency injection."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Learn from an existing operation","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#learn-from-an-existing-operation","Text":"Example What it demonstrates Persisted account workflow HTTP input → service → repository → CLI read Account onboarding Event → task → notification → channel delivery SQL and notification intent Atomic business change and persisted delivery intent Media A platform capability with owner-local types and an integration package Identity's authentication transactions enforce special security rules. Read their explanation before copying them into normal CRUD operations."},{"Title":"Your first module","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Check your changes","Parent":"","URL":"modules.html#check-your-changes","Text":"gofmt -w internal/modules/catalog/register.go internal/app/modules.go go test ./internal/modules/catalog ./tests/app ./tests/architecture Place business service and repository tests in tests/catalog, following tests/identity. Keep app tests in tests/app. Architecture checks enforce import direction and test placement; behavior tests still verify authorization, errors and persistence. Before merging application changes, run the full verification workflow. External-driver tests require disposable services; a skip is not an integration success. Continue with directory structure for a code map, module registration for the complete assembly flow, or routing to develop the new endpoint."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html","Text":"Choose one environment, use one configuration profile consistently, and run the same module contracts in HTTP, CLI and workers. This guide covers the day-to-day loop after installation."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Choose your setup","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#choose-your-setup","Text":"Setup Best fit Infrastructure Host Go process Fast edit/restart and debugger use Tutorial PostgreSQL/Redis containers Docker Compose A containerized app and retained local data Services in deployments/compose.yaml Tilt Kubernetes development and resource inspection Local cluster, Docker and Tilt Compose and Tilt publish overlapping host ports. Stop one before starting the other. Redis is required for tasks; the application has no memory queue driver. Events remain process-local in every setup."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run on the host","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#run-on-the-host","Text":"Start the tutorial dependencies, then select the same profile for migrations, HTTP and commands: export APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/cli migrate up go run ./cmd/app After editing Go code, stop the app with Ctrl+C and rerun it. These entry points do not watch source files. In a second terminal: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/cli route list Expect a route table, with no new HTTP listener or workers. After configuring Identity and creating an account, inspect it with: APP_ENV_FILE=.env go run ./cmd/cli identity show '\u003cuser-id\u003e' Use the profile that created the account; .env here represents your configured local profile, not a requirement to rename it."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run with Docker Compose","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#run-with-docker-compose","Text":"docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml up --build -d docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml logs -f api Compose runs migrations before the app and retains service data in named volumes. It rewrites infrastructure addresses for communication between containers. Check the deployment guide for credentials and all exposed ports. Host and container addresses differ: Caller PostgreSQL example Redis example Host quickstart 127.0.0.1:55432 127.0.0.1:56379 App inside Compose postgres:5432 redis:6379 Do not copy a container-only hostname into a host app's connection URL."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run with Tilt","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#run-with-tilt","Text":"tilt up Tilt builds the app for the local cluster and starts MinIO and observability by default. Configure the local application Secret before running enabled providers. The public ConfigMap and Secret have separate responsibilities; see deployment. To omit optional stacks: tilt up -- --storage=false --observability=false Use the Tilt dashboard to inspect build failures, migration completion and app logs. This is the Kubernetes workflow; a successful image build alone does not prove that migrations or dependency connections succeeded."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Optional object storage","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#optional-object-storage","Text":"For Compose, enable the MinIO profile and select its disk together: FILESYSTEM_DISK=minio docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml --profile storage up --build -d The bucket initialization service runs before the app in this profile. Tilt does the equivalent by default. FILESYSTEM_DISK from an existing .env can affect Compose's selected disk. To explicitly run without storage: FILESYSTEM_DISK=disabled docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml up --build -d Disabling storage preserves existing objects and volumes. See file storage for connections and Media for upload metadata, visibility and URLs. A public asset's direct URL also requires an appropriate bucket policy and a client-reachable address."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Read local logs","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#read-local-logs","Text":"LOG_CHANNELS=stderr This uses readable terminal output. Select stderr,file to also write files, or include observability to export structured logs directly over OTLP. File collection is not needed for Loki. The current file channel writes warnings and errors to storage/logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log, using the UTC date and retaining 14 days. Formats, levels, single/daily mode and retention are configured in internal/app/config/observability.go; see single or daily files."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Keep file logs with Tilt or Compose","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#keep-file-logs-with-tilt-or-compose","Text":"Tilt's Docker Desktop kubeadm setup mounts the checkout's storage/logs at /app/storage/logs. Apply through Tilt so the path and UID/GID are set correctly. The Kind provisioner does not expose Mac folders through the same hostPath arrangement. Compose instead shares the named app-storage volume; copy logs out when needed: mkdir -p storage/logs docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml cp api:/app/storage/logs/. storage/logs/ For request-to-trace correlation and Grafana, follow observability."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Run separate processes","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#run-separate-processes","Text":"First change the selection in cmd/app/main.go to app.Processes{HTTP: true}. Then use the same database and queue settings in each terminal: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/app APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/worker The HTTP process must keep its own subscribers: GoChannel cannot move an event to the worker process. Redis tasks can cross that boundary. Enable recurring schedules with one cmd/scheduler process; the dedicated worker never starts a scheduler. When metrics are enabled on several host processes, assign each listener a different port. See entry points for explicit process selections and queues for task routing."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Optional brokers","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#optional-brokers","Text":"RabbitMQ and NATS are opt-in infrastructure experiments. Starting them does not install an application driver or change local event guarantees: docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml --profile extra-brokers up --build -d tilt up -- --extra-brokers=true Use one of these commands for your selected environment, not both. Default launches omit these brokers."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Stop development services","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#stop-development-services","Text":"Action Effect Ctrl+C in a host app Drain that process, then close its clients docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml stop Stop containers and retain volumes Compose down Remove the stack's containers/networks; retain named volumes Compose down --volumes Also delete volume data Stop the Tilt process Leave Kubernetes resources running tilt down Remove managed resources; namespace/volume cleanup can delete development data Host-mounted logs survive Pod removal. Database, broker and object-store data have their own volumes and backup needs. Do not use destructive cleanup to fix a configuration error in data you want to retain."},{"Title":"Local development","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Diagnose startup problems","Parent":"","URL":"local-development.html#diagnose-startup-problems","Text":"Symptom Check Redis connection refused Selected profile, host/container address and port Unexpected dependency opens Shell overrides and enabled features Address already in use Another host process or development stack Enqueue succeeds but nothing runs Worker queue names and Redis database Local event never arrives Handler registered and subscribers running in the producer process No file entries after an INFO request The default file channel records WARN and ERROR Grafana has no data Collector/export settings as well as the dashboard Continue with troubleshooting for detailed diagnosis, testing for disposable test services, or your first module to add application behavior."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html","Text":"Labena supplies the starting source for an application. Your repository owns the customized application, its deployments and its updates. Read the support policy for release coverage and compatibility."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Version 0.2: push-subscription table rename","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#version-02-push-subscription-table-rename","Text":"Version 0.2 renames notification_subscriptions to push_subscriptions so its name describes device registrations rather than notification preferences. Its indexes and constraints are renamed too. The new migration is 20261019120000_push_subscriptions.sql; keep the original 20261001120100_notifications.sql unchanged. This is a coordinated schema upgrade: old binaries require the old table name, and new binaries require the new one. Do not run mixed versions against this schema. Update custom SQL, reports and operational scripts that reference the old table or index names. There is no data copy, token reset or registration backfill. Recipient ownership, installation IDs, session bindings, tokens, disabled state, versions and timestamps are preserved. Existing delivery rows and queued jobs retain their IDs and frozen subscription versions; they need no payload migration. HTTP subscription routes, Go APIs, configuration, task names and event contracts are unchanged by this rename."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Upgrade an existing application","Parent":"Version 0.2: push-subscription table rename","URL":"upgrading.html#upgrade-an-existing-application","Text":"Port the new migration and the matching notification queries/Bun mapping into your application. Back up the database and rehearse on a disposable restored copy, including any customized schema names. Pause the scheduler and incoming traffic, then gracefully stop all application and worker processes. Let active work finish before changing the table name. Pending delivery intent and Redis jobs can remain queued. Using the new application CLI and the intended database configuration, run migrate up and check migrate status. From a source checkout, the commands are: go run ./cmd/cli migrate up go run ./cmd/cli migrate status Confirm migration 20261019120000 is applied, then start the new workers and application, restore traffic, and resume the scheduler. Check readiness, registration refresh and notification delivery. Normal expiry and session eligibility checks still apply to queued work. Fresh installations run the complete migration history normally. The table exists even when push is disabled, so every installation uses the same schema history."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Roll back the rename","Parent":"Version 0.2: push-subscription table rename","URL":"upgrading.html#roll-back-the-rename","Text":"Stop all processes again. While still using the new CLI, check migrate version. If 20261019120000 is the latest applied migration, run migrate down once to restore the old table, index and constraint names, then deploy the previous binaries. This reversal preserves registrations and delivery intent. If your application has later migrations, use its reviewed rollback plan first: migrate down always reverts the latest migration, not a named one. Do not use migrate refresh for an upgrade or rollback; it destroys application data."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Record your starting point","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#record-your-starting-point","Text":"Prefer the installer to create an application from a published release. It records the upstream version and checksum in .labena.json and changes imports and branding automatically. Keep that provenance file. For a manual clone or template copy, record the source yourself. Before changing the source, record the upstream repository URL, release tag and exact commit in your application's maintenance notes. A template copy may not retain upstream history, so keep this record even if its Git history starts at your own first commit. For an evaluation snapshot, record its commit and note that it is unreleased. Keep a clean initial snapshot or access to the original release so later fixes can be compared against the source you adopted. Preserve the project's license and applicable third-party notices. Follow upstream releases and security advisories; copied applications do not receive automatic updates. You can run the installation tutorial with the existing github.com/wisalgroup/labena path before adopting the source for your own application."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Choose your application module path","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#choose-your-application-module-path","Text":"The installer handles this step. For manual adoption, the template uses github.com/wisalgroup/labena. Replace it with the path belonging to your application repository. For example, substituting your actual owner and name: go mod edit -module=github.com/your-owner/your-app In your editor, replace the github.com/wisalgroup/labena/ prefix with that same path plus / in all Go files, including examples and tests. Update active documentation imports too. Include any module-path strings in custom tests; the supplied architecture checks read the path from go.mod. The module declaration command does not rewrite imports. Avoid replacing the bare project name throughout every file: database names, container names and persisted identifiers have separate meanings. Configure your application name and deployment resources deliberately. Historical review records describe earlier source and need not be rewritten. Check for remaining old imports, tidy dependencies and build the application: rg -n '\"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/' --glob '*.go' go mod tidy go test ./tests/architecture go build ./cmd/... ./examples/... go run docs/build.go The search should have no matches. Rebuild the documentation after editing its Markdown sources; do not edit generated HTML or Markdown downloads directly. Then run the required verification suites for your application. pkg contains reusable source maintained within this template release. No package currently has an independent external release or support promise. Adoption does not require adding this repository as a dependency or introducing a second module."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Labena rename compatibility","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#labena-rename-compatibility","Text":"The Labena rename changes the Go module/import prefix from go-modular to github.com/wisalgroup/labena. Instrumentation scopes use the new prefix too. The default APP_NAME is labena. Application API titles, mail defaults and the log viewer now inherit APP_NAME. Update service-name and instrumentation-scope filters if you adopt these defaults; explicitly configured names still take precedence. The supplied log queries use service_name=\"labena\". Database names, JWT issuer/audience defaults, rate-limit namespaces, problem-type URNs, migration locks, Kubernetes namespaces, image names, Prometheus job names and dashboard UIDs retain their existing identifiers. This source rename does not migrate stored data, invalidate tokens, or reset throttling state. Change those identifiers separately only with an application-specific migration plan. Historical review records and the original license notice are preserved."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Application branding","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#application-branding","Text":"APP_NAME must be nonblank and supplies API titles, the log viewer and mail names, as well as telemetry service identity. Empty MAIL_FROM_NAME and MAIL_BRAND_NAME inherit it; explicit mail overrides continue to work. The default logo is now empty, so mail renders text branding without fetching placeholder artwork. Remove old example mail-name overrides if you want them to follow your application name. For Go configuration callers, pass cfg.App.AppName to cfg.Mail.Brand(...), SMTPConfig(...) and Validate(...). Inheritance is resolved during translation, so changing an app name after config.Defaults() does not leave stale mail defaults. Standalone api.New falls back to “Application API”; app always supplies its own title. Standalone log viewers can set logviewer.Config.AppName. These changes require no retained-state migration. If you change APP_NAME, update telemetry filters and dashboards that select its previous service identity."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Platform and Identity ownership","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#platform-and-identity-ownership","Text":"The authenticated principal moved from internal/modules/shared/contracts/auth to internal/platform/auth. Update imports; its user/session fields are unchanged. Platform packages no longer import any business definitions. Identity now registers the user media, notification and push-subscription HTTP operations. Supply constructed Resources.Media and Resources.Notifier before calling identity.Register. Custom HTTP composition can use identityhttp.RegisterRoutes with Routes.Media, Routes.Notifications and Routes.Auth. Omitted capability fields omit their routes. Keep the protected API authenticated; subscription registration also rejects a missing AuthService. Remove calls to the former media/notification RegisterHTTP helpers and RegisterSubscriptionsHTTP. Platform media's RegisterContentHTTP remains for public/signed content. Notification integration.Register now takes only the notifier and targets; the subscription callback was removed. The Identity handler calls AuthService.SubscribePush directly. Custom non-Identity code can still use Notifier.Subscribe, with its own recipient authorization and session coordination. HTTP paths, operation IDs, payloads and authentication requirements are unchanged. No database, task, event or retained-state migration is needed for this move."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Response metadata moves to headers","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#response-metadata-moves-to-headers","Text":"Success and problem bodies no longer include request_id or api_version. Read X-Request-ID and X-API-Version instead. These headers were already present and remain available for successes, errors, empty responses and streams. Capture the request ID with client-side errors so logs can still be correlated. This is a breaking HTTP body and OpenAPI schema change. Update clients to read headers before rolling out the server change; they can use those headers with both the old and new server. Regenerate clients that rely on the removed body fields. Custom CORS configurations must expose both headers to browser clients; the supplied defaults already do. No database or retained-state migration is needed. For customized Go response code, response.Metadata was removed. Optional Meta now belongs directly to response.Envelope[T] and response.Problem; replace embedded Metadata literals with the direct field. Custom middleware using response.WithMetadata(ctx, id, version) should use response.WithRequestID(ctx, id) and continue setting the response headers. Ordinary handlers still use response.OK, Created, Accepted, WithMeta and response.Error unchanged."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Optional local log viewer","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#optional-local-log-viewer","Text":"The viewer is disabled by default. Set LOG_VIEWER_ENABLED=true with LOG_VIEWER_USERNAME and LOG_VIEWER_PASSWORD to mount /admin/logs/ on the application HTTP process. No collector or migration is required. Applications porting custom logging construction can install the viewer's WrapHandler through logging.Config.WrapHandler; keep the manager's handler intact so named-channel selection continues to work. Read the scope and limits before relying on live or file history across processes."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Apply an upstream update","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#apply-an-upstream-update","Text":""},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Version 0.1 hardening changes","Parent":"Apply an upstream update","URL":"upgrading.html#version-01-hardening-changes","Text":"Port internal/platform/adminhttp, queue-dashboard wrapping, and the log viewer integration together. Business CORS now excludes /admin; administrative reads, preflights and mutations reject cross-origin browser requests. Preserve Host at TLS termination and update clients that embedded dashboards across origins. Port the subscription-pruning query and its concurrency regression. It skips locked registrations and rechecks staleness, preserving concurrent refreshes. No schema migration, task-payload change or queue drain is required. Deploy the corrected maintenance worker before resuming scheduled pruning on older builds. Rename calls to Notifier.Delivery to Notifier.GetDelivery. The returned metadata, HTTP operations and stored delivery representation are unchanged. Configure HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES only for a secured, sanitized ingress path. Empty preserves direct-peer behavior. Application HTTP/OTP limits now use api.ClientIP; api.PeerIP still returns the immediate connection peer. Behind an enabled proxy policy, clients get separate budgets instead of sharing the ingress address. Existing session/token formats remain unchanged. Compose now explicitly enables dependency readiness, matching the full example and Kubernetes profile. A database or required Redis outage makes /readyz unavailable; /healthz stays live. Update probe configuration before rollout. Release images accept VERSION and REVISION build arguments. Update the custom build tooling when renaming the Go module; the supplied Dockerfile derives its linker package path from go.mod. OpenAPI and CLI/startup output share the release identity; X-API-Version remains v1. Delivery records and media tombstones have no automatic purge in version 0.1. Follow the retention contract before implementing application-specific erasure or expiration."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Update workflow","Parent":"Apply an upstream update","URL":"upgrading.html#update-workflow","Text":"Identify the change. Compare your last incorporated upstream tag/commit with the target release. Read all intervening release notes and security advisories, including affected versions, configuration changes and migration instructions. Port it on a branch. Review the upstream patch against your customized code. Apply the relevant implementation, tests and dependency changes, adapting import paths and local business behavior. A clean cherry-pick alone is not proof that a fix works in your application. Plan retained-state changes. Add new migrations instead of editing ones already applied. Account for queued task payloads, stored notification intent, event contracts and old/new worker compatibility. Follow the release's rollout order; stop incompatible producers or drain old work when its instructions require that. Verify your result. Run regression tests, the PostgreSQL/Redis integration gate, builds and the dependency vulnerability scan. Check the providers and deployment targets your application actually uses. Rehearse schema and data changes on a disposable restored copy before production deployment. Record and deploy. Record the target upstream release, patches incorporated, any deferred changes and verification results. Deploy with the migration and recovery plan appropriate to your application. Retain its own release artifacts. Keep security corrections traceable even when you port them without adopting all other upstream changes. Upstream maintains the latest release; you own fixes to customized or older application copies. Merely running go get -u does not update copied template source."},{"Title":"Adoption and updates","Section":"Getting started","Heading":"Prepare a template release","Parent":"","URL":"upgrading.html#prepare-a-template-release","Text":"Maintainers publish migration notes alongside each release. Use the compatibility policy to cover API, configuration, schema, task and event changes, then record candidate verification in release readiness. Enable and verify the private security reporting channel before inviting public adoption."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html","Text":"Business modules own operations and data access. App assembly connects their explicit dependencies; reusable packages own technical behavior. The project conventions define the current defaults and ownership rules."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Find a capability","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html#find-a-capability","Text":"cmd/ app/ HTTP, workers and local subscribers worker/ Workers and local subscribers scheduler/ Recurring enqueue only cli/ Console entry point labena/ Template installer; omitted from generated applications internal/ app/ Composition, process selection and lifecycle ownership config/ Typed environment settings and owner translations modules/ identity/ Accounts and authentication shared/ Definitions shared across business modules entities/ Owned business entities, including accepted Bun mappings enums/ Business values grouped by concept events/ Published business event contracts and occurrence metadata domain/ Common entity identity and value objects migrations/ Global and published infrastructure SQL platform/ auth/ Authenticated caller identity, independent of business entities notification/ JSON notifications, email and durable per-channel delivery media/ Public/private assets and storage recovery lifecycle/ Process readiness, ordered drain and resource cleanup dependencies/ Shared resources and borrowed registration targets api/ HTTP decoding, responses, middleware and health routes mail/ Message types, SMTP, tracing and optional queued sending messaging/ Watermill GoChannel integration cli/ Console and scheduled-command integration seeders/ Explicit seed operations pkg/ asynqx/ Native Asynq integration and middleware database/ Connections, transactions and telemetry migrate/ Reusable Goose registration and execution cache/ Cache contracts, drivers and telemetry logging/ Structured logging and context correlation tests/ App, cross-module and queue integration suites examples/ Standalone capability examples deployments/ Local and deployment configuration docs/ Guide sources and static site This is a map of the main responsibilities, not an exhaustive package listing. modules groups business code without discovery or a registration catalog. modules/shared holds definitions and is not registered as a module. Platform packages never import business modules, including their shared definitions. Shared definitions import neither those modules, platform packages nor app. Notifications implements database, email and push delivery; Media implements uploads, public/private access, internal/direct URLs and deletion recovery. SMS and media conversions remain extensions."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Package index","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html#package-index","Text":"Use the existing capability directly when it fits. App and module assembly pass borrowed resources; independent callers construct only the collaborators they need and own any resources they open. internal packages are usable within a copied application template, not as public libraries imported by an unrelated Go module. Application or platform package Responsibility and guide internal/app, platform/lifecycle Construction, process ownership and shutdown internal/app/config Typed configuration and defaults dependencies Borrowed Resources and registration Targets api Routing, validation, middleware and responses auth Transport-independent authenticated user/session principal cli Cobra commands and scheduled commands mail, mail/integration SMTP and optional queued mail; integration installs the existing worker handler media, media/integration, media/migrations Media assets, transport registration and owned SQL notification, notification/integration, notification/migrations Channel intent and delivery, transports and owned SQL messaging, messaging/middleware Local typed events, native Watermill execution and instrumentation seeders Explicit named seed operations testdb Disposable integration schemas; test-only, never a production dependency internal/reference Private syntax checks shared by Media and Notifications; not authorization or a model superclass Reusable package Responsibility and usage apperror Safe application errors and inspectable causes asynqx, asynqx/middleware Native tasks, routing, worker/scheduler lifetime and instrumentation cache and its memory/database/redis drivers Bounded cache operations; backend semantics remain explicit database, database/migrate Connections, transactions and Goose migrations hasher Standalone string hashing; see pkg/hasher/README.md; Identity does not use passwords httpserver HTTP lifetime and upgraded-connection drain httpclient Tagged outbound requests, body encodings, middleware and provider integrations logging Named log outputs and context correlation mapper Typed collection transformations; inspect with go doc ./pkg/mapper mailview Embedded HTML/text rendering without a mail transport metrics, tracing Optional OpenTelemetry providers with explicit ownership otp, token Cryptographic code/token primitives; Identity owns account policy and session records pagination Bounded page inputs and responses push Independent Expo sending and receipt lookup ratelimit Shared Redis admission; Identity's usage redis Explicit Redis connection setup; see pkg/redis/README.md; queue/cache/limiter ownership remains separate storage Native Go CDK buckets; Media adds metadata and recovery For package-level APIs, run go doc ./internal/platform/notification or go doc ./pkg/storage from the repository root. These commands inspect source without constructing the application. Package READMEs contain additional native usage; they are not all published as separate website pages."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Follow startup","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html#follow-startup","Text":"Read Responsibility cmd/app/main.go Process entry point and selected services internal/app/app.go Public entry points and private application state internal/app/infrastructure.go Acquire resources and register their cleanup internal/app/auth.go Construct authentication and bind notification delivery's active-session check internal/app/modules.go Call business registration functions internal/app/open.go Initialize the application, bind security operations and clean up failures internal/app/processes.go Select execution, readiness dependencies and shutdown phases internal/app/schedules.go Declare recurring timing and conditions internal/app/console.go Execute commands, migrations and table publication internal/platform/media/library.go Construct and use the media Library internal/platform/media/integration/register.go Register a constructed Library with selected transports internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/routes.go Register account, user media, notification and session-bound subscription endpoints internal/platform/lifecycle/lifecycle.go Supervise readiness, staged drain and resource cleanup Configuration loads before serving. Registration does not perform I/O or start workers. Open functions acquire connections; New functions assemble borrowed dependencies. App owns cleanup. Use the documented constructor and registration contracts for each capability. All CLI commands, including help, construct the application before execution. See lifecycle and registration."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Grow a module only when needed","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html#grow-a-module-only-when-needed","Text":"The first module tutorial starts with one file. A module with persistence and several transports can grow into: internal/modules/\u003cmodule\u003e/ register.go Internal construction and all registrations domain/ Policies and operation errors service/ service.go Public business-service interfaces user.go, auth.go Private implementations and constructors verification.go Related methods on the same service receiver session.go Substantial authentication use cases repository/ repository.go Focused persistence interfaces user.go, ... Private repositories, constructors and queries transaction.go Shared transaction helpers migrations/ Embedded module-owned SQL jobs/ Native Asynq task types, payloads and constructors notifications/ Business notification content and channel choices mailviews/ Email content using the shared branded layout integrations/ External provider contracts and implementations delivery/ http/ Requests, responses, routes and handlers cli/ Commands events/ Event handlers jobs/ Queue handlers Create only the directories your behavior needs. A matching interface for every struct, a module registry, and a generic repository are not required. Service and repository directories contain production code only; Identity's public-behavior and persistence tests live under tests/identity."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Preserve ownership across shared definitions","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html#preserve-ownership-across-shared-definitions","Text":"Business entities and enums belong in shared; each business module retains ownership of its operations and writes. Identity follows this convention. Platform capabilities own their records, inputs, statuses, errors and storage contracts inside their packages. Mail, Media and Notifications register generic transports through integration packages; only persisted capabilities own migrations. These cores import neither app nor Resources. Identity owns user-specific media and notification HTTP operations. The generic public/signed media content route remains in platform; no platform package imports shared business definitions. Entities may carry Bun mappings where the shapes agree. Do not duplicate an entity merely to populate a models directory. Published business event contracts and occurrence metadata live in internal/modules/shared/events. Producers own the meaning and version; consumers own handlers in delivery/events. Domain errors remain beside their owning module, entity or platform capability, using pkg/apperror for common classification and safe codes. Each definition has an owner. That module controls writes, queries, mapping and invariants. Shared packages contain neither module services nor configuration or infrastructure setup. Cross-module SQL reads remain deliberate repository-level coupling, not permission to bypass another module's operations. pkg must not import internal. Put reusable technical behavior there; put application assembly in internal/app and shared technical capabilities in internal/platform."},{"Title":"Directory structure","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Locate a change","Parent":"","URL":"structure.html#locate-a-change","Text":"Change Location HTTP input/output Module delivery/http Business operation or its inputs Module service Query or persistence mapping Owner's repository Capability entity, input, error or status Owning platform package Business event definition internal/modules/shared/events Job constructor / handler Module jobs / delivery/jobs Notification content / mail view Module notifications / mailviews Event handler Module delivery/events Recurring timing internal/app/schedules.go Queue routing and priorities internal/app/config/queues.go Infrastructure acquisition App assembly using pkg constructors Environment setting internal/app/config Module SQL internal/modules/\u003cmodule\u003e/migrations Register module migration sources through targets.Migrations in register.go. Module and shared SQL use one Goose history with globally unique timestamp versions. Preserve applied migrations and add new versions for schema changes. Normal application startup never runs DDL. See migrations for ordering and rollout, and shared types for definition ownership."},{"Title":"Entry points","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"entry-points.html","Text":"Application executables load the same typed settings and use one application assembly path. Each main chooses its processes explicitly in Go. The separate cmd/labena installer creates new applications and is not part of their runtime. See creating an application."},{"Title":"Entry points","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Select a process","Parent":"","URL":"entry-points.html#select-a-process","Text":"Entry point Runs go run ./cmd/app HTTP, queue workers, notification dispatch and local event consumers go run ./cmd/worker Queue workers, notification dispatch and local event consumers go run ./cmd/scheduler Central recurring schedules; workers may run elsewhere go run ./cmd/cli \u003ccommand\u003e One command, then cleanup go run ./cmd/cli serve HTTP and workers, as selected in cmd/cli/main.go APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/app For recurring maintenance, run a separate terminal with the same settings: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/scheduler Run exactly one scheduler per schedule set. Asynq scheduling has no distributed leader election. Ordinary app and worker replicas do not start schedules."},{"Title":"Entry points","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Define a server entry point","Parent":"","URL":"entry-points.html#define-a-server-entry-point","Text":"This is the complete cmd/app/main.go: package main import ( \"context\" \"fmt\" \"os\" \"os/signal\" \"syscall\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/app\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/app/config\" ) func main() { ctx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM) defer stop() if err := run(ctx); err != nil { fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, err) os.Exit(1) } } func run(ctx context.Context) error { cfg, err := config.Load() if err != nil { return err } return app.Serve(ctx, cfg, app.Processes{HTTP: true, Workers: true}) } The executable owns configuration loading, signal handling and exit status. Serve validates, assembles, runs and cleans up before returning. To make this executable HTTP-only, change its explicit selection to: return app.Serve(ctx, cfg, app.Processes{HTTP: true}) Then run cmd/worker separately against the same queue Redis. HTTP and workers include local event consumers automatically. GoChannel does not share events between processes; each producing process keeps its own consumers. A zero Processes value is an error for serving. Environment settings cannot change the selection. Existing deployments should remove APP_ROLES, QUEUE_SCHEDULER_ENABLED and QUEUE_COMMAND_SCHEDULING_ENABLED, then choose the appropriate executable or explicit Processes value. Scheduled-command handling is always registered."},{"Title":"Entry points","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Keep process responsibilities distinct","Parent":"","URL":"entry-points.html#keep-process-responsibilities-distinct","Text":"Work HTTP selection Workers selection Scheduler selection Ordinary CLI Serve HTTP Yes No No No Consume local events Yes Yes No No Consume queued tasks No Yes No No Dispatch notification intents No Yes No Explicit command only Enqueue recurring work No No Yes No Construct configured dependencies and register modules Yes Yes Yes Yes Process selection controls execution. The module graph and command registrations remain complete for all executables."},{"Title":"Entry points","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Handle stop signals","Parent":"","URL":"entry-points.html#handle-stop-signals","Text":"Interrupt and SIGTERM cancel the invocation context. App stops admission, drains work and releases clients. Do not call os.Exit in a service or handler. The main reports errors and chooses the exit code after app has completed cleanup. See console, scheduling and application lifecycle for execution details."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html","Text":"Use app.Serve for long-running services and app.Execute for console commands. Both construct the application once and own its resources until execution finishes. flowchart TB accTitle: One owner from construction to cleanup accDescr: App validates configuration, acquires resources and registers modules before execution. Serve drains its runners; Execute finishes one command. Resources close after their users finish. Config[Load and validate settings] --\u003e Resources[Open infrastructure] Resources --\u003e Modules[Construct services and register modules] Modules --\u003e Serve[Serve: start selected runners] Modules --\u003e Execute[Execute: run one command] Serve --\u003e Drain[Stop admission and drain work] Drain --\u003e Close[Close resources in reverse order] Execute --\u003e Close"},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Supply settings and select processes","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#supply-settings-and-select-processes","Text":"Serve owns the complete invocation. The entry point supplies typed configuration, signals and exit handling: package startup import ( \"context\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/app\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/app/config\" ) func Run(ctx context.Context, cfg config.Config) error { return app.Serve(ctx, cfg, app.Processes{ HTTP: true, Workers: true, }) } Use config.Load() for environment settings or config.Defaults() as a baseline for programmatic settings. Treat settings collections as immutable during Serve. app.Execute(ctx, cfg, processes, args) owns a console invocation. Its caller loads settings just as for Serve; processes applies only to the serve command. The application instance is private; callers never coordinate Run and Close."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"What construction does","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#what-construction-does","Text":"App validates configuration, opens configured resources, then constructs authentication and registers module handlers, commands and SQL before installing the centrally declared schedules. Module Register functions construct their local repositories and services without I/O. The finished console tree and SQL history are validated before execution. Resource acquisition can contact configured infrastructure. Construction starts no listeners, task consumers or recurring jobs and applies no DDL. Opened clients may own background work, such as the tracing exporter's batch worker; app registers their cleanup as soon as acquisition succeeds. Failed construction closes everything acquired so far. Modules never acquire or close shared resources."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Startup and readiness","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#startup-and-readiness","Text":"Serving validates installed-module dependencies and the selected processes and prepares registered event subscriptions before admitting producers. It starts selected services concurrently and waits for their readiness signals when available. curl -i http://localhost:8080/healthz curl -i http://localhost:8080/readyz Endpoint Meaning /healthz The HTTP listener can respond /readyz Selected services have passed startup readiness and shutdown has not begun These endpoints are defined in internal/platform/api/health.go and registered on the application server. The app owns their readiness and stopping signals. Readiness returns 503 during startup and shutdown, and 200 after startup succeeds. By default it is not continuous database or broker monitoring. With HTTP_READINESS_DEPENDENCIES=true, each ready probe checks the configured database, queue Redis and rate-limit Redis within HTTP_READINESS_TIMEOUT (default 1s). Checks run sequentially under one deadline and at most one probe runs at a time; concurrent probes return 503. A failed check returns 503 unavailable, and the next successful probe restores 200. Cache, mail and telemetry are excluded. Ping checks establish connectivity, not schema compatibility or permission for every operation. Every app runner supplies a readiness signal. An unexpected service exit, including a nil return before cancellation, fails the run and initiates shutdown of its siblings. A panic in a runner is reported as an error."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Shutdown order","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#shutdown-order","Text":"On cancellation or service failure, the app marks readiness unavailable and drains these groups in order: Follow an orderly shutdown The next group stays available until the previous group finishes. Step through the sequence to see which dependencies still have users. Stop admission. Mark readiness unavailable. Stop HTTP, the scheduler and the notification dispatcher, and finish their admitted work. Queue workers and local event subscribers are still available. Drain workers. Stop taking new tasks and finish or cancel active handlers. Keep local subscribers and shared clients alive while handlers use them. Pending Redis tasks remain for a later worker. Drain subscribers. Once the producers have stopped, finish admitted event handlers and join their work. The app does not close clients underneath a running handler. Stop metrics. Keep scraping available through the business-service drain, then stop the metrics listener. Shutdown groups share one drain budget. Release resources. Close owned resources in reverse construction order. Cleanup has its own budget. If drain times out with handlers still running, resources they use stay open and the executable reports failure. Every service in a group receives cancellation before the app waits for that group to finish. The next group remains available until the previous group finishes. This drains active work, not the entire backlog. Durable pending messages remain for a later worker. Separate processes require deployment-level coordination; one app can only order its own services. Normal cancellation returns nil after a successful drain. Other failures remain errors."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Set time budgets","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#set-time-budgets","Text":"APP_STARTUP_TIMEOUT=30s APP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=45s APP_CLEANUP_TIMEOUT=10s Construction and runner startup each use the startup setting. Shutdown has one budget shared across its ordered groups. Resource cleanup has a separate budget. Driver and HTTP-specific timeouts still apply; allow enough deployment termination time for both drain and cleanup. Cancellation is cooperative. Handlers, runners, and cleanup functions must observe their contexts and return. A timeout does not forcibly stop a goroutine. If drain times out, app cancels all remaining runners and returns app.ErrShutdownTimeout. Resources still used by handlers stay open; the supplied executables report failure and exit. A timeout cannot forcibly stop a goroutine. Cleanup errors are preserved alongside startup or runtime failures. Modules borrow dependencies. HTTP shutdown also closes upgraded connections, including WebSockets. Keep session work inside its handler so shutdown can join it; protocol close messages remain the module's responsibility."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Bootstrap phases and extension points","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#bootstrap-phases-and-extension-points","Text":"The startup diagram above maps to a few explicit files. There is no hidden container or discovery phase between construction and execution. One private application holds configuration, resources, targets, native runners and the selected process list. open.go shows initialization and failure cleanup; infrastructure.go acquires resources and immediately records cleanup with the application's lifecycle owner. auth.go constructs authentication before protected targets. modules.go lists registration calls. Each module constructs its local services from borrowed dependencies.Resources and receives preconstructed business collaborators as explicit arguments. It registers through dependencies.Targets. App owns resources; modules borrow them. No runtime container lookup or automatic discovery is involved. To add or change Go to Infrastructure settings and defaults config/\u003ccapability\u003e.go Infrastructure construction, global middleware and cleanup app/infrastructure.go, in acquisition order under openInfrastructure Business dependencies and module registration app/modules.go and the module's Register function HTTP, task, event or command handlers The owning module's delivery packages Recurring tasks or commands internal/app/schedules.go Process services, readiness prerequisites and shutdown phases app/processes.go configureProcesses fills the application's process list without starting work. The application's run method validates serving requirements and passes that list to its lifecycle owner. HTTP and scheduled admission stop before workers, subscribers and metrics; borrowed resources close only after their users finish. Single CLI commands share the same app ownership boundary but do not start service runners. Native clients and middleware remain available at these explicit integration points."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Command startup boundaries","Parent":"Bootstrap phases and extension points","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#command-startup-boundaries","Text":"Every CLI command, including help and completion, constructs the application first. All configuration and registered migration sources must be valid. Resources checked during acquisition must be reachable; lazy clients may report connectivity errors only on first use. Queue Redis is lazy, SMTP connects when sending, and opening a remote storage client does not prove its bucket is reachable. There are no separate offline command declarations or application invocation callbacks. Handlers borrow already constructed services. App closes resources when execution returns, including errors and panics. Migrations execute only on explicit selection and use their own dedicated database session for the Goose lock/refresh contract. Queue administration shares app's queue Redis pool. cmd/app and cli serve explicitly select HTTP and workers. HTTP and workers include local event consumers automatically. cmd/worker selects workers and cmd/scheduler selects scheduling. Run one scheduler per schedule set. Production deployments must set APP_ENV=production. The messaging runner explicitly closes Watermill and joins admitted calls, including after a native shutdown timeout or partial subscription failure. An empty subscription set starts no native delivery loop. Serve and cli serve enforce the same installed-module DB requirement; route-only construction without a DB is not a serving application."},{"Title":"Application lifecycle","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Diagnose a lifecycle failure","Parent":"","URL":"application-lifecycle.html#diagnose-a-lifecycle-failure","Text":"Observation Meaning and next step Construction fails No application runners started; check the named dependency or invalid registration HTTP is not admitting traffic It waits for selected worker/subscriber startup; check their readiness /readyz becomes 503 Startup/shutdown state or an enabled dependency probe failed One runner exits unexpectedly App treats it as failure and drains its siblings Shutdown returns ErrShutdownTimeout A consumer exceeded the shared drain budget; inspect blocked work and context handling Cleanup also fails The returned error preserves cleanup alongside the original failure Use observability for correlated logs and traces, and deployment to align termination grace periods with these budgets."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html","Text":"Each business module exposes a package-level Register function. It constructs its local collaborators and installs transports once during startup. Services needed before registration are constructed by app and passed explicitly. Follow your first module for the smallest example."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Read the assembly sequence","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#read-the-assembly-sequence","Text":"App opens resources → constructs Mail, Notifier and Media → constructs the authentication service → builds authenticated HTTP targets → registers business modules and capability transports → validates commands and migration sources → installs central schedules → starts selected processes App selects the order because dependencies are explicit. There is no discovery, reflection, provider catalog or Module struct to implement."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Application composition","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#application-composition","Text":"internal/app/modules.go lists business registrations on the private application and passes their explicit collaborators. This is the complete compiled file: package app import ( \"fmt\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/service\" ) // registerModules lists business registrations in dependency order. // App translates settings and passes business collaborators explicitly. func (a *application) registerModules(authentication service.AuthService) error { if err := identity.Register(a.resources, a.targets, authentication, a.cfg.Auth.GoogleEnabled); err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"register identity: %w\", err) } return nil } infrastructure.go opens clients and constructs Mail, Notifier and Media. auth.go constructs one Identity AuthService from cfg.Auth, using existing repositories, provider integrations, AuthOptions and domain policies. open.go passes that service to the API's existing Authenticator interface and to Identity's Register. The application stores clients in a.resources and registries in a.targets. Infrastructure setup creates the native job and event registries there, and workers consume those same instances. Console execution uses a.targets.RootCommand. Modules borrow these two bundles; they never receive the application itself. Identity registers user media, notification and push-subscription HTTP routes using the available Media and Notifier resources. Its push handler calls the supplied AuthService's SubscribePush operation directly, preserving the session transaction and lock. Disabled authentication supplies a nil authenticator, so protected routes fail closed; a missing AuthService also rejects subscription registration. There is no HTTP fallback to standalone notifier registration. App still supplies notification delivery's active-session check. Platform registration installs generic media content serving, commands, tasks and migrations. It does not register user-specific HTTP routes or import business definitions. The principal contract lives in internal/platform/auth. Environment fields, decoding and configuration validation live in app/config. There is no duplicate Identity Config or cfg.Identity() conversion. The domain owns session policies, and services validate their collaborators. Business modules never import app configuration; app passes values or constructed services."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Business modules","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#business-modules","Text":"Identity accepts authentication and the Google route selection explicitly: func Register(resources dependencies.Resources, targets dependencies.Targets, authentication service.AuthService, googleEnabled bool) error This is the function signature; the implementation is in internal/modules/identity/register.go. It constructs the account collaborators as local variables: users, err := service.NewUserService(repository.NewUserRepository(resources.DB)) if err != nil { return err } This function-body fragment uses Identity's repository/service imports. The repository borrows the app database; the service borrows the repository. Neither owns the pool's lifetime. Registered handlers retain the same service instance; a Module struct is unnecessary. Attach available transports with those local variables: if targets.RootCommand != nil { targets.RootCommand.AddCommand(modulecli.New(users)) } if targets.Migrations != nil { if err := targets.Migrations.Register(\"internal/modules/identity/migrations\", migrations.Files); err != nil { return err } } This is an illustrative registration excerpt. Identity's full function also registers welcome handlers, authentication routes and maintenance commands."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Choose the correct target","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#choose-the-correct-target","Text":"You are registering… Target Native action Public HTTP operation HTTP huma.Register Session-protected operation AuthenticatedHTTP huma.Register CLI group RootCommand AddCommand Task handler TaskRouter Asynq Handle or HandleFunc Event handler EventRouter and EventSubscriber Watermill AddConsumerHandler Embedded SQL Migrations Register(source, files) Named seed operation Seeders Register Schedulable CLI action ScheduledCommands Register a fresh command factory Targets are borrowed startup registries, not dependencies looked up by services. For clients and collaborators, use Resources."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Required and optional targets","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#required-and-optional-targets","Text":"A nil target means omit that transport. It does not mean disable validation for an enabled transport. For example, a registered event consumer needs both its subscriber and the queue client used by its handler. Validate required collaborators before mutating any registry. Register sequentially, before runners start. Return the original error with useful context. Discard the partial application if registration fails. Do not retry Register against partly mutated targets. Native routers can reject duplicate handlers or conflicting paths, and registration is not transactional."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Commands and migrations","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#commands-and-migrations","Text":"Register commands with already constructed services. Use cmd.Context() during execution; do not construct a second app from a command callback. Help intentionally constructs the same app. See console commands. Register embedded SQL; execute it later through migrate up. Registration never runs DDL. Module and platform SQL share one Goose history, so versions must be globally unique. See migrations."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Keep recurring timing centralized","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#keep-recurring-timing-centralized","Text":"A module owns task handlers and command factories. App owns recurring timing, feature conditions and enqueue options in internal/app/schedules.go. Schedules are installed after registration so their destinations exist. Adding a task does not schedule it. Adding a schedule does not start its scheduler; that process is selected separately. See scheduling."},{"Title":"Module registration","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Where a new feature changes code","Parent":"","URL":"module-registration.html#where-a-new-feature-changes-code","Text":"Change Required assembly edits Add an endpoint to an existing module Its HTTP registration and handler Add a command or job Its delivery package and module Register Add a business module Its Register function, plus one app composition entry Add recurring maintenance Register its handler locally; add timing centrally Add infrastructure Typed config, app acquisition/cleanup, explicit consumer wiring Registration starts no network work, listeners or business operations. App keeps resources alive until consumers drain. Continue with dependency injection and application lifecycle."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html","Text":"Pass dependencies through ordinary Go constructors. App creates shared clients; module assembly selects the clients its services need. A handler receives its operation directly, so it never needs to discover or construct dependencies."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Construction and ownership","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#construction-and-ownership","Text":"flowchart TB accTitle: Construct once, borrow explicitly accDescr: App opens and owns infrastructure, constructs capabilities and authentication, and passes shared operations into HTTP, CLI, task and event handlers. Consumers borrow dependencies and never close app resources. App[App assembly] --\u003e|Opens and owns| Resources[(Pools and clients)] Resources --\u003e Capabilities[Notifier and Media library] Resources --\u003e Module[Module registration] App --\u003e Auth[Authentication service] Auth --\u003e Module Capabilities --\u003e Module Module --\u003e Service[Business service] Service --\u003e HTTP[HTTP handler] Service --\u003e CLI[CLI command] Service --\u003e Jobs[Task and event handlers] Two assembly structs have different purposes: Struct Contains Used by dependencies.Resources Existing clients, pools and shared capabilities Module Register functions and app assembly dependencies.Targets Routers and registration destinations Module Register functions Copying Resources shares the clients; it does not clone connections. Modules never mutate its maps or close borrowed clients. Services and handlers receive specific fields rather than retaining the complete bundle. App keeps both bundles on one private application, alongside configuration, the lifecycle owner and process handles. Native registries live directly in application.targets; the worker and module handlers use the same instances. The private application is never passed into modules or platform capabilities."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Construct a service explicitly","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#construct-a-service-explicitly","Text":"Identity assembles its profile operations with ordinary constructors: repo := repository.NewUserRepository(resources.DB) users, err := service.NewUserService(repo) if err != nil { return err } This module-registration fragment imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/repository and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/service. resources is the borrowed dependencies.Resources argument. No query runs here. The same users value is passed to HTTP registration and modulecli.New(users). They call the same rules; only their input/output adaptation differs. Follow the persisted HTTP and CLI workflow to see both callers."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Service and repository contracts","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#service-and-repository-contracts","Text":"Keep public contracts in the owner's package: identity/service/ service.go UserService and AuthService interfaces user.go userService and NewUserService auth.go authService and NewAuthService session.go Authentication methods on *authService identity/repository/ repository.go Focused repository interfaces user.go userRepository and NewUserRepository session.go sessionRepository and NewSessionRepository A larger use case can live in another file on the same receiver. File organization does not require another abstraction, service instance or constructor. Multiple consumers import the same owner contract. Avoid duplicating UserService, mail.Sender or messaging.Publisher in each caller just to change the package name. Add interfaces where substitution or a stable business API is useful; concrete capabilities such as *notification.Notifier and *media.Library are intentional too."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Required arguments and options","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#required-arguments-and-options","Text":"Use explicit arguments for a small required dependency set. Use a cohesive options struct when construction has several collaborators or optional controls: notifier, err := notification.New(db, notification.Options{ Queue: queue, Mailer: mailer, MailRenderer: views, }) if err != nil { return err } This construction fragment assumes already constructed clients and imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/notification. The caller retains the resulting notifier for its operations; constructing it sends nothing. Config describes settings; Options describes constructor controls and collaborators. A business service must not import app or read environment variables to complete its construction."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Cross-module calls and transactions","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#cross-module-calls-and-transactions","Text":"Pass a business service from one module to another as an explicit assembly argument. Keep business services out of Resources. App may construct services needed before registration using owner constructors; transport handlers never construct repositories. Use a direct call when the current operation requires a result. Use an event for an independent reaction with an accepted delivery guarantee. The local event bus does not make a required operation reliable across a process crash. Interaction Ownership boundary Read/update an account Call Identity's service and apply caller authorization Store notification intent with a business change Use Notifier.Enqueue inside the same database transaction Send email or write an object External effect; cannot roll back with SQL Publish a local event after commit Commit remains valid if publication fails DB and Transactions must use the same pool. Repositories resolve their executor from context to join the transaction. Normal service operations own transaction scope; Identity's security operations have explicit exceptions for durable failed proof counters and refresh-reuse revocation. See transactions."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Context carries scope, not dependencies","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#context-carries-scope-not-dependencies","Text":"Propagate the caller's context to every operation. It carries cancellation, deadlines, transaction scope and logging/trace metadata. Inject clients through arguments instead of retrieving them from context or a global container. Handlers must finish their work before returning. Put deferred processing on the queue instead of spawning an unowned goroutine that outlives the handler."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Optional capabilities","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#optional-capabilities","Text":"Resource Disabled behavior Cache, Pusher, RateLimiter May be nil when their feature is disabled Disks Only selected disks are opened; disabled storage has no usable disk TracerProvider, MeterProvider App supplies providers; disabled telemetry is no-op Mailer Always constructed; SMTP connects on sending DB/Transactions Can be absent for limited assembly; installed modules cannot serve without them Validate the dependencies required by an enabled operation during construction. Do not defer an inevitable nil dereference until a request. A nil registration target omits a transport; it does not grant access or change a service policy."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Use a capability independently","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#use-a-capability-independently","Text":"Within this application template, instantiate a platform capability from its actual clients and options without importing app or Resources. Its integration package is needed only when registering framework transports. For reuse from another Go module, choose a pkg capability: Go's internal import rule deliberately limits platform packages. See the package index."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Optional push","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#optional-push","Text":"Resources.Pusher is the optional push.Sender; it is nil when disabled. App also passes it to Notifier for durable channel delivery. Choose direct push for an immediate provider call or notifications for persisted recipient delivery. Inject the selected collaborator into your service."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Account and authentication operations","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#account-and-authentication-operations","Text":"Identity separates profile operations (UserService) from proof/token/session operations (AuthService). Both expose owner contracts with private implementations. SignInResult carries the committed user, tokens and Created flag; HTTP maps the flag to 201/200. See Identity for account policy and its security transaction exceptions."},{"Title":"Dependency injection","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Verify wiring","Parent":"","URL":"dependency-injection.html#verify-wiring","Text":"Use narrow fakes for service decisions and real disposable backends for SQL, Redis, retries and ownership isolation. App tests call public Serve/Execute; module tests can supply only the targets they need. Keep business tests under tests/\u003cmodule\u003e. Continue with module registration, shared types, or lifecycle."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html","Text":"Shared definitions make modules agree on business identity and published contracts. They do not transfer ownership: another module can refer to a User, but Identity still controls account operations and writes."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Choose a type's home","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html#choose-a-types-home","Text":"Type Home Example Business entity and invariants internal/modules/shared/entities User, Session Business enum internal/modules/shared/enums User status, authentication platform Polymorphic type constant internal/modules/shared/entities/entity_types.go UserEntityType Published event and occurrence metadata internal/modules/shared/events UserRegistered, Event Service input/result Owning module's service UpdateProfileInput, SignInResult Repository projection Owning module's repository A query-specific result HTTP request/response Owning delivery/http Public account response Capability record/input/error Owning platform package media.Asset, notification.Record Authenticated caller internal/platform/auth Principal with user and session IDs Operation error Owning domain/capability Identity authentication failure Do not move every reusable struct into shared. media.Owner and notification.Recipient have different meanings even though both identify a business model with a type and ID."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Records and transport shapes","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html#records-and-transport-shapes","Text":"Business entities may carry Bun mappings when the persisted and business shapes agree. This avoids an otherwise identical model plus a conversion layer. Add a separate private query shape only when persistence actually differs. HTTP responses select public fields deliberately. A Bun entity's JSON tags do not prove that every field is appropriate for every caller. Service inputs likewise exclude transport concerns such as HTTP headers and Huma validation tags. Migrations change schemas; struct tags do not execute DDL. The owning module registers its migration source and the operator applies it explicitly."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Identity and references","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html#identity-and-references","Text":"Use shared entity-type constants at business call sites: owner := media.Owner{ Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: user.ID.String(), } recipient := notification.Recipient{ Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: user.ID.String(), } This reference fragment assumes an existing *entities.User and imports internal/modules/shared/entities, internal/platform/media and internal/platform/notification under the github.com/wisalgroup/labena module path. The business caller checks existence and authorization. A (type, id) value is not permission, an automatic model lookup, or an ordinary foreign key across arbitrary tables. The caller also owns deletion policy for attached records. Type names match [a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,63}. IDs are nonempty UTF-8 strings of at most 200 bytes, with no surrounding whitespace or control characters. Media and Notifications share private syntax validation while keeping their public types and errors separate."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Publish a business contract","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html#publish-a-business-contract","Text":"internal/modules/shared/events/user_registered.go defines Identity's account-created contract. Constructing an event only describes an occurrence: event := events.NewUserRegistered(user.ID) This fragment imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/events; it does not publish. The event contains UserID and metadata (ID, Type, OccurredAt). Identity owns the meaning and the versioned topic. Consumers own handlers in their own delivery/events package. Keep contracts limited to IDs and event-specific values. Do not embed complete entities or import repositories, services, application config or transports. Publication and delivery are explicit operations covered in events."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Keep errors with their behavior","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html#keep-errors-with-their-behavior","Text":"Use pkg/apperror for common classification, safe public codes and validation issues. Define the actual failure beside the module, entity or capability that owns its meaning. There is no global internal/modules/shared/errors catalog. Across a future service boundary, share serialized schemas and stable error codes. Go interface identity, database mappings and error values are not a transport contract. Extract the contracts needed by consumers rather than exporting the entire shared directory."},{"Title":"Shared types","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Common helpers","Parent":"","URL":"shared-types.html#common-helpers","Text":"shared/domain.Entity supplies UUID identity and timestamps. shared/domain/valueobjects.Email owns validated email representation and its JSON/database conversion. events.Event is plain occurrence metadata; it neither buffers nor publishes events. Technical helpers belong in pkg and must not import shared application types. For example, pagination.Pagination remains a reusable paging input; HTTP envelopes belong to internal/platform/api/response. Continue with conventions, events, and entities and queries."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html","Text":"This is the canonical architecture guide for contributors. Use these defaults for new code and refactoring. The other guides explain usage; they should not introduce competing patterns. This is a modular application template built with explicit Go composition, not a runtime dependency container."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Read this alongside the guides","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#read-this-alongside-the-guides","Text":"This page is the architecture contract. Use your first module for an introduction, module registration for assembly, and dependency injection for ownership examples. The rules below describe the current design, not a migration roadmap. Historical reviews and plans do not override current APIs or this contract."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"1. Package responsibilities","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#1-package-responsibilities","Text":"Kind Owns Examples Application composition Resource ownership, business graph and process selection internal/app Environment configuration Loading, defaults and translation to owner settings internal/app/config Business module Business rules, use cases, persistence and transports internal/modules/identity Shared business definitions Entities, enums, published events and common contracts internal/modules/shared Platform capability Shared application behavior with its own public types internal/platform/notification, media, mail Reusable package Technical behavior independent of this application pkg/database, storage, mailview, push, hasher Handlers call business services. Services call their repositories and shared capabilities. Platform capabilities compose reusable packages. App constructs and connects them. pkg must never import internal. internal/modules groups business code; it is not a Go package or a registration catalog. Its shared directory contains definitions, not a registrable module: it has no Module, New or Register. Platform packages never import internal/modules, including shared business definitions. Shared definitions must not import business modules, platform packages or app. App configuration lives in internal/app/config; owners receive translated settings, never the environment configuration package. Platform capability cores do not import app or dependencies.Resources. Their integration packages connect them to application transports and registries. Keep this separation to avoid import cycles and permit independent use. The authenticated Principal belongs to internal/platform/auth, independently of tokens, transports and business entities. Identity owns sign-in, verification and session persistence. Its HTTP delivery also owns user-specific media, notification and push-subscription endpoints, translating the principal into business owner/recipient references. Platform media integration retains generic public/signed content serving; other platform integration handles CLI, tasks and migrations without business identity assumptions. Use a direct service call for a required business operation. Use an event for a reaction with an explicitly accepted delivery guarantee. Do not introduce an event, job, repository or interface just to fill out a directory structure."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"2. One business-module shape","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#2-one-business-module-shape","Text":"internal/modules/\u003cmodule\u003e/ register.go Construction and registration service/ service.go Public service interfaces \u003csubject\u003e.go Private implementation and constructor \u003cuse_case\u003e.go Substantial method on the same receiver, when useful repository/ repository.go Public persistence interfaces \u003csubject\u003e.go Private implementation, constructor and queries delivery/ http/ Routes, requests, responses and handlers cli/ Commands events/ Event handlers jobs/ Job handlers jobs/ Task payloads, names and constructors notifications/ Named notification content and channel choices mailviews/ Email content using the shared renderer integrations/ External provider integration domain/ Shared module policies and operation errors, if needed migrations/ Module-owned SQL tests/\u003cmodule\u003e/ Business integration and public-behavior tests Create only the directories required by actual behavior. A transport-only module can start with a single Register function in register.go. A larger use case can occupy another file without creating another service. Keep nested module folders; do not flatten them or introduce a service per table or endpoint."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"3. Type ownership","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#3-type-ownership","Text":"Type Location Business entity and its invariants internal/modules/shared/entities Shared business enum internal/modules/shared/enums Polymorphic business type name internal/modules/shared/entities/entity_types.go Operation input or result Owning module's service package SQL projection or repository result contract Owning module's repository package; export only what its service must consume HTTP request or response Owning module's delivery/http package Authenticated caller identity internal/platform/auth.Principal Published business event internal/modules/shared/events; publishing module owns meaning and version Common event occurrence metadata internal/modules/shared/events Domain error Owning module's domain package or the entity that enforces the invariant Capability record, input, status or error Owning platform package Shared business entities are an intentional convention of this monolith. Their owning module still controls writes and business operations. A shared type is not permission to bypass its owner. Cross-module SQL reads must be deliberate and remain in repositories. Published event contracts share internal/modules/shared/events so consumers can import their definitions without depending on a producer's implementation. Keep this package limited to payloads, topic constants, occurrence metadata and constructors. Use IDs and event-specific values instead of embedding entities; do not import services, repositories, platform capabilities, application config or transports. The publishing module controls schema compatibility and the topic version. Handlers remain in each consuming module's delivery/events package. Domain errors stay with the behavior that defines them. Reuse pkg/apperror for classification and safe error codes; do not create a global shared/errors catalog. Across a future service boundary, publish stable schemas and codes rather than sharing implementation errors or assuming Go error identity survives transport. Entities may carry Bun mappings when the shapes agree. Do not add a parallel models package and conversion layer by default. Use a separate private query shape only when persistence actually differs. Viewer-dependent values such as unread counts are operation results, not intrinsic entity state. HTTP inputs own Huma schema/validation tags. Typed handlers register once through huma.Register for routing and OpenAPI. The platform runs Huma on http.ServeMux; modules receive native Huma groups. Authentication and authorization read the principal from context.Context. Services validate business rules for all callers, including jobs and CLI. Responses explicitly select public fields; do not expose an entity merely because it has JSON tags. HTTP resolves api.ClientIP once using explicit trusted proxy CIDRs; modules never reinterpret forwarding headers. Empty trust uses the immediate connection. api.PeerIP always retains that transport peer. Client addresses can select rate budgets but never establish a principal or grant authorization. Administrative HTTP endpoints reuse platform/adminhttp and stay outside business CORS policy. Within a business module's delivery/http, keep handlers in *_handler.go, request shapes and input mappings in requests.go, response shapes and projections in responses.go, and typed operation registration in routes.go. Media's Asset and Notification's Record stay with their capabilities. Shared entities is not a destination for every struct. Keep recipient, owner and audience semantics explicit; a (type, id) reference does not authorize access. Use the shared entity-type constants at business call sites, not module-local literals. Media owners and notification recipients share one reference syntax: type names match [a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,63}; IDs are nonempty UTF-8 strings of at most 200 bytes, without surrounding whitespace or control characters. The implementation lives in platform/internal/reference, not a new public base type. Capability-specific errors and authorization remain with each owner."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"4. Contracts and names","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#4-contracts-and-names","Text":"Role Convention Example Business service interface \u003cSubject\u003eService UserService Private service implementation \u003csubject\u003eService userService Constructor beside implementation New\u003cSubject\u003eService NewUserService Repository interface \u003cSubject\u003eRepository UserRepository Private repository implementation \u003csubject\u003eRepository userRepository Constructor beside implementation New\u003cSubject\u003eRepository NewUserRepository Operation input \u003cOperation\u003eInput SendMessageInput HTTP shapes \u003cOperation\u003eRequest, \u003cSubject\u003eResponse SendMessageRequest Failure Err\u003cMeaning\u003e ErrNotFound Name values for what they contain: account identifiers use UserID, event timestamps use OccurredAt, and a notifier dependency is Notifier. Named storage connections are Disks, with DefaultDisk selecting one. Use SignIn consistently for the operation; SocialSignInInput contains provider sign-in context. Use Get for one persisted record and List for collections, including paginated ones. Keep pagination.Pagination for paging parameters and pagination.Page[T] for results. Use Valid() bool for membership/validity checks, Validate() error for diagnostic validation, and MarkReady/MarkDeleted for persisted state changes. Name same-typed interface parameters so their order and purpose are clear in editor hints. Transport implementation types stay private when registration functions are the public API. Request Input(...) methods keep mapping beside their request types; handlers supply trusted context and perform authorization. Public business contracts live in service/service.go and repository/repository.go, not separate contracts packages or shared contract catalogs. Multiple consumers import the owner's contract. Keep each contract cohesive; do not create an interface per consumer or mirror every private helper. Platform capabilities may expose meaningful concrete types such as Notifier, and Library. Reuse existing package contracts such as mail.Sender, mail.Mailer and messaging.Publisher; do not redeclare them in each module. These two shapes are intentional: business APIs and reusable capability APIs have different owners. Use descriptive operation verbs. Within an authentication service, RequestSignInCode is clearer than Send. Avoid Impl, Manager, generic Service and generic Repository when a business subject supplies a useful name. Keep established third-party names instead of wrapping them solely to rename them. Config describes settings; Options holds optional constructor controls and collaborators. Required small dependency sets are explicit constructor arguments. Configuration translation helpers name the returned owner settings, such as ServerConfig, ConnectionConfig and SMTPConfig; native Options types retain their names. Media settings use media.Config and media.DiskConfig. A larger cohesive options struct is acceptable; do not introduce another resource bundle for every module. Use New\u003cSubject\u003e for named event, task and notification constructors, such as events.NewUserRegistered, jobs.NewWelcome and notifications.NewWelcome. mailviews.NewWelcome constructs its email content. Construction alone performs no publication or delivery; callers use Publish, Enqueue or Send explicitly. Infallible constructors return the value directly. Renaming a constructor does not change its serialized fields, notification type or stable occurrence key."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"5. Construction, registration and lifetime","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#5-construction-registration-and-lifetime","Text":"New… validates and assembles without network I/O or background work. Open… acquires owned resources, including background exporters, and makes cleanup explicit. Register… may construct local collaborators and installs definitions before execution; it performs no I/O or business work. Run(ctx) owns execution until completion or cancellation. Close… releases owned resources after their users finish; retain native terms such as tracing’s Shutdown. Module assembly borrows dependencies.Resources and passes specific dependencies to services. Business services do not retain the complete bundle. App fills it before module construction; modules never mutate it or close its clients. Business-to-business dependencies are explicit arguments, not entries in Resources. Each business module exposes a package-level Register function in register.go. It constructs module-local collaborators and installs selected transports once. There is no Module struct or duplicate module configuration schema. App owns the environment configuration and passes specific settings and business services as explicit arguments. Collaborators needed before registration, such as Identity's AuthService, are constructed in app using their existing owner constructors. Services keep validating their policies and dependencies. App registers each module once; registration does not start work or acquire resources. Invalid runtime configuration and missing capability dependencies fail at assembly, without a late nil dereference. Native transport helpers retain explicit programmer preconditions; panics are reserved for misuse such as invalid static defaults. Disabled capabilities have documented nil/no-op behavior. tracing.Open owns a batch worker and requires Shutdown. messaging.RegisterRunner installs router lifecycle hooks before handlers; its returned runner starts work only in Run. The app's bounded messaging.LocalBus rejects overloaded publication and settles failed deliveries after one retry cycle; it remains best-effort. cli.ScheduledCommands has a usable zero value, implements the native Asynq handler, and is installed explicitly with mux.Handle(cli.CommandTask, commands). There is no second scheduling registry or wrapper constructor to learn. Application-specific configuration can translate pure owner policies, such as cfg.Auth.SessionPolicy(). The owner defines validation; configuration and service constructors reuse that validation. Owners never import application config. Targets contains borrowed Huma APIs and native command/job/event registries. Nil targets omit unused transports. Validate required collaborators before mutation, register sequentially, and discard partial application construction on error. No discovery, reflection, service locator, module startup hooks or lazy application callbacks are needed. The intended registration location is register.go for HTTP, CLI, jobs, events, migrations. Scheduling is the intentional exception: app/schedules.go centrally declares recurring timing, feature conditions and enqueue options using existing asynqx.TaskSchedule and asynqx.CommandSchedule definitions. Modules retain task handlers and command factories. App validates and installs schedules after module registration, and owns scheduler execution and operational configuration. App owns resource acquisition, process readiness, ordered drain and reverse-order cleanup. Each executable loads configuration and supplies explicit app.Processes flags. HTTP and Workers automatically include local event consumers; Workers also includes notification dispatch. Scheduler is a separate selection. No process selection comes from environment variables. The app exposes only Serve and Execute; Execute uses the supplied selection only for its serve command. Zero processes is valid for ordinary commands and rejected when serving. internal/app/modules.go contains only explicit business registration calls. auth.go constructs Identity's AuthService and binds notification delivery's active-session check. Identity's HTTP delivery calls that AuthService directly for session-aware push registration. open.go owns cross-cutting security wiring and failure cleanup; infrastructure.go opens clients, constructs capabilities and registers their transports. One private application holds configuration, Resources, Targets, native runner handles and the selected process list. Registries live in Targets; console execution uses Targets.RootCommand. App helpers operate on this state, with no separate platform, console or process-plan state objects. Only app accesses the application; modules borrow Resources and Targets and receive explicit business collaborators. Business modules and platform packages never import internal/app or its configuration package. Application CLI help intentionally constructs the configured application too. The standalone cmd/labena project installer owns no application state and never loads runtime configuration; its help is independent of application assembly. Missing required resources fail construction or serving validation; intentionally disabled features have documented unavailable behavior. Preserve supported offline assembly without treating it as permission to serve with missing required dependencies."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"6. Transactions, errors and delivery","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#6-transactions-errors-and-delivery","Text":"Normal business transactions belong to service operations. Repositories resolve the executor from context and join the same database transaction. DB and Transactions must use the same pool. External network or blob operations do not become atomic by receiving a transaction-bearing context. Framework publication, app queueing, mail and external notification/media effects reject that context using database.RequireNoTransaction. Native clients bypass this policy. The guard does not provide a generic outbox; post-commit publication can still fail. Identity's security operations are explicit exceptions: failed-proof counters and refresh-reuse revocation may need to commit even when authentication is denied. Preserve their repository-owned transaction rules. Do not mechanically wrap them in an outer transaction. Identity creates accounts through authenticated OTP or provider proofs, in the same transaction as the session. SignInResult distinguishes a new account from a returning one and includes the user. Registration events publish after commit; publication failure never turns a committed signup into a failed response. HTTP owns peer extraction and formatting; the service owns the sequence. Mechanism Meaning Direct service call Required operation; caller receives its outcome Local Watermill event Process-local, best-effort reaction Asynq enqueue Background work after queue acceptance; not atomic with business SQL Notification Enqueue SQL delivery intent that can commit with the business change Notification Send Immediate attempt; caller handles uncertainty Use stable occurrence keys where retries must converge. Persisted intent is not proof of delivery, provider acceptance is not user receipt, and socket delivery is not a read acknowledgment. Do not hide these distinctions behind a universal Dispatch abstraction. Module notifications own content; platform delivery owns channel retries and receipts. Use apperror for intentional safe application failures. Wrap operational errors with context and %w; preserve errors.Is/As. Typed HTTP handlers return response.Error(ctx, err) to translate service failures into RFC 9457 problem details. Register operations directly with huma.Register. Huma middleware uses response.WriteError; native Huma status errors can be returned directly. Application JSON responses use response.JSON[T] and its status constructors. The typed result lives in data, alongside optional operation-specific meta. Errors retain flat Huma problem details, an errors list and optional meta. Use apperror.InvalidFields for multiple application validation messages. Bodyless responses use response.Empty; custom outputs and streams retain native Huma types. Declare success statuses on the operation too, so OpenAPI matches runtime behavior. Server-generated request IDs and API versions are returned only in X-Request-ID and X-API-Version headers, including for errors, empty responses and streams. Request IDs remain available in request context and logs; clients capture the header for diagnostics. Infrastructure handlers retain standard HTTP semantics. The optional local log viewer is a platform HTTP capability mounted by app. It borrows configured local log channels through the logging handler decorator, keeps bounded process-local copies and reads only configured log files. It owns no listener or background worker, and uses separate Basic Auth credentials. Services do not write HTTP responses or expose provider secrets. Routine logs and spans use bounded error categories and safe public codes, never arbitrary driver/provider error text or payloads. Attempt spans describe attempts; the outer delivery boundary logs the final retry-cycle failure once. A committed business operation followed by an optional failure needs an explicit result; it must not look like the whole operation rolled back."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Verification and change discipline","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#verification-and-change-discipline","Text":"The support policy defines template adoption, compatibility and release-note requirements. Document changes that adopters must port into their customized applications, including security corrections and retained-state migrations; use the update guide for the consumer workflow. Keep service/repository packages and app free of test files. Put business integration and public-behavior tests in tests/\u003cmodule\u003e; reusable package and platform tests may stay beside their owners. Moving tests must preserve coverage. Protect behavior, transaction boundaries and authorization. Architecture checks should enforce dependency direction and ownership, not arbitrary file counts. Examples must compile against shipped APIs; proposed APIs must be labeled. For a normal feature: define the shared entity, implement repository behavior, implement the service operation, add the transport and register locally. Add a new abstraction only when it removes repeated knowledge or supports an actual variation. Prefer one coherent convention over another convenience wrapper."},{"Title":"Project conventions","Section":"Architecture","Heading":"Automated checks","Parent":"","URL":"conventions.html#automated-checks","Text":"go test ./tests/architecture scans production imports in internal, pkg and examples, including build-tagged files. It checks that: pkg does not import application internals. Business modules and platform packages do not import app or environment config. Platform packages do not import any business modules, including shared definitions. Shared definitions do not import platform or business implementations. Business services/repositories do not import app assembly, Resources/Targets, HTTP adapters, delivery packages, Huma, Gin or Cobra. Repositories do not import business services. Mail, Media, Notification and Messaging cores do not import business modules, app or Resources/Targets; application wiring belongs in their integrations. Production packages do not import Gin, Gin middleware or the Huma Gin adapter. Independent mail/storage examples may load app/config, but do not import app assembly. App and business service/repository packages contain no test files. The first-module tests compile the Catalog starter, exercise its route without infrastructure and ensure the copyable guide matches the compiled source. Existing CI runs both checks through go test -race ./.... Names, file counts and every possible cross-module relationship are deliberately left to review; an import check cannot prove business correctness or safe authorization."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html","Text":"A route connects an HTTP method and path to a typed Go handler. Register it with huma.Register: the same declaration supplies routing, input validation, and OpenAPI. Modules receive ready-to-use APIs through dependencies.Targets. Start with your first module if you have not added one yet. This page explains how its HTTP routes fit together; handlers covers the code that runs after a request has been validated."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Register a route","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#register-a-route","Text":"Inside the module's Register function: if targets.HTTP != nil { huma.Register(targets.HTTP, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"catalog.greet\", Method: \"GET\", Path: \"/catalog/hello/{name}\", Summary: \"Greet a visitor\", }, func(ctx context.Context, input *GreetingRequest) (*response.JSON[GreetingResponse], error) { return response.OK(GreetingResponse{Message: \"Hello, \" + input.Name}), nil }) } Imports: context, github.com/danielgtaylor/huma/v2, and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api/response. The request and response types are: type GreetingRequest struct { Name string `path:\"name\" minLength:\"1\"` } type GreetingResponse struct { Message string `json:\"message\"` } App supplies a group prefixed with /api/v1, so this operation is available at: curl http://localhost:8080/api/v1/catalog/hello/Ada { \"data\": {\"message\": \"Hello, Ada\"} } This is an alternative registration for the existing Catalog example; do not register both versions at the same path. The complete compiling starter lives in examples/module/catalog/register.go. A nil HTTP target means that transport is omitted. Skip its registration and continue registering other module capabilities. Registration installs definitions; it starts no listeners, goroutines, or business work."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Choose a public or protected route","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#choose-a-public-or-protected-route","Text":"Target Use it for What it supplies targets.HTTP Public operations such as requesting a sign-in code The business API prefix and common policies targets.AuthenticatedHTTP Operations requiring a signed-in user The same prefix plus bearer enforcement and OpenAPI security Use the protected target for account-specific routes: if targets.AuthenticatedHTTP != nil { registerAccountRoutes(targets.AuthenticatedHTTP, users) } Here registerAccountRoutes is a module-owned helper accepting huma.API and its existing service contract. It receives the service directly; it does not look up application resources during a request. Authentication proves who is calling. The handler or service must still authorize the requested resource. See handler authorization and Identity."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Group routes and policies","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#group-routes-and-policies","Text":"Create a group for a shared prefix, tag, or policy. This example belongs in a registration helper after checking the target is non-nil: catalog := huma.NewGroup(targets.HTTP, \"/catalog\") catalog.UseSimpleModifier(func(op *huma.Operation) { op.Tags = []string{\"catalog\"} }) huma.Register(catalog, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"catalog.listProducts\", Method: \"GET\", Path: \"/products\", Summary: \"List products\", }, handler.list) handler.list is the typed handler you implement for that operation. Its effective path is /api/v1/catalog/products. Do not repeat /api/v1 in the module. Install group middleware and modifiers before registering operations. Prefer a module-owned child group when changing tags or policies, so unrelated modules do not inherit them. Use huma.Register and huma.Operation directly; there is no second framework registration API."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Describe an operation","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#describe-an-operation","Text":"The most useful operation fields are: Field When to set it OperationID A stable, unique name for generated clients and route identification Method, Path The HTTP method and resource path Summary, Description, Tags Short purpose, additional contract details, and documentation grouping DefaultStatus Fixed successful status other than the inferred default Errors Failure statuses you want explicitly listed in OpenAPI MaxBodyBytes, BodyReadTimeout Body size and read deadline appropriate to this operation Middlewares A policy belonging only to this operation For example, registering a create handler: huma.Register(catalog, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"catalog.createProduct\", Method: \"POST\", Path: \"/products\", Summary: \"Create a product\", DefaultStatus: 201, Errors: []int{401, 409, 422}, MaxBodyBytes: 4096, BodyReadTimeout: 5 * time.Second, }, handler.create) Import time for this fragment. The handler returns response.Created(result). The status constructor controls the actual response; DefaultStatus describes it in OpenAPI. For operations returning more than one successful status, see responses. Operation IDs are derived when omitted. Use explicit IDs for a public contract and keep them stable when only documentation wording changes. Huma registration and ServeMux pattern conflicts are programmer errors; validate routes in tests before serving."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Path parameters and query values","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#path-parameters-and-query-values","Text":"Use {name} for a path parameter and declare its source on the input: type ProductRequest struct { ID string `path:\"id\" minLength:\"1\" maxLength:\"200\"` Locale string `query:\"locale\" default:\"en\" enum:\"en,ar\"` } Register /products/{id}. The handler reads input.ID and input.Locale after binding and validation; it does not parse the URL again. Add body or header fields to the same input when needed. See requests."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Trailing slashes","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#trailing-slashes","Text":"Register resource paths without a trailing slash. An unregistered trailing-slash variant such as /api/v1/auth/otp/send/ redirects to /api/v1/auth/otp/send with 308, preserving the method and body. Query strings and escaped path parameters are preserved. Use the canonical path to avoid a round trip. /docs/ redirects to /docs, keeping its relative OpenAPI URL correct. Explicit trailing-slash and subtree registrations retain ServeMux semantics. Unknown paths remain 404. Repeated separators and dot segments follow Go's standard path cleaning; do not use them as alternate resource URLs."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Server setup and infrastructure","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#server-setup-and-infrastructure","Text":"api.New(api.Options{}) constructs a Huma API backed by http.ServeMux. App creates the /api/v1 group and supplies it to modules. HTTP_ADDRESS selects the listener. App owns serving, readiness, and shutdown. Route Purpose GET /healthz Liveness GET /readyz Startup, dependency, and shutdown readiness /api/v1/* Typed business operations GET /docs Live interactive API reference GET /openapi.json OpenAPI from registered operations Infrastructure handlers mount on server.Mux using standard http.Handler APIs. Serve server so correlation, logging, recovery, and observability also cover those handlers. Serving server.Mux directly bypasses the surrounding policies. Health, metrics, dashboards, and standard 404/405 responses retain their own formats. For use without full app assembly, construct an API server directly and supply it to a standard HTTP server or pkg/httpserver. Finish registration before accepting requests."},{"Title":"Routing","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Inspect and verify routes","Parent":"","URL":"routing.html#inspect-and-verify-routes","Text":"go run ./cmd/cli route list curl --fail http://localhost:8080/openapi.json The CLI constructs the application and its configured connections; it does not start workers merely to inspect routes. The live specification reflects the running instance, so restart after Go code changes. See API reference. Symptom Check 404 after adding a route The module Register function is called; the HTTP target is non-nil; the prefix is correct 405 The path exists but the HTTP method does not match 401 The operation uses the protected target and needs a valid bearer token New route absent from /docs Restart the running binary and confirm its feature settings Duplicate registration panic One method/path is being registered more than once or patterns conflict Continue with handlers, request validation, and middleware."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html","Text":"A handler translates a validated HTTP request into a service call, then selects public response fields. Huma supplies typed inputs and outputs; business services receive ordinary context.Context and service-owned inputs. The usual flow is short: Bound request → trusted caller context → authorization → service → public response Start with the Catalog route for a database-free example. The profile update below shows the same pattern with a real service."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Register and handle once","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#register-and-handle-once","Text":"Identity keeps its handler private in internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/user_handler.go. This is its profile-update flow: func (h *userHandler) updateProfile(ctx context.Context, req *UpdateProfileRequest) (*response.JSON[UserResponse], error) { id, err := api.CurrentUserID(ctx) if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } user, err := h.svc.UpdateProfile(ctx, id, req.Input()) if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } return response.OK(newUserResponse(user)), nil } Imports are context, github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api, and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api/response. The receiver holds Identity's existing service.UserService; UpdateProfileRequest and UserResponse belong to the same HTTP package. See request mapping for Input. Registration in the same package uses the borrowed protected API: huma.Register(protected, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"identity.updateProfile\", Method: \"PATCH\", Path: \"/users/me\", Summary: \"Update your profile\", MaxBodyBytes: 4096, }, handler.updateProfile) This describes the existing Identity route; do not register another copy. A request with {\"name\":\"Ada\"} updates the signed-in account and returns its public fields under data. The account ID comes from the principal, never the request body."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Input and output","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#input-and-output","Text":"A typed handler has this shape: func(ctx context.Context, input *Input) (*Output, error) Part Responsibility Input HTTP sources, JSON shape, Huma validation and documentation tags Input.Input(...) Straightforward conversion into a service input, where useful Handler Trusted caller context, transport-specific authorization and response conversion Service Business decisions and validation shared by HTTP, jobs, events, and CLI Output Public response body, headers, and status Use struct{ Body T } for a JSON-only request. Use a named input when it has several sources or a mapping method. Return response.JSON[T] for the shared success envelope. Use response.Empty for no body and native Huma output types for streams or special headers. See responses. Keep request and response types in the transport package. Returning a Bun entity directly can expose fields unrelated to the endpoint, including future additions. An explicit conversion makes that public boundary reviewable."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Authentication and policy","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#authentication-and-policy","Text":"Protected routes use targets.AuthenticatedHTTP, built with api.Authenticated. It combines bearer validation with its OpenAPI security declaration. Inside the handler: Helper Result api.Principal(ctx) Authenticated user and session identifiers api.CurrentUserID(ctx) Account ID, without loading the account api.RequireCurrentUser(ctx, id) Requires the supplied ID to match the caller; mismatch returns not found For a self-service /users/{id} route, Identity checks ownership before reading: id := uuid.MustParse(req.ID) // Huma already validated format:\"uuid\". if err := api.RequireCurrentUser(ctx, id); err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } user, err := h.svc.Get(ctx, id) if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } return response.OK(newUserResponse(user)), nil This fragment uses github.com/google/uuid and Identity's existing handler types. A validated UUID is only a well-formed identifier. For shared documents, teams, roles, or other permissions, implement the actual policy in the owning service; RequireCurrentUser is specifically a self-service account check. Pass trusted values explicitly to services. Do not accept owner_id, session IDs, or privileged flags from a request when they are determined by authentication."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Keep context and deadlines","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#keep-context-and-deadlines","Text":"Pass the handler's ctx into every service, database, storage, and provider call. It carries cancellation, tracing, and the context logger. Do not replace it with context.Background() to keep work running after the request. If a call has a shorter deadline than its caller, derive a child context: lookupCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 2*time.Second) defer cancel() user, err := h.svc.Get(lookupCtx, id) This fragment imports time and belongs before the usual error/response mapping. For work intended to outlive the request, enqueue a task and return a documented acceptance result. Cancellation does not undo a committed database transaction or an accepted task. See transactions and queues."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Pagination, uploads and streaming","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#pagination-uploads-and-streaming","Text":"These are native Huma inputs and outputs with a few shared helpers. Use the smallest shape appropriate to the operation."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Return a page","Parent":"Pagination, uploads and streaming","URL":"handlers.html#return-a-page","Text":"Embed api.PaginationInput, pass input.Pagination() to the service, and convert only the returned items. The application type remains pagination.Pagination. type ListProductsRequest struct { api.PaginationInput Search string `query:\"search\" maxLength:\"100\"` } A service returns *pagination.Page[Product]; the handler should expose its public ProductResponse shape while preserving page metadata. See page responses and database pagination."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Receive an upload","Parent":"Pagination, uploads and streaming","URL":"handlers.html#receive-an-upload","Text":"Use RawBody multipart.Form and api.Multipart on the operation. Set an explicit positive MaxBodyBytes for the whole request, not just the file contents. Authentication should run before multipart parsing. The handler must still validate permitted parts, open the selected file, and close it. The middleware bounds parsing, spools large files, and removes temporary files after return or panic. See the multipart example."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Stream a response","Parent":"Pagination, uploads and streaming","URL":"handlers.html#stream-a-response","Text":"Native Huma outputs can supply Body func(huma.Context). This helper is a complete pattern for an already-authorized, already-opened download reader: // Imports: context, io, net/http, Huma, and platform/api/response. type downloadOutput struct { Body func(huma.Context) } func streamDownload(ctx context.Context, reader io.ReadCloser) *downloadOutput { return \u0026downloadOutput{Body: func(c huma.Context) { defer func() { response.LogError(ctx, reader.Close()) }() c.SetHeader(\"Content-Type\", \"application/octet-stream\") c.SetHeader(\"Content-Disposition\", \"attachment\") c.SetHeader(\"X-Content-Type-Options\", \"nosniff\") c.SetHeader(\"Cache-Control\", \"no-store\") if _, err := io.Copy(c.BodyWriter(), reader); err != nil { response.LogError(ctx, err) panic(http.ErrAbortHandler) } }} } Open the reader using the request context before returning this output, so open/authorization failures can still return response.Error. The output callback owns closing the reader. Declare the binary media type in the operation's Responses; Identity's registerMedia in internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/routes.go is the working example. Once headers or bytes are committed, do not append a JSON problem to the stream. http.ErrAbortHandler tells the server to abort the transport; the shared recovery preserves that signal. Flush, hijack, and response-controller deadlines remain available through the underlying HTTP support."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Independent use","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#independent-use","Text":"You can construct the platform API without the complete application. This example belongs inside the repository because internal/platform/api is an internal package: server, err := api.New(api.Options{Title: \"Catalog API\", Version: \"1.0.0\"}) if err != nil { return err } public := huma.NewGroup(server, \"/api/v1\") protected := api.Authenticated(public, authenticator) // Register your operations on public or protected before serving. authenticator implements api.Authenticator. A nil authenticator fails closed on protected routes. server is an http.Handler: pass it to pkg/httpserver or a standard HTTP server whose listener and graceful shutdown you own. Infrastructure handlers mount on server.Mux, but serve the server object itself."},{"Title":"Handlers","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Test a handler through HTTP","Parent":"","URL":"handlers.html#test-a-handler-through-http","Text":"Use a freshly constructed API and httptest to exercise the route, binding, policies, metadata, and response together. The following assertions fit a test that already registered its Catalog route on server: request := httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/api/v1/catalog/hello/Ada\", nil) result := httptest.NewRecorder() server.ServeHTTP(result, request) if result.Code != http.StatusOK { t.Fatalf(\"status = %d; body = %s\", result.Code, result.Body.String()) } if result.Header().Get(\"X-Request-ID\") == \"\" { t.Fatal(\"missing request ID\") } Imports are net/http and net/http/httptest; t is the test's *testing.T. Use the Catalog tests in tests/modules as a complete infrastructure-free starting point. Keep business behavior tests in tests/\u003cmodule\u003e, including authorization, invalid input, and failure cases. Calling a handler directly bypasses Huma binding and must not be treated as validation coverage. Continue with requests, responses, and error handling."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html","Text":"Declare HTTP inputs as Go structs. Huma binds path, query, header, and body values, then validates them before calling the handler. The same fields and tags describe the request in the live OpenAPI document. Services still enforce business rules. CLI commands, jobs, and event handlers call them without passing through HTTP validation."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"JSON inputs","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#json-inputs","Text":"This complete registration helper echoes a validated name. Add it to a transport package and call it from module registration with the chosen huma.API: package http import ( \"context\" \"github.com/danielgtaylor/huma/v2\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api/response\" ) type NameRequest struct { Name string `json:\"name\" minLength:\"1\" maxLength:\"100\"` } func RegisterEcho(router huma.API) { huma.Register(router, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"example.echoName\", Method: \"POST\", Path: \"/examples/name\", Summary: \"Echo a validated name\", MaxBodyBytes: 4096, }, func(ctx context.Context, input *struct{ Body NameRequest }) (*response.JSON[NameRequest], error) { return response.OK(input.Body), nil }) } With the app's /api/v1 target: curl --fail-with-body http://localhost:8080/api/v1/examples/name \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"name\":\"Ada\"}' The response is 200 with data: {\"name\":\"Ada\"} and request metadata. This example echoes input; it does not save an account. Remove the example route when it has served its learning purpose."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Declare constraints next to fields","Parent":"JSON inputs","URL":"requests.html#declare-constraints-next-to-fields","Text":"Tag Example Meaning minLength, maxLength maxLength:\"100\" String length bounds minimum, maximum minimum:\"1\" Numeric bounds format format:\"uuid\" A supported formatted value pattern pattern:\"^[0-9]{6}$\" A string pattern, useful for OTPs with leading zeroes enum enum:\"ios,android,web\" Accepted values default default:\"20\" A default for an omitted value required required:\"false\" Explicitly allow omission nullable nullable:\"true\" Explicitly allow JSON null doc, example doc:\"Display name\" Schema explanation and example Keep validation tags on HTTP inputs. A maximum name length in the schema improves client feedback; the service must still enforce the same business rule for its other callers. Avoid separate validator frameworks for the same request."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Path, query and headers","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#path-query-and-headers","Text":"Combine sources in one input: type ShowRequest struct { ID string `path:\"id\" format:\"uuid\"` Locale string `query:\"locale\" default:\"en\" enum:\"en,ar\"` Client string `header:\"X-Client\" required:\"false\" maxLength:\"100\"` } Register a path such as /items/{id}. Add a Body field when JSON is also needed. The handler receives already bound values; do not look them up again in a second query parser. A valid UUID does not establish ownership. Read the principal from context and authorize the resource before the protected operation. Credential handling belongs to the protected route policy. Do not add an access token body/query field to replace the standard Authorization: Bearer … header."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Optional fields and partial updates","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#optional-fields-and-partial-updates","Text":"Use pointers when the service must distinguish an omitted field from its zero value. Identity's PATCH /users/me request is: type UpdateProfileRequest struct { Body struct { Name *string `json:\"name\" maxLength:\"100\" nullable:\"true\" required:\"false\"` } } Its current service behavior is: JSON body Mapped value Outcome {} Name == nil 422: provide at least one profile field {\"name\":null} Name == nil 422: provide at least one profile field {\"name\":\"Ada\"} Pointer to \"Ada\" Validate and update the name {\"name\":\"\"} Pointer to empty string 422: the entity requires a nonempty display name A pointer distinguishes missing from empty, but this request deliberately treats omitted and null alike: neither supplies a change, so the current single-field operation rejects both. If a future operation requires three states—omit, clear, and set—design an explicit representation and test all three. Do not assume a plain pointer preserves that distinction. Huma handles the input pointer; mapping methods may also use pointer receivers, as Identity does. That convention does not move mutation or business rules into the request type."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Map to a service input","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#map-to-a-service-input","Text":"Keep simple conversion beside the request. Identity's existing mapper in internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/requests.go is: func (req *UpdateProfileRequest) Input() service.UpdateProfileInput { return service.UpdateProfileInput{Name: req.Body.Name} } The file imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/service. The handler supplies the trusted account ID separately: user, err := h.svc.UpdateProfile(ctx, userID, req.Input()) For sign-in, SignInRequest.Input(ip) also receives the peer address extracted at the HTTP boundary. The request mapper does structural conversion. Authentication, authorization, external calls, and business orchestration remain in the handler and service where their context is explicit. Do not add HTTP schema tags to service.UpdateProfileInput. That type is also usable by a command, task, or another module."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Pagination","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#pagination","Text":"Embed the shared input instead of implementing page parsing again: type ListProductsRequest struct { api.PaginationInput Search string `query:\"search\" maxLength:\"100\"` } // Inside the handler: page := input.Pagination() // pagination.Pagination Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api. Pass page to the service. Its Huma resolver runs automatically; no pagination middleware registration is required. Request Outcome No pagination parameters Page 1, 20 items per page ?page=2\u0026per_page=10 Validated pagination.Pagination{Page: 2, PerPage: 10} ?per_page=101 422; HTTP rejects values above 100 ?page=, ?page=1\u0026page=2, malformed integer 422 Page too large to represent its SQL offset 422 pagination.NewPagination normalizes programmatic values for non-HTTP callers; the HTTP layer rejects invalid input instead of silently normalizing it. Repositories still need stable ordering. See page queries."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"JSON decoding and limits","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#json-decoding-and-limits","Text":"A request contains exactly one JSON value matching its declared body type. The platform applies these rules before the handler runs: Input Status Malformed JSON, duplicate members including case variants, trailing JSON value, invalid UTF-8 400 Unknown object fields or schema/type violations 422 Full body exceeds the operation's limit 413 Unsupported media type, compression, or non-UTF-8 charset 415 JSON objects, arrays, and scalar bodies are supported. JSON and structured +json media types are accepted; an absent Content-Type defaults to JSON. Compression is not enabled. Content-Encoding may be absent or identity. The default body limit is 1 MiB, including trailing whitespace. Nested JSON is bounded to 1,000 container levels. Huma's default body-read timeout is five seconds. Set Operation.MaxBodyBytes and BodyReadTimeout for each operation that needs different bounds. A file-content limit and a whole multipart-request limit are separate budgets."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Multipart bodies","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#multipart-bodies","Text":"Use Huma's raw multipart form together with the platform's bounded parser: // Import mime/multipart. type UploadRequest struct { RawBody multipart.Form } Register after authentication. This example uses a one-file operation with a 2 MiB whole-request limit and a handler you implement: huma.Register(protected, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"example.upload\", Method: \"POST\", Path: \"/uploads\", DefaultStatus: 201, MaxBodyBytes: 2 \u003c\u003c 20, Middlewares: huma.Middlewares{api.Multipart}, RequestBody: \u0026huma.RequestBody{Required: true, Content: map[string]*huma.MediaType{ \"multipart/form-data\": {Schema: \u0026huma.Schema{ Type: \"object\", Required: []string{\"file\"}, AdditionalProperties: false, Properties: map[string]*huma.Schema{ \"file\": {Type: \"string\", Format: \"binary\"}, }, }}, }}, }, handler.upload) The schema describes the upload. The handler still checks the permitted parts: form := \u0026input.RawBody if len(form.File) != 1 || len(form.File[\"file\"]) != 1 || len(form.Value) != 0 { return nil, response.Error(ctx, apperror.Validation(\"Provide one file part named file.\")) } file, err := form.File[\"file\"][0].Open() if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } defer file.Close() // Pass file and ctx to the owning capability, then return its public result. Imports include github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/apperror and the response package. The middleware bounds the full request, spools files above 1 MiB to disk, and removes temporary files after the handler returns or panics. It does not decide content policy, ownership, allowed filenames, or business authorization. For the installed Media endpoint, use its existing integration instead of writing another upload controller: curl --fail-with-body http://localhost:8080/api/v1/media \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\ -F 'file=@./avatar.png' \\ -F 'visibility=private' Set ACCESS_TOKEN to your current access token. See Media for its 20 MiB file policy, default visibility, and returned URL."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Application validation errors","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#application-validation-errors","Text":"Huma handles structural validation. After it succeeds, a service can return one or more safe business validation messages: return apperror.InvalidFields(map[string][]string{ \"name\": {\"Choose a display name.\"}, \"email\": {\"Use an eligible email address.\"}, }) The HTTP boundary translates these into the same errors list shape as native Huma validation. Huma's own diagnostic values are redacted; application messages are intentionally public. See error handling and the wire response."},{"Title":"Requests \u0026 validation","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Verify the boundary","Parent":"","URL":"requests.html#verify-the-boundary","Text":"Test through server.ServeHTTP, not only by invoking the handler. Include a valid request, an omitted optional field, a boundary value, unknown fields, malformed JSON, an oversized body, and unauthorized access where applicable. For uploads, include unexpected parts and cleanup after failure. The installed HTTP behavior is covered by internal/platform/api and docs/api/contract_test.go; business scenarios belong in tests/\u003cmodule\u003e. Continue with responses and middleware."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html","Text":"Application JSON endpoints return response.JSON[T] from internal/platform/api/response. The payload stays typed under data, with optional operation-specific meta. Request IDs and API versions are response headers only. Huma owns serialization and OpenAPI; handlers still register directly with huma.Register. A normal handler constructs a response; it does not write JSON or headers itself. Huma writes the returned output after the handler finishes. Custom files and streams can retain their own body format."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Return a result","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#return-a-result","Text":"// In a handler returning (*response.JSON[UserResponse], error): return response.OK(result), nil // Optional metadata is a JSON object. Use serializable values. return response.OK(result).WithMeta(map[string]any{\"source\": \"cache\"}), nil { \"meta\": {\"source\": \"cache\"}, \"data\": {\"id\": \"\u003cresource-id\u003e\", \"name\": \"Ada Example\"} } Helper Handler output HTTP status response.OK(data) *response.JSON[T] 200 response.Created(data) *response.JSON[T] 201 response.Accepted(data) *response.JSON[T] 202 response.NewJSON(data, status) *response.JSON[T] Explicit success status permitting a body response.NoContent() *response.Empty 204, with no body Use the constructors: Huma reads JSON.Status directly, so a literal with an unset status is invalid. NewJSON panics on a non-success status or 204/205; these are programmer errors. Return failures with response.Error instead. For NoContent, omit Operation.DefaultStatus or set it to 204. Set Operation.DefaultStatus: 201 or 202 for those fixed outcomes. A constructor runs at request time, so it cannot update the OpenAPI operation defined at startup. For dynamic success statuses, document each status explicitly; Identity sign-in returns 200 for existing accounts and 201 for new ones. Its route group shares the generated success schema between both statuses. Always select public response fields instead of exposing persistence entities. data is always present, including null for a nil payload. meta is omitted when empty. WithMeta copies the outer map; nested values remain caller-owned. Do not modify a response while another goroutine is sending it. Pagination stays under data with its typed items, page, per_page, total and total_pages. Extra metadata does not replace that established page contract. Identity sign-in uses the same token-and-user response for both 201 (new account) and 200 (existing account). The frontend checks data.user.profile_complete after either status to decide whether to request profile details."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Declare multiple success statuses","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#declare-multiple-success-statuses","Text":"Some operations have more than one successful outcome. Identity's OTP and social verification return 201 for a new account and 200 for an existing one, using the same body schema. Its registration group declares the additional status: signIns := huma.NewGroup(routes) signIns.UseSimpleModifier(func(op *huma.Operation) { created := *op.Responses[\"200\"] created.Description = \"New account created\" op.Responses[\"201\"] = \u0026created }) // Register only operations with that same 200/201 body contract on signIns. This fragment imports Huma and assumes routes is the module's API. The handler chooses response.Created(result) or response.OK(result) at runtime. The modifier copies the generated 200 response after Huma has built its typed schema; do not apply it to bodyless or differently shaped operations."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Return a page","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#return-a-page","Text":"Return a typed page under data, preserving pagination.Pagination as the paging input. This handler fragment assumes page is *pagination.Page[*entities.User] and uses Identity's existing public response conversion: items := make([]UserResponse, len(page.Items)) for i, user := range page.Items { items[i] = newUserResponse(user) } paging := pagination.NewPagination(page.Page, page.PerPage) return response.OK(pagination.NewPage(items, paging, page.Total)), nil Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/pagination. An empty result has this shape: { \"data\": { \"items\": [], \"page\": 1, \"per_page\": 20, \"total\": 0, \"total_pages\": 0 } } pagination.NewPage makes items a non-nil slice and calculates total_pages. It does not query the database. See page requests and page queries for the other two steps."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Response headers","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#response-headers","Text":"The API server generates a fresh request ID and returns it in X-Request-ID. Incoming request IDs are not trusted. The same ID is attached to request logs; use response.RequestID(ctx) when an HTTP handler needs the correlation ID. It is not inserted into JSON bodies. X-API-Version defaults to v1. Set api.Options.APIVersion when constructing a server for another API version. This setting does not change route prefixes; keep the Huma group's path consistent with it. Options.Version is the application release shown in OpenAPI and does not change X-API-Version. The API version is scoped to the server, not inferred from arbitrary request paths. Successes, errors, empty responses and streams receive both headers. This requires serving the platform server itself, not bypassing it through server.Mux. The default CORS configuration exposes both headers to browser clients. Preserve that exposure when customizing CORS. Clients should capture X-Request-ID alongside an error; a copied JSON body alone has no correlation ID."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Return an error","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#return-an-error","Text":"if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } Services use transport-independent apperror errors; see error ownership and wrapping. At the HTTP boundary, response.Error(ctx, err) recognizes wrapped errors, preserves their causes and hides private operational diagnostics. Huma handles the returned status error. Use response.WriteError to reject an application failure inside Huma middleware and return without calling next. Native Huma status errors and their headers remain supported when returned from typed handlers. Standard net/http handlers can use response.WriteHTTPError(ctx, w, err) before committing their response. These writers share the same service-error mapping, safe logging. The API server supplies the response headers and installs Huma's error-sanitization hook automatically; ordinary handlers need no extra setup. Both application and Huma validation errors use application/problem+json with flat RFC 9457 fields and optional operation-specific meta. There is no outer error or data field on a problem. OpenAPI describes this body without request_id or api_version, and Huma's global error hooks remain unchanged."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Multiple validation messages","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#multiple-validation-messages","Text":"Collect application validation messages in the existing apperror.InvalidFields: // In a service; no Huma or response imports are needed. return apperror.InvalidFields(map[string][]string{ \"email\": {\"Use your work email.\", \"This address is not eligible.\"}, \"profile.name\": {\"Choose a display name.\"}, }) The handler returns nil, response.Error(ctx, err). Its response is: { \"type\": \"urn:go-modular:error:validation_failed\", \"title\": \"Unprocessable Entity\", \"status\": 422, \"detail\": \"The given data was invalid.\", \"errors\": [ {\"message\": \"Use your work email.\", \"location\": \"body.email\"}, {\"message\": \"This address is not eligible.\", \"location\": \"body.email\"}, {\"message\": \"Choose a display name.\", \"location\": \"body.profile.name\"} ] } Every message becomes a separate Huma error detail. Field keys are sorted for stable ordering; message order within each field is preserved. Bare names refer to the body, including nested paths such as items[0].name. Explicit HTTP paths such as query.page, path.id, header.X-Client or body.email are preserved. Keep transport-specific paths at the HTTP boundary when possible. An empty key refers to the whole body. Use one InvalidFields to aggregate field messages; errors.Join is not a validation collection API. Clients branch on type and HTTP status, not human-readable messages. Both native and application validation expose an errors array with message and location. Submitted values and parser internals are omitted. Huma schema diagnostics are redacted; application messages are deliberately public and preserved. Never put provider text, credentials or submitted secrets in those messages. Huma may report several native validation errors before the handler runs; business validation runs after that stage succeeds. Error category HTTP status Invalid JSON/input 400 Unauthenticated / forbidden 401 / 403 Not found / conflict 404 / 409 Body too large / unsupported media 413 / 415 Schema or application validation 422 Rate limited 429 Internal or unknown failure 500 Unavailable 503 Huma documents a default problem response. Use Operation.Errors for explicit failure statuses when clients need them in the schema."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Custom outputs and streams","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#custom-outputs-and-streams","Text":"Use native Huma outputs for custom headers. Declare special fields explicitly; do not embed response.JSON[T] into an output struct. type CreatedUserOutput struct { Location string `header:\"Location\"` Body response.Envelope[UserResponse] } // Register with Operation.DefaultStatus: 201. return \u0026CreatedUserOutput{ Location: \"/api/v1/users/\" + result.ID.String(), Body: response.Envelope[UserResponse]{Data: result}, }, nil The envelope receives automatic metadata. Native bare bodies are also supported when an endpoint deliberately needs a different wire contract; they are not silently wrapped. File streams use Body func(huma.Context) and keep their bytes. Health probes, dashboards, metrics and standard router 404/405 responses retain their native formats, with metadata headers. The request logger covers them too. Once a response is committed, a later failure must abort the transport rather than append a problem body. See handlers for streaming cleanup. Cancellation does not undo a committed transaction or accepted task; retries must account for duplicate or uncertain outcomes."},{"Title":"Responses","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Verify the response contract","Parent":"","URL":"responses.html#verify-the-response-contract","Text":"For an endpoint, check the runtime status and body and its /openapi.json entry. Common mistakes have straightforward fixes: Symptom Fix Runtime 201 but docs show 200 Set DefaultStatus: 201 on the operation Unset or invalid status Construct response.JSON[T] with its helper instead of a zero-valued literal Missing response headers in an integration test Serve the platform server, not only server.Mux items: null Construct the page with pagination.NewPage Private entity fields appear Map to a transport-owned response type Client cannot find validation messages Read the top-level problem errors array; errors have no data envelope Success clients read data; problem clients read top-level fields. Use the X-Request-ID response header to investigate failures through observability. Continue with error handling and live API documentation."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html","Text":"Application middleware uses Huma's func(huma.Context, func(huma.Context)) shape. Server-wide policies use standard func(http.Handler) http.Handler middleware."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Request logging is built in","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#request-logging-is-built-in","Text":"api.New installs one request logger around recovery. Completed and aborted requests produce an HTTP request entry with the server, method, route template, status and duration. Unmatched routes use unmatched. The context logger supplies request/trace correlation. Raw paths, query strings, headers and bodies are excluded. Responses below 400 use INFO, 4xx WARN and 5xx ERROR. A stream's recorded status is the status already committed; an aborted transport is marked separately. Upgrades record handler lifetime rather than individual messages. Redirects and unmatched routes also pass through the same policies. Logging lives in api/http.go. See logging for output settings."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Write and scope middleware","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#write-and-scope-middleware","Text":"A module can add bounded context fields: func CatalogContext(ctx huma.Context, next func(huma.Context)) { request := ctx.Context() logger := logging.FromContext(request).With(\"component\", \"catalog.http\") next(huma.WithContext(ctx, logging.WithLogger(request, logger))) } Imports are Huma and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/logging. Apply a policy once at the intended scope: // All subsequently registered operations on this API. public.UseMiddleware(CatalogContext) // Or only operations on a module-owned group. catalog := huma.NewGroup(public, \"/catalog\") catalog.UseMiddleware(CatalogContext) // Or use Operation.Middlewares for one operation. These are alternatives, not three layers to install together. Use native Huma middleware and groups; there is no framework-specific middleware interface."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Reject a request","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#reject-a-request","Text":"Before calling next, write a safe failure and return: response.WriteError(ctx, apperror.Forbidden(\"This operation is unavailable.\")) return response.WriteError uses the same problem details and error classification as handler errors. Typed handlers should instead return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) for a service failure. Never write another response after headers or stream bytes are committed."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Add a standard HTTP policy","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#add-a-standard-http-policy","Text":"Use api.Options.Middleware when a policy must cover infrastructure routes, redirects, and unmatched paths too. This example sets a static response header: func serviceHeader(next http.Handler) http.Handler { return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) { w.Header().Set(\"X-Service\", \"catalog\") next.ServeHTTP(w, r) }) } // During API construction, before registering routes: server, err := api.New(api.Options{ Middleware: []func(http.Handler) http.Handler{serviceHeader}, }) Import net/http and the API package, handle the constructor error, and serve server. The list runs in declaration order on the way in, then unwinds on return. App already supplies its CORS policy; avoid installing another competing layer. Scope API Typical purpose Every HTTP request api.Options.Middleware Headers, CORS, proxy-specific policy Business API group group.UseMiddleware Shared authentication or module context One operation huma.Operation.Middlewares Multipart parsing or an endpoint-specific check"},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Understand order","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#understand-order","Text":"The application chain is: Server correlation, access logging, recovery, tracing/metrics and standard middleware (CORS). Huma public API rate limiting. Bearer authentication on protected groups. Operation middleware, including multipart bounds. Huma binding, schema validation, resolvers (including pagination), then the handler. Authentication and rate-limit rejection happen before body reads. Policies run once; code after next(ctx) runs on return. Use defer for cleanup that must also run when an operation panics or aborts a stream. api.Authenticated combines bearer enforcement and its OpenAPI security declaration. It fails closed with a nil authenticator, preserves operational authentication errors, and accepts credentials only from one Authorization header. A trusted principal lives in context.Context; handlers use api.Principal, CurrentUserID or RequireCurrentUser. A principal is not automatic permission to access another user's resource. Broad limits use the direct connection address, hashed before it becomes a key. Forwarding headers cannot alter the budget. Identity also has explicit service-level challenge limits. Configure CORS through HTTP_CORS_* and broad limits through API_RATE_LIMIT_*; see the configuration reference. The API uses bearer headers, not authentication cookies; CSRF middleware is not installed."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Use the context logger","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#use-the-context-logger","Text":"huma.WithContext propagates a derived context through the downstream Huma handler. Derived fields do not mutate the outer HTTP request or its access logger. Services receive cancellation, tracing and log correlation using the same standard context. Do not store a resource container or arbitrary dependencies there. Asynq and Watermill keep their own native middleware contracts: these describe different execution lifecycles. See tasks and events."},{"Title":"Middleware","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Test rejection and propagation","Parent":"","URL":"middleware.html#test-rejection-and-propagation","Text":"Exercise middleware through an HTTP request to the configured server. Verify that an early rejection does not call the downstream handler, headers are correct on both success and failure, and the derived context reaches the service. For multipart endpoints, confirm authentication rejects an unauthorized request before file parsing. Huma middleware only runs for registered operations. To apply a policy to a 404, health probe, or dashboard, use the standard HTTP layer. Context fields added inside a Huma group do not retroactively change the outer request access log. Continue with request validation, error handling, and observability."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html","Text":"Return errors from the operation that knows what failed. Preserve their identity through intermediate layers, then translate them once at the delivery boundary. Business services use ordinary Go errors and pkg/apperror; they do not import HTTP."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Choose an error kind","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#choose-an-error-kind","Text":"Situation Return Caller behavior A public, expected business failure An owning apperror value Show its safe message and classify it Several invalid fields apperror.InvalidFields Present all field messages Database, storage, or provider failure An error wrapped with %w Preserve the cause; avoid exposing raw details Context canceled or deadline expired Preserve the original cause Stop work; assess whether effects already happened Invalid static programmer setup Follow the API's documented constructor error or panic contract Fix construction or registration Use the same classification for HTTP, CLI, and background callers. The transport chooses how to present it."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Define errors beside their owner","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#define-errors-beside-their-owner","Text":"For a new Catalog capability, internal/modules/catalog/domain/errors.go could contain: package domain import \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/apperror\" var ( ErrProductNotFound = apperror.NotFound(\"The product was not found.\") ErrProductArchived = apperror.Conflict(\"Archived products cannot be changed.\") ) These are illustrative additions, not shipped Catalog errors. The package-level values provide stable identity for callers using errors.Is. An entity invariant may instead own its error beside the entity; a platform capability owns its errors in that capability. Do not create a global application error catalog. apperror.Error keeps its fields private and copies validation maps, so shared sentinels cannot be mutated accidentally. Its code and message are explicitly public. Never put submitted secrets, SQL text, credentials, or raw provider responses into an application message."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Preserve error identity","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#preserve-error-identity","Text":"Wrap an operational failure with meaningful context: if err := s.products.Create(ctx, product); err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"create catalog product: %w\", err) } This service fragment imports fmt and uses its injected repository. %w keeps the underlying error available. Callers inspect it with errors.Is or errors.As: if errors.Is(err, domain.ErrProductNotFound) { // Apply the caller's explicit missing-product behavior. } var failure *apperror.Error if errors.As(err, \u0026failure) { code := failure.Code() _ = code // Classify without matching human-readable strings. } Do not wrap an operational error by copying its text into apperror.Validation. That would expose private diagnostics and incorrectly turn a server failure into a client mistake. Unknown errors become a generic 500 at the HTTP boundary."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Return several validation messages","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#return-several-validation-messages","Text":"Collect independent business validation results into one value: fields := map[string][]string{} if name == \"\" { fields[\"name\"] = append(fields[\"name\"], \"Choose a display name.\") } if !eligible { fields[\"email\"] = append(fields[\"email\"], \"This address is not eligible.\") } if len(fields) != 0 { return apperror.InvalidFields(fields) } This is a service fragment: name and eligible are values determined by the operation. Keys name public inputs; values are safe messages. Use dot paths for nested fields. HTTP adds the body. source prefix to ordinary keys. errors.Join combines independent error causes, often during cleanup. It is not the field-validation collection API. InvalidFields preserves every message and produces predictable ordering at the HTTP boundary."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Translate at the boundary","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#translate-at-the-boundary","Text":"A typed HTTP handler returns: if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } return response.OK(result), nil Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api/response. It recognizes wrapped application errors, preserves their causes, maps public codes, and hides unknown operational details. Native Huma status errors can also be returned directly. See responses for the RFC 9457 body and metadata. Where the failure occurs API Typed Huma handler Return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) Huma middleware before calling next response.WriteError(ctx, err), then return Standard net/http handler before commitment response.WriteHTTPError(ctx, w, err), then return Stream after commitment response.LogError(ctx, err), then abort the transport CLI or task Return an error to that execution boundary; apply its retry/output policy The error writers translate application failures; they are not a replacement for arbitrary native Huma response/header handling. Do not attempt a second response after headers or body bytes are committed."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Log at the responsible boundary","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#log-at-the-responsible-boundary","Text":"Avoid logging the same error in the repository, service, and handler. The API response boundary records operational failures with bounded error_category and safe error_code fields. Its request logger records method, route, status, duration, and correlation separately. Use the context logger when your operation owns a meaningful event or handles a failure without returning it. Prefer stable fields over arbitrary error text. Provider/library errors can contain secrets even when the public response is safe. Background retry attempts and final delivery failures have different logging owners; see queues and notifications."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Cancellation and uncertain outcomes","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#cancellation-and-uncertain-outcomes","Text":"Propagate the caller's context and preserve cancellation causes: if err := ctx.Err(); err != nil { return err } Cancellation tells work to stop. It does not prove a transaction failed to commit, a provider rejected a message, or a task was never accepted. Before retrying, determine whether the operation is idempotent and whether its durable result can be inspected. See transaction outcomes."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Panics and streaming failures","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#panics-and-streaming-failures","Text":"Use errors for expected failures. The API recovers programming panics and writes a safe 500 problem if the response has not started. After commitment it must abort the transport. A broken stream should explicitly signal http.ErrAbortHandler; recovery preserves this signal so clients can detect incomplete delivery. Recovery cannot roll back external side effects or repair partial application state. Test cleanup and transaction behavior around panics where those boundaries matter. See streaming."},{"Title":"Application errors","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Verify error behavior","Parent":"","URL":"errors.html#verify-error-behavior","Text":"Check both the service outcome and its delivery representation: errors.Is still identifies an owned error after wrapping. Missing resources and authorization failures reveal only intended information. Multiple application messages appear in the HTTP errors list. Driver/provider error text and submitted secrets do not appear in responses. Cancellation stops dependent work, with no background goroutines left behind. Retries respect the operation's commit and delivery guarantees. Business tests belong in tests/\u003cmodule\u003e; reusable error-model and platform tests may live beside their owners. Continue with responses, logging, and observability."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html","Text":"Send external HTTP requests using small, tagged operation structs. The client builds URLs, encodes bodies and runs middleware over native net/http. It works independently of application configuration, modules, databases and workers."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Send your first request","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#send-your-first-request","Text":"Define the operation once. Put it in the integration that owns the external API. type GetProductRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"GET\" path:\"products/{id}\"` ID string `path:\"id\"` Fields []string `query:\"fields,omitempty\"` } type ProductResponse struct { ID string `json:\"id\"` Name string `json:\"name\"` } Create and reuse a client: client, err := httpclient.New(httpclient.Config{ BaseURL: \"https://api.example.com/v1/\", Timeout: 15 * time.Second, Header: http.Header{ \"Accept\": {\"application/json\"}, }, }) if err != nil { return err } defer client.CloseIdleConnections() response, err := client.Do(ctx, GetProductRequest{ ID: \"123\", Fields: []string{\"name\", \"price\"}, }) if err != nil { return err } if err := response.CheckStatus(); err != nil { return err } var product ProductResponse if err := response.DecodeJSON(\u0026product); err != nil { return err } Try the compiled local example in examples/httpclient/main.go: go run ./examples/httpclient It starts a disposable local provider with sample catalog data. No real credentials, internet service or application infrastructure are needed."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Define paths, query parameters and headers","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#define-paths-query-parameters-and-headers","Text":"Declaration Meaning Embedded httpclient.Request Required operation marker; declares method and path path:\"id\" Substitute {id} with one escaped value query:\"tag\" Encode a query parameter; slices become repeated keys header:\"Accept-Language\" Set a request header; replaces matching client defaults query:\"tag,omitempty\" Omit empty values header:\"Accept-Language,omitempty\" Omit empty values and retain any client default body:\"json\" Encode this field as the JSON body Tags belong on direct, exported fields. Embed one marker; no registration or request methods are required. Untagged fields are ignored. Parameter groups are not recursively flattened. Missing placeholders, duplicate parameter declarations, conflicting tags and unsupported encodings fail before sending. Tag spelling is not compiler-checked; exercise new operations with a local test server. The base URL is an API prefix. Both products and /products append to https://api.example.com/v1/. Request paths cannot switch hosts, contain query strings or fragments, or escape the prefix using dot segments. Use query tags for query parameters and DoRequest for a fully specified native URL. type ListProductsRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"GET\" path:\"products\"` Tags []string `query:\"tag,omitempty\"` Active *bool `query:\"active,omitempty\"` Language string `header:\"Accept-Language,omitempty\"` } Tags: []string{\"sale\", \"new\"} produces tag=sale\u0026tag=new. A nil Active omits the parameter; a pointer to false sends active=false. Non-pointer zero values are sent unless omitempty is present. Parameters support scalar values, slices and encoding.TextMarshaler, including time.Time. Path values must be nonempty; slashes inside values are escaped. Path, query and header tags describe serialization, not business validation. Keep validation in the integration or calling use case. Incoming HTTP validation remains owned by Huma; this package does not depend on it."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Send JSON and XML","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#send-json-and-xml","Text":"Put the payload in a separate field, conventionally named Body. Only that field is serialized. Ordinary json and xml tags and custom marshalers retain their standard library behavior. type UpdateProductRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"PATCH\" path:\"products/{id}\"` ID string `path:\"id\"` IfMatch string `header:\"If-Match,omitempty\"` Body struct { Name *string `json:\"name,omitempty\"` Published *bool `json:\"published,omitempty\"` } `body:\"json\"` } For partial updates, a nil pointer omits a field, while a pointer to false, zero or an empty string explicitly sends that value. A named payload struct works too. type SubmitOrderRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"POST\" path:\"orders\"` Body struct { XMLName xml.Name `xml:\"order\"` ID string `xml:\"id\"` } `body:\"xml\"` } The encoder supplies Content-Type. Explicit compatible types are accepted, including application/merge-patch+json and UTF-8 charset parameters. Conflicting types fail before sending. Set Accept separately to describe the response you want."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Submit forms","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#submit-forms","Text":"type CreateContactRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"POST\" path:\"contacts\"` Body struct { Email string `form:\"email\"` Tags []string `form:\"tag,omitempty\"` } `body:\"form\"` } The content type is application/x-www-form-urlencoded. Use url.Values as the body for dynamically named fields. Form payloads use form tags; untagged fields are ignored. form:\"-\" excludes a field explicitly."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Upload files with multipart","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#upload-files-with-multipart","Text":"type UploadDocumentRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"POST\" path:\"documents\"` Body struct { Title string `form:\"title\"` File httpclient.File `form:\"file\"` } `body:\"multipart\"` } file, err := os.Open(\"invoice.pdf\") if err != nil { return err } defer file.Close() // Also covers a validation failure before the client owns it. request := UploadDocumentRequest{} request.Body.Title = \"Invoice\" request.Body.File = httpclient.File{ Filename: \"invoice.pdf\", ContentType: \"application/pdf\", Content: file, } response, err := client.Do(ctx, request) File, *File and []File support one, optional or multiple file parts. The client streams file contents and generates the multipart boundary. Do not supply your own boundary. Filename is metadata; the client never opens filesystem paths for you. Build does not read files or start a producer. Sending starts the stream. Closing the request body releases the pipe and closable file readers. Readers without a Close method must arrange to unblock their own reads on cancellation. Supply fresh readers for another upload; multipart streams are not automatically replayable."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Send text, bytes or a stream","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#send-text-bytes-or-a-stream","Text":"type ImportCSVRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"POST\" path:\"imports\"` ContentType string `header:\"Content-Type\"` Body io.Reader `body:\"raw\"` } response, err := client.Do(ctx, ImportCSVRequest{ ContentType: \"text/csv\", Body: strings.NewReader(\"name,email\\nAlex,alex@example.com\\n\"), }) Raw bodies accept string, []byte or io.Reader. Set the content type explicitly for text, CSV, protobuf or another format; otherwise it defaults to application/octet-stream. Closable raw readers follow native HTTP body ownership. Known byte/string readers retain native content length and replay support. The package adds no retry loop; provider idempotency and stream replay remain explicit."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Write middleware","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#write-middleware","Text":"Middleware is one function receiving the native request and next. Headers are already cloned, so ordinary header edits need no transport wrapper or cloning code. func addRequestID(request *http.Request, next httpclient.Next) (*http.Response, error) { request.Header.Set(\"X-Request-ID\", \"example-request\") return next(request) } client, err := httpclient.New(httpclient.Config{ BaseURL: \"https://api.example.com/v1/\", Middleware: []httpclient.Middleware{addRequestID}, }) Run code before and after next for timing, logging or response-header inspection: func observe(request *http.Request, next httpclient.Next) (*http.Response, error) { started := time.Now() response, err := next(request) logging.FromContext(request.Context()).DebugContext(request.Context(), \"outbound HTTP exchange\", \"headers_duration\", time.Since(started), \"failed\", err != nil, ) return response, err } First registered is first entered and last exited. The chain is assembled on configuration and shared by concurrent callers. Middleware runs for each exchange, including redirect hops when you explicitly enable them. Call next synchronously, normally once. Middleware state must be concurrency-safe. Static headers belong in Config.Header; dynamic authentication must restrict credentials to its intended origin. Both Do and DoRequest run middleware; Build does not. Middleware surrounds transport execution through response headers. Body consumption and JSON decoding happen later. Do not read/close a successful response body in middleware unless you replace it with an equivalent readable body. Replacing a request body also makes the middleware responsible for the old body and correct replay/length metadata."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Read responses and handle failures","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#read-responses-and-handle-failures","Text":"response.StatusCode // Numeric status response.Successful() // Is the HTTP status 2xx? response.Header.Get(\"Content-Type\") // Response header response.ContentType() // Media type without charset or other parameters response.Trailer.Get(\"X-Checksum\") // Complete after a successful body read response.Body // Retained bytes response.Text() // Retained bytes as text response.BodyError() // Read/limit/close error, also returned by Do response.DecodeJSON(\u0026result) // Decode without consuming Body response.DecodeXML(\u0026result) // Decode XML without consuming Body Do closes the network response body and retains at most MaxResponseBytes. Decoding can be repeated. No JSON envelope or content-type assumption is imposed on responses. Both decoders reject extra trailing values and refuse bodies whose read, size limit or close failed, even if the retained prefix looks valid. XML allows trailing whitespace, comments and processing instructions. Successful and CheckStatus classify only the HTTP status. Always check the error from Do first: HTTP 200 with an interrupted body is still an incomplete response. Result Meaning Build/transport error No usable buffered response; preserve cancellation with errors.Is HTTP 4xx/5xx Do succeeds; call CheckStatus to classify the status *httpclient.StatusError Response remains available; error text omits private provider details httpclient.ErrResponseTooLarge Partial response returned; body contains only the configured limit Body read/close error Response returned with the retained bytes; decoders reject it *httpclient.DecodeError Format and original cause available through errors.As / errors.Is Empty body, including HTTP 204 Skip decoding when no payload is expected; decoding returns an error wrapping io.EOF Provider error inside HTTP 200 Integration interprets it, for example GraphQL's errors"},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Decode typed or dynamic JSON","Parent":"Read responses and handle failures","URL":"http-client.html#decode-typed-or-dynamic-json","Text":"Typed response structs are the usual choice. For a dynamic payload, numbers remain json.Number instead of becoming floating-point values that could round a large ID: var payload map[string]any if err := response.DecodeJSON(\u0026payload); err != nil { return err } if number, ok := payload[\"id\"].(json.Number); ok { id := number.String() // Keeps the original numeric text, e.g. 9007199254740993. _ = id } Typed int64, float64 and other fields retain normal encoding/json behavior. Unknown JSON fields are accepted for forward compatibility. Use the retained bytes with a native decoder when an integration needs stricter rules or another format. Ignore the destination value when decoding fails; standard decoders may partially populate it before reporting an error."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Inspect failures without losing the response","Parent":"Read responses and handle failures","URL":"http-client.html#inspect-failures-without-losing-the-response","Text":"response, err := client.Do(ctx, GetProductRequest{ID: \"123\"}) if err != nil { // response can be non-nil after a body read or size-limit failure. // Its bytes remain available, but DecodeJSON/DecodeXML will refuse them. return err } if err := response.CheckStatus(); err != nil { var status *httpclient.StatusError if errors.As(err, \u0026status) \u0026\u0026 status.Response.StatusCode == http.StatusNotFound { return ErrProductNotFound // Your integration's error. } return err } var product ProductResponse if err := response.DecodeJSON(\u0026product); err != nil { var mismatch *json.UnmarshalTypeError if errors.As(err, \u0026mismatch) { // The provider changed a field's type; the original cause is preserved. } return err } StatusError and DecodeError messages omit response content. Their inspectable response and underlying cause can still contain private provider data."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Read a retry hint","Parent":"Read responses and handle failures","URL":"http-client.html#read-a-retry-hint","Text":"if response.StatusCode == http.StatusTooManyRequests { if delay, ok := response.RetryAfter(); ok { // Supply delay to your queue or retry policy, if the operation is safe to retry. _ = delay } } RetryAfter supports seconds and HTTP dates. A valid past date returns zero; the boolean distinguishes that from a missing, malformed or unrepresentable value. The client never sleeps or retries automatically. A transport error after sending does not prove the provider rejected the operation. Use provider idempotency support for operations you may retry. Headers and bodies can contain secrets; logging and tracing are opt-in and should record selected fields."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Stream responses and customize native requests","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#stream-responses-and-customize-native-requests","Text":"request, err := client.Build(ctx, GetProductRequest{ID: \"123\"}) if err != nil { return err } request.Header.Set(\"X-Custom\", \"value\") response, err := client.DoRequest(request) if err != nil { return err } defer response.Body.Close() if response.StatusCode != http.StatusOK { return fmt.Errorf(\"unexpected download status: %d\", response.StatusCode) } _, err = io.Copy(destination, response.Body) DoRequest returns native *http.Response. It applies middleware, timeout, cookie and redirect policies, but does not add default headers or resolve the URL again. It also accepts a request created directly with http.NewRequestWithContext. Use native fields for Host, ContentLength, trailers and unusual protocol controls; these cannot be set through header tags. If you abandon a built request, close its body yourself. After sending successfully, close the returned response body."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Configure timeouts, transports and runtime changes","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#configure-timeouts-transports-and-runtime-changes","Text":"Setting Default and behavior BaseURL Required for tagged requests; optional for native-only use Header Copied defaults for tagged requests Timeout Zero selects 30 seconds, including response-body reading; -1 disables the client timeout MaxResponseBytes Zero selects 10 MiB; positive limit for Do; DoRequest streams without this cap Middleware Ordered functions shared by both execution paths Transport Private clone of the standard default transport; explicit custom transports are borrowed CheckRedirect Redirects disabled; nil restores this default Jar Optional native cookie jar Configure TLS, proxies, dialing and connection limits on an http.Transport before using it. Reuse clients and transports. The default pool is kept across configuration updates. Custom transport owners must retire replaced pools after active users and response bodies finish; Configure does not close borrowed infrastructure. cfg := client.Config() cfg.Timeout = 45 * time.Second cfg.Header.Set(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \" + newToken) if err := client.Configure(cfg); err != nil { return err } Config returns copied headers and a copied middleware slice. Configure validates the entire replacement before publishing it; failures retain the last good settings. Each operation captures one snapshot, including its response limit. In-flight requests keep their original settings. Serialize concurrent read-modify-write configuration updates to avoid overwriting another administrator's changes. Borrowed collaborators and closure state are not deep-copied. Use a request context for an individual operation's deadline. For long-lived response streams, choose a suitable timeout or Timeout: -1 and manage cancellation yourself. CloseIdleConnections releases idle pools without interrupting active requests. It can also affect other clients sharing an explicitly supplied transport."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Why the native client is private","Parent":"Configure timeouts, transports and runtime changes","URL":"http-client.html#why-the-native-client-is-private","Text":"Embedding *http.Client would expose mutable Timeout, Transport and Jar fields outside Configure. Editing those during requests could race and bypass validated configuration snapshots. Its promoted Get and Post methods would also return native responses without our tagged request building or buffered-body limit: Go embedding promotes methods but does not make their internal calls dispatch to the outer Do method. Use Config for native transport, cookie and redirect controls, and DoRequest when you want the full native request/response API. An integration can embed our *httpclient.Client directly, as the catalog example below does. Reuse the existing tracing transport without a second middleware API: client, err := httpclient.New(httpclient.Config{ BaseURL: \"https://trusted-provider.example/v1/\", Transport: tracing.NewHTTPTransport(nil, tracer), }) The tracing wrapper propagates context to this deliberately trusted destination. See observability for provider ownership and shutdown."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Build an integration","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#build-an-integration","Text":"internal/modules/\u003cmodule\u003e/integrations/catalog/ client.go Client construction and credentials requests.go Tagged operations and payloads responses.go Provider response types The compiled REST example in examples/httpclient/catalog/ has just these three production files. It demonstrates a fictional catalog API, with no external account or credentials required to run the local example. In client.go, embed the framework client and set provider defaults once: type Client struct{ *httpclient.Client } func New(baseURL, token string) (*Client, error) { if baseURL == \"\" { return nil, errors.New(\"catalog: base URL is required\") } header := http.Header{\"Accept\": {\"application/json\"}} if token != \"\" { header.Set(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer \"+token) } client, err := httpclient.New(httpclient.Config{ BaseURL: baseURL, Header: header, }) if err != nil { return nil, err } return \u0026Client{Client: client}, nil } Do, Build, Config, Configure, DoRequest and CloseIdleConnections are available directly. No forwarding methods or second configuration type are needed. Embedding fits this example because it adds defaults, with no extra execution policy. If a provider needs mandatory signing or custom error interpretation, enforce that in middleware or explicit integration methods. Keep operations in requests.go and payload types in responses.go: // requests.go type GetProductRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"GET\" path:\"products/{id}\"` ID string `path:\"id\"` } type CreateProductRequest struct { httpclient.Request `method:\"POST\" path:\"products\"` Body CreateProductBody `body:\"json\"` } type CreateProductBody struct { Name string `json:\"name\"` } // responses.go type ProductResponse struct { ID string `json:\"id\"` Name string `json:\"name\"` } The call site stays the same for get and create operations: client, err := catalog.New(baseURL, token) if err != nil { return err } defer client.CloseIdleConnections() response, err := client.Do(ctx, catalog.CreateProductRequest{ Body: catalog.CreateProductBody{Name: \"Keyboard\"}, }) if err != nil { return err } if err := response.CheckStatus(); err != nil { return err } var product catalog.ProductResponse if err := response.DecodeJSON(\u0026product); err != nil { return err } Construct integrations in their owning module and pass them explicitly to consumers. There is no global client registry or app resource entry. Keep maintained provider SDKs when they already handle specialized protocols correctly, such as Google's identity-token validation."},{"Title":"HTTP client","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Verify an integration","Parent":"","URL":"http-client.html#verify-an-integration","Text":"Use httptest.NewServer to inspect the actual URL, headers, body and error handling. Inject a native transport for a provider whose hostname must stay fixed. Package tests cover encoding, middleware order, concurrent configuration, cancellation, bounded bodies and multipart cleanup: go test -race ./pkg/httpclient ./examples/httpclient/... Continue with module construction, dependency ownership, logging and testing HTTP."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html","Text":"The HTTP server supports connection upgrades and tracks hijacked connections during shutdown. The application has no WebSocket endpoint, hub or room registry. This guide describes the transport lifecycle hooks available when adding your own protocol. Choose a protocol implementation for your application and mount its handler on the server mux. This page describes the existing integration boundary without adding a dependency."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Understand the available boundary","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#understand-the-available-boundary","Text":"Provided by the framework Supplied by your application/integration Standard HTTP handler mount and upgrade-compatible response handling WebSocket protocol implementation and handshake Request correlation and handler-lifetime logging Authentication, origin checks, and message authorization Tracking/closing hijacked connections during shutdown Protocol close frames, heartbeats, backpressure, and reconnection Application lifecycle ownership Cross-replica broadcast, room state, history, and replay There is no built-in chat or realtime notification channel. Push notification support is a separate Expo capability; it does not provide an active WebSocket connection or message-history protocol."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Register a handler","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#register-a-handler","Text":"Protocol upgrades are a platform transport integration, outside the typed business API. Install an explicitly constructed http.Handler from platform HTTP setup, where the native router is owned: server.Mux.Handle(\"GET /ws\", socketHandler) Here server is the platform's *api.Server and socketHandler is a standard http.Handler implementing the handshake and session protocol. This illustrative mount is not implemented by the boilerplate. It does not belong on dependencies.Targets.HTTP, which is a Huma API. Keep native adapter details in platform setup; modules and services retain standard contexts and business inputs."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Keep the session inside the handler","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#keep-the-session-inside-the-handler","Text":"The handler should remain active for the session lifetime. If it starts read/write goroutines, stop and join them before returning. Returning immediately while detached goroutines use app dependencies defeats the server's handler-drain ownership. Pass r.Context() into application operations. A protocol read must also stop when its connection closes; context cancellation alone does not necessarily interrupt a library's blocking read. Bound incoming message size, outbound queues, and write deadlines in the chosen implementation. Decide whether a slow consumer is disconnected or messages are dropped. Authenticate and check browser origins before upgrading, then authorize individual operations as needed."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Configure the listener","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#configure-the-listener","Text":"WebSocket handlers share the application server's address and HTTP settings: HTTP_ADDRESS=:8080 HTTP_READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT=5s HTTP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=10s HTTP limits govern the handshake and normal requests; protocol-level message limits and heartbeat behavior belong to the WebSocket implementation. Configure post-upgrade connection deadlines deliberately and verify how your library handles inherited deadlines. Reverse proxies need upgrade forwarding and suitable connection timeouts."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Shutdown behavior","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#shutdown-behavior","Text":"When HTTP shutdown begins, the server stops admission and closes tracked upgraded connections. It then joins active handlers before their dependencies can be released. This closes the transport connection; it does not automatically send a WebSocket close frame. If the product needs protocol-level farewell messages or session draining, implement and coordinate that behavior in the application. Keep it bounded within the app's shutdown budget. Ordinary request contexts are preserved during HTTP drain, then canceled. Do not rely only on immediate context cancellation to notice WebSocket shutdown; socket closure is the direct signal at shutdown start."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Logging and scaling","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#logging-and-scaling","Text":"The built-in HTTP request log covers the handler lifetime, not individual frames. Add context logging for relevant session outcomes and errors without recording sensitive message bodies. Correlation and connection identifiers belong in logs rather than unbounded metric labels. A process-local connection registry and GoChannel bus reach only that process. With multiple replicas, add an explicit cross-process broadcast design; the current bus cannot deliver to every replica. Match routing and retention to the product rather than treating queue workers as a broadcast hub. Reconnect, replay, ordering, presence, and offline delivery remain application contracts. Define them before exposing those guarantees to clients."},{"Title":"WebSockets","Section":"HTTP APIs","Heading":"Verify a protocol integration","Parent":"","URL":"websockets.html#verify-a-protocol-integration","Text":"Use a real local HTTP server and a protocol client for tests that depend on upgrades; a response recorder cannot establish socket behavior. Exercise: Authentication/origin rejection before upgrade. A permitted session exchanging messages without unbounded buffers. A slow or disconnected client and a blocked protocol read. Application shutdown with an active session and joined goroutines. Reconnect behavior and any promised replay/ordering rules. Only advertise the semantics implemented by your chosen integration. The existing HTTP shutdown support is a lifecycle building block, not proof of chat delivery or multi-replica consistency. Continue with HTTP handlers, application lifecycle, and events."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"database.html","Text":"Set the database connection in your environment file. App assembly opens one Bun pool and shares it with the repositories and database drivers that need it. DATABASE_DRIVER=postgres DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:password@127.0.0.1:5432/app?sslmode=disable The credentials above are local placeholders. Use verified TLS and appropriate credentials for remote connections. Configuration is loaded centrally; repositories do not read environment variables."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Choose a driver","Parent":"","URL":"database.html#choose-a-driver","Text":"Driver Scope postgres Installed modules, Bun queries, transactions, migrations and database cache mysql Reusable database/cache packages and custom MySQL migrations; installed module SQL is PostgreSQL sqlite Available through standalone pkg/database; not supported by the app's combined migration history For independent MySQL components, use its native DSN: DATABASE_DRIVER=mysql DATABASE_URL=app:password@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/app The connection package enables MySQL time parsing, UTC decoding, and a UTC session time zone. Other driver options, including TLS options, remain available. Changing DATABASE_DRIVER does not translate existing SQL or migrate data. Keep one chosen engine for an application's migration history. The shipped app rejects a mixed migration dialect even for CLI help. A MySQL application requires porting or replacing the installed modules' queries and SQL; it is not an alternative quickstart profile. The required integration gate verifies PostgreSQL and Redis; optional engines need their own verification."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Use the app's connection","Parent":"","URL":"database.html#use-the-apps-connection","Text":"App assembly opens a database when DATABASE_URL is nonempty. Database cache requires that connection. With no URL and database cache disabled, a.resources.DB and a.resources.Transactions remain nil; Redis tasks and local events do not require SQL independently. Serving the installed business modules still requires PostgreSQL. Inside the owning module’s register.go, inject its borrowed pool into the repository: catalogRepo, err := catalogrepository.NewProductRepository(deps.DB) if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"app: catalog repository: %w\", err) } This fragment assumes the repository from Models and queries has been added and imported as catalogrepository. Pass the repository to the owning service, then pass that service to package registration. Do not open another pool per request or module. The app owns the pool and closes it after its consumers stop. Repositories and the database cache driver borrow it."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Configure the pool","Parent":"","URL":"database.html#configure-the-pool","Text":"These are the current application defaults: DATABASE_NAME= DATABASE_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=20 DATABASE_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=5 DATABASE_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=30m DATABASE_CONN_MAX_IDLE_TIME=5m DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=5s DATABASE_PING_TIMEOUT=5s DATABASE_SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD=500ms DATABASE_NAME is a diagnostic name; the DSN selects the actual database. Opening a pool includes a ping and closes the pool if initialization fails. Budget connections across every process and replica. Ten replicas with a limit of 20 can open 200 connections, before migration tools and other services are counted. More connections are not automatically more throughput. Connection and ping timeouts do not limit query duration. Give each operation a bounded context: ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 3*time.Second) defer cancel() product, err := repository.Get(ctx, id) This fragment belongs in a caller that owns the operation deadline. Pass the derived context onward, handle the returned error, and use the returned product."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Open a standalone pool","Parent":"","URL":"database.html#open-a-standalone-pool","Text":"Use pkg/database directly for tooling that does not use app assembly. This complete function borrows its caller's configuration and context, verifies access, and closes its own pool: package probe import ( \"context\" \"errors\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/database\" ) func Check(ctx context.Context, cfg database.Config) (err error) { db, err := database.Open(ctx, cfg) if err != nil { return err } defer func() { err = errors.Join(err, db.Close()) }() var value int return db.NewRaw(\"SELECT 1\").Scan(ctx, \u0026value) } Start with database.DefaultConfig() and set its DSN before calling. Open returns *bun.DB. OpenSQL returns a native *sql.DB for SQL-only callers. The Bun pool's db.DB is its underlying SQL pool, not a second connection owner; close the owner once. A literal database.Config with MaxIdleConns: 0 disables idle retention. DefaultConfig supplies five idle connections. Private in-memory SQLite databases use one retained connection so their schema survives between calls."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Query logs","Parent":"","URL":"database.html#query-logs","Text":"Bun queries resolve logging.FromContext(ctx) at execution time, preserving request and job fields. Successful queries log metadata at DEBUG, slow queries at WARN, and failures at ERROR. Cancellation is DEBUG; deadline expiry is WARN. Missing rows are not logged as database failures. Set the desired channel’s Options.Level to slog.LevelDebug in internal/app/config/observability.go to include successful queries. DATABASE_SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD=500ms The hook records operation, duration, and database name. It omits SQL text, bound values, credentials, and server error text. Queries through the underlying db.DB bypass Bun hooks. Returned driver errors may still contain sensitive detail; translate them before sending responses. To inspect SQL for a request, open its database span in Grafana and read db.query.text. Tracing captures sanitized query structure, with values replaced by ? and comments removed; enabling DEBUG does not add SQL to logs. See request SQL in Grafana for navigation, supported queries and capture limits. See Transactions before adding multi-query workflows, and Migrations before creating tables."},{"Title":"Connections","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Verify a connection and its schema","Parent":"","URL":"database.html#verify-a-connection-and-its-schema","Text":"After configuring the intended database: go run ./cmd/cli migrate status go run ./cmd/cli migrate up The first command inspects migration state; the second changes schema. A successful connection ping proves access to the server, not that every required table or permission exists. Exercise a representative request after applying migrations. Symptom Investigate Connection refused or startup timeout Host/port reachability and whether the dependency is running Authentication/TLS failure Credentials, certificate configuration, and server policy; do not print the DSN CLI fails before printing help CLI intentionally constructs configured resources; required connections must work Pool waits during load Total process/replica connection budget, long queries, and unclosed rows/transactions Context deadline during a query Query duration and caller deadline, separately from connect/ping timeouts Table missing after a successful connection Registered migration history, selected database/schema, and deployed binary For deployment sizing, reserve connections for migrations, administration, and other applications too. Readiness dependency checks are bounded; they do not replace query-level error handling or recovery. Continue with entities and queries, database tests, and observability."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html","Text":"Keep Bun table mappings in internal/modules/shared/entities and queries in the owning module's repository. The owning module constructs the repository once and injects it into its service. Extend the database-free Catalog starter when it needs persisted products. The following entity and repository are tutorial additions, not shipped Product types. Add its migration before executing queries. It does not change the database-free Catalog greeting example."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Define an entity","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#define-an-entity","Text":"Create internal/modules/shared/entities/product.go: package entities import \"github.com/uptrace/bun\" // Product is owned by the catalog module. type Product struct { bun.BaseModel `bun:\"table:products,alias:p\"` ID string `bun:\"id,pk\"` Name string `bun:\"name,notnull\"` } Model tags map fields; they do not create schema. Keep important invariants in database constraints and service validation. This example uses application-assigned string IDs and a non-null name. The entity carries its Bun mapping and business state together. Keep HTTP request/response shapes in transports. Introduce a private persistence projection only when the shapes differ; a parallel model layer is not the default. See project conventions and shared types."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Create a repository","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#create-a-repository","Text":"Create the public contract in internal/modules/catalog/repository/repository.go: package repository import ( \"context\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/entities\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/pagination\" ) type ProductRepository interface { Create(ctx context.Context, product *entities.Product) error Get(ctx context.Context, id string) (*entities.Product, error) List(ctx context.Context, page pagination.Pagination) ([]entities.Product, int, error) } Put the private implementation and its constructor in internal/modules/catalog/repository/product.go: package repository import ( \"context\" \"database/sql\" \"errors\" \"github.com/uptrace/bun\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/entities\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/apperror\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/database\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/pagination\" ) var ErrProductNotFound = apperror.NotFound(\"The product was not found.\") type productRepository struct { db *bun.DB } func NewProductRepository(db *bun.DB) (ProductRepository, error) { if db == nil { return nil, errors.New(\"catalog: database is required\") } return \u0026productRepository{db: db}, nil } func (r *productRepository) Create(ctx context.Context, product *entities.Product) error { if product == nil { return errors.New(\"catalog: product is required\") } executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return err } _, err = executor.NewInsert().Model(product).Exec(ctx) return err } func (r *productRepository) Get(ctx context.Context, id string) (*entities.Product, error) { executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return nil, err } product := new(entities.Product) err = executor.NewSelect().Model(product).Where(\"p.id = ?\", id).Scan(ctx) if errors.Is(err, sql.ErrNoRows) { return nil, ErrProductNotFound } if err != nil { return nil, err } return product, nil } func (r *productRepository) List(ctx context.Context, page pagination.Pagination) ([]entities.Product, int, error) { executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return nil, 0, err } page = pagination.NewPagination(page.Page, page.PerPage) products := make([]entities.Product, 0) total, err := executor.NewSelect().Model(\u0026products). OrderExpr(\"p.id ASC\").Limit(page.PerPage).Offset(page.Offset()). ScanAndCount(ctx) if err != nil { return nil, 0, err } return products, total, nil } All three operations resolve the executor from context. They work outside a transaction and join the caller's transaction when one exists. Calling r.db.NewInsert() directly would bypass that scope. Get translates a missing row into a module error. Other errors remain available to the caller. Service operations should validate IDs and names before writing; repositories do not replace business validation. ErrProductNotFound is a safe public application error owned by this repository. If several Catalog behaviors share it, place the sentinel in the module's domain package and reuse it. Do not replace raw database errors with public validation messages. See error ownership."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Return a page","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#return-a-page","Text":"List receives pagination.Pagination and returns rows plus the total. The service constructs the standard page; the handler converts entities into public responses: // Inside the proposed product service; s.products is ProductRepository. items, total, err := s.products.List(ctx, page) if err != nil { return nil, err } return pagination.NewPage(items, page, total), nil Normalize programmatic input with pagination.NewPagination and use deterministic ordering. This example orders by unique product ID, following database collation, not creation time. For chronological results, use a timestamp plus unique ID and an index matching that order. ScanAndCount reads rows and count separately. Concurrent writes can change the total between them; a page is not automatically a consistent snapshot. Stronger snapshot requirements need a deliberately chosen transaction/isolation policy. Deep offsets can also become expensive; measure actual query plans. For cursor-based traversal, define a separate operation with a stable ordering and an explicit cursor contract. The equivalent query fragment for ID ordering is: query := executor.NewSelect().Model(\u0026products).OrderExpr(\"p.id ASC\").Limit(limit) if after != \"\" { query.Where(\"p.id \u003e ?\", after) } if err := query.Scan(ctx); err != nil { return nil, err } Here limit is validated to 1–100, after is the previous final ID, and the method returns the bounded slice. It does not provide a total count or replace the framework's pagination.Pagination convention for normal list endpoints."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Update selected fields","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#update-selected-fields","Text":"Add this method to product.go and add its signature to ProductRepository when the service needs this operation: func (r *productRepository) Rename(ctx context.Context, id, name string) error { executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return err } _, err = executor.NewUpdate().Model((*entities.Product)(nil)). Set(\"name = ?\", name).Where(\"id = ?\", id).Exec(ctx) return err } This method updates only the name. Its contract does not distinguish a missing row from a successful update with no changes. If that distinction matters, design it explicitly: affected-row behavior for unchanged values differs by driver settings. Do not assume every zero count means “not found.” For concurrent state transitions, include the expected state or version in the update condition and define what a failed condition means. A read followed by an unconditional update is not a concurrency guarantee."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Delete by identity","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#delete-by-identity","Text":"Add Delete(context.Context, string) error to the interface if this operation is needed, and put the implementation in product.go. func (r *productRepository) Delete(ctx context.Context, id string) error { executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return err } _, err = executor.NewDelete().Model((*entities.Product)(nil)). Where(\"id = ?\", id).Exec(ctx) return err } This is a hard delete; a missing row is a successful no-op. The service must decide whether deletion is allowed. Foreign-key constraints remain authoritative. Add soft deletion only when the product needs its retention and query rules."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Load relationships deliberately","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#load-relationships-deliberately","Text":"After defining a Bun relationship field named Items on a model, a query can load it with: err := executor.NewSelect().Model(\u0026order). Relation(\"Items\").Where(\"o.id = ?\", id).Scan(ctx) This fragment requires an Order mapping with alias o, the relation tags, and corresponding schema; the sample project does not contain those persisted models. Load only the relationships an operation needs. Inspect query count and size; avoid issuing a relation query for every result in a loop. Cross-module joins stay in deliberate repository queries. Central model definitions do not grant permission to write another module's data."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Raw SQL and dynamic input","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#raw-sql-and-dynamic-input","Text":"Use the same executor for raw SQL inside a transaction: var count int err := executor.NewRaw(\"SELECT count(*) FROM products WHERE name = ?\", name). Scan(ctx, \u0026count) Bind values through placeholders. Choose table names, sort columns, and directions from an allowlist; do not concatenate request input into SQL. Keep result sets bounded and add indexes for actual filters and ordering."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Construct once and reuse","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#construct-once-and-reuse","Text":"In Catalog's New, borrow Resources.DB, construct NewProductRepository, and pass its interface to NewProductService. The service holds that repository, not the entire resource bundle. Neither package opens or closes the shared pool. See dependency injection for the construction pattern."},{"Title":"Entities and queries","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Verify queries and diagnose failures","Parent":"","URL":"database-queries.html#verify-queries-and-diagnose-failures","Text":"Test repository behavior against the actual database engine in tests/catalog: create/get, missing records, deterministic pages, update/delete semantics, and joining a transaction that rolls back. Keep tests out of the business repository package itself. Symptom Check Relation or column missing Migration applied to the same database used by the application Writes survive an expected rollback Every query uses database.Executor and the callback context A changed row appears on two pages Stable ordering and concurrent-write expectations A query slows as data grows Bound results, indexes, query plan, and relation query count Driver details leak to clients Return wrapped operational errors; translate only at the boundary Use request SQL traces to inspect sanitized query structure. Continue with transactions, migrations, and database tests."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html","Text":"Use a transaction when several database changes must succeed together. App assembly supplies Resources.Transactions, backed by the same Bun pool as Resources.DB. For a single independent write, the database statement may already provide the atomicity you need. Add an explicit transaction when several writes or a read-modify-write invariant must share a commit boundary. Work Can join the SQL transaction? Repositories using database.Executor and the same pool Yes Database cache with the same pool Yes Notification Enqueue using the same pool Yes; it persists delivery intent Redis cache, raw queue submission, local event publication No Mail delivery, push delivery, blob storage No"},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Execute an atomic operation","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#execute-an-atomic-operation","Text":"Accept the existing *database.TxManager from construction. Repositories use the executor carried by the callback context and share its database pool. Business services do not import the dependency bundle or redeclare the transaction contract. Inside an operation with the borrowed transaction manager: return s.transactions.Execute(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { if err := s.products.Create(ctx, product); err != nil { return err } return s.audit.Record(ctx, product.ID) }) This is a workflow fragment: s.products and s.audit are service dependencies you implement. Both must use database.Executor(ctx, db) with the same database. Audit in this example means a database write, not an external logging or network operation. Returning nil commits the outer transaction. Returning an error or cancellation rolls it back. Panics also trigger rollback and are rethrown. Do not commit or roll back manually inside Execute; it owns completion."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Resolve the transaction in repositories","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#resolve-the-transaction-in-repositories","Text":"executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return err } _, err = executor.NewInsert().Model(product).Exec(ctx) return err Without a transaction, the executor is the pool. Within a scope, it is the active Bun transaction. Passing the original parent context instead of the callback context loses that scope. Using the raw pool bypasses it too; the package cannot intercept those calls. A context containing a transaction for another database is rejected with database.ErrDifferentDatabase. Keep the transaction manager and repository pool paired."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Nested calls join the transaction","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#nested-calls-join-the-transaction","Text":"flowchart TB accTitle: Nested Execute has one commit boundary accDescr: Inner Execute calls share the outer transaction. An inner error marks the whole scope rollback-only, even when the caller ignores that error. Only the outer Execute can commit. Outer[Outer Execute opens a transaction] --\u003e Inner[Inner Execute joins it] Inner --\u003e|Returns nil| Continue[Continue outer work] Inner --\u003e|Returns an error| Failed[Mark shared scope rollback-only] Failed --\u003e|Even if caller ignores the error| Rollback[Outer Execute rolls back] Continue --\u003e|All work succeeds| Commit[Outer Execute commits] Continue --\u003e|Error, cancellation or panic| Rollback An inner Execute joins the existing scope. It does not commit independently or create a savepoint. If an inner operation fails, the outer scope becomes rollback-only even when a caller ignores the error. err := transactions.Execute(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { _ = transactions.Execute(ctx, func(context.Context) error { return errors.New(\"required write failed\") }) return nil }) // err remains non-nil: the outer transaction cannot commit. Use ordinary error propagation in application code; the ignored error above only demonstrates the protection. Transaction contexts must not escape their scope or be used concurrently by goroutines. The manager itself can be shared across requests."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Configure isolation","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#configure-isolation","Text":"When a workflow needs explicit transaction options, construct a manager with the same pool: transactions, err := database.NewTxManager(db, \u0026sql.TxOptions{ Isolation: sql.LevelSerializable, }) Import database/sql. Handle the constructor error and inject the manager into the relevant service. Options are copied. Nil options inherit an active scope; explicit nested options must match the outer options or the call fails. Choose isolation for the operation's invariants and supported backend. Serialization or deadlock failures may require retrying a whole operation, but the package does not retry automatically. First establish which errors and effects are safe to repeat."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Use a savepoint for recoverable child work","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#use-a-savepoint-for-recoverable-child-work","Text":"Savepoint is available on the same *database.TxManager supplied by app assembly. It requires an active transaction: err := manager.Execute(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { childErr := manager.Savepoint(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { return optionalWrite(ctx) }) if childErr != nil { return childErr } return requiredWrite(ctx) }) Here optionalWrite and requiredWrite are functions supplied by the workflow. To continue after a child error, inspect it and deliberately handle an expected failure. Savepoint errors undo child writes; successful child writes still depend on the outer commit. Cleanup failure marks the parent rollback-only. A child panic is rolled back and rethrown. Ordinary service workflows usually need only Execute. Do not replace it with savepoints just to avoid returning errors."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Couple writes with publication","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#couple-writes-with-publication","Text":"Redis tasks and GoChannel events do not join Bun transactions. A commit followed by publication can fail between those actions; publishing inside the transaction can expose work before a rollback. Neither sequence makes both actions atomic. Notifier.Enqueue persists channel intent in the caller's SQL transaction. Its delivery ledger supplies a focused outbox for notifications. Other durable publications need their own deliberate outbox or compatible publisher. Keep remote effects outside retryable transactions and tolerate duplicate delivery. database.RequireNoTransaction guards Mailer sends/enqueues, immediate notification delivery/dispatch, media mutation, framework Publisher calls and app queue enqueue calls. These reject transaction-scoped or already canceled contexts before effects. Standalone Asynq clients can opt into middleware.RequireNoTransaction() as the outermost client middleware; raw native clients bypass this policy. The guard does not make publication after commit atomic. For a complete compiled transaction workflow, see Profile update with notification intent and examples/module/profile/update.go. Keep a write and its notification together Use this sequence when the notification intent must survive with the business change. The notification ledger is the boundary between SQL and external work. Begin one scope. Execute supplies a transaction-bearing callback context. Pass that same context to each participating repository and to Enqueue. All participants must use the same database pool. Write both records. Save the business change and the notification intent through the transaction's executor. Do not send mail or enqueue a raw Redis task inside this scope. Commit or roll back. If the operation returns nil, the outer Execute attempts commit. An operation error rolls back both sets of writes. A commit error can have an uncertain outcome; do not blindly rerun the callback. Deliver after commit. The dispatcher sees committed notification intent and queues it for channel workers. External delivery is retryable work. Consumers still need duplicate tolerance."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Deadlines and uncertain commits","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#deadlines-and-uncertain-commits","Text":"Use a bounded operation context and keep transactions short. A failed commit may have an uncertain outcome; do not automatically rerun the callback. Preserve errors for diagnosis while keeping sensitive driver details out of responses. Begin, Commit, and Rollback are available for explicit ownership, but require careful cleanup on every exit path. Prefer Execute for business operations. TxFromContext exposes the native transaction to adapters without transferring commit ownership."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Preserve owned security transactions","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#preserve-owned-security-transactions","Text":"Identity's failed-proof counters and refresh-token reuse revocation can need to commit even when authentication is denied. Those operations deliberately own their security transactions. Do not wrap them mechanically in an outer transaction that rolls back on every returned authentication error. See Identity for the operation's behavior before composing it into another workflow."},{"Title":"Transactions","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Verify the commit boundary","Parent":"","URL":"database-transactions.html#verify-the-commit-boundary","Text":"Use database integration tests for the invariants that matter: Scenario Expected result Two writes, callback succeeds Both persist Second write fails Neither persists Nested Execute fails and caller ignores it Outer transaction still rolls back Repository uses another pool ErrDifferentDatabase; no cross-pool atomicity Remote effect attempted with transaction context Framework guard rejects it before the effect Savepoint child fails and expected error is handled Child writes are undone; parent can continue The disposable PostgreSQL tests in pkg/database cover manager behavior. Business workflow coverage belongs in tests/\u003cmodule\u003e and should observe persisted state, not merely that callbacks were invoked. Continue with transactional notifications and database testing."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html","Text":"Goose applies shared and module SQL as one timestamp-ordered history. Run migrations explicitly through the console; application startup never changes schema. go run ./cmd/cli migrate status go run ./cmd/cli migrate up go run ./cmd/cli migrate version Set DATABASE_DRIVER and DATABASE_URL first. An empty history needs a business migration or published infrastructure tables before Goose can run it."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Follow a schema change from source to database","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#follow-a-schema-change-from-source-to-database","Text":"SQL file → embedded module filesystem → module registration → combined Goose history → migrate up Adding a table does not require another application registry. Add its SQL in the owning module, keep its directory registered, rebuild the binary, and run the migration command. Startup itself does not apply pending schema changes."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Choose where SQL belongs","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#choose-where-sql-belongs","Text":"Directory Contents internal/modules/shared/migrations Global schema and published infrastructure SQL internal/modules/catalog/migrations Catalog-owned schema, once that module is added internal/modules/identity/migrations Account and authentication schema internal/platform/notification/migrations One baseline: polymorphic JSON notifications and delivery ledger internal/platform/media/migrations Baseline metadata for polymorphic assets; collections are an asset attribute Files sit directly in each directory. The registry combines explicitly registered directories; it does not select SQL by driver automatically. Write SQL for the selected engine. Use UTC YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_description.sql filenames. Versions must be unique across every registered directory. The order is global timestamp order, not module registration order."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Write a business migration","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#write-a-business-migration","Text":"For the proposed Product model, add a new file such as 20260928090000_create_products.sql in internal/modules/catalog/migrations. Choose a fresh timestamp after relevant existing migrations; this name is illustrative. For PostgreSQL: -- +goose Up CREATE TABLE products ( id VARCHAR(200) PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL ); -- +goose Down DROP TABLE products; The shipped modules require PostgreSQL. The generic migration package also supports MySQL, but adopting it for the application requires porting module SQL. For a separate MySQL application, use the same columns with InnoDB and mark the file as nontransactional: -- +goose NO TRANSACTION -- +goose Up CREATE TABLE products ( id VARCHAR(200) PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL ) ENGINE=InnoDB; -- +goose Down DROP TABLE products; Choose one version, not both. MySQL DDL commits implicitly; a failure can leave partial changes. The Down operation deletes the table and its data. The optional Goose CLI can generate timestamped files: go install github.com/pressly/goose/v3/cmd/goose@v3.28.0 TZ=UTC goose -dir internal/modules/catalog/migrations create create_products sql Create the directory first and use the CLI version matching go.mod. The application console does not require the CLI and has no migrate create subcommand. You may create timestamped files manually instead."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Embed and register module SQL","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#embed-and-register-module-sql","Text":"Add internal/modules/catalog/migrations/files.go: package migrations import \"embed\" //go:embed *.sql var Files embed.FS This pattern requires at least one SQL file. Add real module SQL before registering an embedded migration source; do not add empty histories merely to mirror a layout. Register the source in your module's Register function, importing its migrations package. The registry is supplied through dependencies.Targets: if targets.Migrations != nil { if err := targets.Migrations.Register(\"internal/modules/catalog/migrations\", migrations.Files); err != nil { return err } } App calls the module's Register function once for all its handlers and SQL. There is no additional migration catalog to edit in app. New SQL files included by the embed pattern need no registration edit. Registering files executes no SQL. App validates the combined history during construction. It includes direct SQL files only; nested directories are not discovered automatically. Shared migrations are already registered by app assembly."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Apply and roll back","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#apply-and-roll-back","Text":"go run ./cmd/cli migrate up --timeout=10m go run ./cmd/cli migrate down MIGRATION_TIMEOUT defaults to five minutes. --timeout overrides the migration and lock deadline. down rolls back the latest applied migration across all sources; it is not a module-specific rollback and can destroy data. Migration commands construct the application before execution, just like other commands. Configured resources must be available and the registered SQL history must match DATABASE_DRIVER. The shipped business migrations target PostgreSQL; using another dialect requires replacing that history and its repositories. Migration execution opens a dedicated database pool for its lock/session contract. It starts no application runners and closes all acquired resources on return. Publish or add SQL before building a release binary or image. SQL is embedded at build time. go run rebuilds; an existing console binary needs rebuilding to see new files."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Maintain one history","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#maintain-one-history","Text":"All sources use one goose_db_version table and one migration lock. Do not execute a single module directory independently against that history. Keep applied migrations registered even when a module's workers are disabled. PostgreSQL uses session advisory locks; MySQL uses database-scoped GET_LOCK. Use direct connections or session pooling for migration execution. Transaction-mode PgBouncer does not preserve the required session ownership. External migration tools must coordinate with the same lock. Never edit or rename applied migrations. Add a new migration for changes and plan compatible schema transitions for rolling deployments. Inspect status and actual schema after failure, particularly for MySQL's nontransactional DDL. Publishing files does not inspect or repair database state. If interrupted publication leaves .publish.lock, verify the publisher has stopped before removing it. Existing files remain intact; rerun to publish any remaining files. Seeders are separate operations with no Goose history. See Seeders."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Existing development databases","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#existing-development-databases","Text":"Historical async and Watermill queue migrations remain immutable. Asynq now stores jobs in Redis and requires no SQL queue tables. This runtime does not consume or convert historical pending rows. Drain or export old work using the previous runtime before cutover. Do not delete migration history or assume a mixed deployment shares jobs."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Rebuild a development database","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#rebuild-a-development-database","Text":"go run ./cmd/cli migrate refresh --force This permanently deletes data and database objects, including objects not listed in migrations, then applies all registered shared and module migrations from the beginning. It does not call Down migrations, run seeders, or reset external Redis/broker data. Unlike truncating rows, it rebuilds changed columns and clears Goose's version history. Stop other application processes before running it. The migration session lock covers both reset and migration replay, but does not stop HTTP requests or workers. MIGRATION_TIMEOUT applies to the whole operation; override it with migrate refresh --force --timeout=2m. The command runs immediately without an interactive confirmation. Use it only when the selected data may be discarded. PostgreSQL: recreates current_schema() from the connection's search path, preserving its owner. DROP SCHEMA ... CASCADE also removes dependent objects, potentially including dependencies in other schemas. Schema-specific grants and settings must be recreated by migrations or provisioning. MySQL: recreates the selected database, preserving its default character set and collation. The account needs DROP and CREATE database privileges. System schemas/databases are rejected. Invalid or empty migration histories are rejected before deletion, but invalid SQL can still fail during replay. Refresh is not atomic end-to-end: MySQL DDL commits implicitly, and migration replay can leave an empty or partially rebuilt database after a failure. Fix the cause and rerun refresh only when discarding the remaining state is intentional. This resets only the configured target; it never recreates the database server. Rebuild deployed binaries after editing embedded migration files. Refresh requires --force and APP_ENV=development or test. Staging and production refuse it even with the flag. It deletes all schema data; take a backup and stop other processes first. This command is not an upgrade or rollback."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Development baselines for platform capabilities","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#development-baselines-for-platform-capabilities","Text":"Notifications and Media now have one initial PostgreSQL migration each. The former creates notifications and notification_deliveries; the latter creates only media_assets. Earlier user-only tables and alteration chains are removed. Use a new development database or explicitly recreate the old schema and its migration history before applying these baselines. Merely running migrate up on an existing database will not transform an already-applied migration. No startup path resets a database automatically. Once a release has consumers, preserve applied baselines and use new forward migrations."},{"Title":"Migrations","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Inspect a failed migration","Parent":"","URL":"migrations.html#inspect-a-failed-migration","Text":"Start with read-only inspection: go run ./cmd/cli migrate status go run ./cmd/cli migrate version Check the selected database and actual schema before deciding how to recover. A failed DDL statement may not have the same rollback behavior on every engine. Do not repair a shared database by deleting version rows or editing previously applied files without a deliberate recovery procedure. Symptom Check New SQL is absent from status Direct file location, embed pattern, source registration, and binary rebuild Duplicate version Timestamps must be unique across every registered source Dialect validation failure Installed PostgreSQL history cannot be run as MySQL Waiting on migration lock Another migrator and the command deadline; use session-compatible connections Published tables exist as files but not in DB Publishing and applying are separate steps Refresh refused It requires both --force and development/test environment For a release, test the complete fresh history and an upgrade from the previous supported schema using disposable databases. Rehearse restore procedures for data changes whose Down migration cannot recover lost information. Continue with seeders, database testing, and deployment."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html","Text":"Seeders create or update deliberate initial data through named service operations. Run them explicitly from the CLI after applying migrations. They do not run during startup, have no automatic history, and must be safe to rerun."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Run a seeder","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html#run-a-seeder","Text":"List the registered operations: go run ./cmd/cli seed The shipped modules currently register no seeders. The Catalog operation below is an example you add before invoking it: go run ./cmd/cli seed catalog.defaults go run ./cmd/cli seed catalog.defaults --timeout=10m The default total deadline is five minutes. Seeders receive the command context and its logger. CLI construction opens configured dependencies; executing a seeder does not start HTTP, queue workers, or event processing."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Register a service operation","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html#register-a-service-operation","Text":"Extend the Catalog products tutorial with a SeedDefaults(ctx context.Context) error method on its ProductService interface in internal/modules/catalog/service/service.go. Construct that service with its existing repository, then register the bound method in internal/modules/catalog/register.go: // Inside Register; products is the constructed ProductService. if targets.Seeders != nil { if err := targets.Seeders.Register(\"catalog.defaults\", products.SeedDefaults); err != nil { return err } } targets is dependencies.Targets. It carries a registry, not a database. The service already has the repository it needs; do not reach into app resources when the command runs. Registration installs the function without executing it. Use module-qualified names. Valid names match [a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,127} and must be unique. A nil function or duplicate name is rejected during registration."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Insert defaults safely","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html#insert-defaults-safely","Text":"Keep the SQL in the repository. Add EnsureDefaults(ctx context.Context) error to Catalog's ProductRepository and this method to internal/modules/catalog/repository/product.go from the query tutorial: func (r *productRepository) EnsureDefaults(ctx context.Context) error { executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, r.db) if err != nil { return err } _, err = executor.NewRaw( \"INSERT INTO products (id, name) VALUES (?, ?) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING\", \"starter\", \"Starter product\", ).Exec(ctx) return err } The file already imports context and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/database. This PostgreSQL statement preserves an existing product. Its primary key makes concurrent runs converge on one row. Apply the tutorial's products migration first. The service operation delegates to its repository: // In internal/modules/catalog/service/product.go; s.products is ProductRepository. func (s *productService) SeedDefaults(ctx context.Context) error { if err := s.products.EnsureDefaults(ctx); err != nil { return err } logging.FromContext(ctx).InfoContext(ctx, \"catalog defaults ensured\") return nil } Import context and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/logging in that service file. This method illustrates an addition to the proposed service, not an existing shipped type. Do not log the seeded payload if it contains sensitive data. For an independently ported MySQL application, use the corresponding statement: INSERT INTO products (id, name) VALUES (?, ?) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE id = id The installed modules and tutorial use PostgreSQL. Choose the SQL for your actual engine; changing the connection driver does not port the schema."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Choose a rerun policy","Parent":"Insert defaults safely","URL":"seeders.html#choose-a-rerun-policy","Text":"Desired behavior Pattern Insert missing defaults only Stable key plus conflict no-op Update selected system-owned values Explicit upsert of those columns Preserve user-edited values Never overwrite them through a broad upsert Detect unexpected existing state Read/validate and return a clear failure Do not assume “seed” means destructive replacement. Document the policy in the operation and test a second invocation against existing data."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Run several seeders","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html#run-several-seeders","Text":"Once both example operations are registered: go run ./cmd/cli seed catalog.defaults catalog.prices The CLI validates the whole selection first. Unknown or duplicate names fail before any selected operation runs. Valid selections execute sequentially in the requested order and stop on the first error or cancellation. Previously completed operations remain completed. There is no automatic all-seeders selection, retry, rollback, or once-only tracking. If order matters, make it explicit in the command or in one orchestrating operation."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Own transaction boundaries","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html#own-transaction-boundaries","Text":"A seeder with several dependent writes should use its injected transaction manager: return s.transactions.Execute(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { if err := s.products.EnsureDefaults(ctx); err != nil { return err } return s.prices.EnsureDefaults(ctx) }) This fragment assumes two repository operations using database.Executor with the same pool and callback context. The registry does not create a transaction for you. Keep mail, raw queue submissions, and other external effects outside that scope. Notification intent can use its documented SQL transaction path. Stable keys and constraints make reruns predictable. Cancellation or a failed commit can leave an uncertain outcome; inspect durable state before retrying work with external effects. See transactions."},{"Title":"Seeders","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Verify and troubleshoot","Parent":"","URL":"seeders.html#verify-and-troubleshoot","Text":"A useful seeder test applies migrations to a disposable database, runs the seeder twice, and asserts the intended final rows and preserved user data. Put business coverage in tests/catalog, not the service or repository package. Symptom Check Seeder does not appear in seed Module registration and non-nil Targets.Seeders Duplicate registration The name is global; qualify it with the module Missing table Apply migrations before running seeders Earlier seeder persisted after later failure Selection is sequential, not one automatic transaction Deadline expired Batch size, query context propagation, and the total command timeout Continue with console commands, migrations, and database testing."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html","Text":"Cache keeps replaceable data close to an operation. Inject cache.Store into the service that needs it, choose a finite TTL, and keep authoritative data in its owning repository. Start with cache.Remember to load a value only when absent."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Configure a driver","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#configure-a-driver","Text":"For one-process development: CACHE_DRIVER=memory For shared cache across application replicas: CACHE_DRIVER=redis CACHE_REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0 CACHE_NAMESPACE=myapp The code default is disabled, which leaves Resources.Cache nil. The example environment selects Redis. Required cache dependencies should be checked during service construction; optional caching needs an explicit bypass path. Driver Scope and capacity Expiration and ownership memory One process; bounded count and total bytes; rejects full writes Lazy expiration; app owns and closes the store redis Shared backend; capacity and eviction are backend policy Native TTL; store borrows a Redis client database Shared PostgreSQL/MySQL table Expired rows are unreadable; explicit cleanup reclaims them; pool is borrowed Changing a driver does not transfer cached values. Namespaces separate keys, not permissions. Do not cache secrets or cross-tenant results without a deliberate access and retention policy."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Load on a miss and work with multiple values","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#load-on-a-miss-and-work-with-multiple-values","Text":"The two loading forms share the same behavior: Need API Return value Preserve a known Go result type cache.Remember(ctx, store, key, ttl, load) Your loader's type Work with dynamic JSON store.Remember(ctx, key, ttl, load) any containing JSON objects, arrays, or scalars Batch operations are covered below; use Remember first for an ordinary single-resource lookup."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Store and typed loading","Parent":"Load on a miss and work with multiple values","URL":"cache.html#store-and-typed-loading","Text":"This complete helper caches a transport-independent product summary. Place it in the service that owns the query and inject its store and loader: package service import ( \"context\" \"time\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/cache\" ) type ProductSummary struct { ID string `json:\"id\"` Name string `json:\"name\"` } func CachedProduct(ctx context.Context, store cache.Store, id string, load func(context.Context) (ProductSummary, error), ) (ProductSummary, error) { return cache.Remember(ctx, store, \"catalog:v1:product:\"+id, 5*time.Minute, load) } This proposed Catalog helper requires a non-nil store. The caller authorizes the operation and chooses a key containing every dimension that changes the result, such as tenant or locale. The loader reads authoritative data using its supplied context. The first successful call loads and caches; later calls return the cached summary until expiry or deletion. The dynamic form uses the same cache path: value, err := store.Remember(ctx, \"settings:v1\", 5*time.Minute, func(ctx context.Context) (any, error) { return map[string]any{\"theme\": \"dark\"}, nil }) if err != nil { return err } // value is a map[string]any on both misses and hits. _ = value Dynamic objects become map[string]any, arrays become []any, and numbers become json.Number. The typed helper restores its concrete type. Both forms JSON-encode and decode on a miss as well as a hit, producing caller-owned values with the same shape on both paths. Values must round-trip through JSON."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Understand Remember's guarantees","Parent":"Load on a miss and work with multiple values","URL":"cache.html#understand-remembers-guarantees","Text":"Event Behavior Hit Decode and return; keep the existing TTL Miss Call the loader, encode, then store; TTL starts after loading Loader failure Return it; do not cache the failure Backend read failure or corrupt cached JSON Return an error; do not call the loader Encoding or write failure Return an error, even if loading succeeded Concurrent misses More than one loader may run Cancellation during loading Loader should stop; canceled results are not published afterward There is no automatic stampede lock or stale-while-revalidate behavior. A loader racing Delete or Clear can refill a key. Use versioned keys or an explicit consistency strategy where this matters. Do not put irreversible work in a loader."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Store, read, and delete","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#store-read-and-delete","Text":"The base store reads and writes raw bytes. This fragment belongs in an operation that already has a store and serialized encodedProduct: const key = \"catalog:v1:product:42\" if err := store.Set(ctx, key, encodedProduct, 5*time.Minute); err != nil { return err } value, found, err := store.Get(ctx, key) if err != nil { return err } if found { // Decode value using the format used by the writer. _ = value } return store.Delete(ctx, key) Import time; the injected interface is github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/cache.Store. Nil and empty byte slices are valid entries, so use found, not len(value), to recognize a hit. Deleting a missing key succeeds. Set atomically replaces one value and its expiry. TTL must be positive; zero does not mean forever. Get does not extend TTL. Expired entries are misses, and remote drivers round positive TTLs up to whole milliseconds. Keys contain 1–255 UTF-8 bytes without NUL, are case-sensitive, and preserve trailing spaces. The size limit includes key and value. Returned data belongs to the caller; retained inputs are copied, but do not mutate slices or maps while a call is running."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Check presence","Parent":"Store, read, and delete","URL":"cache.html#check-presence","Text":"found, err := store.Exists(ctx, \"catalog:v1:product:42\") if err != nil { return err } Use found for a presence-only operation. An Exists followed by Set is not an atomic “create if absent” operation; the interface provides no uniqueness lock."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Raw and typed batches","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#raw-and-typed-batches","Text":"Use store methods for bytes: if err := store.SetMulti(ctx, map[string][]byte{ \"binary:v1\": {0, 255, 128}, \"empty:v1\": nil, }, time.Minute); err != nil { return err } raw, err := store.GetMulti(ctx, []string{\"binary:v1\", \"empty:v1\", \"missing:v1\"}) if err != nil { return err } // raw contains the two hits; missing keys are omitted. _ = raw Use package helpers for JSON: if err := cache.SetMulti(ctx, store, map[string]ProductSummary{ \"catalog:v1:product:42\": {ID: \"42\", Name: \"Starter product\"}, }, time.Minute); err != nil { return err } products, err := cache.GetMulti[ProductSummary](ctx, store, []string{\"catalog:v1:product:42\"}) if err != nil { return err } _ = products ProductSummary is the type defined above. cache.GetMulti[any] supports dynamic JSON. Do not read arbitrary binary values with JSON helpers; incompatible or corrupt data is an error, not a miss. Batches accept at most 1,000 keys/items and 8 MiB of key/value data, subject to each entry's size limit. Writes validate the batch first; a later backend failure may leave partial writes. Reads are not a cross-key snapshot. A read or JSON-decoding error returns no partial result and does not mutate cached entries."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Invalidate after a write","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#invalidate-after-a-write","Text":"Delete the relevant key after the authoritative write has committed. This optional cache example runs outside any caller SQL transaction. A successful repository call inside a joined transaction is not yet a commit; the outer operation must invalidate only after its transaction completes. if err := s.products.Rename(ctx, id, name); err != nil { return err } if err := s.cache.Delete(ctx, \"catalog:v1:product:\"+id); err != nil { logging.FromContext(ctx).WarnContext(ctx, \"product cache invalidation failed\", \"error_category\", logging.ErrorCategory(err)) } return nil This proposed service fragment uses its repository and cache dependencies and imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/logging. It returns the committed result even if cache invalidation fails. The cached value can remain stale until its TTL expires; the earlier example bounds that lifetime to five minutes. Choose this policy only when that stale window is acceptable. Stricter consistency needs an explicit design; reporting an error cannot roll back an already committed SQL write. Commit-before-invalidation alone does not eliminate stale-fill races. Version keys when a type or representation changes, and use a deliberate policy for concurrent writes and loaders. Cache is not the source of truth."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Clear a namespace","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#clear-a-namespace","Text":"if err := store.Clear(ctx); err != nil { return err } This administrative operation keeps the store usable. Memory clears the instance; Redis scans/unlinks matching namespace keys across primaries; SQL deletes within its namespace and current transaction. A remote error may leave a partial clear, and concurrent writers can repopulate data. Quiesce writers when a complete clear is required. Clear never closes a store or its borrowed connection."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Capacity, timeouts, and tuning","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#capacity-timeouts-and-tuning","Text":"The main defaults are: Setting Default Meaning CACHE_NAMESPACE default Remote key namespace CACHE_MAX_ENTRY_BYTES 1048576 Key plus value limit CACHE_MEMORY_CAPACITY 10000 Retained entry count CACHE_MEMORY_MAX_BYTES 67108864 Retained key/value budget; 64 MiB CACHE_OPERATION_TIMEOUT 5s Remote operation bound CACHE_DATABASE_TABLE cache_entries SQL table Only configure values you need to change. Process memory also includes map metadata and in-flight copies. Memory returns cache.ErrFull instead of evicting live entries; a rejected replacement leaves its old value intact. Expired entries are reclaimed on reads or admission pressure, without a maintenance goroutine. Concrete memory stores additionally expose Usage(), PruneExpired(ctx, limit), and GetInto(ctx, key, buf[:0]) for explicit cleanup or buffer reuse. Portable services should depend on cache.Store unless they need those controls. Redis keys use cache:\u003cnamespace\u003e:\u003ckey\u003e. The URL-based app setup opens a standalone client. pkg/redis.Open also supports explicit Sentinel/Cluster configuration for independent integrations; changing a URL alone does not describe every cluster topology. Backend eviction can remove entries before their TTL."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Database cache","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#database-cache","Text":"Set CACHE_DRIVER=database with the database driver and URL, then publish and apply the cache table: go run ./cmd/cli cache tables go run ./cmd/cli migrate up Expired rows remain stored until cleanup. With a concrete database cache store: removed, err := store.PruneExpired(ctx, 1000) Handle err before using the deleted count. PruneExpired belongs to cache/database.Store, not the portable interface. It removes a bounded batch in that namespace; locked rows can remain for a later pass. Schedule maintenance explicitly using the application's central scheduling. Database cache operations resolve database.Executor(ctx, pool) and join a SQL transaction using the same pool. Rollback undoes its writes and deletes. TTL starts at Set, not commit. Memory and Redis do not share that transaction behavior. See transactions."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Misses, failures, and invalidation","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#misses-failures-and-invalidation","Text":"A backend failure is not a miss. Decide explicitly whether your service should fail or fall back to authoritative storage. Remember itself propagates the error; it does not silently fall back. Avoid logging cached payloads. Symptom Check Cache dependency is nil CACHE_DRIVER and constructor validation Every replica loads separately The memory driver is process-local ErrInvalidTTL Supply a positive duration; no forever entries ErrFull Memory capacity/byte budget and expired-entry cleanup ErrTooLarge Key + value size and batch limits ErrInvalidValue Writer/reader encoding and key version agree SQL table missing Publish cache tables, rebuild, and apply migrations Deleted value reappears Concurrent loader or writer refilled it Use errors.Is with stable cache.Err… values. There are no portable counters, locks, compare-and-swap operations, or atomic multi-key transactions in this store."},{"Title":"Cache","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Ownership and example","Parent":"","URL":"cache.html#ownership-and-example","Text":"App owns resource cleanup once, after users stop. Remote stores borrow their clients; modules must not close shared clients or pools. Constructors do not create tables or start background work. Standalone code owns the resources it opens. CACHE_DRIVER=memory go run ./examples/cache The example loads central configuration, writes and reads an entry, and closes its resources. Remote variants need the selected backend and, for SQL, applied migrations. Test hit/miss behavior, expiry, loader failures, wrong encodings, and invalidation outcomes against the driver your application actually uses. Continue with database queries, transactions, and testing."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html","Text":"A disk is a named storage connection. App opens selected disks and shares native Go CDK *blob.Bucket values. Use those buckets for exports, documents or other raw objects. For owned uploads, public/private asset URLs and deletion recovery, use Media above these primitives."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Select a disk","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#select-a-disk","Text":"Start locally without a cloud provider: FILESYSTEM_DISK=local FILESYSTEM_LOCAL_ROOT=storage/app FILESYSTEM_DISK selects a name, not a driver. Named definitions live in internal/app/config/filesystems.go: Disk name Driver Environment prefix local Local filesystem FILESYSTEM_LOCAL_ s3 Amazon S3 FILESYSTEM_S3_ minio S3-compatible MinIO FILESYSTEM_MINIO_ spaces S3-compatible Spaces FILESYSTEM_SPACES_ gcs Google Cloud Storage FILESYSTEM_GCS_ azure Azure Blob Storage FILESYSTEM_AZURE_ App opens the default plus FILESYSTEM_ADDITIONAL_DISKS once. disabled omits the default, but explicitly selected additional disks still open. Unselected providers need no credentials/bucket. Media URL settings are validated for all definitions; provider requirements are validated when a disk is selected or explicitly opened. The Go default selects MinIO; the tutorial profile disables storage. Tilt enables MinIO by default, while Compose needs its storage profile. See local development."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Try a complete storage operation","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#try-a-complete-storage-operation","Text":"APP_ENV_FILE='' FILESYSTEM_DISK=local go run ./examples/storage Expect Hello from the configured filesystem. The example writes a uniquely named small object, reads it, deletes it, and closes its connection. No full app or migration is needed. It uses the existing configuration loader but constructs only storage; inherited process settings still affect configuration validation."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Inject and use the bucket","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#inject-and-use-the-bucket","Text":"During module construction, choose from resources.Disks and pass that bucket to the service that needs it. Fail construction if an enabled operation requires an unavailable disk; do not look it up from the resource bundle on every call. For a small bounded object inside the service: if err := bucket.WriteAll(ctx, \"exports/example.txt\", []byte(\"hello\"), nil); err != nil { return err } body, err := bucket.ReadAll(ctx, \"exports/example.txt\") if err != nil { return err } This operation fragment assumes a borrowed *blob.Bucket and context. Consume body as the result. App owns the connection; the service must not close it. Generate or validate object keys in the business operation. A request filename is not an authorization rule."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Stream larger objects","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#stream-larger-objects","Text":"Use readers/writers when the object should not be buffered in memory. Complete helper for a borrowed bucket, authorized key and caller-owned reader: package files import ( \"context\" \"errors\" \"io\" \"gocloud.dev/blob\" ) func Upload(ctx context.Context, bucket *blob.Bucket, key string, src io.Reader) error { ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(ctx) defer cancel() writer, err := bucket.NewWriter(ctx, key, nil) if err != nil { return err } _, copyErr := io.Copy(writer, src) if copyErr != nil { cancel() } return errors.Join(copyErr, writer.Close()) } The caller bounds input size and duration. Writer Close finalizes an upload and can fail; preserve its error. Cancel before closing if input copying fails. Cancellation still cannot prove that a remote write rolled back after acceptance. Use stable keys and reconciliation when an outcome is uncertain."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Read and close an object","Parent":"Stream larger objects","URL":"filesystems.html#read-and-close-an-object","Text":"reader, err := bucket.NewReader(ctx, key, nil) if err != nil { return err } _, copyErr := io.Copy(destination, reader) return errors.Join(copyErr, reader.Close()) This function-body fragment imports io and errors; destination is an io.Writer. Finish all readers/writers before the owner closes the connection. Native range reads are available when your operation needs only part of an object."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Open another named disk","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#open-another-named-disk","Text":"Add a name to the definitions in FilesystemConfig.load: \"invoices\": {Driver: \"s3\"}, Then supply its own settings and select it alongside the default: FILESYSTEM_ADDITIONAL_DISKS=invoices FILESYSTEM_INVOICES_BUCKET=example-invoices FILESYSTEM_INVOICES_REGION=eu-west-1 Use provider-managed credentials or the disk's explicit credential fields. Names and drivers are defined in Go; setting an environment prefix does not create a new disk. Settings are immutable after assembly. For a connection you own independently: options, err := cfg.Filesystem.StorageConfig(\"invoices\") if err != nil { return err } connection, err := storage.Open(ctx, options) if err != nil { return err } This acquisition fragment imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/storage. Pass connection.Bucket to consumers, then preserve connection.Close() errors after all users finish. Calls are not cached. See examples/storage/main.go for a full function with cleanup, or the pkg/storage/README.md constructor reference."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Listing, deletion and signed URLs","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#listing-deletion-and-signed-urls","Text":"Native operation Use Attributes Read size and object metadata List Iterate a prefix with a caller-defined result limit NewRangeReader Read a byte range Copy Copy an object within the bucket Delete Remove an object SignedURL Grant temporary provider access Handle iterator errors as well as the end of iteration. Use gcerrors.Code(err) for portable error classification. Copy then delete is not a portable atomic rename; blob operations do not become atomic with SQL. After authorizing an object, issue a native download link: url, err := bucket.SignedURL(ctx, key, \u0026blob.SignedURLOptions{ Expiry: 5 * time.Minute, }) if err != nil { return err } This fragment imports time and gocloud.dev/blob. Its url is a bearer credential. Store the disk/key, not the expiring link. A signed PUT URL is possible through native options, but does not verify that an uploaded file is safe or create a managed Media asset. Provider Signing requirement in this integration S3-compatible SDK signing credentials and a client-reachable endpoint GCS Service-account credentials containing email/private key; IAM remote signing is not configured Azure Shared-key client; signed PUT also needs x-ms-blob-type: BlockBlob Local Native signing is not configured; use Media's internal links"},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Media visibility and URLs","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#media-visibility-and-urls","Text":"These disk settings apply to Media assets, not raw bucket writes or operating system permissions: FILESYSTEM_MINIO_VISIBILITY=public FILESYSTEM_MINIO_URL_MODE=internal FILESYSTEM_MINIO_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:9000/uploads MEDIA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/api/v1/media Setting Controls VISIBILITY=public|private Default for new uploads; an explicit upload value overrides it URL_MODE=internal Return application content/download routes URL_MODE=direct Public permanent provider URLs and private signed provider URLs PUBLIC_URL Browser-reachable public bucket/CDN root, including any bucket path MEDIA_BASE_URL Application media route origin/prefix; not the storage endpoint Direct mode requires PUBLIC_URL, even when the default visibility is private. Local disks require internal mode. The Media URL matrix shows precisely which links require a bearer header and which expire."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Configure a direct public root","Parent":"Media visibility and URLs","URL":"filesystems.html#configure-a-direct-public-root","Text":"Examples: S3 virtual-host: https://BUCKET.s3.REGION.amazonaws.com MinIO path-style: https://storage.example.com/BUCKET CDN: https://media.example.com This setting builds public URLs. Private signatures use the native client's Endpoint, not PublicURL. Compose/Tilt's internal http://minio:9000 is useful for app-to-storage traffic but inaccessible to an ordinary browser outside that network. Choose an endpoint reachable by both before enabling private direct links. Do not rewrite the hostname after signing: the signature includes it. Temporary provider credentials may expire sooner than the requested link lifetime."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Grant public access to public objects only","Parent":"Media visibility and URLs","URL":"filesystems.html#grant-public-access-to-public-objects-only","Text":"Media writes public assets under public/assets/\u003cid\u003e and private assets under assets/\u003cid\u003e. For permanent public links, provision anonymous read only on public/*, leaving uploads/deletes authenticated. Example S3/MinIO statement for an uploads bucket: { \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Principal\": \"*\", \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\", \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::uploads/public/*\" } Place this in a bucket policy's Statement array with version 2012-10-17 and check account-level public-access restrictions. The framework does not change ACLs or policies. For providers lacking suitable prefix restrictions, use separate public and private disks instead of exposing a mixed container. Direct reads bypass app headers, visibility/deletion status checks and rate limits. Objects may be readable before metadata is ready, and CDN caches may retain deleted bytes. Use provider/CDN controls and a separate media origin for untrusted content. Changing to internal URL mode does not revoke existing public storage permissions. Legacy public objects outside public/ retain internal URLs until explicitly copied and their keys updated. Configuration changes do not move objects."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Storage limits and verification","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#storage-limits-and-verification","Text":"Remote buckets/containers must already exist. Constructing a client does not probe bucket existence or permissions; the first operation can still fail. Use HTTPS for remote endpoints. Workload identity or operator-managed credentials belong in configuration, never upload inputs. Local roots are created with private permissions; existing permissions are not rewritten. Keep Go CDK attribute sidecars when backing up. Protect the root against untrusted writes/symlinks and provision shared storage deliberately for replicas. Symptom Check App starts but upload is denied Bucket existence and operation-specific credentials An additional disk is missing Add its Go definition and select it in Additional disks File exists on one replica only Local storage is host-specific without a shared volume Read succeeds but signing fails Provider-specific signing credentials Direct URL opens only inside a container Endpoint/PublicURL reachability Local and MinIO tests do not prove real-cloud permissions or recovery semantics. Use disposable provider acceptance resources for the operations you rely on."},{"Title":"File storage","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Media asset URLs","Parent":"","URL":"filesystems.html#media-asset-urls","Text":"For database-backed uploads, use Media and its HTTP url field or Library.URL(ctx, asset) after authorization. The library returns a Link; HTTP asset responses map its expiry to url_expires_at. Explicit temporary links return expires_at. See private-link examples for client code and signing-failure behavior."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"media.html","Text":"Media adds owned asset records, visibility, URLs and recoverable deletion above file storage. PostgreSQL stores metadata; a named Go CDK disk stores bytes. Use it for files attached to users or other business models. Use raw storage for an object that needs no asset lifecycle."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Before uploading","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#before-uploading","Text":"For a local first run, configure a disk and a client-reachable media origin: FILESYSTEM_DISK=local FILESYSTEM_LOCAL_ROOT=storage/app FILESYSTEM_LOCAL_VISIBILITY=public FILESYSTEM_LOCAL_URL_MODE=internal MEDIA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/media Keep the working PostgreSQL/Redis settings from installation. Apply migrations with go run ./cmd/cli migrate up. The supplied HTTP routes also require Identity authentication; sign in and save the access token before trying the commands below. App supplies Resources.Media, a constructed *media.Library. Modules borrow it; the library starts no workers and never closes app-owned buckets."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Upload and use an asset","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#upload-and-use-an-asset","Text":"The default disk visibility is public. Upload a file through the existing API: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/media \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' \\ -F 'file=@photo.png' Expect 201. The response's data contains the asset ID, filename, content type, size, collection, status, visibility and url. Use data.url to display the public asset. Storage keys and disk names are deliberately omitted from HTTP responses. For a sensitive document, choose private visibility explicitly: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/media \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' \\ -F 'file=@statement.pdf' \\ -F 'visibility=private' The generic HTTP upload accepts exactly one file part and an optional visibility field, uses the default collection, and limits the file to 20 MiB. It does not accept an arbitrary owner, disk or collection from the client. Build a business route when those choices require your own policy."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Upload from a business operation","Parent":"Upload and use an asset","URL":"media.html#upload-from-a-business-operation","Text":"After authorizing the target user, call the borrowed library: asset, err := library.Upload(ctx, media.UploadInput{ Owner: media.Owner{ Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: userID.String(), }, Collection: \"avatar\", Filename: filename, Body: file, Policy: media.Policy{ MaxBytes: 5 \u003c\u003c 20, ContentTypes: []string{\"image/jpeg\", \"image/png\"}, }, }) if err != nil { return err } This service fragment assumes library, ctx, userID, filename and an open reader. Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/media and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/entities. The caller closes its reader and supplies its deadline. An explicit Visibility: media.Private overrides the disk default. Policy.Disk selects an already opened disk; omission uses the default. Upload returns an Asset, not an enriched HTTP response. Call URL after a successful upload when you need a link. Decide how to report later link-resolution failure without telling a client that its already committed upload rolled back."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Validate upload policy","Parent":"Upload and use an asset","URL":"media.html#validate-upload-policy","Text":"Policy Behavior MaxBytes Required; 1 byte through 1 GiB ContentTypes Optional list of detected MIME types; empty allows any detected type Disk Optional configured/opened disk name Filename Sanitized display metadata; never an object key Media sniffs a small prefix and streams the remainder with a byte limit. Generated UUID keys prevent user filenames from choosing object paths. MIME detection does not prove that content is malware-free or a decodable image. If your application requires scanning, add a deliberate quarantine/processing policy before exposure."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Public URLs and private links","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#public-urls-and-private-links","Text":"Visibility and delivery mode are separate decisions: Visibility Disk URL mode URL / HTTP url Reader needs Public internal App /media/{id}/content No bearer token Private internal App /media/{id}/download Owner's bearer token Public direct Permanent provider/CDN URL Public-read storage policy Private direct Provider-signed URL, normally 15 minutes Possession of the link New uploads inherit the selected disk's visibility; explicit upload visibility wins. Changing the default affects new uploads only. Visibility is fixed for an existing asset; replacing it creates another asset ID. For direct mode, configure public prefixes, signing credentials and a client-reachable endpoint as described in filesystem URL settings. Local disks require internal mode. Internal mode does not remove a bucket's existing public permissions."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Resolve an existing asset URL","Parent":"Public URLs and private links","URL":"media.html#resolve-an-existing-asset-url","Text":"asset, err := library.Get(ctx, owner, assetID) if err != nil { return err } link, err := library.URL(ctx, asset) if err != nil { return err } This read fragment assumes an already authorized owner. Get scopes the record to that owner. URL itself does not authorize the supplied Asset or reload its state: call it only after authorization. Private direct URLs grant bearer access. A nil/non-ready asset yields an empty link. media.Link exposes URL and ExpiresAt; zero expiry means a stable URL. Public legacy objects whose keys lack public/ retain internal URLs until an explicit storage/metadata migration."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Request a temporary link","Parent":"Public URLs and private links","URL":"media.html#request-a-temporary-link","Text":"For a private asset displayed where an Authorization header is impractical: curl -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/media/ASSET_ID/url \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"expires_in\":900}' Expect 200, with data.url and data.expires_at. The endpoint authorizes the owner and checks readiness. For Go callers: link, err := library.TemporaryURL(ctx, owner, assetID, 15*time.Minute) if err != nil { return err } Import time. The Go API uses 15 minutes for zero duration; the HTTP field defaults to 900 seconds. Allowed lifetime is 1 second through 24 hours. Response/call Expiry field Upload/list/metadata HTTP asset url_expires_at, omitted for stable URLs Temporary-link HTTP response expires_at Library.URL or TemporaryURL result Link.ExpiresAt Links are reusable bearer credentials until expiry, not single-use tokens. They do not end when an Identity session logs out. Provider credentials may expire before the requested lifetime. Do not persist an expiring URL as an asset identifier; store the asset ID and request a current link."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Configure internal signing","Parent":"Public URLs and private links","URL":"media.html#configure-internal-signing","Text":"Internal temporary links need a separate 32-byte secret: openssl rand -base64 32 Store it as MEDIA_SIGNING_KEY and share it across replicas. Keep MEDIA_BASE_URL at the exact public origin/path used by the registered routes. The application default is http://localhost:8080/api/v1/media; direct media.New callers get /api/v1/media when BaseURL is empty. Without a key, public internal URLs and authenticated downloads still work; internal temporary-link creation returns 503. Direct temporary links use provider signing instead. Rotating the media key or changing BaseURL invalidates existing internal signatures."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Understand link failures","Parent":"Public URLs and private links","URL":"media.html#understand-link-failures","Text":"Private direct signing can fail because a disk is missing, credentials cannot sign, or the provider operation fails. Library.URL returns an authenticated internal fallback and an error in that case. The supplied upload HTTP handler returns its already committed asset with that fallback. Metadata/list requests surface the signing error, and explicit temporary link requests return an error. A successful upload response therefore does not prove provider signing is configured. Always inspect the returned URL mode/expiry when diagnosing client access."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Content behavior","Parent":"Public URLs and private links","URL":"media.html#content-behavior","Text":"Internal content reads check ready state and visibility/signature on each request. Private, missing, deleted and invalid-link assets return the same not-found result. Supplying invalid signature parameters is rejected even for public assets. App content uses no-store, nosniff, sandbox and no-referrer headers. Only an allowlist of image/audio/video MIME types displays inline; HTML, SVG and other active formats download as attachments. The download endpoint always uses an attachment and application/octet-stream. This integration does not implement range-request/video-streaming behavior. Direct URLs bypass app headers, rate limits and metadata checks. A direct public object can become readable as soon as its bytes exist, before metadata is marked ready. It stops being directly readable when the object is deleted, subject to provider/CDN caches. Choose internal delivery when these app checks are required."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Relationships and collections","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#relationships-and-collections","Text":"Every asset has one owner and one collection. The owner is the model the file belongs to, not necessarily the actor uploading it. user/42 and admin/42 are different owners. Add stable business type names in internal/modules/shared/entities/entity_types.go; no platform model registry is needed. An omitted collection becomes default. List a particular collection: page, err := library.List(ctx, owner, \"avatar\", pagination.NewPagination(1, 20)) if err != nil { return err } Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/pagination. An empty collection filter lists all ready assets for that owner. Type/collection names match [a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,63}; owner IDs are nonempty UTF-8 strings up to 200 bytes without surrounding whitespace or controls. Collections group assets; they do not automatically enforce quotas, ordering or single-avatar replacement. Define those rules in the business operation. For replacement: Upload and validate the new asset. Commit the business relationship to the new asset ID. Delete the old asset after commit, retaining failures for recovery. If relationships require deletion checks, adapt or disable the generic delete route; Media cannot infer arbitrary future relationships. There is no many-to-many shared-attachment registry."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Delete an owner safely","Parent":"Relationships and collections","URL":"media.html#delete-an-owner-safely","Text":"Polymorphic references cannot enforce foreign keys across arbitrary owner tables. The business operation checks existence and access, stops new uploads for a deleting owner, coordinates in-flight work, then deletes assets through Library. Deleting the owner row or publishing a best-effort local event alone does not remove blobs. if err := library.Delete(ctx, owner, assetID); err != nil { return err } Delete is owner-scoped and repeatable for retained tombstones. A foreign owner cannot delete the record. Open also checks ownership/readiness and returns a native reader which its caller must close."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Disks and lifecycle","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#disks-and-lifecycle","Text":"Resources.Disks contains app-owned opened buckets; DefaultDisk selects one. Media copies the map and borrows the bucket pointers. Construct independently inside this application template using existing clients: library, err := media.New(db, disks, defaultDisk, media.Config{ BaseURL: \"/api/v1/media\", SigningKey: signingKey, Disks: diskOptions, }) if err != nil { return err } This construction fragment assumes a Bun DB, named native buckets and validated options. It opens nothing and starts no goroutines. Use integration.Register for public/signed content serving, CLI, jobs and migrations. Identity registers user management endpoints separately through its own HTTP delivery. Platform media imports no business modules or shared business definitions. Keep names/backing locations stable while assets reference them. Changing the default affects new uploads; changing an endpoint does not migrate objects. Copy/verify bytes and update metadata explicitly when changing providers. Local storage needs an intentionally shared filesystem across replicas. Remote permissions must match the selected visibility/URL policy."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Upload and deletion state","Parent":"Disks and lifecycle","URL":"media.html#upload-and-deletion-state","Text":"stateDiagram-v2 accTitle: Asset state follows the object lifecycle accDescr: Upload saves pending metadata, writes and closes the object, then marks it ready. Failed uploads and explicit deletion become deleting work. Recovery retries removal before leaving a deleted tombstone. [*] --\u003e Pending: Save metadata Pending --\u003e Ready: Write object and close writer Pending --\u003e Deleting: Failure or stale upload Ready --\u003e Deleting: Delete requested Deleting --\u003e Deleting: Removal failed Deleting --\u003e Deleted: Object removed or absent Blob I/O cannot join a SQL transaction. Upload, Delete and Recover reject a caller SQL transaction context. Upload has a two-minute deadline; failed/canceled uploads attempt cleanup with a separate five-second budget and preserve cleanup errors. SQL metadata remains recoverable if cleanup fails. Deletion stores intent before touching bytes. Missing objects count as removed; metadata becomes a tombstone only after removal. Unready uploads remain eligible for repeated deletion through the ten-minute crash grace to catch late remote writes after cancellation."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Recover interrupted work","Parent":"Disks and lifecycle","URL":"media.html#recover-interrupted-work","Text":"go run ./cmd/cli media recover Recovery marks uploads older than ten minutes for cleanup and attempts at most 100 deletion rows per batch. Failed rows rotate through the backlog; concurrent and repeated runs are supported. It scans known metadata, not arbitrary bucket objects. A provider capable of completing writes beyond this grace requires a longer deliberate recovery policy. App schedules media.recover.v1 every five minutes when disks exist. Run one scheduler and workers, or invoke recovery through an operator schedule. Monitor old pending/deleting rows and task failures. Back up metadata and objects together; a database restored behind its bucket needs explicit inventory reconciliation. Tombstone retention is a separate policy because removing tombstones changes repeated-delete behavior."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"HTTP and CLI","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#http-and-cli","Text":"Identity owns the authenticated user management routes in internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/media_handler.go, with registration in Identity's routes.go. Platform media integration owns only the public/signed content route and generic streaming behavior. All routes below are beneath /api/v1: Method and route Behavior POST /media Owner-authenticated multipart upload; 201 GET /media?page=1\u0026per_page=20\u0026collection=avatar Owner-scoped ready assets GET /media/{id} Owner-scoped metadata and resolved URL GET /media/{id}/download Owner-authenticated attachment GET /media/{id}/content Ready public content or a valid signed link POST /media/{id}/url Owner-authorized temporary link DELETE /media/{id} Owner-scoped deletion; 204 The current user's ID determines management ownership; callers cannot substitute a body owner. Metadata stays private even for public bytes. The upload adapter bounds the complete multipart body and cleans temporary files. Trusted operators can select other owner types: go run ./cmd/cli media upload user USER_ID ./document.pdf --collection documents --visibility private go run ./cmd/cli media list user USER_ID --collection documents go run ./cmd/cli media delete user USER_ID ASSET_ID go run ./cmd/cli media recover CLI output is the capability record, not the enriched HTTP AssetResponse. Use the HTTP metadata endpoint or Library.URL when you need a resolved URL."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Compatibility and extension","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#compatibility-and-extension","Text":"The visibility migration preserves existing rows as private and defaults new rows to public. Apply it with migrate up; no database reset is needed. Internal mode needs no public bucket policy. Direct public mode requires explicit scoped permissions, as documented in file storage. Image variants, antivirus scanning, direct-upload verification and video processing are extension work. Native storage can create signed PUT URLs, but Media does not provide a managed direct-upload/finalization flow. Use bounded jobs carrying asset IDs when adding processors. Business workflows decide whether an outcome should notify; Media does not automatically call Notifications."},{"Title":"Media assets","Section":"Data and storage","Heading":"Troubleshoot and verify","Parent":"","URL":"media.html#troubleshoot-and-verify","Text":"Symptom Check Public URL returns not found Ready metadata, visibility and disk/object availability Private URL fails in an image element Internal download needs a bearer header; request a temporary link Direct link uses minio:9000 Provider endpoint must be reachable by the client, not only app containers Signature fails after replacing its hostname Signatures cover the original host; configure the endpoint before signing Temporary internal link returns 503 Shared MEDIA_SIGNING_KEY configuration Public direct link returns access denied Read-only policy on public/*, account restrictions and correct PublicURL Upload succeeded but returned an internal download URL Provider signing failed and the upload adapter used its fallback Deleted direct content is still visible Object removal or CDN/provider caching Tests cover owner separation, visibility, URL modes, cleanup and recovery. Use disposable integration services to verify PostgreSQL and storage behavior; local/MinIO tests do not establish real-cloud permissions. Continue with file storage, scheduling, and observability."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html","Text":"Identity provides passwordless accounts, email OTP and Google sign-in, profile updates, access tokens and revocable sessions. A successful proof either creates an account or signs into an existing one; clients use the same flow for both."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Choose a flow","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#choose-a-flow","Text":"Task Start here Configure your first sign-in Email OTP setup Register or sign in with email Request and confirm a code Complete the user's name Update a profile Keep the user signed in Refresh and lifetimes Add Google sign-in Social login Protect your own operation Protected routes Investigate authentication failure Troubleshooting For a guided end-to-end example, follow the persisted HTTP and CLI operation."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Email OTP configuration and security","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#email-otp-configuration-and-security","Text":"Start with the PostgreSQL/Redis setup in installation. Copy the quickstart profile into your ignored local configuration, retaining its connection addresses. Configure these additional values: AUTH_ENABLED=true AUTH_VERIFICATION_KEY=\u003cfirst-independent-base64-32-byte-secret\u003e AUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN_KEY=\u003csecond-independent-base64-32-byte-secret\u003e RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:56379/2 MAIL_HOST=\u003cyour-SMTP-host\u003e MAIL_PORT=587 MAIL_USERNAME=\u003cyour-SMTP-user\u003e MAIL_PASSWORD=\u003cyour-SMTP-password\u003e MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=\u003cyour-verified-sender-address\u003e This is a configuration fragment for the tutorial Redis port. Use your own SMTP provider's port and credentials. Mail has no enable switch; it is constructed by the app and connects when sending. Authentication additionally requires a working rate-limit Redis connection, even when the broad API limiter is off. Generate each key independently: openssl rand -base64 32 Run it twice, save different outputs in the two fields, and keep them stable across restarts and shared across replicas. Never publish the values. Replacing the OTP verification key invalidates outstanding challenges and changes rate-limit keys. Apply migrations and restart with that profile: export APP_ENV_FILE=.env go run ./cmd/cli migrate up go run ./cmd/app Check configuration precedence if a shell variable overrides the file. Use a fresh local schema for a first run; startup never resets or migrates a database automatically."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Code policy","Parent":"Email OTP configuration and security","URL":"identity.html#code-policy","Text":"Rule Default Where it is enforced Code format Six decimal digits, including leading zeros HTTP and proof processing Lifetime 10 minutes, including email delivery time Stored challenge expiry Resend cooldown 1 minute per normalized address/purpose PostgreSQL Evaluated attempts 5 per challenge, including a successful submission PostgreSQL, even when proof is denied Shared request/attempt budgets Destination and accepted client IP Redis Synchronous email-send deadline 10 seconds Authentication service A new request invalidates earlier unused codes for that address. Use the challenge ID and code from the same email request. Resending does not reset shared Redis budgets. Only keyed code digests are stored; plaintext codes are not put in logs, responses, events, jobs or the notification ledger. SMTP acceptance is not inbox delivery. Delivery failure returns a safe 503, creates no account and does not refund abuse budgets. Wait for the cooldown, then request a fresh code. See Mail for delivery behavior."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#email-otp-sign-in-and-bearer-sessions","Text":"Request code → temporary challenge → confirm email proof ├─ unused email → create account + session → 201 ├─ active account → create session → 200 └─ disabled/deleted account → deny Sending a code creates no account. Successful confirmation consumes the proof, creates or verifies the account, and creates its session in one transaction. There is no separate signup or standalone email-verification endpoint."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Sign in","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#sign-in","Text":"Request a code for an address you control: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/otp/send \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"email\":\"person@example.com\"}' Expect 202 Accepted for both new and existing addresses: { \"data\": { \"challenge_id\": \"\u003cchallenge-uuid\u003e\", \"message\": \"Check your email for a sign-in code.\" } } Save data.challenge_id, read the code from the email, then submit both: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/otp/verify \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"challenge_id\":\"CHALLENGE_UUID\",\"code\":\"CODE_FROM_EMAIL\",\"platform\":\"web\",\"device_name\":\"My browser\"}' Replace both placeholders. Send the code as a string so a leading zero survives. platform is web, ios or android; device_name is an optional display label, not a trusted device identifier. An optional name applies only to a new account. Expect 201 for creation or 200 for an existing account. Both use the same data shape: Field Client use access_token Send in the Authorization header refresh_token Save securely; replace after every refresh token_type Bearer expires_in Remaining access lifetime in seconds refresh_expires_at Refresh deadline, or null for no time-based expiry session_id This login's session ID user Public account fields, including id, name, email and profile_complete The API discloses account existence only after proof succeeds. It does not set authentication cookies and does not accept tokens in URLs. Identity responses use Cache-Control: no-store."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Update a profile","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#update-a-profile","Text":"After either 200 or 201, inspect data.user.profile_complete. Currently a nonempty name makes the profile complete. New email accounts can have an empty name; social accounts use the provider name when available. Returning sign-ins never overwrite the existing profile. curl -i -X PATCH http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/users/me \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"name\":\"Ada Lovelace\"}' Expect 200, the updated public user and profile_complete: true. Names are trimmed and must contain 1–100 characters. At least one editable field is required; unknown fields, email, account status and user IDs are rejected. The completion flag is derived from the current profile rather than stored separately. Read the state on later application launches: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/users/me \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' Add future editable fields explicitly to UpdateProfileRequest and service.UpdateProfileInput, with validation in the operation/entity as needed."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Understand token and session lifetimes","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#understand-token-and-session-lifetimes","Text":"Policy field Environment key Default Meaning AccessTokenLifetime AUTH_ACCESS_TTL 168h (7 days) Expiry of one access JWT RefreshTokenLifetime AUTH_REFRESH_TTL 0 Lifetime granted to each new refresh credential; positive values slide on rotation AbsoluteSessionLifetime AUTH_SESSION_TTL 0 Maximum age from sign-in; activity never moves this deadline Access lifetime must be between 1 minute and 7 days. Positive refresh lifetime must be at least the access lifetime and at most 30 days. Positive session lifetime must cover the access and positive refresh lifetime and be at most 90 days. Zero disables only the respective refresh/session time limit. Scenario Result Defaults; user returns months later Access expired; the saved current refresh credential can obtain new tokens Refresh 168h, session 0; refresh on day 6 The replacement refresh credential receives another 7 days Refresh 168h; no refresh for 8 days Sign-in required; normal API reads do not renew refresh expiry Session 720h; continuous activity Sign-in required after 30 days; refresh cannot extend that deadline Logout or security revocation Subsequent access and refresh fail regardless of unlimited lifetimes Positive token lifetimes are capped by the stored session deadline. Unlimited expiry is SQL NULL and JSON null. Configuration changes affect new sessions and newly issued tokens; existing sessions retain their stored absolute deadline, and a refresh checks its current stored expiry before issuing a successor. Unlimited lifetime does not mean automatic client refresh. The client must retain its refresh credential securely, refresh when needed, and sign in again if that credential is lost."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Refresh and replay behavior","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#refresh-and-replay-behavior","Text":"curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/refresh \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"refresh_token\":\"CURRENT_REFRESH_TOKEN\"}' Expect 200 with new token fields in data. Replace both saved credentials. The response is a token result, without the sign-in response's user object. Rotation consumes the old credential and saves its successor atomically. Tokens contain 256 bits of randomness; only SHA-256 hashes are stored. Reusing a consumed credential revokes its session, including the newest access token. Serialize refreshes in the client. Two concurrent refreshes with one token can cause a rotation followed by replay revocation. If the response is lost, sign in again instead of replaying the old token. There is no grace period or cached plaintext successor. An uncertain database commit also requires fresh sign-in. Consumed hashes remain while a session is usable. Unlimited active sessions therefore accumulate rotation history until revoked or expired. Replay revocation commits even though the operation returns an authentication error."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Manage sessions","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#manage-sessions","Text":"All operations below use the current access token: Method and route Result GET /api/v1/auth/sessions?page=1\u0026per_page=20 Page of active sessions; current identifies this login DELETE /api/v1/auth/sessions/{id} Revoke one owned session; 204 DELETE /api/v1/auth/sessions Revoke all owned sessions, including this one; 204 POST /api/v1/auth/logout Revoke the current session; 204 curl -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/logout \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' Discard the saved credentials after logout. Session last_used_at reflects sign-in/refresh activity, not presence; a device name is an untrusted label. Sign-in updates the user's last_active_at; background refresh does not claim meaningful account activity. domain.AllowConcurrentSessions defaults to true. Setting it false revokes other sessions under the account lock during a new sign-in. Concurrent sign-ins cannot leave two active sessions. Changing the constant alone does not revoke existing sessions; use explicit revocation to reconcile them."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Protected routes and compatibility","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#protected-routes-and-compatibility","Text":"Register private operations on targets.AuthenticatedHTTP. App constructs that Huma group using Identity's authenticator; middleware verifies the bearer token and current PostgreSQL account/session state on each request. Inside a typed handler, obtain the current user ID: userID, err := api.CurrentUserID(ctx) if err != nil { return nil, response.Error(ctx, err) } This handler fragment imports platform api and api/response; use userID for the authorized service operation. For a route containing an account ID, use api.RequireCurrentUser(ctx, id) before the read. It returns not found on a mismatch and grants no administrator rights. api.Principal(ctx) returns auth.Principal{UserID, SessionID} after authentication. The principal contract lives in internal/platform/auth; account/session behavior and persistence remain owned by Identity. It is the verified identity for the current operation, not the raw JWT claims or a role/permission model. A valid principal does not authorize access to every record. Boundary Behavior Disabled authentication Protected routes fail with 401 /users/{id} Only the current user's ID is permitted Notifications/private media Recipient/owner derived from the authenticated user Forged user headers or query tokens Do not establish a principal Account suspension, deletion state, contact changes or logout Subsequent protected requests fail Database outage Operational failure; never anonymous access Reactivating an account does not revive revoked sessions. Requests admitted before revocation may finish; revocation is not cancellation of in-flight work. Checking state costs a database read per protected request. Account listing and status changes remain internal service operations; callers must supply administrative policy where appropriate. Use HTTPS beyond loopback. Keep mobile tokens in platform secure storage and choose browser token custody/BFF design explicitly. Never put bearer credentials in URLs, logs, analytics or error reports."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Cleanup and verification","Parent":"Email OTP sign-in and bearer sessions","URL":"identity.html#cleanup-and-verification","Text":"go run ./cmd/cli identity prune-verification go run ./cmd/cli identity prune-sessions These operator commands remove bounded batches. Session cleanup removes up to 1000 token rows, then up to 1000 empty revoked/absolutely expired sessions. Active sessions retain replay history, including when their lifetime is unlimited. App declares five-minute command schedules when authentication and command scheduling are enabled. A designated scheduler and workers must run; neither configuration nor registration executes cleanup by itself. Monitor whether the batch rate keeps up with expired data. Redis owns limiter-key expiry."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Google and unified social login","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#google-and-unified-social-login","Text":"Google exchanges a verified provider ID token for the same application session used by OTP. Configure the provider alongside authentication: AUTH_GOOGLE_ENABLED=true AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=\u003callowed-audience\u003e.apps.googleusercontent.com # Optional additional azp presenters, comma-separated without spaces. AUTH_GOOGLE_TRUSTED_PRESENTER_IDS=\u003cmobile-client\u003e.apps.googleusercontent.com The client ID is the accepted audience. Additional presenters never become extra audiences. When azp exists, it must be a nonempty string matching the audience client or an explicitly trusted presenter. Tokens without it can pass the other checks. For a browser Google button, use a Web application client with registered JavaScript origins; an installed-app client is not interchangeable. Obtain a Google ID token using your client integration, then send JSON: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/social/google \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"id_token\":\"GOOGLE_ID_TOKEN\",\"platform\":\"web\",\"device_name\":\"Chrome\"}' Expect 201 for a new account or 200 for an already linked provider identity, with the same tokens-and-user response as OTP. Never send a provider access token, email address or user ID as a substitute for the ID token. No Google client secret is needed by this API."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Account matching and provider validation","Parent":"Google and unified social login","URL":"identity.html#account-matching-and-provider-validation","Text":"Provider result Behavior Known (provider, subject) Sign into its linked active, verified account New subject, unused authoritative verified email Create account, provider identity and session atomically New subject, email already belongs to an account 409; no automatic linking or merging New account with non-authoritative Google email 422; use email OTP instead Suspended/deleted account Deny access Gmail and verified Google Workspace addresses can establish current email ownership. Other Google-account email addresses may have been verified only in the past and cannot create an account through this flow. Provider changes do not overwrite a linked account's local email or name. The configured verifier uses Google's ID-token validator for signatures, audience, expiry and cached key rotation, with explicit issuer, subject, issued-at and presenter checks. It never uses caller-selected issuer/key URLs. The client owns callback state, PKCE for redirect flows and nonce validation when requested. Google's direct HTML form-post callback is not supported; submit JSON. An ID token is reusable until its expiry, subject to account/session policy and rate limits. There is no one-time provider exchange challenge. No Google access/refresh tokens are persisted. Protect the ID token as a bearer credential."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Test with the supplied installed-app credentials","Parent":"Google and unified social login","URL":"identity.html#test-with-the-supplied-installed-app-credentials","Text":"If you have a local installed-app credential file and the API is configured for its audience: go run ./examples/social-login -credentials client_secret_*.json Open the printed URL. The helper uses a loopback callback, state, PKCE and a locally validated nonce. It saves the API response in .env.google-session.json with owner-only permissions, never prints tokens, and refuses to overwrite an existing file. Use -output .env.google-session-2.json for another attempt. The Google project's consent/test-user settings must allow your account. Read Google server-side verification and native-app authorization when building the client integration."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Shared rate limiting","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#shared-rate-limiting","Text":"Authentication uses shared Redis GCRA budgets: an initial burst replenishes gradually, not at calendar-hour boundaries. The defaults in domain.DefaultAuthRateLimitPolicy() are: Budget Burst and replenishment per hour Code requests per normalized destination 5 Code requests per accepted client IP 30 Proof/provider attempts per identity 10 Proof/provider attempts per peer IP 50 OTP identity budgets cover the destination across purposes. Social verification checks the peer before provider verification and the verified subject afterward. Successful and failed admitted work both count. One check may consume budget before a second denies access; failures do not refund it. RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL defaults to Redis database 2. Keep the namespace and policies consistent across replicas and separate from queue/cache databases. Loss, eviction or clearing of limiter data resets budgets; choose persistence and memory policy accordingly. Logical Redis databases do not isolate server memory or availability. The optional broad /api/v1 limiter is separate: API_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true API_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=120 API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=120 API_RATE_LIMIT_PERIOD=1m It exempts common health routes. Rejections return 429; the global middleware includes Retry-After. Redis failure returns 503 rather than bypassing admission. HTTP supplies api.ClientIP, which uses the immediate TCP peer by default. HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES enables a bounded, validated X-Forwarded-For chain only from explicitly trusted ingress peers. With no trust configured, clients behind a proxy/NAT share its budget. See the ingress contract."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Boundaries and verification","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#boundaries-and-verification","Text":"Authentication repositories intentionally own proof/refresh transactions. Failed proof counters and replay revocation must commit even when authentication is denied. Do not wrap these operations in a transaction that rolls back their security effects. Ordinary profile operations can join a caller-owned transaction. The proof contains the challenge ID, keyed digest, purpose and policy. The repository checks and consumes it atomically; a challenge is not an authenticated session. Wrong, expired, superseded, exhausted or consumed codes create neither account nor session. Concurrent confirmations cannot create duplicate accounts. If an account is created between request and proof, confirmation signs into that account without replacing its profile. A proof bound to an existing account cannot create another account using its old address after a contact change. Deleted addresses remain reserved. A different-purpose verification code cannot be used for sign-in. Access JWTs use HS256 with issuer, audience, expiry, issued-at, user/session UUIDs and typ: at+jwt. They contain IDs, not contact fields or authorization roles. The signing key is distinct from the OTP key. Replacing it invalidates access tokens; valid refresh credentials can obtain tokens signed with the new key. Overlapping signing-key rotation is not implemented. Share this symmetric key only with trusted instances able to mint tokens."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Complete module example: account onboarding","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#complete-module-example-account-onboarding","Text":"Account creation publishes UserRegistered only after commit. Existing-account sign-in does not publish it again. The canonical walkthrough is account onboarding, which includes complete event, job, notification and mail-view examples."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Follow one registration","Parent":"Complete module example: account onboarding","URL":"identity.html#follow-one-registration","Text":"Committed new account → local UserRegistered event → identity.welcome.v1 task in Redis → reload the current account → persist database/email notification intent → worker dispatcher enqueues delivery IDs → channel handlers attempt delivery The welcome job skips missing, deleted and inactive users. Eligible users receive database content and, with the configured sender and a verified address, email. Its stable welcome key converges retries for each recipient/channel; saved content is not overwritten. A replay can add an eligible email channel later. The initial event remains process-local and best-effort. The SQL ledger cannot recover a reaction that never reached it. Signup remains committed if publication fails. Use transactional notification intent for operations requiring atomic business state and intent. SMTP can still repeat after an ambiguous external failure."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Mail views: the mailable equivalent","Parent":"Complete module example: account onboarding","URL":"identity.html#mail-views-the-mailable-equivalent","Text":"Identity supplies content through its mailviews package; pkg/mailview supplies the common layout, branding, escaping and text alternative. Preview without SMTP: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./examples/mail Open storage/app/mail-preview/welcome.html and otp.html, plus their text versions. The sign-in service sends short-lived codes synchronously; they never enter welcome jobs or durable notifications. See Mail for rendering and optional queued ordinary emails."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Run and inspect the welcome flow","Parent":"Complete module example: account onboarding","URL":"identity.html#run-and-inspect-the-welcome-flow","Text":"Use the sign-in response's user ID and the same configured environment: go run ./cmd/cli identity welcome '\u003cuser-id\u003e' go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status pending go run ./cmd/cli notifications list user '\u003cuser-id\u003e' The first command enqueues; workers must run before records appear. Signup already requests this flow, so manual replay is an operator recovery action. Job completion means intents are saved, not email received. See notifications for delivery inspection."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"HTTP organization","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#http-organization","Text":"delivery/http/routes.go owns typed operation registration, requests.go owns wire inputs and mappings, and responses.go selects public outputs. Profile and authentication handlers call their services; reusable middleware lives in platform API. Live /docs and /openapi.json describe the enabled operations. media_handler.go, notifications_handler.go and subscriptions_handler.go handle the authenticated user's assets, notifications and push installations. Their wire inputs live in requests.go, output shapes and projections in responses.go, and operation registration in routes.go, alongside the existing account/authentication endpoints. Handlers derive owner/recipient scope from the platform principal and call the existing platform capabilities. Push registration calls Identity's AuthService directly so the account lock and session transaction remain authoritative. Platform media integration separately serves public/signed content and owns streaming cleanup."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Module flow and route ownership","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#module-flow-and-route-ownership","Text":"All application routes below are prefixed by /api/v1: Route Access POST /auth/otp/send, POST /auth/otp/verify Public proof flow when authentication is enabled POST /auth/social/{provider} Public, explicitly enabled provider POST /auth/refresh Current refresh credential GET/PATCH /users/me, GET /users/{id} Bearer session and ownership /auth/logout, /auth/sessions Bearer session /media, /media/{id}, /media/{id}/download, /media/{id}/url Bearer session and user asset ownership /notifications, /notifications/unread-count, /notifications/{id}/read Bearer session and recipient ownership /notifications/subscriptions, /notifications/subscriptions/{id} Bearer session; registration rechecks the session under Identity's account lock register.go constructs account collaborators and registers transports. App constructs the AuthService in internal/app/auth.go, creates the authenticated group, and passes the service into identity.Register. For architecture examples, see module registration and dependency injection."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Authentication dependencies","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#authentication-dependencies","Text":"Owner Responsibility UserService Profile reads/edits, listing and internal account-status operations AuthService OTP/provider proof, token issuance, session management and cleanup VerificationCodePolicy Code lifetime, cooldown and attempts AuthRateLimitPolicy Shared refill/burst budgets SessionPolicy Token/session lifetime and concurrent sign-ins integrations.SocialProvider Configured provider verification Service/repository interfaces live in their owner packages; implementations are private and constructors live beside them. AuthOptions borrows repositories, policies, token issuer, mail renderer/sender, limiter, publisher and provider implementations. Framework contracts are imported from their owner rather than redeclared. Construction performs no I/O or background work."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Organizing a substantial use case","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#organizing-a-substantial-use-case","Text":"session.go, verification.go and social.go contain methods on the same private authService. A substantial use case can move to its own file without another service or registration step. Follow the module growth guide."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Operation inputs","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#operation-inputs","Text":"For direct service use, pass trusted transport context explicitly: result, err := authentication.SignInWithCode(ctx, service.SignInInput{ Name: \"Ada\", Proof: service.ProofInput{ ChallengeID: challengeID, Code: code, ClientIP: trustedClientIP, }, Device: service.Device{Platform: enums.PlatformWeb, Name: \"Browser\"}, }) if err != nil { return err } This service-call fragment assumes a constructed service.AuthService, context and proof values. Imports are Identity service and shared enums. The caller consumes result.User, result.Tokens and result.Created; an HTTP adapter maps Created to 201/200. Client IP comes from the trusted boundary, never a JSON field. Social sign-in uses SocialSignInInput{ClientIP, Device} and a provider credential."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Push registrations and logout","Parent":"Operation inputs","URL":"identity.html#push-registrations-and-logout","Text":"When Notifier is injected, explicit session revocation detaches matching push registrations in the same SQL transaction. Revoking all sessions detaches all registrations for that user. Cleanup failure rolls back revocation; an external send already admitted cannot be retracted. Registration validates the active session under the same account lock, so a request authenticated just before logout cannot reattach a revoked session. Other account-lifecycle operations must choose their own push-cleanup policy. See notification subscriptions."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Passwordless scope","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#passwordless-scope","Text":"Implemented: email OTP, Google sign-in, profiles, refresh rotation and session revocation. Not implemented: passwords, phone/SMS proof, Apple, provider linking or unlinking, lost-email recovery, roles and public administrative account APIs. pkg/hasher remains available independently for other needs."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Troubleshoot sign-in","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#troubleshoot-sign-in","Text":"Result Check OTP 422 Same challenge/code pair, six digits, expiry, resend replacement and consumed/attempt-exhausted state OTP send 503 SMTP configuration/reachability or shared rate-limit Redis; wait for cooldown before retry 429 Destination/peer budgets; resending does not replenish them Protected request 401 Authorization header, token expiry, enabled auth and current session state Tokens stop working after refresh Concurrent refresh or replay may have revoked the session; sign in again Google 409 Existing email is not automatically linked; use its current sign-in method Google 422 on new account The provider email may not establish current ownership; use OTP Profile 422 Supply a nonempty trimmed name; omit unsupported fields Welcome is missing Inspect the local event/queue/ledger stages; sign-in success does not prove delivery Use the X-Request-ID response header with observability. Never attach raw codes or tokens to logs while diagnosing."},{"Title":"Authentication and sessions","Section":"Security","Heading":"Test the behavior","Parent":"","URL":"identity.html#test-the-behavior","Text":"Business service and repository tests live in tests/identity. They use public constructors, isolated PostgreSQL/Redis and captured mail. They cover concurrent proof consumption and refresh, durable denial state, profile policy, account changes, expiry, revocation and push/session isolation. HTTP tests cover validation, no-store behavior and rejection of spoofed forwarding headers. The integration testing guide supplies disposable backend setup. tests/modules/workflow_test.go follows signup → profile → returning sign-in → notifications → refresh → logout. Tests do not send external email; real Google consent is a separate manual client exercise."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"console.html","Text":"Cobra commands expose operations to trusted operators. They borrow the same services as HTTP and task handlers; they do not own infrastructure cleanup. Commands use a native Cobra group and action: route list, cache tables, migrate up, identity show and queue list. Table publication only writes migration files; migrate up applies them separately."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Use an existing command","Parent":"","URL":"console.html#use-an-existing-command","Text":"After following installation, inspect the registered commands and routes: export APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/cli --help go run ./cmd/cli route list go run ./cmd/cli identity --help The quickstart does not create an account. To use account commands, complete Identity setup, sign in, and select the same custom environment file used by that running application. Replace the placeholder below with the returned user ID: go run ./cmd/cli identity show '\u003cuser-id\u003e' go run ./cmd/cli identity welcome '\u003cuser-id\u003e' go run ./cmd/cli notifications list user '\u003cuser-id\u003e' go run ./cmd/cli help, --help, and invocation without arguments list every available command, including full nested paths such as identity show, notifications dispatch and completion bash. Module additions appear automatically. Hidden and deprecated branches stay hidden. Use go run ./cmd/cli help identity show or identity show --help for arguments, examples and flags specific to that command. identity show and notifications list read the database immediately. identity welcome submits an Asynq task; a running worker must process it and persist channel intents. The worker dispatcher submits channel delivery work every second; notifications dispatch is also available for manual operation. Listing immediately may precede either stage. These are operator commands, not publicly authenticated endpoints. Restrict access to the executable and credentials."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Command reference","Parent":"","URL":"console.html#command-reference","Text":"Run each command after go run ./cmd/cli. All groups share eager construction; operator access is trusted and is not scoped by an HTTP bearer token. Placeholders such as USER_ID and DELIVERY_ID must be replaced with actual IDs. Command Availability and effect serve Run the explicit Processes selection in cmd/cli/main.go route list Read the complete registered HTTP route list without serving migrate status, migrate version Read the configured database's Goose history migrate up, migrate down Apply pending SQL or roll back the latest migration; --timeout overrides MIGRATION_TIMEOUT migrate refresh --force Delete the configured schema/database contents and rerun migrations; development/test only; never a recovery shortcut seed List registered seeders; none are selected implicitly seed NAME... Execute named seeders in order; --timeout defaults to 5m cache tables Publish cache migration source; --dir defaults to internal/modules/shared/migrations; does not apply SQL identity show USER_ID Read an account through its service identity welcome USER_ID Enqueue the stable welcome task; requires a worker for completion identity prune-verification Delete a bounded batch of expired challenges; available when challenge support is enabled identity prune-sessions Delete bounded expired/revoked session history; available when auth is enabled notifications list TYPE ID, notifications unread TYPE ID Read a recipient's stored records or unread count notifications read TYPE ID NOTIFICATION_ID Mark one recipient-owned record read notifications deliveries Inspect ledger metadata; --status defaults to failed, empty means all; --page defaults to 1 notifications delivery DELIVERY_ID Inspect one delivery's metadata notifications dispatch Submit due persisted delivery IDs to Redis; does not synchronously deliver notifications retry DELIVERY_ID Reset a terminal failed delivery for retry; external effects can repeat notifications prune-subscriptions Disable a bounded batch of inactive push registrations; app schedules pruning when push and its scheduler are enabled media upload TYPE ID FILE Upload up to 20 MiB using disk visibility; optional --collection and --visibility media list TYPE ID List ready assets; optional --collection filter media delete TYPE ID ASSET_ID Record deletion intent and delete the owned object media recover Run a bounded interrupted-upload/deletion recovery batch queue list, queue inspect QUEUE ID Read queue names or task state/payload queue replay QUEUE ID Move eligible work to pending without resetting retries; effects can repeat queue archive QUEUE ID, queue delete QUEUE ID Archive/delete non-active work; delete is permanent queue pause QUEUE, queue resume QUEUE Stop/resume new admission; active work continues queue cancel ID Request cooperative cancellation of active work completion SHELL Generate Cobra completion for bash, zsh, fish or powershell Queue commands are always registered. Capability commands may be present even when an operation's storage or provider is unavailable; execution reports the missing dependency. Use COMMAND --help for current arguments and flags. Task payload inspection may expose private content. See migrations, failures and retries, notifications and Media before operations that change state."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Register commands explicitly","Parent":"","URL":"console.html#register-commands-explicitly","Text":"Keep native constructors under internal/modules/\u003cmodule\u003e/delivery/cli. Each constructor receives its service directly, for example New(users service.UserService). The owning module's Register function adds its group to the supplied Cobra root: if targets.RootCommand != nil { targets.RootCommand.AddCommand(modulecli.New(users)) } App calls the module Register function once with borrowed resources and targets. There is no separate command list in app. Each command validates input, calls its service with cmd.Context(), and writes output. App supplies logging/tracing and owns resource cleanup. Adding a command only changes its owning module."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Arguments and flags","Parent":"","URL":"console.html#arguments-and-flags","Text":"Cobra validates arguments and binds flags directly. This complete standalone greeting command demonstrates both: package cli import ( \"fmt\" \"github.com/spf13/cobra\" ) func NewGreet() *cobra.Command { var name string command := \u0026cobra.Command{ Use: \"greet\", Short: \"Print a greeting\", Args: cobra.NoArgs, RunE: func(cmd *cobra.Command, _ []string) error { _, err := fmt.Fprintf(cmd.OutOrStdout(), \"Hello, %s!\\n\", name) return err }, } command.Flags().StringVar(\u0026name, \"name\", \"world\", \"Name to greet\") return command } Add cli.NewGreet() to your module’s group in its Register function to expose the greeting beneath that group. For positional inputs, select an appropriate Cobra argument validator and validate business values inside the service too. Only cmd/cli parses application commands and flags. Other entry points select their services in code. See Entry points."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Output and errors","Parent":"","URL":"console.html#output-and-errors","Text":"Use cmd.OutOrStdout() for results, cmd.ErrOrStderr() for diagnostics, and cmd.InOrStdin() for input. Return errors from RunE; do not call os.Exit or log.Fatal. Pass cmd.Context() into operations; it includes app logging and tracing. App cleans up after success or failure, rather than relying on Cobra post-run hooks. Commands contain mutable flag state. Return fresh trees and do not execute one tree concurrently. Constructors and registration describe behavior without I/O. The app checks names, aliases and ownership before execution."},{"Title":"Console commands","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Startup and execution","Parent":"","URL":"console.html#startup-and-execution","Text":"All commands use one startup path: load and validate configuration, open resources, construct authentication, register modules and validate commands/migrations, then execute Cobra. Help and completion also require successful application construction. Bad settings, unavailable configured resources or incompatible SQL sources can prevent execution. Input validation happens after construction, before business operations. serve starts processes selected in cmd/cli/main.go; other commands start no HTTP or metrics listeners, queue workers, local event consumers, notification dispatcher or scheduler, even when those processes are selected in main. Migrations use a dedicated database pool for their lock/session contract and run only when explicitly selected. The configured resource clients still open normally and close after the command. Connection maintenance and telemetry export are distinct from processing business work. Watermill registration and cleanup messages are debug diagnostics; normal help and completion are quiet at the default info level. Startup, command and cleanup errors remain visible. A command must join its work before returning. Use Redis tasks to hand work to another process. GoChannel publication in a CLI process does not reach another process's subscribers. See entry points and scheduling for distinct execution boundaries. go run ./examples/cli greet --name Ada go run ./cmd/cli completion bash The standalone example uses native Cobra through the platform CLI setup. Each app.Execute call owns an independent invocation. Platform capabilities put native command definitions in their integration packages. Notifications commands accept RECIPIENT_TYPE RECIPIENT_ID; media commands accept OWNER_TYPE OWNER_ID. Media upload/list support --collection. Both use already constructed package instances, with no application invocation callbacks."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html","Text":"A task asks a worker to do one operation later. The producer submits it to Redis; a worker selects its handler and processes it outside the original request. Use a queue for work that needs retries, delayed execution, or survival across application restarts. flowchart TB accTitle: One task, one worker per delivery accDescr: Producers enqueue into Redis. A worker runs the matching handler. A task may complete, retry after a delay, or be archived; another delivery can repeat the operation. Producer[HTTP, CLI or scheduler] --\u003e|Enqueue| Redis[(Redis queue)] Redis --\u003e|One competing worker| Handler[ServeMux → handler → service] Handler --\u003e|Success| Complete[Complete] Handler --\u003e|Retryable error| Retry[Retry after a delay] Retry --\u003e Redis Handler --\u003e|Retries exhausted or skipped| Archive[Archive] Several workers compete for tasks from the same queue. They do not each receive a copy. Retries and recovery can deliver a task again, so handlers must tolerate repeat execution. For independent reactions to one fact, see events. Choose the entry point that fits the operation: Need Use Success acknowledges Run one operation later queue.EnqueueContext Redis accepted a task React to a fact in the current process Events Local publication, not completion Run an operation repeatedly Scheduling A future tick may enqueue a task Commit a notification with a business write Notifier.Enqueue SQL intent, subject to commit The task model, options, handler and administration APIs are native Asynq. The framework adds shared routing, observability, transaction guards and lifecycle ownership. There is no additional Job interface to learn."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Try the existing task","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#try-the-existing-task","Text":"Use the configured profile from installation. Redis, PostgreSQL, the application migrations and an existing Identity user are required. For automatic signup, enable and configure authentication first. The minimal quickstart keeps authentication off; it cannot create a user through OTP as-is. Run the combined app, which includes workers by default: go run ./cmd/app In another terminal, enqueue the existing welcome task for a real user ID: go run ./cmd/cli identity welcome '\u003cuser-id\u003e' go run ./cmd/cli notifications list user '\u003cuser-id\u003e' Use the same environment file and database/Redis settings in both terminals. The command prints a task ID after Redis accepts the work. The worker then reloads the account and persists eligible notification channel intents. A worker-owned dispatcher scans those intents every second; delivery workers attempt each channel. The list may initially be empty while that work is pending. Welcome uses a stable key per recipient and channel. Repeating the command does not create another database welcome notification. Verified addresses also receive email when a mailer is available. The normal app always supplies its configured mailer, but SMTP must be reachable for email to succeed. No cron scheduler is required for notification delivery."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Distinguish the names","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#distinguish-the-names","Text":"Name Example Purpose Task type identity.welcome.v1 Selects the handler; a persisted contract Task ID Generated by Asynq Identifies one submitted task Queue default or high Selects the workload from which workers pull Handler Identity welcome handler Reloads the account and persists channel intents Task types are persisted contracts: renaming one requires a plan for queued tasks. The application uses native Asynq tasks, without a Job interface."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Keep task code with its module","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#keep-task-code-with-its-module","Text":"File Owns internal/modules/identity/jobs/welcome.go Stable task type, serializable payload and constructor internal/modules/identity/delivery/jobs/welcome.go Decoding, validation and invocation of business behavior internal/modules/identity/register.go Dependency construction and handler registration internal/app/schedules.go Central recurring schedules, when needed Pass IDs rather than whole entities, credentials or rendered private data. Reload current account state when the handler runs. Large use cases remain methods on an existing service; creating a task does not require another service abstraction."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Define a task","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#define-a-task","Text":"Keep the task type, payload and constructor together in internal/modules/\u003cmodule\u003e/jobs. Use \u003cOperation\u003eType, \u003cOperation\u003e and New\u003cOperation\u003e consistently. This is the complete existing internal/modules/identity/jobs/welcome.go: // Package jobs defines Identity's native Asynq tasks and their serializable inputs. package jobs import ( \"encoding/json\" \"errors\" \"time\" \"github.com/google/uuid\" \"github.com/hibiken/asynq\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/asynqx\" ) const WelcomeType = \"identity.welcome.v1\" // Welcome carries only identity. The worker reloads account state and contact data; // names, email addresses, credentials and notification content never enter this job. type Welcome struct { UserID uuid.UUID `json:\"user_id\"` } // NewWelcome constructs a fresh task; enqueue through the app's borrowed client. // Queue routing comes from application configuration. Retries are safe because // the notification's occurrence key is stable across attempts. func NewWelcome(userID uuid.UUID) (*asynq.Task, error) { if userID == uuid.Nil { return nil, errors.New(\"identity: welcome job requires a user ID\") } data, err := json.Marshal(Welcome{UserID: userID}) if err != nil { return nil, err } return asynqx.NewTask(WelcomeType, data, asynq.MaxRetry(5), asynq.Timeout(30*time.Second)), nil } asynqx.NewTask initializes headers for client trace propagation. Native options remain available. Create a fresh task for each enqueue; middleware updates its headers, so sharing a task concurrently is unsafe."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Write and register a handler","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#write-and-register-a-handler","Text":"A handler decodes the task, calls the owning service or capability, and returns its outcome. Reuse owner contracts such as service.UserService. This complete existing handler lives in internal/modules/identity/delivery/jobs/welcome.go: // Package jobs handles Identity's native Asynq tasks. package jobs import ( \"context\" \"encoding/json\" \"errors\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/entities\" \"github.com/google/uuid\" \"github.com/hibiken/asynq\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/domain\" identityjobs \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/jobs\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/notifications\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/service\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/enums\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/notification\" ) // Welcome reloads current account state and persists channel intents. Completion // means notification intent is saved, not that email has reached the recipient. // Platform notification workers own actual channel delivery and independent retries. type Welcome struct { Users service.UserService Notifier *notification.Notifier EmailEnabled bool } func (h Welcome) Handle(ctx context.Context, task *asynq.Task) error { var input identityjobs.Welcome if task == nil { return errors.Join(errors.New(\"identity: missing welcome job\"), asynq.SkipRetry) } if err := json.Unmarshal(task.Payload(), \u0026input); err != nil || input.UserID == uuid.Nil { return errors.Join(errors.New(\"identity: invalid welcome job\"), asynq.SkipRetry) } user, err := h.Users.Get(ctx, input.UserID) if errors.Is(err, domain.ErrUserNotFound) { return nil } if err != nil { return err } if user == nil { return domain.ErrUserRequired } if user.DeletedAt != nil || user.Status != enums.UserActive { return nil } content := notifications.NewWelcome(user.Name) if !h.EmailEnabled || user.EmailVerifiedAt == nil { content.Email = nil } recipient := notification.Recipient{Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: user.ID.String(), Email: user.Email.String()} _, err = h.Notifier.Enqueue(ctx, recipient, content) return err } Module construction supplies the handler's collaborators. Registration stays in identity/register.go, using the native mux before workers start: mux.HandleFunc(jobs.WelcomeType, welcome.Handle) Here jobs imports the task definitions, welcome is the constructed handler, and mux is targets.TaskRouter. A larger business operation belongs on its existing service receiver; transport decoding stays in the handler. Keep persisted task names and payload fields compatible with work already in Redis. The welcome job saves notification intent; notification workers perform channel delivery. Its stable occurrence key makes repeated attempts converge on the same intents. See notifications for those guarantees."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Enqueue work","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#enqueue-work","Text":"Module assembly passes deps.Queue, the existing asynqx.Client, to operations that need it. No module-specific queue contract is needed. This function-body fragment assumes a borrowed queue, ctx, a UUID userID, and a (string, error) result: task, err := jobs.NewWelcome(userID) if err != nil { return \"\", err } info, err := queue.EnqueueContext(ctx, task) if err != nil { return \"\", err } return info.ID, nil Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/jobs. The returned ID acknowledges queue acceptance; it does not mean the notification has been delivered. Raw enqueue cannot commit atomically with application SQL; see transactions. The app client rejects active framework SQL transaction scopes before enqueue. Standalone clients can install middleware.RequireNoTransaction() outermost; raw native clients bypass this policy. SQL notification intent is available when the notification must commit with a business change."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Enqueue from a service","Parent":"Enqueue work","URL":"queues.html#enqueue-from-a-service","Text":"HTTP, CLI and event handlers use the same borrowed asynqx.Client method shown above. Pass that dependency to the service that owns the operation during module construction; do not inject the complete Resources bundle into the service. App client middleware already supplies routing, transaction guards and tracing. Constructing a fresh task with asynqx.NewTask prepares its propagation headers."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Keep configuration central","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#keep-configuration-central","Text":"internal/app/config/queues.go defines the available queues. Both use the same Redis connection and worker pool: QUEUE_REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/1 QUEUE_DEFAULT=default QUEUE_DEFAULT_PRIORITY=1 QUEUE_HIGH_PRIORITY=3 QUEUE_CONCURRENCY=4 QUEUE_ROUTES=identity.welcome.v1=high A task's destination is resolved in this order: asynq.Queue(...) passed at enqueue time or in schedule options. An exact task-type entry in QUEUE_ROUTES. QUEUE_DEFAULT. Unlisted task types use the default queue. Unknown queue names fail before publication. Priorities are selection weights, not reserved worker counts. Cache Redis uses database 0 by default; queue Redis uses database 1. To introduce a third named queue, add it to QueueConfig.loadQueues in internal/app/config/queues.go. Then restart producers and workers with the same definition. QUEUE_ROUTES only selects among configured queues; it does not create a queue definition. Adding a queue name does not create another worker pool."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Configure options and delays","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#configure-options-and-delays","Text":"Native options pass through unchanged, except that app routing owns queue selection: info, err := queue.EnqueueContext(ctx, task, asynq.MaxRetry(3), asynq.Timeout(30*time.Second), asynq.ProcessIn(5*time.Minute), asynq.Retention(24*time.Hour), ) This fragment imports time and Asynq. Check the error before reading info. ProcessIn delays eligibility once; recurring schedules enqueue new tasks repeatedly. Capacity and polling affect actual start time. Option Purpose Queue(name) Override central task routing with a configured queue MaxRetry(n) Retry budget after the initial attempt Timeout(d), Deadline(t) Relative or absolute execution limit ProcessIn(d), ProcessAt(t) One-time delayed eligibility; choose one Unique(ttl) Temporary type/queue/payload enqueue deduplication TaskID(id) Explicit task identity Retention(d) Keep completed tasks and results Group(name) Native grouping; requires explicit server aggregator wiring Explicit enqueue options take native precedence over constructor options. Queue options in constructors are overridden by central routing; put queue policy in configuration or enqueue options. Handle asynq.ErrDuplicateTask and asynq.ErrTaskIDConflict with errors.Is when the caller needs to distinguish duplicate acceptance. Uniqueness does not prevent all duplicate effects."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Choose task outcomes","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#choose-task-outcomes","Text":"Handler result Asynq behavior nil Complete the task; retain it only when configured Ordinary error Retry while its budget permits, then archive Error wrapping asynq.SkipRetry Archive without another retry Reject an invalid task at the handler boundary: if task == nil || task.Type() != jobs.WelcomeType { return errors.Join(errors.New(\"unexpected task type\"), asynq.SkipRetry) } This fragment imports errors, Asynq and internal/modules/identity/jobs. Decode and validate its payload before calling the service. Exact-type checks matter because native ServeMux routing also supports prefixes. Do not classify transient infrastructure failures as invalid payloads."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Set retries per task","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#set-retries-per-task","Text":"In an operation with an injected queue, set native enqueue options: info, err := queue.EnqueueContext(ctx, task, asynq.MaxRetry(3), asynq.Timeout(30*time.Second), ) Handle err before using info. Three retries means up to four ordinary attempts. A timeout cancels cooperative work; it does not reverse an email or database change. MaxRetry(0) disables retries after the initial attempt, not duplicate delivery caused by every possible failure."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Make effects safe to repeat","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#make-effects-safe-to-repeat","Text":"Use an operation identity that survives retries. Enforce it with a database unique constraint, conditional transition or downstream idempotency key. Record the effect and its identity atomically where possible. Task uniqueness only reduces duplicate enqueues for a retention window; it does not make an external action atomic with Asynq completion. A database commit followed by Redis enqueue can fail between the two actions. If both must succeed together, add a deliberate outbox workflow; this app does not provide one automatically. Identity's welcome handler demonstrates one useful boundary: NewWelcome gives its notification the key welcome, and persisted notification intents enforce recipient/key/channel uniqueness. A repeated task converges on existing intents. The database channel is stored once; an uncertain external email attempt can still duplicate. Choose an idempotency key for the business occurrence, not a fresh random value inside every retry."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Know the boundary","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#know-the-boundary","Text":"Asynq is the only queue backend in this app. Redis persistence and recovery settings determine durability. A successful enqueue is not an exactly-once guarantee, and raw SQL changes are not atomic with Redis publication. The notification delivery ledger provides a focused transactional outbox when business operations explicitly use its Enqueue API; it does not make arbitrary tasks transactional. Keep arbitrary Redis enqueue outside database.TxManager.Execute. The app client returns a transaction error before publishing if the supplied context carries an active framework transaction. A post-commit enqueue can still fail: expose or recover that failure explicitly rather than claiming the SQL write rolled back. For notifications that must be guaranteed by the commit, use the transactional notification workflow."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Read delivery metadata","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#read-delivery-metadata","Text":"Inside a delivered task handler: id, ok := asynq.GetTaskID(ctx) if !ok { return errors.New(\"task delivery metadata is missing\") } queueName, _ := asynq.GetQueueName(ctx) retries, _ := asynq.GetRetryCount(ctx) maxRetries, _ := asynq.GetMaxRetry(ctx) Use these values for diagnostics or an operation that requires them. A direct unit test call has no worker metadata unless explicitly supplied. task.Type(), Payload() and Headers() remain available. Treat delivered task data as read-only. Custom failure classification can preserve the retry counter, so it is not always the total invocation count."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Write a result","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#write-a-result","Text":"A delivered task has a native ResultWriter. Write a small result before returning: result, err := json.Marshal(struct { Delivered bool `json:\"delivered\"` }{Delivered: true}) if err != nil { return err } _, err = task.ResultWriter().Write(result) return err Constructed tasks have no result writer. Do not retain it after the handler returns. Writes replace the result rather than append; Retention keeps successful results available. Store large artifacts elsewhere and return their reference. Inspect state alongside result bytes: an earlier attempt may have written a result before failing. Result persistence is not atomic with an external effect; a write failure after sending a receipt can cause another send on retry."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Start a worker","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#start-a-worker","Text":"Use the same Redis settings as the producer: go run ./cmd/worker This starts Asynq workers, local event subscribers and the notification dispatcher. It opens no business HTTP listener and does not select the cron scheduler. cmd/app already runs workers; use a separate worker process when you want to scale or deploy task execution independently. Enable the cron scheduler on one designated app process per schedule set. Multiple schedulers would independently enqueue the same recurring work; worker replicas can safely compete for pending deliveries."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Set capacity","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#set-capacity","Text":"QUEUE_CONCURRENCY=4 QUEUE_DEFAULT_PRIORITY=1 QUEUE_HIGH_PRIORITY=3 QUEUE_STRICT_PRIORITY=false QUEUE_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=15s Concurrency is the total number of active tasks per process, across both queues. Three processes configured with 4 can run up to 12 tasks at once. Budget database connections and downstream capacity accordingly. With weighted selection, high is preferred relative to default; weights do not promise a completion ratio or latency. Strict priority always prefers higher priority work and can starve lower-priority queues. One shared pool is configured; independent pools require explicit native Asynq server wiring in app assembly."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Register before starting","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#register-before-starting","Text":"App construction creates the native ServeMux, installs global worker middleware, and calls module registration. A module's register.go maps job types to handlers: if targets.TaskRouter != nil \u0026\u0026 welcome != nil { targets.TaskRouter.HandleFunc(jobs.WelcomeType, welcome.Handle) } This function-body fragment uses Identity's register.go registration pattern; jobs imports its job definitions and welcome is its constructed delivery handler. It borrows the same user service and notifier as the module. Register every task type that these workers may consume, including app.command when command scheduling is enabled. Do not modify routes after startup."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Readiness and shutdown","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#readiness-and-shutdown","Text":"flowchart TB accTitle: Keep subscribers alive while workers drain accDescr: Worker readiness waits for local event subscribers. On shutdown, stop admission first, drain active tasks next, then drain event handlers before closing shared resources. subgraph Startup[Startup dependency] Subscribers[Local subscribers ready] --\u003e Worker[Worker verifies Redis and becomes ready] end subgraph Shutdown[Shutdown order] Admission[Stop HTTP, scheduling and dispatch] --\u003e Tasks[Drain active tasks] Tasks --\u003e Events[Drain local event handlers] Events --\u003e Resources[Close shared resources] end Only selected processes run. In particular, cmd/worker does not start HTTP or the cron scheduler; the shutdown sequence omits services absent from that process. Redis connectivity is checked before worker readiness. Readiness is a startup signal, not continuous Redis health monitoring. A worker-only process has no /readyz HTTP endpoint; metrics can run on their separate optional listener. During shutdown, Asynq stops fetching and waits for active handlers within its budget. Handlers must honor cancellation and join their work. Pending Redis tasks remain for another worker; shutdown does not drain the entire backlog. The app closes clients only after their users stop. See lifecycle."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Inspection and administration","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#inspection-and-administration","Text":"These operator commands are always available: go run ./cmd/cli queue list go run ./cmd/cli queue inspect default TASK_ID go run ./cmd/cli queue pause high go run ./cmd/cli queue resume high go run ./cmd/cli queue cancel TASK_ID go run ./cmd/cli queue archive default TASK_ID go run ./cmd/cli queue replay default TASK_ID go run ./cmd/cli queue delete default TASK_ID Use the task's actual queue. Inspection prints JSON with byte fields encoded as base64. In app assembly, a.inspector.GetTaskInfo(queueName, taskID) returns native TaskInfo with raw Result bytes. The native pagination and bulk APIs remain available. Inspector calls have Redis I/O timeouts, not per-call contexts. Action Limit Pause/resume Changes admission; active handlers continue Cancel Cooperative; does not delete work or prevent retry Archive Supported non-active tasks only Replay Scheduled/retry/archived to pending; retry history remains Delete Supported non-active tasks; effects cannot be undone Completed tasks cannot be replayed through native RunTask. Restrict operator access to retained payloads, results and errors. App owns Inspector connections; borrowers must not close them. See safe replay before replaying."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Asynqmon dashboard","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#asynqmon-dashboard","Text":"Enable the embedded dashboard independently of CLI administration: QUEUE_DASHBOARD_ENABLED=true QUEUE_DASHBOARD_USERNAME=operator QUEUE_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-secret Restart an HTTP-enabled process and open http://localhost:8080/admin/queues/. The browser requests Basic Auth credentials. UI and API share the existing HTTP listener and use QUEUE_REDIS_URL, including its database index. Use HTTPS for remote access. Disable the setting and restart to remove routes and connections."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Inspect and recover","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#inspect-and-recover","Text":"Queue administration commands are always available. Inspect before replaying: go run ./cmd/cli queue list go run ./cmd/cli queue inspect default TASK_ID go run ./cmd/cli queue replay default TASK_ID default must match the task's actual queue. Fix the cause and verify that its business effect can safely repeat. Native replay moves supported scheduled, retry or archived work to pending; it does not reset retry history. Completed tasks need an intentional new enqueue rather than RunTask replay. The dashboard provides a separate authenticated UI. Pause/resume affects new admission, cancellation is cooperative, and deletion cannot undo effects. See administration for the full command list and state restrictions."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Customize backoff and classification","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#customize-backoff-and-classification","Text":"After loading config and before app.Serve, optional native callbacks can change retry timing and whether an error counts toward failure statistics: cfg.Queue.RetryDelay = func(n int, err error, task *asynq.Task) time.Duration { limit := time.Second \u003c\u003c min(n, 6) return limit/2 + time.Duration(rand.Int64N(int64(limit/2))) } This uses time, math/rand/v2 and Asynq. It bounds exponential delay with jitter. Keep callbacks fast and safe for concurrent calls. Nil callbacks keep native defaults. cfg.Queue.IsFailure = func(err error) bool { return !errors.Is(err, ErrTemporarilyThrottled) } Define ErrTemporarilyThrottled in your application. Returning false does not make the task successful: while retries remain it can retry without incrementing the counter. Repeated non-failures can therefore continue indefinitely. Define a terminal policy; classification does not override an already exhausted budget."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Tracing and metrics","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#tracing-and-metrics","Text":"app/infrastructure.go installs global client routing and tracing, native worker middleware, and Asynq's official Prometheus collector. Workers create consumer spans linked to producer context. Registered mux patterns bound task-name metric labels; IDs belong in logs and traces. Baggage, payloads and results are not copied into routine telemetry. Native recurring schedules bypass the client chain. Routing is resolved at registration; each execution still has a worker span without a stale startup trace. See observability."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Inspect capacity and failures","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#inspect-capacity-and-failures","Text":"Enable the queue dashboard to inspect pending, active, delayed, retry and archived tasks. With metrics enabled, the official Asynq collector exposes queue state and pending latency; handler duration records each attempt. Long queues can mean insufficient capacity, slow dependencies or paused workers—not necessarily failed publication. Use failure handling to define retries and replay safely."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Troubleshoot pending or repeated tasks","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#troubleshoot-pending-or-repeated-tasks","Text":"Symptom Check first What to do Enqueue returns an error Caller context, transaction scope, Redis URL and configured queue Resolve it before reporting acceptance; an uncertain network result may already have created a task Task stays pending Worker selection, queue name, paused state, process health Start a handler-capable worker sharing the same Redis database Task is scheduled ProcessIn / ProcessAt and current time Wait until eligible; start time also depends on capacity No matching handler Task type and the deployed module registration Deploy the handler before publishing its versioned task type Task is archived Inspect retained error and retry count Fix the cause and verify idempotency before replay Welcome task completed but notification absent Notification delivery ledger and worker dispatcher Inspect notifications deliveries; task completion acknowledged intent only An email was sent twice Provider uncertainty, retry history, business key Treat external delivery as potentially repeated; uniqueness is not exactly once More worker processes do not receive local signup events GoChannel process boundary Keep local subscribers with the publishing process; scale workers for Redis tasks Task payloads, results and retained errors are operator data. Restrict Redis, dashboard and backup access, especially for queued mail. Avoid logging bodies, credentials or arbitrary provider error text in application telemetry."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Test tasks and their integration","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#test-tasks-and-their-integration","Text":"Unit-test decoding, invalid payloads, missing records and repeat-safe effects by calling handlers directly with a task and test collaborators. Such calls have no worker metadata or result writer. Keep business tests under tests/\u003cmodule\u003e; delivery adapters and reusable queue middleware may have tests beside their owners. go test ./pkg/asynqx/... ./internal/modules/identity/delivery/jobs/... go test ./tests/modules -run TestWorkflowGuidesMatchCompiledSources -count=1 Those checks do not prove Redis delivery. The database and Redis integration gate exercises signup → event → task → notification storage. Use task testing for cancellation, retries and lifecycle tests."},{"Title":"Queues and tasks","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Use queues independently","Parent":"","URL":"queues.html#use-queues-independently","Text":"pkg/asynqx can be used without app assembly. A native *asynq.Client satisfies asynqx.Client; the caller owns its connection and worker setup. This function-body fragment assumes ctx, userID and options (asynq.RedisClientOpt) are supplied: client := asynq.NewClient(options) defer client.Close() task, err := jobs.NewWelcome(userID) if err != nil { return err } _, err = client.EnqueueContext(ctx, task, asynq.Queue(\"high\")) return err The raw native client does not install the framework's routing, tracing or SQL transaction guard. To retain the guard with native enqueueing, wrap once using github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/asynqx/middleware: queue := middleware.Chain(client.EnqueueContext, middleware.RequireNoTransaction()) Route(routing) and ObserveClient(provider) are optional members of that same chain. They are application assembly concerns; business callers still use the same EnqueueContext method. asynqx.NewWorker(server, handler, redisPool, startupTimeout) borrows its clients and checks Redis when Run(ctx) starts. Register handlers first, run once, wait for shutdown to finish, then close owned clients. Handlers must cooperate with cancellation: Go cannot forcibly terminate a goroutine. Do not also call native Start, Run or Shutdown on a server managed by this adapter. Continue with recurring schedules, events, or the complete onboarding workflow."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"events.html","Text":"An event announces a fact that already happened. Other modules can react without the publisher knowing their implementations. This application uses Watermill GoChannel only: a process-local, in-memory pub/sub bus."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Signup flow","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#signup-flow","Text":"flowchart TB accTitle: From signup to a welcome notification accDescr: The local event is best-effort. Redis only protects a task after enqueue; the notification ledger only protects intent after it is committed. subgraph Local[Publishing process] Account[Account committed] -. best-effort event .-\u003e Bus[GoChannel: UserRegistered] end Bus --\u003e|Identity event handler enqueues| Job[(Welcome task in Redis)] Job --\u003e|Worker reloads account| Intents[(Channel intents in PostgreSQL)] Intents --\u003e|Dispatcher and delivery workers| Database[Database notification] Intents --\u003e|Verified address and mailer available| Email[Welcome email] Account creation completes before publication. Identity owns the welcome content and subscribes through identity/register.go. Signing in to an existing account publishes no registration event. These reactions are best-effort. A successful publish does not mean a subscriber completed its work. Events can be lost when no subscriber is running, during a restart, or after a process failure. GoChannel does not replay missed events and cannot communicate between API and worker processes or between replicas."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Terms and ownership","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#terms-and-ownership","Text":"Term Meaning here Event A fact, such as a user being registered Topic The string used to route that event Publisher Sends an occurrence to a topic Subscriber Receives occurrences in the same process Handler name Unique router identity used for registration and diagnostics Payload The typed data encoded as JSON Published business event definitions and common occurrence metadata live in internal/modules/shared/events. Identity owns the meaning and version of UserRegistered; handlers belong to consuming modules in delivery/events. App assembly owns the bus, middleware, router startup and shutdown; modules borrow the publisher and registration targets. Handler names are not durable consumer groups or broker queues. events.Event exposes ID, Type and OccurredAt, serialized as event_id, event_type and occurred_at. It has no embedded interface, event buffer or dispatch hooks. UserRegistered embeds this metadata and adds user_id; its topic remains identity.user_registered.v1. Keep this package small: payload structs, topic constants, occurrence metadata and New… constructors. Use IDs and event-specific values, without importing business entities, services, repositories, platform capabilities, config or transport types. Constructors describe occurrences; they do not publish or register handlers. Producers own schema compatibility and introduce a new topic version for an incompatible contract. Consumers can learn the contract without importing the producer's implementation."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Publish an event","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#publish-an-event","Text":"Publish after a successful operation, outside a database transaction. Here events is github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/events: event := events.NewUserRegistered(user.ID) if err := publisher.PublishWithID(ctx, events.UserRegisteredType, event.ID.String(), event); err != nil { // The business commit already succeeded. Report this optional reaction failure. logging.FromContext(ctx).ErrorContext(ctx, \"registration event publication failed\") } This fragment assumes an already committed user, the caller's ctx and a borrowed messaging.Publisher. It imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/logging for the safe failure log. Do not log the whole event or return an error that implies the committed account was rolled back. Required follow-up work needs a persisted intent, not this local event. Identity performs this for OTP signup and first social login. It logs publication failure without returning a signup error: the account is already committed, so retrying signup would be misleading. Repositories perform persistence only; they do not publish events."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Define an event contract","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#define-an-event-contract","Text":"File: internal/modules/shared/events/user_registered.go (the existing contract): package events import \"github.com/google/uuid\" // UserRegisteredType is the topic for Identity account creation. const UserRegisteredType = \"identity.user_registered.v1\" // UserRegistered records an account created by Identity after its transaction // commits. Consuming modules own their reactions. type UserRegistered struct { Event UserID uuid.UUID `json:\"user_id\"` } func NewUserRegistered(userID uuid.UUID) UserRegistered { return UserRegistered{Event: NewEvent(UserRegisteredType), UserID: userID} } NewEvent supplies a fresh UUID and UTC occurrence time. Creating this value has no network effects. Reuse the occurrence ID when retrying publication of the same fact; creating a new event each retry would describe several occurrences. Keep payloads small and versioned. Avoid embedding a user entity: future fields may expose private data, and consumers rarely need the producer's full database shape."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Adding a contract or extracting a service","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#adding-a-contract-or-extracting-a-service","Text":"Add a definition and New… constructor to internal/modules/shared/events, publish it after the producer's successful operation, then register reactions in the consuming modules. Keep payloads limited to what consumers need. Handlers and their registration stay out of the shared package. This location provides a common contract inside the application. Go's internal boundary still restricts imports from separate projects. If a service is later extracted, publish only the required versioned schemas or a small contract module; do not export the whole shared directory. Keep domain errors with their owners, reuse pkg/apperror locally, and transmit stable error codes across boundaries. Moving definitions does not change delivery: the current GoChannel bus is local and best-effort. Cross-process delivery requires a separate transport decision and explicit durability guarantees."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Register a handler","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#register-a-handler","Text":"Modules register handlers before the router starts: router.AddConsumerHandler( \"identity.welcome\", events.UserRegisteredType, subscriber, eventhandlers.UserRegistered{Queue: queue}.Handle, ) Here subscriber is the app-owned GoChannel bus, queue is the borrowed Asynq client, and eventhandlers imports internal/modules/identity/delivery/events. The handler decodes the event and enqueues a task containing the required user ID. The job reloads the account and persists eligible welcome channels through the Notifier. All Identity registrations are in internal/modules/identity/register.go. Handlers tolerate duplicates. Database notification uniqueness is enforced per (recipient_type, recipient_id, key), and delivery intents additionally include the channel and destination (a subscription ID for push). This protects repeated database effects; it does not establish exactly-once email."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Notifications and tasks","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#notifications-and-tasks","Text":"Pub/sub distributes facts; Notifications implements JSON database notifications, email and durable channel delivery. Business modules supply content and recipients. Identity's identity welcome USER_ID command enqueues the existing identity.welcome.v1 task, defined in internal/modules/identity/jobs. Its handler in delivery/jobs saves the same welcome occurrence as automatic registration. It requests a database notification and, for verified addresses when a mailer is available, an email. The platform dispatcher and delivery worker carry out those channel intents. Workers dispatch pending intents every second; the cron scheduler is not required. GoChannel publication remains ephemeral. Enqueueing from a local event handler cannot recover an event lost before that handler ran. When notification intent must commit with a business change, call Notifier.Enqueue in the same SQL transaction. Its PostgreSQL delivery ledger is dispatched to Asynq after commit. Framework Publisher and app queue calls reject an active framework SQL transaction before serialization or publication. Raw native clients bypass this guard and still do not join SQL transactions; post-commit publication remains best-effort. OTP policy, expiry and validation remain Identity responsibilities. Expo push is available independently and through Notifications. SMS and WhatsApp are not implemented."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Independent construction","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#independent-construction","Text":"messaging.NewPublisher(nativePublisher) returns (Publisher, error) and borrows the transport. It performs no I/O and rejects a missing publisher immediately. The caller retains responsibility for transport cleanup. messaging.RegisterRunner(router) returns (*Runner, error). It installs the lifecycle middleware on a non-nil native Watermill router, so register it once before other middleware and handlers. It starts no work. The runner's Run(ctx) then owns router execution and shutdown, joining admitted calls before it returns. The bus and other borrowed clients stay open until all their users finish."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Lifecycle and failure handling","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#lifecycle-and-failure-handling","Text":"EVENTS_DRIVER=gochannel is the supported setting. Run event subscribers in every process that publishes local events; a separate worker cannot receive API events. App registration is sequential, and router readiness precedes normal serving. The app explicitly closes the router and joins admitted handler calls before releasing borrowed resources. A native router timeout cannot close dependencies under a handler. With no registered subscriptions, no native delivery loop starts. The app's messaging.LocalBus retains native GoChannel and adds admission limits: 128 outstanding events, 8 MiB of retained message data, and 64 KiB per event (including payload, metadata, ID and topic). Publication rejects a full or oversized batch before copying it; successful return means in-memory admission. Serialization and Go object overhead are additional. Subscriptions are installed before serving; fanout copies and handler work depend on that fixed subscription set. Event ordering is not an application guarantee. At most 16 handler retry cycles execute concurrently across topics. Middleware supplies tracing, metrics, safe failure logging, recovery and a 20-second handler context budget once a cycle starts. Each delivery attempts the handler once plus up to three retries with backoff. An exhausted cycle is logged, acknowledged and discarded, releasing admission capacity; it does not begin another GoChannel redelivery cycle. Each attempt has its own span. Oversized/capacity rejections are reported as publication errors and logged without payloads. Handlers must respect cancellation and remain idempotent. There is no persistent dead-letter store or restart recovery. A bare native GoChannel used independently retains its own Nack redelivery behavior; use LocalBus with its DeliveryMiddleware for these limits. EVENTS_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT bounds router shutdown. In-flight events can still be lost when shutdown times out. Logs/traces carry correlation identifiers; metric labels must remain bounded."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Test and diagnose a reaction","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#test-and-diagnose-a-reaction","Text":"A handler test should check valid payloads, invalid payloads, downstream failures and repeated delivery. Identity's handler is shown in the onboarding walkthrough. Use a fake queue client for its unit behavior and the real router in integration tests. go test ./internal/modules/identity/delivery/events/... ./internal/platform/messaging/... Symptom Likely boundary Check Signup succeeded but welcome did not enqueue Optional post-commit publication Safe publication failure log and local subscribers A worker process receives no API event GoChannel cannot cross processes Subscribers in the publishing process Handler keeps failing Payload or downstream service Trace each attempt, then fix the handler/dependency; no durable failed-event queue exists One reaction succeeds and another fails Handlers are independent Reconcile the failed operation; successful reactions are not rolled back Task exists but email does not Notification delivery happens later Follow task ID → delivery IDs → notification status A successful publish is not an acknowledgement from every consumer. Test durable outcomes at their own boundary; do not assert eventual email receipt from event publication alone. See testing asynchronous work."},{"Title":"Events and subscriptions","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Development schema","Parent":"","URL":"events.html#development-schema","Text":"The shipped bus needs no SQL event/offset tables. PostgreSQL notification intent starts only when Notifier.Enqueue persists a channel snapshot. Apply the normal module migrations for that capability; a running event router does not create or migrate tables. Continue with queues for work across processes, transactional notifications for intent committed with SQL, or the complete signup reaction."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html","Text":"A recurring schedule decides when to enqueue work. A worker decides when that queued task executes. Recurring timing is centralized in internal/app/schedules.go: one place to review cadence, feature conditions and enqueue options across the application. Modules own task handlers and fresh command factories. This is the scheduling exception to module-local registration."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Enable the scheduler","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html#enable-the-scheduler","Text":"APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./cmd/scheduler Its main selects app.Processes{Scheduler: true}. App and worker mains do not select a scheduler. Ordinary CLI commands and help construct registrations without running schedules. Workers can execute the enqueued tasks in other processes. Scheduler startup checks Redis before signaling readiness. A failed startup returns an error; later enqueue failures are logged and missed ticks are not replayed. Run exactly one scheduler owner per schedule set. Other replicas may process tasks but must not independently publish the same schedules. Native Asynq scheduling is not a distributed leader-election mechanism."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Edit the central definitions","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html#edit-the-central-definitions","Text":"App's registerSchedules declares task and command lists, then calls asynqx.RegisterSchedules once after module registration. With filesystems configured, Media recovery runs every five minutes. When push is enabled, notifications.prune_subscriptions.v1 runs hourly with a one-minute attempt timeout and one-hour enqueue uniqueness. Each execution disables at most 1000 registrations inactive for 90 days, using a fresh cutoff. The worker dispatcher handles delivery and receipts; it performs no retention cleanup on startup or on a hidden timer. Identity cleanup commands run every five minutes: Task or command Schedule Required configuration Media recovery Every five minutes At least one opened disk Push subscription pruning Every hour Push enabled Identity verification cleanup Every five minutes Authentication enabled Identity session cleanup Every five minutes Authentication enabled These are the current definitions in app/schedules.go; there is no schedule discovery. These conditions register definitions; the scheduler must also be running to publish them. Workers-only deployments still deliver notifications, but need a scheduler owner or explicit notifications prune-subscriptions invocations for retention. Large retention backlogs need repeated bounded runs. For an existing deployment, install handler-capable workers before enabling the new schedule. On rollback, stop scheduling first and drain pending pruning tasks before removing their handler. Enqueue uniqueness is not leader election or exactly-once execution. Modules never start a scheduler or declare recurring timing. Standalone consumers can supply their own task and command lists to asynqx.RegisterSchedules with their own native scheduler, routing and command registry. To add a recurring task, append it to the central tasks list: tasks = append(tasks, asynqx.TaskSchedule{ Spec: \"*/5 * * * *\", Task: func() (*asynq.Task, error) { return asynqx.NewTask(media.RecoverTask, nil), nil }, Options: []asynq.Option{ asynq.Unique(5 * time.Minute), asynq.Timeout(2 * time.Minute), }, }) This is the existing Media recovery definition: edit it in place, do not append a second copy. It imports time, Asynq, pkg/asynqx and internal/platform/media. A new capability supplies its own stable task type and registered handler. The factory has signature func() (*asynq.Task, error) and builds a task without external effects. Factories run during registration, not on each tick. Compute values such as “90 days ago” inside the handler so they stay fresh across ticks. The registrar validates definitions, resolves routing and builds tasks before registering entries. Failure during native registration removes entries added by that call and preserves rollback errors. Discard failed startup state."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Schedule commands","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html#schedule-commands","Text":"Command handling is always installed during app assembly. Running the scheduler is a separate process selection. Command support registers the native task type app.command. Modules explicitly allowlist standalone command factories; Identity uses this for verification and session pruning. A factory must return a fresh Cobra command with no parent and no shared flag state. Do not reuse a command already attached to the console tree. ScheduledCommands has a usable zero value. App assembly explicitly installs it as a native Asynq handler; constructing the allowlist does not mutate a router. Standalone assembly uses the same two statements: commands := \u0026cli.ScheduledCommands{} mux.Handle(cli.CommandTask, commands) This function-body fragment imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/cli; mux is an existing *asynq.ServeMux. Register factories before workers start and then leave the registry unchanged. The task type remains app.command, so queued invocations keep their existing format. You can register factories and create tasks without a mux in a producer-only process. Register the command factory in its module, then declare its timing centrally. Identity's existing registration follows this pattern in register.go: if err := targets.ScheduledCommands.Register( PruneSessionsCommand, func() *cobra.Command { return modulecli.NewPruneSessions(authentication) }, ); err != nil { return err } This fragment runs only after checking authentication and a non-nil targets.ScheduledCommands. modulecli imports the module's delivery/cli package. The factory builds a fresh command for each worker execution. For example, inside the appropriate configuration guard in app/schedules.go: commands = append(commands, asynqx.CommandSchedule{ Spec: \"*/5 * * * *\", Command: identity.PruneVerificationCommand, }) Arguments are tokenized values passed to Cobra, never shell commands. Output is bounded to 64 KiB and written through Asynq's result writer when available; configure native result retention if operators need to inspect completed results later. Every app worker has command handling installed. Module feature conditions still control which factories exist. Worker replicas need no scheduler selection. Removing a command handler while its tasks remain queued leaves them unhandled."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Timing and routing","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html#timing-and-routing","Text":"Expressions use UTC in this app. @daily is midnight UTC. A tick makes a task eligible; it does not promise exact execution time. Runs can overlap, and missed ticks are not replayed after downtime. Entry-level asynq.Queue options override configured task routes, then the default queue applies. All scheduled commands share task type app.command; use entry options when different commands need different queues. The native scheduler bypasses client middleware; worker instrumentation still traces execution."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Remove a native entry","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html#remove-a-native-entry","Text":"For an explicit runtime extension, the app-owned native scheduler supports IDs: id, err := scheduler.Register(\"@every 30m\", task, asynq.Queue(\"high\")) if err != nil { return err } // At a later removal decision: return scheduler.Unregister(id) These are native APIs; direct calls bypass the list registrar's validation and routing. An entry ID identifies a schedule, not a task occurrence. Two schedules for the same task have separate IDs. Removal stops future ticks but does not cancel already-enqueued tasks. The app has no dynamic schedule-management endpoint or persistent schedule-definition store: its configured lists rebuild at startup."},{"Title":"Recurring schedules","Section":"Background work","Heading":"Verify recurring work","Parent":"","URL":"scheduling.html#verify-recurring-work","Text":"Confirm the schedule and its feature condition in app/schedules.go. Start one scheduler owner and at least one worker with the same Redis database. Inspect go run ./cmd/cli queue list and the dashboard. Observe a tick and the handler's business result; a registered definition alone proves neither. Stop the scheduler and confirm that future ticks stop while already queued work can finish. go test ./pkg/asynqx/... ./internal/platform/cli/... Those tests cover schedule validation, rollback and fresh command execution. They do not wait for real recurring production jobs. For a long interval, test the underlying handler directly or use an explicit operator command before deployment. Symptom Check No recurring tasks Scheduler process, enablement, configuration guard and shared Redis URL Duplicate occurrences Multiple scheduler owners, overlapping execution or an uncertain enqueue A scheduled command fails as unknown Requested factory registered on every consuming worker Old cutoff date used repeatedly Values captured in the startup factory instead of computed in the handler Removed schedule still runs Already queued tasks; removal only affects future ticks See worker shutdown, task retries and console conventions. Publication success is separate from execution success."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html","Text":"Send an email now, queue its rendered content for later, or reuse the same content inside a notification. Business modules supply message content; the platform owns SMTP and optional Asynq delivery. App constructs Resources.Mailer and Resources.MailRenderer once. Neither construction nor CLI help sends mail. Part Responsibility Example Module mailviews Named content builders mailviews.NewWelcome(name) pkg/mailview Escaped HTML, plain text and shared branding views.Render(content) internal/platform/mail Immediate SMTP or queued rendered messages mailer.Send / mailer.Enqueue Notifications Recipients, multiple channels and SQL delivery intent notifier.Enqueue Start with a local preview to learn the content model without SMTP. Then configure a sandbox sender and choose immediate or queued delivery."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Preview without sending","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#preview-without-sending","Text":"The repository includes real Identity welcome and sign-in views. Run: APP_ENV_FILE=.env.quickstart.example go run ./examples/mail Open storage/app/mail-preview/welcome.html and otp.html. Their .txt companions show the plain-text alternatives. The preview uses configured branding and a sample code, creates no authentication challenge and never connects to SMTP. Use -output /path/to/previews to select a different directory. Review both HTML and plain text, desktop and narrow layouts. Browser previews and escaping tests do not establish compatibility with every inbox: send sandbox messages to the actual email clients you support before launching a branded campaign."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Send now or enqueue","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#send-now-or-enqueue","Text":"Send immediately at the call site: err := mailer.Send(ctx, message) Or enqueue that message for an existing Asynq worker: info, err := mailer.Enqueue(ctx, message) These are separate function-body fragments. mailer is the borrowed internal/platform/mail.Mailer; message is a mail.Message. Check errors before using info. A nil queue permits Send; Enqueue returns ErrQueueUnavailable and never falls back to sending. Services that only send immediately can keep using the narrower mail.Sender contract from the same package. The app always constructs its mailer and installs mail.send.v1 in its normal task mux. SMTP connects only when sending, never during construction or CLI help. Queued sending requires a worker sharing the queue. Routing, tracing and worker metrics use the existing Asynq integration. Resources.Mailer is available after normal app assembly; standalone capability callers still provide their own sender. Operation Success means Transaction behavior Mailer.Send Immediate transport accepted the attempt Rejects a caller SQL transaction Mailer.Enqueue Redis accepted the task Rejects a caller SQL transaction Notifier.Enqueue Notification intent persisted, subject to commit Can join the business SQL transaction Use Notifications for recipient models, database read state, multiple channels, or delivery intent that must commit with SQL. Ordinary queued mail does not need a notification recipient, occurrence key, or SQL table. Identity's OTP mail remains synchronous and is never queued automatically."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Configure delivery and branding","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#configure-delivery-and-branding","Text":"Settings live in internal/app/config/mail.go. Delivery is always configured; rendering and startup do not connect to SMTP. MAIL_HOST=sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io MAIL_PORT=2525 MAIL_USERNAME=your-sandbox-username MAIL_PASSWORD=your-sandbox-password MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=no-reply@example.com APP_NAME=MyApp MAIL_FROM_NAME= MAIL_BRAND_NAME= MAIL_ACCENT_COLOR=\"#1D4ED8\" MAIL_LOGO_URL= MAIL_WEBSITE_URL=https://example.com MAIL_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@example.com MAIL_FOOTER=\"Account updates, thoughtfully delivered.\" Blank sender and brand names inherit APP_NAME; set either to override it. The logo is optional. Keep credentials in ignored .env for local runs or a secret manager for deployment. The accent accepts a six-digit hex color; logo, website and action links require HTTPS. Configure your own artwork and addresses before real delivery. Brand text remains visible when images are blocked. A renderer with an empty LogoURL omits the image entirely. Tilt also loads an optional, ignored .env.mail.yaml Kubernetes Secret named app-mail in namespace go-modular-dev. Its stringData contains the same MAIL_* keys. Both the migration container and application reference it. Local .env values are not automatically copied into Kubernetes; configure the Secret separately. Never commit this file or bake credentials into an image. App SMTP uses verified STARTTLS on the configured port (2525 by default) and a ten-second connection timeout. A username/password pair enables automatic authentication negotiation; omitting both permits a relay that requires no SMTP authentication. Supplying only one is rejected before connection. Mailtrap Sandbox captures test mail; switch to a delivery provider and verified sender for real recipients. Sending opens and closes its connection; construction does not connect."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Send a message","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#send-a-message","Text":"This complete service function borrows the mailer and sets a deadline: package service import ( \"context\" \"time\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/mail\" ) func SendWelcome(ctx context.Context, mailer mail.Sender, recipient string) error { ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 15*time.Second) defer cancel() return mailer.Send(ctx, mail.Message{ To: []mail.Address{{Email: recipient}}, Subject: \"Welcome\", TextBody: \"Your account is ready.\", HTMLBody: \"\u003cp\u003eYour account is ready.\u003c/p\u003e\", }) } A message requires a To recipient, subject, and at least one body. Providing both bodies creates plain text with an HTML alternative. From overrides the configured sender when provided. CC, BCC, and ReplyTo accept address slices. Use mail views to render variable HTML with escaping. Do not concatenate untrusted text into HTML bodies."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Render an email","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#render-an-email","Text":"The app supplies a shared renderer as Resources.MailRenderer. A module passes that specific dependency to the service that renders messages. This function-body fragment assumes views, mailer, a destination address and a caller ctx: content, err := views.Render(mailview.Email{ Subject: \"Your report is ready\", Title: \"Report ready\", Paragraphs: []string{\"Your monthly report is available in your account.\"}, Action: \u0026mailview.Action{ Label: \"View report\", URL: \"https://example.com/reports\", }, }) if err != nil { return err } return mailer.Send(ctx, mail.Message{ To: []mail.Address{{Email: address}}, Subject: content.Subject, HTMLBody: content.HTML, TextBody: content.Text, }) Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/mailview and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/mail. Rendering returns a subject plus both body formats; it performs no delivery. Use the destination validated and authorized by your operation. mailview.Email field Use Subject Required, single-line subject Title Required visible heading Preheader Inbox preview text; never put an OTP or secret here Eyebrow Short contextual label above the title Paragraphs Escaped text paragraphs Code Optional value, label and hint; value must be nonempty, single-line and at most 32 bytes Action Optional button label and absolute HTTPS URL, with a plain-text link Note Optional supporting or security note All text is escaped in HTML. Raw HTML and CSS are intentionally not accepted by this content model. This does not sanitize an arbitrary mail.Message.HTMLBody that you construct yourself."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Define a module mail view","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#define-a-module-mail-view","Text":"Keep named content beside its owning module. There is no base class or view registry. This is the existing File: internal/modules/identity/mailviews/welcome.go: // Package mailviews defines Identity email content for the default branded layout. package mailviews import ( \"strings\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/mailview\" ) // NewWelcome is Identity's mailable content builder. The shared Renderer supplies // layout, logo, colors, footer, escaping and a plain-text alternative. This content // can be rendered for a local preview, sent directly, or snapshotted by a Notifier. func NewWelcome(name string) mailview.Email { greeting := \"Welcome aboard.\" if name = strings.TrimSpace(name); name != \"\" { greeting = \"Welcome, \" + name + \".\" } return mailview.Email{ Subject: \"Welcome to your account\", Preheader: \"Your account is ready. Here are your next steps.\", Eyebrow: \"YOUR ACCOUNT\", Title: greeting, Paragraphs: []string{ \"Your account is ready to use.\", \"Review your profile and keep your sign-in methods up to date. You can review and sign out your active sessions from your account settings.\", }, Note: \"We will never ask you to share a sign-in code by email.\", } } Call views.Render(mailviews.NewWelcome(user.Name)) for a preview or direct mail. A notification builder can use Email: \u0026content after constructing the same value. It does not need to render or send it itself: the notifier prepares the snapshot. Keep eligibility, business policy and recipient selection outside the layout."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Queue a rendered mail view","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#queue-a-rendered-mail-view","Text":"This complete, compiled example is examples/mail/delivery/delivery.go. Module mailviews can supply the same mailview.Email value instead of building it inline. // Package delivery demonstrates queued mail using borrowed application clients. package delivery import ( \"context\" \"github.com/hibiken/asynq\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/mail\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/mailview\" ) // EnqueueReport renders once before Redis accepts the immutable message snapshot. // The caller authorizes and validates the destination before invoking this operation. func EnqueueReport(ctx context.Context, mailer mail.Mailer, views *mailview.Renderer, address string) (*asynq.TaskInfo, error) { content, err := views.Render(mailview.Email{ Subject: \"Your monthly report is ready\", Title: \"Report ready\", Paragraphs: []string{ \"Your monthly report is available in your account.\", }, Action: \u0026mailview.Action{Label: \"View report\", URL: \"https://example.com/reports\"}, }) if err != nil { return nil, err } return mailer.Enqueue(ctx, mail.Message{ To: []mail.Address{{Email: address}}, Subject: content.Subject, HTMLBody: content.HTML, TextBody: content.Text, }) } The worker sends the saved subject and bodies without rerendering a changed view. If From is omitted, the worker transport's configured sender applies. Enqueue forwards native Asynq options, including ProcessIn, MaxRetry, TaskID and Retention. Its defaults are three retries and a 30-second attempt. Successful tasks are removed unless a retention option is supplied. Queued messages support From, To, CC, BCC, ReplyTo, subject, text/HTML bodies, headers and transport tags. The full encoded payload is limited to 64 KiB. Attachment readers and CloseAttachments are rejected before enqueue. Immediate sending retains attachment support. For large reports or attachments, use a module-owned job containing an authorized storage reference and reopen bounded readers in that job; do not serialize readers or temporary file paths. Redis stores recipient addresses and rendered content. Restrict queue, dashboard, backup and inspection access accordingly; never enqueue credentials or OTPs. Worker errors omit provider response text while preserving wrapped causes for retry policies. Successful Redis acceptance does not guarantee delivery, and an uncertain SMTP result or worker crash can cause duplicate email on retry. Task uniqueness limits enqueue duplication; it cannot make SMTP exactly-once."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Delay a message or retain its result","Parent":"Queue a rendered mail view","URL":"mail.html#delay-a-message-or-retain-its-result","Text":"This fragment uses the same mailer, message and caller context as above: info, err := mailer.Enqueue(ctx, message, asynq.ProcessIn(5*time.Minute), asynq.MaxRetry(3), asynq.Timeout(30*time.Second), asynq.Retention(24*time.Hour), ) if err != nil { return err } // Persist or return info.ID when the caller needs to inspect task state. Import time and Asynq. Delaying changes eligibility, not an exact delivery time. Use the same queue routing settings on the producer and worker. The normal module registration already installs mail.send.v1; no per-email worker registration is needed. See queues for options and retries."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Attach files","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#attach-files","Text":"message.Attachments = []mail.Attachment{ mail.NewAttachment(\"invoice.pdf\", \"application/pdf\", bytes.NewReader(pdf)), } This fragment assumes message and bounded pdf bytes already exist. Import bytes. SMTP buffers attachments under one aggregate raw-byte budget: 20 MiB by default. SMTPConfig.MaxAttachmentBytes overrides it; zero uses the default and negative values are invalid. App callers can set cfg.Mail.MaxAttachmentBytes in Go before assembly. No new environment variable is required. MIME encoding, message bodies and library overhead are additional memory. Oversized input returns mail.ErrAttachmentsTooLarge before SMTP delivery, after reading at most the remaining budget plus one byte. Seekable readers are rewound. Other readers are consumed, so recreate them before retries. A nil body is skipped, while the filename is required. Empty content type defaults to application/octet-stream. Prefer caller-owned cleanup for file readers, usually a defer file.Close() in the operation that opened them, with close errors handled there. With CloseAttachments=true, SMTP closes every closable input once on every return path, including validation, read and transport failures. Do not list the same owned reader twice. Close errors join the returned error, even after SMTP accepted the message; do not blindly resend on a cleanup failure. Already canceled calls read nothing. Cooperative cancellation is checked before and between reads. An arbitrary reader blocked inside Read or Seek cannot be interrupted by context alone; give remote readers their own deadlines. SMTP starts no detached reader goroutine. The Mailer wrapper may reject a transaction before entering the SMTP transport, so keep caller-owned cleanup when rejection can occur."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Headers and delivery results","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#headers-and-delivery-results","Text":"Headers adds SMTP headers. Supply only trusted names and values; the wrapper does not define an application allowlist. Tags is currently unused by the SMTP implementation. A successful Send means the SMTP operation succeeded, not that the recipient read the message or that it reached an inbox. Immediate SMTP sending does not retry; queued mail uses its configured Asynq retry policy. There is no built-in bounce handling, tracking or provider event consumer."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Send through a task","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#send-through-a-task","Text":"Inject the mailer into a task's service and send using the handler context. Enqueue identifiers and serializable data; do not place readers or client instances in the payload. Workers reconstruct attachment readers for each attempt. A timeout or connection failure can leave delivery uncertain. Retrying may send another email; queue uniqueness cannot make SMTP effects atomic with task completion. Choose retry behavior appropriate to the message. Use logging.FromContext(ctx) at the boundary responsible for reporting failures. Avoid recording message bodies, credentials, or recipient lists in routine logs. The mail package itself does not install logging or persistent delivery storage."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Reuse the layout","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#reuse-the-layout","Text":"The shared templates live in pkg/mailview/templates and are embedded in the binary. Change these files for a framework-wide layout change; change a module's builder for one message. Rebuild after changing templates. The layout uses semantic headings and paragraphs inside presentation tables, inline CSS, a mobile enhancement and an Outlook width fallback. Each render produces HTML and plain text. No JavaScript, remote stylesheet or CSS build step is required. Branding remains visible when images are blocked; an empty logo URL omits the image. Identity's sign-in view uses a code block with an expiry hint and safety note. It keeps the code out of the subject and preheader. Rendering does not generate, store or validate a challenge; those rules remain in the Identity service. OTP mail is sent synchronously after the challenge is prepared, with its own failure policy. See authentication for the complete operation."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Reuse components","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#reuse-components","Text":"Use mailview.Email, Code and Action for semantic content instead of copying layout HTML. The renderer parses templates once, copies its brand settings and is safe for concurrent rendering. Changing brand configuration requires constructing a new renderer (normally an application restart). For an unrelated HTML template, use Go's html/template with embedded files owned by the module. For plain text, use text/template. There is no generic application template engine to configure; mailview is deliberately email-specific."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Use mail independently","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#use-mail-independently","Text":"Construct an SMTP sender with mail.NewSMTP(mail.SMTPConfig{...}), or provide any mail.Sender. Then compose it with an optional native or instrumented Asynq client: mailer, err := mail.New(sender, queue) if err != nil { return err } handler, err := mail.NewHandler(mailer) if err != nil { return err } mux.Handle(mail.SendTask, handler) This function-body fragment imports mail from github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/mail. The caller owns sender, queue, the native Asynq mux, and worker execution/cleanup. Use New(sender, nil) for immediate-only mail. Application modules borrow the already configured deps.Mailer; they do not construct another worker or register the platform handler again. The mail package is under this application's internal boundary. These examples mean “without app bootstrap inside this repository,” not a public package another unrelated Go module can import. The reusable rendering package pkg/mailview has no dependency on application internals. Construct an independent renderer with public branding only: views, err := mailview.New(mailview.Brand{ Name: \"Example\", AccentColor: \"#2563eb\", WebsiteURL: \"https://example.com\", SupportEmail: \"support@example.com\", }) if err != nil { return err } LogoURL is optional. The website and optional logo require HTTPS; the accent is a six-digit hex color. Credentials belong to the SMTP sender, never to the renderer."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Configure SMTP","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#configure-smtp","Text":"mailer, err := mail.NewSMTP(mail.SMTPConfig{ Host: \"smtp.example.com\", Port: 587, Username: username, Password: password, From: mail.Address{Email: \"notifications@example.com\", Name: \"Example\"}, UseTLS: true, InsecureSkipVerify: false, }) if err != nil { return err } Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/mail. These host and address values are placeholders; credentials should come from your typed configuration. Construction validates settings and creates the client; sending performs network I/O. UseTLS sets the SMTP client's mandatory TLS policy. It is not an implicit-TLS port selector; this wrapper exposes no separate implicit-TLS option. Configure and verify the connection mode your provider requires. Use NewSMTP with explicit verification settings. Mailtrap Sandbox works through SMTP; no Mailtrap API driver is implemented."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Trace a send","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#trace-a-send","Text":"App assembly already wraps the configured mailer with the borrowed tracer. For a separately constructed mailer, use: mailer := mail.WithTracing(smtpMailer, tracer) err := mailer.Send(ctx, message) This fragment borrows smtpMailer, an OpenTelemetry tracer, ctx and message. It records timing and failure without mail content or recipient data. It adds no retries or ownership. Successful submission does not prove delivery to the inbox. See observability for the full contract."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Test without sending real mail","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#test-without-sending-real-mail","Text":"Use a test sender implementing the package-owned mail.Sender; do not redefine that contract in a module. Capture the message passed to Send, then assert its recipient, subject and content in the owning module's external test suite. A sender returning a chosen error lets you verify the operation's failure policy. go test ./pkg/mailview/... go test ./tests/platform -run 'Mail|SMTP' go test ./tests/queue -run Mail go test ./tests/modules -run TestWorkflowGuidesMatchCompiledSources -count=1 Rendering tests check escaping and content. Mail tests cover serialization, limits, transaction rejection and retry classification without contacting your SMTP provider. The compiled examples/mail/delivery example is checked against the guide. Real provider connectivity and inbox rendering need a separate sandbox send using your credentials."},{"Title":"Mail and mail views","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Troubleshoot delivery","Parent":"","URL":"mail.html#troubleshoot-delivery","Text":"Symptom Check App starts but SMTP cannot send Construction makes no SMTP connection; verify host, port, credentials and provider TLS mode SMTP accepts mail but no inbox message appears Sandbox capture, sender verification, provider delivery/bounce reports and spam filtering ErrQueueUnavailable Independent mailer was constructed with no queue; supply one or explicitly call Send Queued message never runs Worker, Redis database, routing and mail.send.v1 registration Enqueue rejects attachments Use immediate Send or a module task that reopens an authorized file reference Message exceeds 64 KiB Reduce rendered payload or use a job with an external artifact reference Email appears twice Uncertain SMTP outcome, worker crash, task replay or repeated original call A template action fails validation Absolute HTTPS URL and nonempty action label A transaction guard fails Move SMTP/Redis calls after commit, or persist notification intent in the transaction Mail submission, inbox delivery and reading are separate states. The mail facade has no built-in bounce receiver, tracking pixel or provider event consumer. For recipient read state use the database notification channel. For worker inspection use the queue guide."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html","Text":"Send a message through database, email or mobile push channels using one notification.Message. Business modules choose the recipient, content and eligible channels. notification.Notifier stores read state and manages delivery. Start with a database notification. Add email or push only when the operation needs it. For ordinary email without recipient state or SQL delivery intent, use mail. For direct provider push without SQL, use push. Operation Choose it when Successful return means Send You want one immediate attempt Selected channels were attempted; inspect all results Enqueue Intent must survive process failure or join a SQL write PostgreSQL accepted immutable channel intents, subject to commit List / UnreadCount / MarkRead You are building in-app notification UI Recipient-scoped database state was read or changed App constructs the notifier once as Resources.Notifier. Modules borrow it; they do not start dispatchers, register providers or implement another notifier contract."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Stored state","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#stored-state","Text":"Table Purpose notifications In-app inbox content and read state notification_deliveries Durable channel delivery intent, attempts and outcomes push_subscriptions Recipient-owned device installations, push tokens and optional session bindings Push subscriptions are delivery destinations, not notification preferences or topic subscriptions. A recipient can register multiple installations. Token rotation, logout, invalid-token handling and inactivity pruning keep those destinations current. Queued push delivery records retain the subscription ID and version so an older message cannot follow a reassigned installation. The schema is created by the normal migrations even when push is disabled. For applications using the former notification_subscriptions table, follow the version 0.2 migration procedure."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Create your first database notification","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#create-your-first-database-notification","Text":"Apply the normal application migrations and use the existing Resources.Notifier. Database-only Send needs PostgreSQL; it does not need a running worker, Redis, SMTP or Expo. Inside an existing operation, after authorizing the UUID userID: recipient := notification.Recipient{ Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: userID.String(), } message := notification.Message{ Type: \"identity.profile_updated\", Database: json.RawMessage(`{\"title\":\"Profile updated\",\"body\":\"Your changes were saved.\"}`), } _, err := notifier.Send(ctx, recipient, message) return err Import encoding/json, github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/entities and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/notification. ctx and notifier are borrowed by the operation. With no surrounding transaction, success makes the database record available immediately through List or the authenticated HTTP routes. Inside a transaction, visibility still depends on its commit. This example omits Key, so every invocation creates a new occurrence. Read the key rules below before making an operation retryable. For a real profile write plus notification, follow the compiled transactional workflow."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Define a module notification","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#define-a-module-notification","Text":"Modules keep a plural notifications package; the shared capability is singular notification. Both imports work without aliases. A builder is an ordinary Go function, with no base class, service registration or per-notification handler. For an illustrative Orders module, put this complete function in its notifications package and import encoding/json, internal/platform/notification and pkg/mailview: func NewOrderShipped(orderID string) (notification.Message, error) { data, err := json.Marshal(struct { OrderID string `json:\"order_id\"` Title string `json:\"title\"` }{OrderID: orderID, Title: \"Your order has shipped\"}) if err != nil { return notification.Message{}, err } return notification.Message{ Key: \"shipment:\" + orderID, Type: \"order.shipped\", Database: data, Email: \u0026mailview.Email{ Subject: \"Your order has shipped\", Title: \"On its way\", Paragraphs: []string{\"Your order has shipped.\"}, }, }, nil } Key optionally identifies one business occurrence per recipient, not an attempt. Include a shipment version when an order can ship more than once. A retry reuses its key; a new occurrence uses a new key. Type is a stable module-owned name used by clients and operators. Types require no central registration. Explicit keys allow 1–200 UTF-8 bytes; types allow 1–100, without surrounding whitespace or control characters."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Omit the key for a new occurrence on every call","Parent":"Define a module notification","URL":"notifications.html#omit-the-key-for-a-new-occurrence-on-every-call","Text":"message := notification.Message{ Type: \"announcement.published\", Database: json.RawMessage(`{\"title\":\"A new announcement is available\"}`), } receipt, err := notifier.Enqueue(ctx, recipient, message) When Key is empty, each Send or Enqueue call generates a fresh UUID key. All selected channels share that key, and queued worker retries reuse the persisted key. The caller's Message is not modified. Repeating the original call, even with the same Message value, creates a new occurrence and can produce another notification. Use an explicit stable key when an event, job or request may be retried, especially after an uncertain enqueue result. Only the empty string requests generation; whitespace-only or otherwise invalid explicit keys are rejected. Enqueue deduplicates by recipient, key and channel, including completed deliveries. Send deduplicates database records, but attempts external channels immediately—even with an explicit key. Use Enqueue when email retries need persisted deduplication."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Select channels","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#select-channels","Text":"Constant Message field Behavior ChannelDatabase (database) Database json.RawMessage Store JSON data with recipient ownership and read state ChannelEmail (email) Email *mailview.Email Render branded text/HTML using pkg/mailview, then send with internal/platform/mail ChannelPush (push) Push *push.Message Send to the recipient’s registered installations Non-nil content selects a channel. message.Channels() returns the typed channels in delivery order. Set message.Email = nil for a database-only notification, or message.Database = nil for email-only delivery. A message needs at least one channel; there is no separate Via list to keep synchronized with the content. The database name follows Laravel's database channel: notification type and JSON data are stored for the application's interface. Database content must be a valid UTF-8 JSON object, at most MaxDataSize (32 KiB). An empty object is valid; null, arrays, scalars and invalid JSON are rejected. Titles, text, links, identifiers and nested values belong inside this object. There is no fixed Body field and no double JSON encoding. Build dynamic data with json.Marshal; do not interpolate untrusted values into a JSON string. Email accepts the existing mailview.Email directly. Module mailviews builders can compose it with the shared branded layout. Rendering and address validation happen before either channel has effects. Queued email snapshots its rendered subject, text, HTML and destination; a retry does not rerender newer templates. Each encoded channel snapshot is limited to 64 KiB. Rich synchronous attachments remain available through internal/platform/mail; queued content contains no open files or readers. PostgreSQL normalizes JSONB formatting and numbers. Separate storage limits allow 64 KiB for normalized database data and 128 KiB for a normalized channel snapshot; oversized numeric expansion is rejected by database constraints during persistence."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Address any model","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#address-any-model","Text":"recipient := notification.Recipient{ Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: user.ID.String(), Email: user.Email.String(), } receipt, err := notifier.Enqueue(ctx, recipient, message) if err != nil { return err } // receipt.DeliveryIDs identifies the persisted channel intents. This fragment assumes an authorized, loaded user, a prepared message, ctx and the borrowed notifier. Import internal/modules/shared/entities; do not make up module-local discriminators. Add stable admin or other model constants to internal/modules/shared/entities/entity_types.go when introducing those models. Recipient.Type is a stable logical model name, matching [a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,63}. Recipient.ID is a canonical UTF-8 string of 1–200 bytes without surrounding whitespace or control characters. Numeric IDs use decimal strings; UUIDs use their standard form. (type, id) scopes persistence, duplicate suppression and read state. Email is only a destination and is excluded from database-channel snapshots and stored records. Platform code never imports a model to discover its address. The caller authorizes recipients and owns existence/deletion policy. There is no foreign key to arbitrary model tables. Stop new publication and coordinate workers before purging a deleted recipient; process-local pub/sub is not durable cleanup."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Send immediately or queue durably","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#send-immediately-or-queue-durably","Text":"Operation Guarantee and dependencies Send(ctx, recipient, message) One attempt per selected channel; returns []Result with typed channel and error Enqueue(ctx, recipient, message) Atomically persists channel intents; returns Receipt.DeliveryIDs Dispatch(ctx) Submits a bounded batch of due IDs to Asynq; database and queue required Deliver(ctx, id) Claims and attempts one persisted channel; called by the worker Send validates all content before effects. Database-only messages join the caller's SQL transaction, replacing a separate record/write API. Messages containing email or push must run outside transactions. A failed channel does not prevent independent channels from being attempted; inspect the results before deciding what to retry. SMTP acceptance does not prove that a message reached the recipient. Enqueue needs a database but no queue client in the calling process. It performs no Redis, SMTP or pub/sub calls. Its receipt means intent is persisted, subject to the caller's commit. Put business changes and notification intent in the same transaction: See the complete, compiled profile update for this transaction pattern with real framework services. All participating SQL collaborators must share the same database pool. A receipt returned inside a transaction is not proof that its outer caller eventually committed. App exposes the same instance as dependencies.Resources.Notifier. Construct with New(db, Options{}) for database-only use or New(nil, Options{Mailer: sender, MailRenderer: views}) for email-only sends. Missing SQL returns database.ErrNilDB; missing email/queue configuration returns ErrUnavailable. A configured Mailer requires MailRenderer at construction. No instance owns or closes its borrowed clients. Follow a durable notification Each step has a different guarantee. A saved intent, a queued task and an accepted provider call are not the same outcome. Save intent. The business operation calls Enqueue in its SQL transaction. PostgreSQL stores one snapshot per selected channel and destination. No Redis or provider call happens here. A rollback removes both the business changes and the new notification intents. Commit. The outer transaction makes both the business change and its notification intent visible together. A receipt returned inside the transaction still depends on that commit. A successful commit does not mean a notification has been delivered. Dispatch. The worker-owned dispatcher scans due ledger rows and submits delivery IDs to Asynq in Redis. The ledger remains the source of pending work if Redis is unavailable. Dispatch does not require the cron scheduler. Attempt delivery. A worker claims one channel with a lease, reads the saved content and attempts the database, email or push operation. Channels progress independently. A failure in email does not undo a stored database notification. Record or retry. Persist the result. Failed attempts wait until due; expired claims can recover and exhausted deliveries need deliberate retry. A crash after an external provider accepts the message can still cause a duplicate. A lease cannot undo a message already sent."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Handle partial immediate results","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#handle-partial-immediate-results","Text":"Send validates the whole message before effects, then attempts channels independently. A later failure does not roll back an earlier successful channel. In a function that returns ([]notification.Result, error), preserve both outputs: results, err := notifier.Send(ctx, recipient, message) return results, err Result Caller responsibility Validation error with no results Fix content, destination or configuration before trying again Database succeeded, email failed Do not assume the database record disappeared; decide which effect to retry Push has PushTicketID Persist/check its receipt if required; Send does not poll it External result is uncertain A repeat attempt may duplicate the external message A stable Key deduplicates database records even for immediate sends. It does not suppress repeated immediate email/push attempts. Prefer Enqueue for persistent, independent channel retries. Never retry the entire multi-channel call blindly."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Read stored notifications","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#read-stored-notifications","Text":"List, UnreadCount and MarkRead operate on notification.Record values. Reads are bounded and newest-first; marking read preserves the first timestamp. HTTP routes are GET /notifications, GET /notifications/unread-count, and PATCH /notifications/{id}/read. Default routes use the authenticated Identity user; other model types require their own authorized handlers. Responses include type, data, recipient_type, recipient_id and timestamps. data is a JSON object, including nested values. The payload schema is owned by the module, while read state is owned by the notification capability."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Read from Go","Parent":"Read stored notifications","URL":"notifications.html#read-from-go","Text":"These function-body fragments assume an authorized recipient, ctx and notifier: page, err := notifier.List(ctx, recipient, pagination.NewPagination(1, 20)) if err != nil { return err } // page.Items contains records; page.Total is the matching record count. count, err := notifier.UnreadCount(ctx, recipient) if err != nil { return err } // count is an integer, independent of pagination. return notifier.MarkRead(ctx, recipient, notificationID) Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/pagination for the first example. notificationID is a UUID. Repeated MarkRead preserves the first read time. Passing another recipient's ID does not grant access to their record. There is no implicit “mark all read” action."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Read from HTTP","Parent":"Read stored notifications","URL":"notifications.html#read-from-http","Text":"With an Identity access token stored in $ACCESS_TOKEN: curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\ 'http://localhost:8080/api/v1/notifications?page=1\u0026per_page=20' curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\ http://localhost:8080/api/v1/notifications/unread-count curl -X PATCH -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\ http://localhost:8080/api/v1/notifications/NOTIFICATION_ID/read The list returns the framework's data envelope containing items, page, per_page, total and total_pages. Unread count returns data: {\"count\": N}; marking read returns 204 with no body. Normal request metadata is still provided by the response layer. The caller cannot select recipient type or ID through these default routes. They derive it from the authenticated user. An Admin module supplies its own authorized handlers and calls the same recipient-scoped methods."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Push and database notifications","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#push-and-database-notifications","Text":"This fragment assumes exportID, JSON-object data, expiry expires, an authorized recipient and a borrowed notifier. Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/push: message := notification.Message{ Key: \"export:\" + exportID + \":ready\", Type: \"export.ready\", Database: data, Push: \u0026push.Message{ Title: \"Your export is ready\", Body: \"Open the application to download it.\", Data: map[string]string{\"export_id\": exportID}, ExpiresAt: \u0026expires, }, } receipt, err := notifier.Enqueue(ctx, recipient, message) if err != nil { return err } // Persist or return receipt.DeliveryIDs when the operation needs delivery tracking. Queued and synchronous notification push automatically includes reserved string data fields notification_key and notification_type; caller values for these fields are overwritten in a copied payload. Push works without selecting Database. Channel delivery order is not guaranteed. Enqueue freezes the push target set once under a transaction lock. Each destination has an independent delivery row. Repeating the key cannot add newly registered installations or replace content. Registration rotation, reassignment, unsubscribe, expiry or logout prevents an old queued version from following the new destination. Refreshing an unchanged registration only updates its freshness. No registration produces a skipped delivery, rather than a job that later targets new devices. A recipient may have at most 100 active installations per push project. Subscribe enforces this atomically, including concurrent registrations. Existing installations can rotate at capacity. ErrInstallationLimit asks the caller to unsubscribe a device before adding another; it is also returned when legacy data already exceeds the limit. Resolve legacy excess with recipient-scoped Unsubscribe (or DetachSession for a deliberate logout), then retry. Delivery never silently truncates destinations. Subscribe, Unsubscribe, DetachSession and PruneSubscriptions work for any model; callers authorize recipients. HTTP defaults serve Identity users only. Active installations cannot be reassigned to a different recipient without detachment. The provider/project scopes registrations and uniqueness. Device registration is private and never included in HTTP responses, operator responses or logs. Identity's explicit session-revocation methods detach registrations atomically with logout. Before each new provider send, session-bound destinations also call Options.SessionActive. App supplies Identity's authoritative account/session check, so replay revocation, single-session displacement, suspension and expiry prevent new push attempts. A false result disables only the checked subscription version and skips delivery; a backend error is retried without detaching the registration. This also protects already queued intents. A concurrent registration for a fresh session survives the stale check. The check cannot retract an in-flight provider request. Standalone session-bound callers must supply SessionActive; missing checks fail closed. Sessionless recipients remain supported without that callback. Callers still own recipient authorization and may explicitly detach on deletion. Push registration is not itself an authentication credential. App centrally schedules notifications.prune_subscriptions.v1 hourly when push is enabled. Each run disables up to 1000 registrations inactive for 90 days; the cutoff is computed at execution. Delivery/receipt polling does not perform retention cleanup and needs no cron. Workers-only deployments need a scheduler owner or the CLI command notifications prune-subscriptions for retention. See the scheduling guide for rollout and backlog handling. Invalid registrations are disabled, permanent failures stop automatic retries, and transient failures retry independently. Provider Retry-After is respected. Expired messages are skipped. Logs contain delivery ID/channel/attempt only. Optional OTel metrics expose notification.delivery.attempts with channel/outcome and notification.pending.age in seconds; alert on growing backlog age, permanent failures, throttling, and invalid registrations. Retention of delivery history remains an application/operator policy because purging it also removes deduplication."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Register and detach a push installation","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#register-and-detach-a-push-installation","Text":"The default authenticated routes are: Method Path under /api/v1 Result POST /notifications/subscriptions Register or refresh a device installation; returns ID, installation ID and update time DELETE /notifications/subscriptions/{id} Detach a recipient-owned installation; returns 204 The POST body contains only installation_id (a client-persisted UUID) and registration (the Expo push token). Identity supplies recipient and session identity from the access token. The provider registration is never echoed back. Use the Expo client example for permissions, token acquisition and the authenticated request. Refresh unchanged registrations on application startup so inactivity pruning reflects current use. Identity's HTTP delivery calls its AuthService's SubscribePush operation directly. Identity rechecks the session and holds its account lock through registration, serializing it with logout. Direct Notifier.Subscribe calls do not have that Identity policy; custom integrations must authorize their recipients and coordinate session revocation themselves. For a non-Identity integration inside this repository: subscription, err := notifier.Subscribe(ctx, recipient, installationID, sessionID, registration) if err != nil { return err } // Persist subscription.ID for this installation; do not expose its registration. All four values are supplied and authorized by the caller. Nonempty session IDs require a SessionActive callback in the notifier options. sessionID can be empty for a model without sessions. DetachSession(ctx, recipient, \"\") disables all installations for that recipient; a nonempty session ID targets only that session. Coordinate account deletion and stop new publication before purging recipient data."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Expo acceptance and receipts","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#expo-acceptance-and-receipts","Text":"Expo push registrations replace native provider tokens. New registrations must use ExpoPushToken[...] or ExponentPushToken[...]. Configure PUSH_EXPO_PROJECT_ID and optionally PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN (required when enhanced push security is enabled). Accepted push deliveries enter awaiting_receipt. The existing dispatcher/worker checks receipts after 15 minutes; missing receipts and lookup failures are polled again after five minutes without resending or consuming send attempts. An ok receipt confirms provider handoff only. Definitive receipt failures follow normal retry or permanent-failure handling, and invalid destinations are disabled by registration version. No confirmation within 24 hours is a failed, uncertain outcome requiring operator review. Manual retry clears its ticket and can duplicate delivery. Synchronous Send exposes Result.PushTicketID for caller-owned receipt checks. See the push guide for standalone usage and Expo client setup. Receipt state is persisted with the delivery. A worker restart resumes polling that ticket; it does not intentionally resend merely because a receipt is pending."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Delivery lifecycle","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#delivery-lifecycle","Text":"Status Meaning DeliveryPending (pending) Waiting for dispatch or next due attempt DeliverySending (sending) A worker holds the current lease DeliveryAwaitingReceipt (awaiting_receipt) Expo accepted a ticket; provider handoff is not yet confirmed DeliverySent (sent) Database record stored, SMTP accepted, or Expo confirmed provider handoff DeliveryFailed (failed) Terminal failure or exhausted/uncertain delivery requiring operator review DeliverySkipped (skipped) No eligible destination, detached registration or expired content Zero and unknown status values are invalid. “Sent” never means the user read it. One row exists per (recipient_type, recipient_id, key, channel, destination). Destination is empty except for push, which uses a subscription ID. Enqueue calls with the same key return the original IDs and retain the first type, content, destination and state. Channels retry independently; successful channels are not intentionally resent. NewDispatcher(notifier, interval) validates its arguments without starting work; zero selects one second. Invalid intervals or a missing notifier return an error before readiness can be signaled. Worker processes run the dispatcher every second (configurable with NOTIFICATION_DISPATCH_INTERVAL, minimum 100ms). Each scan submits at most 100 IDs using notifications.deliver.v1. No cron scheduler is needed. Replicas coordinate through database claims; notifications dispatch remains available for operators. Without dispatch, intent stays in PostgreSQL. Losing Redis does not remove the persisted delivery backlog. Claims use the database clock, a two-minute lease and an ownership token. Provider attempts have a 30-second deadline, followed by up to five seconds to persist the outcome. The ledger allows eight attempts with exponential delay starting at one minute and up to 20% jitter. Asynq retries infrastructure errors three times; recorded provider failures wait for the next dispatch. Expired claims recover; exhausted deliveries require deliberate RetryDelivery. SMTP cannot guarantee exactly-once delivery: a worker may crash after acceptance. Lease ownership protects database updates but cannot undo an external effect. GetDelivery and ListDeliveries expose metadata only; logs omit content, destinations and provider error details. Keep backups and define retention before purging rows: deleting records also removes duplicate suppression. No automatic purge is implied."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Inspect and retry deliveries","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#inspect-and-retry-deliveries","Text":"Use the same database/Redis profile as the running application: go run ./cmd/cli notifications list user USER_ID go run ./cmd/cli notifications unread user USER_ID go run ./cmd/cli notifications read user USER_ID NOTIFICATION_ID go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status pending go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status awaiting_receipt go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status failed go run ./cmd/cli notifications delivery DELIVERY_ID Use --status '' to include all delivery states and --page 2 for the next bounded page. Operator responses contain metadata, not private channel snapshots or provider registrations. The CLI constructs dependencies but does not start workers. After checking the cause and duplication risk: go run ./cmd/cli notifications retry DELIVERY_ID go run ./cmd/cli notifications dispatch Retry accepts only a terminal failed delivery and makes it eligible again. Dispatch submits a bounded batch of due IDs; a running worker is still required. These are different from replaying an Asynq task in the queue inspector. The SQL ledger owns notification provider retry state. For stale push installations, notifications prune-subscriptions disables a batch inactive for 90 days. The central hourly schedule performs the same operation when push and a scheduler owner are enabled. It is separate from delivery polling."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Use each capability independently","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#use-each-capability-independently","Text":"Database only: notification.New(db, notification.Options{}). Email only: notification.New(nil, notification.Options{Mailer: mailer, MailRenderer: views}). Push only: push.NewExpo, then Send(ctx, registration, message) and Receipt(ctx, ticket.ID). Durable notification delivery: supply PostgreSQL to New, and Asynq to the worker instance. The producer instance needs no Redis connection. Both must have selected channel clients configured for preparation and validation. See push for constructors, credentials, frontend setup and standalone examples. Modules reuse these owned capabilities; do not redefine their contracts. Internal Watermill events remain process-local. For important notifications, persist intent in the business transaction rather than relying on an in-memory event to eventually enqueue it. A producer and worker may use separate notifier instances backed by the same PostgreSQL database. Both need collaborators for the selected channels because preparation validates and snapshots content before storing intent. Only the worker dispatcher needs Redis. notifier, err := notification.New(db, notification.Options{ Queue: queue, Mailer: mailer, MailRenderer: views, Pusher: pusher, PushProject: projectID, }) if err != nil { return err } This is an assembly fragment using already constructed clients; omit unneeded optional fields. The notifier never closes them. For standalone framework integration, integration.Register(notifier, targets) installs selected migrations, commands and tasks and starts no work. Identity's HTTP delivery owns the user notification and push-subscription routes. Other business modules can authorize their own recipients before calling the capability directly. notification.NewDispatcher(notifier, 0) selects the one-second polling interval; call its Run(ctx) once as part of your own worker lifecycle, and wait for it before closing clients. Start the actual Asynq worker separately. A dispatcher alone does not execute delivery tasks. internal/platform remains application-owned Go code; external projects can use pkg/push independently without importing it."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Extend when a provider is ready","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#extend-when-a-provider-is-ready","Text":"Future channels add typed content and a Channel constant, then prepare a durable snapshot and deliver it through the existing retry flow. Keep those two operations in notifier.go; add the provider to Options and permit its channel in SQL. Business notification types still need no registration. There is no generic driver registry or map of untyped values for callers to configure. SMS needs a phone destination and text; WhatsApp commonly needs provider-specific template identifiers and parameters. These channels are not implemented or advertised as valid enum values yet. Add providers and their behavior together. Preferences, localization and recipient eligibility belong in module builders until shared policy is actually needed."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Development migration and Identity example","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#development-migration-and-identity-example","Text":"The capability owns migrations for database records, channel delivery intent and push installations. App registers these through notification/integration and the central migration runner applies them. Constructing a notifier does not create tables. Use the checked-in baseline for a new database. Existing deployments must preserve applied migrations and receive explicit forward changes; runtime startup never resets a schema. Keep delivery history retention deliberate because purging it also removes duplicate suppression. The onboarding walkthrough connects the shipped notifications.NewWelcome builder, mail view, local event and worker. The job reloads current account state and skips inactive/deleted users; unverified users receive no email. The local signup event can be lost before an intent is saved. Use the transactional example when notification intent must commit with the business operation."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Test and troubleshoot notifications","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#test-and-troubleshoot-notifications","Text":"go test ./internal/platform/notification/... ./pkg/push/... go test ./tests/modules -run TestProfileNotificationWorkflowPostgres -count=1 The second test needs DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES pointing to a disposable PostgreSQL database; without it, PostgreSQL-dependent cases skip. It proves commit, rollback, delayed visibility and repeat delivery of database notifications. The broader integration gate also exercises real Redis. Provider tests use local fake HTTP/SMTP behavior, not real recipient delivery. Symptom Inspect Explanation Enqueue succeeded, List is empty Delivery status and dispatcher/worker Intent is not yet a database record Same key returns old content Occurrence identity First persisted content wins; use a new key for a new business occurrence Every request creates another record Empty Key Each original call generates a fresh occurrence Database works but email is unavailable Mailer, renderer and destination All selected channel content must be prepared before persistence Push is skipped Registered target set, version and expiry Enqueue freezes eligible installations; later devices are not added to the same occurrence Push remains awaiting receipt Ticket age and receipt lookup Missing receipts do not justify another send Delivery is failed Attempts, permanent provider classification or uncertain receipt Fix the cause, assess duplication, then retry deliberately Delivery backlog grows Pending age, queue health and worker capacity Durable SQL intent survives Redis outage but still needs execution Alert on increasing notification.pending.age, repeated permanent failures and provider throttling. Use channel/outcome metrics for aggregation and delivery IDs for investigation. Keep payloads, destinations and provider responses out of routine logs. See observability for correlation."},{"Title":"Notifications","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Package responsibilities","Parent":"","URL":"notifications.html#package-responsibilities","Text":"Area Owns notification.go Public content, recipient, channel and record types notifier.go Preparation, immediate sends and record reads delivery.go Persisted delivery lifecycle, dispatch and operator methods store.go SQL behavior and recipient boundaries subscriptions.go, push.go Registration lifecycle, frozen destinations and receipts integration/ CLI, Asynq and migration registration Modules own content and business eligibility. The capability owns delivery state. The underlying mail/push clients own provider calls. App owns construction and shutdown. Keep those roles distinct when adding a channel or custom transport."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"push.html","Text":"pkg/push sends mobile alerts through Expo using the standard HTTP client. It can run without app assembly, PostgreSQL, Redis or a recipient model. The framework notifier composes this same client with stored installations, delivery retries and receipt polling. Expo hands messages to the platform's FCM/APNs infrastructure. This adapter sends OS notifications, not realtime application events, chat messages or browser Web Push. There is no Firebase SDK or native FCM/APNs sender in the Go backend. Start here Use Verify Expo credentials and one destination Standalone example Integrate a mobile client Token registration Send to all eligible devices for a user/admin Durable notifications Diagnose accepted-but-unconfirmed delivery Receipts"},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Prepare Expo and the mobile app","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#prepare-expo-and-the-mobile-app","Text":"Use an Expo development/production build with expo-notifications and configured platform push credentials. Set the EAS project ID explicitly when obtaining an Expo push token. Use a supported physical device or supported emulator/simulator; current requirements and credential setup are in Expo's setup guide. This framework's remote-push walkthrough uses your built application, not Expo Go. Install/configure expo-notifications, rebuild after native configuration changes, and request notification permission before registering a token. The Go backend uses an Expo access token only when your project enables enhanced push security. Never ship that server credential inside the mobile application."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Application configuration","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#application-configuration","Text":"PUSH_ENABLED=true PUSH_EXPO_PROJECT_ID=your-eas-project-id PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=your-expo-enhanced-push-security-token Project ID scopes the persisted registration namespace; Expo routes using the token. Get client tokens using that same EAS project ID. The server cannot establish token ownership or its project from syntax alone. Use authenticated registration endpoints. Enable enhanced push security in Expo and supply its access token in production. The access token is optional for projects which have not enabled that feature. Do not place it in the mobile bundle or commit it to configuration files. These settings are loaded by normal app/worker entrypoints. They construct the shared client but send nothing during startup. The project ID identifies the notification subscription namespace; it is not a recipient, installation or ticket ID."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Try one alert","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#try-one-alert","Text":"First obtain an Expo push token from your configured app. The standalone example reads process environment variables directly; it does not load .env or use APP_ENV_FILE. Supply the values to the shell/process without committing them. PUSH_REGISTRATION='ExpoPushToken[replace-with-your-registration]' \\ go run ./examples/push If enhanced push security is enabled, also export PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN to this process. The example sends a real alert and prints an accepted ticket ID. It requires neither SQL nor Redis. A ticket means Expo accepted work; it does not prove that the device displayed it. After approximately 15 minutes, check that ticket without sending another alert: PUSH_TICKET_ID='replace-with-the-ticket-id' go run ./examples/push The output distinguishes a pending receipt, a handoff confirmation and an error. When PUSH_TICKET_ID is set, the example takes the receipt path even if a registration is also present. A receipt lookup failure is a reason to retry the lookup, not to send another notification."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Send independently","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#send-independently","Text":"This function-body fragment imports os, time and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/push. It assumes ctx, an authorized expoPushToken, and a string exportID: sender, err := push.NewExpo(push.ExpoConfig{ AccessToken: os.Getenv(\"PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN\"), }) if err != nil { return err } expires := time.Now().Add(time.Hour) ticket, err := sender.Send(ctx, expoPushToken, push.Message{ Title: \"Your export is ready\", Body: \"Open the application to download it.\", Data: map[string]string{\"export_id\": exportID}, ExpiresAt: \u0026expires, }) if err != nil { return err } // Persist ticket.ID. Acceptance only means Expo queued this message. Construct once per process and reuse the sender. Calls use their caller's context; do not replace it with a detached background context inside business operations."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Inspect a receipt","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#inspect-a-receipt","Text":"Later, in another request or background job (typically after 15 minutes): receipt, err := sender.Receipt(ctx, ticket.ID) if err != nil { return err // Retry this lookup; do not resend the notification. } if !receipt.Found { return nil // Still pending: schedule another lookup. } if receipt.Error != nil { return receipt.Error // Definitive handoff failure; inspect its classification. } // Expo confirmed handoff to the platform provider, not display or user receipt. Persist the ticket ID if the process can restart before lookup. No background poller runs inside pkg/push; your caller owns that lifecycle. See Expo's send/receipt lifecycle for provider-side behavior. Outcome Meaning Next step Send returns a ticket Expo accepted the request Save the ticket and check later Receipt returns a top-level error Lookup did not complete reliably Retry receipt lookup Found == false Receipt is not available yet Schedule another lookup Found == true, Error == nil Expo confirmed provider handoff Record success; this is not user receipt/read state Found == true, Error != nil Definitive provider handoff failure Inspect classification before retry or disabling the destination The framework notifier supplies this persisted polling lifecycle if you do not want to implement it separately."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Control limits and failures","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#control-limits-and-failures","Text":"The shared push.Sender contract defines both operations once. Ticket acknowledges queue acceptance. Receipt.Found distinguishes pending from success, and Receipt.Error distinguishes a definitive failure from a lookup/network error. *push.Error carries safe codes, permanence, invalid-destination status, and retry timing. Provider bodies and registration values are never included in error messages. NewExpo borrows an optional HTTPClient transport and rejects redirects to avoid forwarding credentials. Each operation has a 30-second deadline, with at most six concurrent requests per shared client. It performs no automatic retries and starts no background workers. Respect Expo's project-level limits across replicas; HTTP 429 and Retry-After are surfaced for backoff. Reuse clients rather than constructing one per message. Configure worker throughput to match your project budget. Only ExpoPushToken[...] and ExponentPushToken[...] registrations are accepted; native FCM/APNs tokens must be replaced by Expo registrations. Content is validated and bounded before I/O. ExpiresAt maps to Expo's expiration timestamp. Never send passwords, login codes, credentials or sensitive content in OS notifications. Field or limit Contract Title Nonblank, at most 200 bytes Body At most 2000 bytes Data String keys and values; resource references, not authoritative state Encoded Message At most 3500 bytes before provider serialization ExpiresAt Optional expiry; elapsed content is rejected/skipped Concurrency At most six HTTP operations per sender instance Operation deadline At most 30 seconds, or a shorter caller deadline Data keys from, message_type, and prefixes google. / gcm. are reserved. The adapter chooses the default notification sound; it currently exposes no sound, badge, Android-channel selector or bulk-send API. Add such options only alongside their provider validation and behavior. Classify errors using errors.As, not provider strings: var failure *push.Error if errors.As(err, \u0026failure) { // failure.InvalidDestination: disable this registration version. // failure.Permanent: correct content/credentials before retrying. // failure.RetryAfter: minimum delay before another eligible attempt. } Import errors. Common safe codes are unregistered, rate_limited, credentials, invalid_argument and unavailable. A network failure during Send can leave acceptance uncertain. The standalone sender deliberately does not choose a retry policy for that operation. See the notifier's delivery lifecycle for the framework's policy."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Durable notifications","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#durable-notifications","Text":"The framework notifier uses the same client. Each installation gets its own delivery row and ticket. After acceptance, status is awaiting_receipt. Workers check after 15 minutes, then every five minutes while a receipt is missing or lookup fails (respecting longer provider Retry-After). Polling does not consume the send-attempt budget. A worker restart resumes lookup of the same persisted ticket. An ok receipt marks the row sent. DeviceNotRegistered disables only the matching registration version. A definitive retryable receipt failure schedules a new send; a receipt API outage does not. After 24 hours without confirmation, the row fails without automatically resending. Operator replay clears the old ticket and may cause a duplicate. A crash after Expo acceptance but before SQL stores its ticket can also cause a duplicate; exactly-once delivery is not claimed. Synchronous Notifier.Send returns Result.PushTicketID; it does not persist or poll that ticket. The caller must check receipts. Prefer Enqueue when this should happen automatically through the existing worker lifecycle. Use notifier.Enqueue with Message.Push after the authenticated installation has been registered. PostgreSQL snapshots each eligible installation and content; only delivery IDs enter Asynq. The dispatcher runs with workers and needs no cron scheduler. Subscription inactivity pruning is a separate centralized schedule. The shipped Identity welcome builder selects database and email; it does not implicitly send a push. Your module selects push explicitly when that message is useful as an OS alert. See selecting channels."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Expo mobile client","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#expo-mobile-client","Text":"After permission and token acquisition, send the Expo registration to the framework's authenticated subscription route. The following helper assumes your application supplies its EAS projectId, a locally persisted installation UUID, API origin and the user's Identity access token: import * as Notifications from 'expo-notifications'; import { Platform } from 'react-native'; export async function registerPush({ projectId, installationID, apiURL, accessToken }) { if (Platform.OS === 'android') { await Notifications.setNotificationChannelAsync('default', { name: 'Default', importance: Notifications.AndroidImportance.DEFAULT, }); } const permission = await Notifications.requestPermissionsAsync(); if (permission.status !== 'granted') return null; const { data: registration } = await Notifications.getExpoPushTokenAsync({ projectId }); const response = await fetch(`${apiURL}/api/v1/notifications/subscriptions`, { method: 'POST', headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json', }, body: JSON.stringify({ installation_id: installationID, registration }), }); if (!response.ok) { throw new Error(`Push registration failed (${response.status})`); } const { data: subscription } = await response.json(); return subscription.id; } apiURL must be reachable from the device; its localhost is not your development computer. Save the returned subscription ID. Retry failed registration through your normal client request policy and refresh it on startup/token rotation. Do not log the registration or access token. Before switching accounts, detach the old subscription: const response = await fetch(`${apiURL}/api/v1/notifications/subscriptions/${subscriptionID}`, { method: 'DELETE', headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}` }, }); if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Push detachment failed (${response.status})`); Use the previous account's valid token. Identity logout also detaches registrations bound to that session. Detachment cannot retract a provider request already in flight. A registration token is a delivery address, not an authentication credential. For foreground presentation, configure Notifications.setNotificationHandler at application startup according to your UX. Subscribe to received/tap events and remove listeners when the owning component unmounts. On a tap, authenticate and fetch current authorized state using the payload's resource ID; the push body must not decide authorization. See Expo's incoming-notification guide. Framework notifications add notification_key and notification_type string data. Use the key to deduplicate client handling. These reserved values override values supplied by a module. Direct pkg/push calls do not add them automatically."},{"Title":"Push","Section":"Communication","Heading":"Test and troubleshoot push","Parent":"","URL":"push.html#test-and-troubleshoot-push","Text":"go test ./pkg/push/... ./internal/platform/notification/... Provider tests use a local fake HTTP transport to exercise deadlines, safe errors and receipt parsing. PostgreSQL-dependent notification tests skip unless DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES is configured; use the integration gate to verify persistence. Automated tests never contact Expo or send a real alert. Symptom Check Token acquisition fails Build credentials, EAS project ID, permission and supported device environment Subscription returns 401 Identity token/session and authentication configuration Subscription returns conflict Installation/registration already active under another recipient; detach it first Send returns credentials Server Expo access token and platform credentials in the Expo project Accepted ticket, no alert Receipt, device notification settings, foreground presentation and app environment Receipt is not found yet Poll later; do not resend solely because it is absent unregistered Disable the matching registration; refresh from the client when it registers again HTTP 429 / rate_limited Respect Retry-After and budget throughput across all replicas Notification delivery is skipped Expired content, no frozen target, detached session or changed registration version Expo/mobile behavior can change independently of this repository. Use the official setup and send/receipt guides for current platform prerequisites. The Go APIs and limits above describe this repository's actual adapter."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html","Text":"Follow one account from email proof to PostgreSQL, then read it through a console command. This uses shipped Identity code. Start with the installation guide, then enable authentication and configure SMTP as described in Identity. The minimal quickstart has no public account-creation route while authentication is disabled."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Make the request","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html#make-the-request","Text":"Request a sign-in code for an address you control: curl -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/otp/send \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"email\":\"workflow@example.com\"}' Expect 202 and save data.challenge_id. Use the code delivered by email: curl -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/auth/otp/verify \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"challenge_id\":\"CHALLENGE_UUID\",\"code\":\"CODE_FROM_EMAIL\",\"platform\":\"web\"}' Expect 201 for a new account or 200 for an existing one. Save data.user.id and the access token. Both responses include data.user.profile_complete; when false, submit the name to authenticated PATCH /api/v1/users/me. GET /users/me exposes the same state on later app launches. Names are optional during sign-in."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Complete the profile","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html#complete-the-profile","Text":"Use the access token returned by sign-in: curl -i -X PATCH http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/users/me \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"name\":\"Ada Lovelace\"}' Expect 200, the updated public user, and profile_complete: true. Read it back: curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1/users/me \\ -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' The handler derives the account ID from the authenticated principal, rather than trusting an ID in the request body. Request mapping supplies the service input; the service/entity still validates the name for non-HTTP callers."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Follow the operation","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html#follow-the-operation","Text":"Step Owner Behavior Assemble internal/modules/identity/register.go Construct services once and share them with transports Route delivery/http/routes.go Connect both OTP operations to the auth handler Decode delivery/http/requests.go Validate bounded JSON and reject unknown fields Decide service/session.go Validate proof and device input, then request atomic sign-in Persist repository/session.go and verification.go Consume proof, create or verify the account and insert its session in one transaction Respond delivery/http/responses.go Return tokens and the public user with profile-completion state Paths after the first row are relative to internal/modules/identity. The shared user entity lives in internal/modules/shared/entities/user.go. Sending a code creates only a temporary challenge. Invalid proof creates no account. Only newly committed accounts publish UserRegistered; returning sign-ins never replace profiles or publish it again. Local publication is best-effort and cannot undo a successful signup."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Read the same account through CLI","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html#read-the-same-account-through-cli","Text":"Use the same configured environment as the app and the ID returned by sign-in. Export the same APP_ENV_FILE in this terminal before running: go run ./cmd/cli identity show '\u003cuser-id\u003e' The command calls the same UserService.Get used by HTTP reads. It receives a constructed service directly; it has no application callback or second repository. Here is its complete source, internal/modules/identity/delivery/cli/commands.go: package cli import ( \"encoding/json\" \"github.com/google/uuid\" \"github.com/spf13/cobra\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/service\" ) func New(users service.UserService) *cobra.Command { group := \u0026cobra.Command{Use: \"identity\", Short: \"Identity commands\"} group.AddCommand(\u0026cobra.Command{ Use: \"show USER_ID\", Short: \"Show an account\", Args: cobra.ExactArgs(1), RunE: func(cmd *cobra.Command, args []string) error { userID, err := uuid.Parse(args[0]) if err != nil { return err } user, err := users.Get(cmd.Context(), userID) if err != nil { return err } return json.NewEncoder(cmd.OutOrStdout()).Encode(user) }, }) return group } CLI execution constructs configured dependencies but does not start HTTP or queue workers. Help also constructs the application. Console access is an operator capability: this command does not borrow an HTTP user's permission checks. An HTTP read requires a bearer session and ownership checks. With authentication disabled, protected routes fail closed; do not remove that middleware to make the quickstart read work. Use the CLI above, or enable passwordless authentication."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Grow your own module","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html#grow-your-own-module","Text":"Begin with the one-file module, then add only the pieces required by a persisted operation: a shared entity, a module migration, a repository and a service when there are business rules. Interfaces belong to their owner; HTTP and CLI consume the same service contract. Construct once in New, register native transports in Register, and keep recurring timing in app assembly. Do not copy Identity's authentication transactions into ordinary operations. Authentication sometimes commits denial state deliberately. Most business changes can join a caller-owned transaction, as shown in SQL and notification intent."},{"Title":"A persisted HTTP and CLI operation","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Verify the behavior","Parent":"","URL":"persistent-workflow.html#verify-the-behavior","Text":"TestModulesPostgresRedisWorkflow exercises real HTTP OTP signup, PostgreSQL persistence, CLI reads, authenticated access and the background reaction. Run the required integration gate with disposable PostgreSQL and Redis; without their settings the integration test skips. Continue with transactional notifications for atomic intent, or account onboarding for optional background work."},{"Title":"SQL and notification intent","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"transactional-notifications.html","Text":"When a profile change must produce a notification, commit both in the same PostgreSQL transaction. Persist the delivery intent during the transaction and let a worker deliver it after commit. This uses existing Identity, transaction and notification APIs; it needs no new framework abstraction. Boundary What happens Service operation Validate and update the profile Same SQL transaction Store one database-channel notification intent Commit Make both changes visible together Worker dispatcher Submit a due delivery ID to Redis Delivery worker Create the recipient's readable notification record This workflow is useful when losing the notification intent would violate the operation's requirements. For optional reactions after a successful commit, compare the onboarding workflow."},{"Title":"SQL and notification intent","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"One complete operation","Parent":"","URL":"transactional-notifications.html#one-complete-operation","Text":"examples/module/profile/update.go is a compiled composition example. It does not install an endpoint or change the shipped profile endpoint. A real module can place this method on its existing service, receiving these collaborators once in its constructor. // Package profile demonstrates a business change with transactional notification intent. package profile import ( \"context\" \"encoding/json\" \"github.com/google/uuid\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/service\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/entities\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/notification\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/database\" ) // Update borrows already constructed collaborators sharing the same database. // The caller must authorize userID before invoking it. No new route is installed. // Success commits the profile and notification intent together unless this call // joins an outer transaction, in which case that caller still owns the commit. func Update(ctx context.Context, transactions *database.TxManager, users service.UserService, notifier *notification.Notifier, userID uuid.UUID, name string) error { return transactions.Execute(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { user, err := users.UpdateProfile(ctx, userID, service.UpdateProfileInput{Name: \u0026name}) if err != nil { return err } _, err = notifier.Enqueue(ctx, notification.Recipient{ Type: string(entities.UserEntityType), ID: user.ID.String(), }, notification.Message{ Type: \"identity.profile_updated\", Database: json.RawMessage(`{\"title\":\"Profile updated\",\"body\":\"Your profile changes were saved.\"}`), }) return err }) } Construct users, notifier and transactions with the same database pool. App already owns that pool and the shared notifier. Pass collaborators into services; keep the complete Resources bundle in module assembly. The caller authorizes the user ID before invoking the operation. UpdateProfile validates the name and locks the account through its repository. It participates in the supplied transaction. Notifier.Enqueue uses that same transaction to persist a JSON database-channel intent. Neither operation contacts Redis or SMTP."},{"Title":"SQL and notification intent","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Commit, rollback and delivery","Parent":"","URL":"transactional-notifications.html#commit-rollback-and-delivery","Text":"Outcome Result Both operations succeed Profile and intent commit together Invalid profile or failed intent insert Neither change commits Enclosing transaction rolls back Both changes roll back, even if this function returned nil Worker is stopped or Redis is unavailable Committed intent waits in PostgreSQL Delivery runs twice for the same intent One database notification is stored Success means the intent is committed, or pending an outer caller's commit. The notification is not readable until delivery has created the database record. The existing notification dispatcher submits due IDs to the worker; with the combined app's workers enabled it runs independently of the cron scheduler. An application that uses the package independently must arrange dispatch and delivery itself. The example intentionally omits Key, so each successful invocation creates a new occurrence. Worker retries reuse its persisted key. Repeating the entire business operation creates another occurrence; use a stable business operation key when that is undesirable, and design the business write's own retry semantics too. Notification deduplication alone does not make an entire use case idempotent."},{"Title":"SQL and notification intent","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Choose the appropriate operation","Parent":"","URL":"transactional-notifications.html#choose-the-appropriate-operation","Text":"Use notifier.Enqueue when intent must commit with business state. The same pattern can persist email or push content; external delivery still happens later. Use mailer.Send or mailer.Enqueue for ordinary email that does not need SQL intent. Both reject a caller SQL transaction, because SMTP and Redis cannot join the PostgreSQL commit. Do not make a local event the only record of required work; local events are best effort. Sensitive authentication operations, including signup, own their commit rules and reject outer transactions. Do not put them inside this example. The profile update is chosen because its contract explicitly supports composition."},{"Title":"SQL and notification intent","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Verify against PostgreSQL","Parent":"","URL":"transactional-notifications.html#verify-against-postgresql","Text":"TestProfileNotificationWorkflowPostgres executes this exact example with the real repositories and migrations. It checks success, outer rollback, a failed notification insert rolling back the earlier profile write, delayed visibility, and replay of the same delivery ID. With DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES pointing to a disposable PostgreSQL database: APP_ENV_FILE='' go test -race ./tests/modules -run TestProfileNotificationWorkflowPostgres -count=1 The test creates and removes an isolated schema. It does not need Redis or a mail provider. The wider integration gate also covers dispatch through real Redis. See notifications for channel selection, occurrence keys, recipient isolation and retry limits."},{"Title":"SQL and notification intent","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Diagnose an unexpected result","Parent":"","URL":"transactional-notifications.html#diagnose-an-unexpected-result","Text":"Observation Meaning Next check Profile returned successfully but notification is not listed Intent may be committed but not delivered Delivery ledger and dispatcher/worker state Outer transaction rolled back Earlier nil return was not a commit guarantee Outer caller's error handling Profile changed but no intent row exists A call lost the transaction context, bypassed the shared executor or ran outside this pattern Transaction context propagation and repository execution Operation returns database.ErrDifferentDatabase A collaborator uses a different pool from the active transaction Construct participating repositories and notifier with the same pool; the executor rejects a foreign pool Repeating the whole request produces another notification This example intentionally omits a stable key Choose a business occurrence key if duplicate requests must converge SMTP/Redis transaction guard rejects an operation An external effect was called inside the transaction Use notifier intent here and deliver after commit Use notification delivery inspection for the next step. Do not retry a committed profile change merely because an optional external channel has not completed."},{"Title":"Account onboarding reactions","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"onboarding.html","Text":"Identity's welcome flow demonstrates an event, a job, notification content and a mail view working together. It runs after account creation and is optional: registration succeeds even if this reaction does not complete. account committed → UserRegistered event (process-local) → welcome task accepted by Redis → worker reloads the account → notification channel intents persisted in PostgreSQL → dispatcher and workers deliver each channel Keep required work in the business transaction. The first event hop is local and can be lost on process failure before Redis accepts the task. Separate event-subscriber processes cannot receive another process's GoChannel events. The combined app hosts HTTP and these local subscribers together."},{"Title":"Account onboarding reactions","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"React to the event","Parent":"","URL":"onboarding.html#react-to-the-event","Text":"An event names a fact; a task requests work. Identity owns both contracts. The event definition lives in internal/modules/shared/events; the task stays in internal/modules/identity/jobs. Identity's register.go registers their handlers alongside HTTP, commands and migrations. No handler is registered in a service or discovered at runtime. This is internal/modules/identity/delivery/events/user_registered.go: // Package events reacts to Identity events using borrowed delivery clients. package events import ( \"encoding/json\" \"errors\" \"github.com/ThreeDotsLabs/watermill/message\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/domain\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/jobs\" sharedevents \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/shared/events\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/asynqx\" ) // UserRegistered queues onboarding work after account creation. GoChannel is local // and best-effort; Redis acceptance cannot recover an event lost before this handler. type UserRegistered struct{ Queue asynqx.Client } func (h UserRegistered) Handle(msg *message.Message) error { var event sharedevents.UserRegistered if err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Payload, \u0026event); err != nil { return errors.New(\"identity: invalid user registration event\") } if h.Queue == nil { return domain.ErrQueueRequired } task, err := jobs.NewWelcome(event.UserID) if err != nil { return err } _, err = h.Queue.EnqueueContext(msg.Context(), task) return err } The handler borrows the existing queue client. It decodes the event, creates the native Asynq task and acknowledges success only after enqueue succeeds. See task definitions and handlers for the compiled task definition and full job handler. The welcome job reloads the account, skips deleted or inactive accounts, and selects eligible channels. Email is omitted when no mailer is provided or the address is unverified. Completing the job means channel intents were saved; it does not mean the email was received."},{"Title":"Account onboarding reactions","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Define the content once","Parent":"","URL":"onboarding.html#define-the-content-once","Text":"This is internal/modules/identity/notifications/welcome.go: // Package notifications defines Identity's named notifications and channel content. package notifications import ( \"encoding/json\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/mailviews\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/notification\" ) // NewWelcome describes one occurrence per account. Its stable key lets duplicate // events, jobs and operator replays converge on the same per-channel intents. // The caller selects eligible channels before enqueueing (for example, omitting // email when the address is unverified or no mailer is provided). func NewWelcome(name string) notification.Message { email := mailviews.NewWelcome(name) return notification.Message{ Key: \"welcome\", Type: \"identity.welcome\", Database: json.RawMessage(`{\"title\":\"Welcome!\",\"body\":\"Your account is ready.\"}`), Email: \u0026email, } } mailviews.NewWelcome returns ordinary mailview.Email content for the shared layout. There is no base notification class, per-message registration or duplicated transport contract. Rendering happens before queued delivery so retries preserve what was accepted. Mail shows that same renderer without notification persistence; choose either entry point according to the operation's needs. The stable welcome key identifies one welcome occurrence per recipient. Duplicate events and operator replays converge on the existing channel intents. A database notification is stored once. External SMTP delivery can still duplicate after an uncertain provider result or crash; a persisted key cannot make SMTP exactly once."},{"Title":"Account onboarding reactions","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Observe the shipped flow","Parent":"","URL":"onboarding.html#observe-the-shipped-flow","Text":"Follow installation to run the combined application, then create a user through OTP or social login. The minimal quickstart disables authentication; configure authentication and SMTP before testing signup. Workers and the notification dispatcher process the committed welcome intents. Use the existing operator commands with the same profile: go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status sent go run ./cmd/cli identity welcome '\u003cuser-id\u003e' The second command enqueues a deliberate welcome replay; it does not bypass the normal worker checks. Inspect queue failures and recovery when work is pending or failed. Use a sandbox SMTP provider when testing email; OTP mail remains synchronous and is never placed in this welcome job."},{"Title":"Account onboarding reactions","Section":"Recipes","Heading":"Verify the complete path","Parent":"","URL":"onboarding.html#verify-the-complete-path","Text":"TestModulesPostgresRedisWorkflow runs real HTTP OTP signup, the local event router, a Redis worker and PostgreSQL notification writes. It also checks replay, recipient access and logout behavior, with captured email instead of an external provider. Run the required integration gate; skipped backend tests do not establish that this workflow works. Use Identity for the detailed account, session and verification contracts. Keep schedules centralized in internal/app/schedules.go when you add a recurring operation."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html","Text":"Test decisions through direct calls, transports through their public boundary, and storage or delivery guarantees against the real backend. A fake tests the caller’s decisions; it cannot establish database or Redis correctness."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Choose the smallest useful check","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#choose-the-smallest-useful-check","Text":"You changed Start here Request schema, response, or middleware go test ./internal/platform/api/... ./docs/api and the owning HTTP package Business decision or repository query go test ./tests/\u003cmodule\u003e; provide the backend for integration cases Module construction or registration go test ./tests/modules ./tests/architecture Task/event behavior The delivery package and tests/queue with Redis Configuration defaults or guides The configuration-reference and documentation checks below These are starting points. Broaden verification when the change crosses a boundary or changes delivery, authorization, persistence, or lifecycle behavior."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Start with focused checks","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#start-with-focused-checks","Text":"go test ./tests/identity ./internal/modules/identity/delivery/http go test ./tests/identity -run 'TestNotification|TestPushRegistration' go test -race ./... go vet ./... External suites skip without their connection settings. Inspect verbose output; a passing default run does not verify PostgreSQL, Redis or MySQL integrations. For the required PostgreSQL/Redis gate, set all four disposable endpoints and run ./scripts/verify-integration.sh: DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES, QUEUE_TEST_REDIS, RATE_LIMIT_TEST_REDIS and CACHE_TEST_REDIS. The script refuses missing values and runs the full suite with race detection and no cached results. The CI service setup in .github/workflows/verify.yml is a reproducible reference. Optional MySQL, Redis Cluster and live provider behavior need separate evidence."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Choose the boundary","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#choose-the-boundary","Text":"Behavior Approach Current reference Business decisions Direct calls and small fakes of owner contracts tests/identity/service_test.go HTTP validation and authorization Fresh Huma server and httptest internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/user_handler_test.go Task validation and retry classification Native task and mux calls internal/modules/identity/delivery/jobs/welcome_test.go Event fanout and cancellation Fresh GoChannel/router with owned shutdown internal/platform/messaging tests Queue persistence and native options Disposable Redis tests/queue/queue_test.go Queries and concurrent constraints Real PostgreSQL tests/identity repository integration tests Application wiring, HTTP and console execution Public entry points with disposable backends tests/app Cross-module transactions and delivery Explicit module registration with PostgreSQL/Redis tests/modules Startup, partial failure and drain Explicit signals and bounded waits internal/platform/lifecycle/lifecycle_test.go Service and repository packages contain production code only. Their tests import public constructors and contracts from tests/\u003cmodule\u003e; avoid private test hooks."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Keep tests useful","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#keep-tests-useful","Text":"Assert outcomes and invariants, including failure paths. A service test should show a decision, not duplicate every internal call. Use deterministic coordination for concurrent work: channels, cancellation and completion signals rather than sleeping and hoping work finished. Every test-created resource needs cleanup. Check malformed input, unauthorized access, duplicate delivery, rollback and cancellation where relevant. Run race tests, but remember that they check only executed schedules. Do not chase coverage percentages or add interfaces solely to make every constructor mockable."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Find the next guide","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#find-the-next-guide","Text":"HTTP and module tests: decode, authorize and render. Task and event tests: native handlers and delivery boundaries. Database tests: real engine fixtures and transactions."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Verify documentation and public examples","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#verify-documentation-and-public-examples","Text":"go test docs/build.go docs/highlight_test.go go test ./tests/modules ./tests/architecture ./docs/api go test ./internal/app/config -run TestConfigurationReferenceMatchesDefaults go run docs/build.go The builder checks local published links, anchors, and assets; the Go suite protects selected copyable examples and configuration defaults. It does not compile every arbitrary Markdown fragment. When adding a substantial complete example, compile it against current APIs or back it with an existing checked source file. Regenerate the site after changing its Markdown sources; do not hand-edit generated HTML. CI rebuilds documentation and expects the checked-in generated artifacts to match. The live HTTP specification has no checked-in generation step: test it with go test ./docs/api."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Run the required integration gate locally","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#run-the-required-integration-gate-locally","Text":"Use disposable PostgreSQL and Redis. The following creates dedicated local test containers; use different names or ports if those are already occupied: docker run --rm -d --name go-modular-test-postgres \\ -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=test-only -p 15432:5432 postgres:17-alpine docker run --rm -d --name go-modular-test-redis \\ -p 16379:6379 redis:7-alpine Wait until both readiness commands succeed: docker exec go-modular-test-postgres pg_isready -U postgres docker exec go-modular-test-redis redis-cli ping Then configure all four test endpoints in that terminal: export DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES='postgres://postgres:test-only@127.0.0.1:15432/postgres?sslmode=disable' export QUEUE_TEST_REDIS='redis://127.0.0.1:16379/5' export RATE_LIMIT_TEST_REDIS='redis://127.0.0.1:16379/2' export CACHE_TEST_REDIS='redis://127.0.0.1:16379/4' ./scripts/verify-integration.sh The script disables dotenv loading and runs go test -race -count=1 ./.... Missing settings fail before the suite starts. Optional MySQL, Redis Cluster, and live provider tests still need their own setup; a core pass does not certify them. When finished, remove only these disposable containers: docker stop go-modular-test-postgres go-modular-test-redis Their --rm option removes the containers. This procedure intentionally supplies no persistent data volume. Do not reuse an application database or Redis instance for convenience."},{"Title":"Testing basics","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Read the result honestly","Parent":"","URL":"testing.html#read-the-result-honestly","Text":"Result What it establishes Unit test passed The exercised behavior under that fixture Integration test skipped No evidence for that backend from this run Race suite passed No race detected in executed schedules Build passed Types and linking are valid; runtime dependencies were not necessarily exercised Provider mock passed Caller behavior, not live delivery or provider compatibility Record the command, toolchain, backend versions, enabled test endpoints, and skipped suites for release evidence. Rerun relevant checks after changing the tested source. Use disposable infrastructure and isolated schemas/namespaces. Never point test settings at application or production data. Dependency/security changes also need govulncheck ./... with the project toolchain; review reachable findings."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html","Text":"Send requests through a fresh platform API and inspect status, headers, and body. Most HTTP tests need neither a listening socket nor full application construction. This exercises Huma binding, shared policies, and response serialization together."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Test decoding and rendering together","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html#test-decoding-and-rendering-together","Text":"Save this complete test in tests/catalog/http_example_test.go. Tests inside this repository may import internal/platform/api; they do not need to live inside the internal package itself. package catalog_test import ( \"context\" \"encoding/json\" \"net/http\" \"net/http/httptest\" \"strings\" \"testing\" \"github.com/danielgtaylor/huma/v2\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/api/response\" ) func TestNameEndpoint(t *testing.T) { for _, tc := range []struct { name string body string status int called bool }{ {\"valid\", `{\"name\":\"Ada\"}`, 200, true}, {\"missing\", `{}`, 422, false}, {\"unknown field\", `{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"role\":\"admin\"}`, 422, false}, {\"duplicate\", `{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"Name\":\"Other\"}`, 400, false}, {\"malformed\", `{`, 400, false}, } { t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) { server, err := api.New(api.Options{}) if err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } type nameBody struct { Name string `json:\"name\" minLength:\"1\" maxLength:\"100\"` } called := false huma.Register(server, huma.Operation{ OperationID: \"test.name\", Method: \"POST\", Path: \"/name\", MaxBodyBytes: 1024, }, func(ctx context.Context, in *struct{ Body nameBody }) (*response.JSON[nameBody], error) { called = true return response.OK(in.Body), nil }) req := httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodPost, \"/name\", strings.NewReader(tc.body)) req.Header.Set(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\") result := httptest.NewRecorder() server.ServeHTTP(result, req) if result.Code != tc.status || called != tc.called { t.Fatalf(\"status=%d called=%v body=%s\", result.Code, called, result.Body.String()) } var body struct { Type string `json:\"type\"` Data nameBody `json:\"data\"` } if err := json.Unmarshal(result.Body.Bytes(), \u0026body); err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } if result.Header().Get(\"X-Request-ID\") == \"\" || result.Header().Get(\"X-API-Version\") != \"v1\" { t.Fatalf(\"missing response headers: %v\", result.Header()) } if tc.called \u0026\u0026 body.Data.Name != \"Ada\" { t.Fatalf(\"data=%+v\", body.Data) } if tc.status == 422 \u0026\u0026 body.Type != \"urn:go-modular:error:validation_failed\" { t.Fatalf(\"problem type=%q\", body.Type) } }) } } Run go test ./tests/catalog after adding the example. It verifies that invalid input never reaches the operation, successful results use data, and both paths have correlation headers. It does not establish persistence or authorization."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Test a real module operation","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html#test-a-real-module-operation","Text":"Use the existing owner service contract for a small fake, then register the actual module transport. Identity's internal/modules/identity/delivery/http/user_handler_test.go shows this pattern. Do not define a second production contract solely for a test. Scenario Useful assertion Valid request Expected service input and selected public response fields Invalid body/path/query Service was never invoked Application field errors Every safe message appears at the intended location Operational failure Generic public problem; no raw provider/driver detail Profile update Omitted/null/empty name follows the current service policy Pagination Defaults, bounds, ambiguous query rejection, and stable page shape Test a policy, not every internal method call. A fake can confirm that the handler passes the authenticated user ID; it cannot prove the repository enforces a concurrent database constraint."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Verify authentication and ownership","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html#verify-authentication-and-ownership","Text":"Protected route tests should cover: No Authorization header, multiple headers, malformed bearer credentials. Valid credentials and an active principal. Operational authenticator failure, which must not become anonymous access. Reading the current account versus another account's resource. A protected group with no authenticator, which must fail closed. Authentication and authorization are different assertions. A UUID that parses or a token that verifies does not grant permission to every resource. Use the same api.Authenticated grouping that production registration uses."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Verify multipart and streaming behavior","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html#verify-multipart-and-streaming-behavior","Text":"For uploads, test the whole request byte limit, permitted parts, authorization before parsing, and cleanup of temporary files after success and failure. For streaming, check opening failures before commitment separately from copy failures after bytes have been written. httptest.ResponseRecorder is useful for ordinary responses. Use a real local httptest.Server when the behavior requires a network connection, disconnect, flush, or transport abort. The platform's streaming and shutdown tests are the reference; a recorder alone cannot establish client-visible truncation behavior."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Check the live OpenAPI contract","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html#check-the-live-openapi-contract","Text":"go test ./docs/api ./internal/platform/api/... These tests inspect the running document and response schemas. When adding a conditional status or binary response, assert that its documented media type, status, required fields, and security match the runtime handler. Rebuilding Go routes updates the live document; there is no generated Swagger file to edit."},{"Title":"HTTP and modules","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Test full application wiring","Parent":"","URL":"testing-http.html#test-full-application-wiring","Text":"go test -race ./internal/modules/identity/delivery/http ./internal/platform/api/... go test ./tests/modules ./tests/app tests/modules includes the database-free Catalog starter and compiled workflows. Some module/app tests need disposable backends and skip without their settings; use the required integration gate for full core coverage. Register fresh APIs for each test. Finish registration before serving. For tests that start listeners or runners, use readiness/completion signals and bounded contexts instead of arbitrary sleeps, then join users before closing dependencies. Continue with database tests, task and event tests, and responses."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html","Text":"Run database tests against the engine used in production. SQLite can check basic transaction flow, but it cannot establish PostgreSQL or MySQL locking, SQL dialect, constraint, or recovery behavior."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Use an isolated database","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#use-an-isolated-database","Text":"internal/platform/testdb.Open(t) selects PostgreSQL by default, or MySQL when DATABASE_TEST_DRIVER=mysql. It reads the matching DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES or DATABASE_TEST_MYSQL DSN and skips when that variable is absent. The helper creates a unique PostgreSQL schema or MySQL database, applies the cache migration templates with Goose, and registers cleanup. It does not apply your module's application migrations. Add those through the migration registry/provider when testing application queries, or create a small explicit test table for a transaction contract as below. Use only disposable database credentials. The helper creates and drops schemas or databases, so the test account needs those permissions. Application accounts do not need the same privileges. Isolation is within the selected database server; it does not isolate server-wide configuration or resource limits."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Apply the schema your test needs","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#apply-the-schema-your-test-needs","Text":"testdb.Open installs cache fixture tables, not all business tables. For an Identity, Media, or Notification repository test, register the matching embedded migration sources and apply their combined history in the isolated schema. tests/modules/profile_test.go and the existing Identity repository tests show complete fixtures for those owners. Avoid creating an approximate table in a repository test when its real constraints are part of the behavior. Small explicit tables are useful for generic transaction manager tests, such as the rollback example below."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Verify a rollback","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#verify-a-rollback","Text":"Place this complete example in tests/identity/rollback_example_test.go, outside the service and repository packages. The internal fixture is for tests, not an application dependency. package identity_test import ( \"context\" \"errors\" \"testing\" \"time\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/platform/testdb\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/database\" ) func TestRollbackExample(t *testing.T) { db := testdb.Open(t) ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(t.Context(), 10*time.Second) defer cancel() if _, err := db.ExecContext(ctx, \"CREATE TABLE test_entries (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY)\"); err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } manager, err := database.NewTxManager(db, nil) if err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } rejected := errors.New(\"operation rejected\") err = manager.Execute(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error { executor, err := database.Executor(ctx, db) if err != nil { return err } if _, err := executor.ExecContext(ctx, \"INSERT INTO test_entries (id) VALUES (?)\", 1); err != nil { return err } return rejected }) if !errors.Is(err, rejected) { t.Fatalf(\"error=%v\", err) } var count int if err := db.QueryRowContext(ctx, \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM test_entries\").Scan(\u0026count); err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } if count != 0 { t.Fatalf(\"rollback left %d rows\", count) } } The insert uses database.Executor(ctx, db), so it participates in the managed transaction. The final query uses the pool after the transaction returns. Calling the raw pool inside the callback would bypass the transaction."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Run PostgreSQL and MySQL suites","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#run-postgresql-and-mysql-suites","Text":"Set DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES or DATABASE_TEST_MYSQL to a disposable database DSN, then run: DATABASE_TEST_DRIVER=postgres go test -race -count=1 ./pkg/database ./pkg/database/migrate ./internal/platform/testdb DATABASE_TEST_DRIVER=mysql go test -race -count=1 ./pkg/database ./pkg/database/migrate ./internal/platform/testdb Add your module's package path to run its integration tests too. Both DSNs may be set: engine-specific tests use their own DSN; DATABASE_TEST_DRIVER selects the backend for shared transaction fixtures. Without backend settings, some database tests run on SQLite and the external suites skip. Inspect verbose output rather than treating that run as PostgreSQL or MySQL verification."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Cover transaction boundaries","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#cover-transaction-boundaries","Text":"Case Assert Successful operation Changes are visible after commit Callback failure No participating writes remain Nested operation fails The outer transaction cannot commit even if the inner error is swallowed Context cancellation No later business work proceeds; rollback is attempted Different pool in a transaction The operation is rejected Concurrent uniqueness conflict The database constraint protects the invariant SQL plus notification intent Verify both commit/rollback together; tests/modules/profile_test.go exercises the existing PostgreSQL ledger SQL plus arbitrary Redis/GoChannel publication No atomic guarantee is supplied; design and verify an explicit outbox if required The existing database tests also cover savepoints and transaction ownership. Do not share a managed transaction context concurrently between goroutines. For concurrency tests, coordinate independent transactions and assert the final stored state; sequential check-then-insert calls do not reproduce a race."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Migrations and cleanup","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#migrations-and-cleanup","Text":"Use a fresh schema to test migration application and the resulting constraints. For upgrade tests, apply the earlier schema, insert representative data, then apply the new migration. A fresh install alone does not test an upgrade. Let each fixture own its resources and clean them up after users stop. Do not truncate shared development tables. Do not wrap every test in a rollback transaction when the behavior under test must commit, publish, or use another connection. See Transactions and Migrations for the production APIs and ownership rules."},{"Title":"Databases and transactions","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Diagnose a skipped or failing integration test","Parent":"","URL":"testing-database.html#diagnose-a-skipped-or-failing-integration-test","Text":"Symptom Check Test says SKIP Matching test DSN is absent; run with -v to see the reason Permission denied creating schema/database Use a disposable test account with fixture privileges A module table is missing Register and apply that module's actual migrations Concurrent uniqueness test passes only sequentially Coordinate separate transactions to overlap intentionally Tests pass on SQLite but fail on PostgreSQL SQL, constraints, isolation, and locking differ; the production engine is authoritative State leaks between tests Use isolated fixtures and wait for goroutines before cleanup Keep query deadlines bounded, including failure paths. A database error is not always proof that no write committed; tests for retries should assert final durable state and idempotency. See integration setup."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html","Text":"Separate the decision to publish from actual delivery. Native task and handler unit tests run locally; retries, scheduling and Redis state need a real backend. Local event tests establish only process-local fanout. There is no durable local event dead-letter or replay store."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Test task construction and handlers","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#test-task-construction-and-handlers","Text":"Construct a task directly and inspect its native type and payload. Call its handler with a bounded context and check business invocation or asynq.SkipRetry for invalid input. Do not expect worker metadata or a ResultWriter on a newly constructed task. go test -race ./internal/modules/identity/delivery/events ./internal/modules/identity/delivery/jobs go test ./internal/modules/identity/mailviews ./internal/modules/identity/notifications Identity's job tests check current-account lookup, inactive-account skipping, verified-address channel selection and retryable persistence failures. The event handler tests reject malformed JSON or missing IDs and check enqueue failures. Mailview tests render through the default layout and check escaping and plain text. tests/modules/workflow_test.go uses real PostgreSQL and Redis to follow signup through the event, welcome job, delivery ledger and channel worker. It captures mail instead of contacting SMTP and checks both JSON database notifications and rendered email delivery. Set DATABASE_TEST_POSTGRES, QUEUE_TEST_REDIS and RATE_LIMIT_TEST_REDIS to run that suite without skips. Test retries through a real worker instead of emulating Asynq internals."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Reject malformed tasks without infrastructure","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#reject-malformed-tasks-without-infrastructure","Text":"This complete test can live in tests/identity/task_example_test.go. The malformed payload must be rejected before the handler accesses its dependencies: package identity_test import ( \"errors\" \"testing\" \"github.com/hibiken/asynq\" jobhandlers \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/delivery/jobs\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/internal/modules/identity/jobs\" ) func TestMalformedWelcomeTask(t *testing.T) { handler := jobhandlers.Welcome{} task := asynq.NewTask(jobs.WelcomeType, []byte(`{\"user_id\":\"invalid\"}`)) if err := handler.Handle(t.Context(), task); !errors.Is(err, asynq.SkipRetry) { t.Fatalf(\"expected a terminal payload error, got %v\", err) } } That test establishes validation and retry classification. It does not prove task persistence, worker acknowledgement, or provider delivery. Use real dependencies for those guarantees."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Test event fanout","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#test-event-fanout","Text":"This complete test can live in internal/platform/messaging/docs_example_test.go: package messaging_test import ( \"context\" \"testing\" \"time\" \"github.com/ThreeDotsLabs/watermill\" \"github.com/ThreeDotsLabs/watermill/message\" \"github.com/ThreeDotsLabs/watermill/pubsub/gochannel\" ) func TestIndependentSubscribers(t *testing.T) { ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(t.Context(), 2*time.Second) defer cancel() bus := gochannel.NewGoChannel(gochannel.Config{OutputChannelBuffer: 1}, watermill.NopLogger{}) defer bus.Close() first, err := bus.Subscribe(ctx, \"example.created\") if err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } second, err := bus.Subscribe(ctx, \"example.created\") if err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } sent := message.NewMessage(\"event-1\", []byte(`{\"id\":\"1\"}`)) if err := bus.Publish(\"example.created\", sent); err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } for _, subscription := range []\u003c-chan *message.Message{first, second} { select { case received, ok := \u003c-subscription: if !ok || received.UUID != sent.UUID { t.Fatal(\"missing expected delivery\") } received.Ack() case \u003c-ctx.Done(): t.Fatal(ctx.Err()) } } } This uses raw subscriptions, so the test acknowledges deliveries. Normal app handlers return an error or nil and let the router acknowledge. Subscribe before publishing; a bounded channel wait verifies progress without arbitrary sleeps."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Test real drivers","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#test-real-drivers","Text":"Start disposable Redis on a dedicated test port in another terminal: docker run --rm --name go-modular-queue-test -p 16379:6379 redis:7-alpine Run the existing suites: QUEUE_TEST_REDIS=redis://127.0.0.1:16379/1 go test -race -count=1 -v ./tests/app ./tests/queue ./pkg/asynqx/... ./internal/platform/cli Stop that container with Ctrl+C afterward. Use a disposable Redis instance, not production credentials. These suites use isolated task/queue identities and clean their work; missing QUEUE_TEST_REDIS causes explicit skips."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Cover the delivery contract","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#cover-the-delivery-contract","Text":"Scenario Verify Enqueue and execute Intended handler receives the native task Transient failure Retry policy and eventual result Invalid payload No business effect; terminal task state Result retention Stored bytes and task state match Cancellation Handler observes cancellation; work actually exits Shutdown Worker joins before borrowed clients close Scheduling Registration, queue routing and due publication Trace propagation Producer link without losing consumer cancellation A local fake cannot establish Redis recovery or crash durability. Likewise, one successful scheduled run does not establish multi-scheduler coordination. The app requires one scheduler per schedule set. Run database tests separately for SQL consistency and cache storage. Neither backend test makes SQL and Redis publication atomic."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Verify the notification ledger separately","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#verify-the-notification-ledger-separately","Text":"An Asynq task can finish after persisting notification intent while channel delivery is still pending. Assert the layer you intend to verify: Boundary Evidence Welcome handler Expected channel intents and stable occurrence key Transactional notification Business change and intents commit or roll back together Dispatcher Due committed intent is submitted with its delivery ID Channel worker Leases, retries, and terminal/receipt state follow the provider outcome SMTP/Expo integration Captured request/content under a fake; live provider behavior requires separate evidence Tests should cover duplicate task execution and an omitted versus stable notification key. Use fixture mail/push senders so ordinary automated tests do not contact real recipients. A provider's accepted response is not proof of inbox or device display."},{"Title":"Tasks and events","Section":"Testing","Heading":"Verify local event limits and exhaustion","Parent":"","URL":"testing-async.html#verify-local-event-limits-and-exhaustion","Text":"The app uses messaging.LocalBus with its DeliveryMiddleware outside retry middleware. Test capacity rejection, bounded handler concurrency, cancellation and one terminal failure after the configured retry cycle. Failed cycles are logged and settled; admission capacity must become available again. A native GoChannel fixture without this adapter can still redeliver Nacked messages, so it does not verify the app's exhaustion policy. For reactions requiring restart recovery, use and test a deliberate durable-intent workflow. Local publication remains best-effort. See events."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html","Text":"Tilt, Compose and release verification share deployments/Dockerfile. For release bundles, fixed build inputs and recorded operational checks, see release readiness. Development manifests and sample credentials remain development-only; they are not a production deployment preset. Run go run ./cmd/app for HTTP, Asynq workers and local event subscribers. Run go run ./cmd/scheduler separately for recurring schedules. Run go run ./cmd/cli migrate up explicitly before serving. The supplied Compose migration service and Tilt init containers do this before starting application processes. Both deployments run the scheduler separately with one replica."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Choose the process shape","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#choose-the-process-shape","Text":"Process Runs Use it for cmd/app HTTP, workers and local event consumers Application service deployment cmd/worker Queue workers and local subscribers; no cron scheduler Dedicated background execution cmd/scheduler Central recurring schedules One deployment per schedule set cmd/cli \u003ccommand\u003e One command after application construction Migration, inspection, maintenance cmd/cli serve Explicit HTTP and workers selection in main Serving through the command tree HTTP producers need their local subscribers in the same process. A different worker process cannot receive another process's GoChannel events. Run exactly one scheduler for a schedule set; ordinary workers and the notification dispatcher do not require cron scheduling. See lifecycle."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Ingress and readiness","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#ingress-and-readiness","Text":"Set HTTP_READINESS_DEPENDENCIES=true in deployed applications. The supplied Compose environment, Kubernetes profile and full .env.example enable it explicitly. /readyz then checks configured database and Redis dependencies; /healthz remains independent of downstream availability. Keep readiness timeouts below the probe timeout and use readiness, not liveness, to remove an unhealthy instance from service. For direct connections, leave HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES empty. Behind a controlled ingress, set its actual source CIDRs and restrict backend access accordingly: # Documentation example only: replace with the actual ingress addresses. HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES=192.0.2.10/32,2001:db8::10/128 The first ingress strips client-supplied X-Forwarded-For and writes the observed client address. Every additional trusted proxy appends its observed peer. Verify the complete path, including alternate maintenance access, before enabling trust. The application uses the first untrusted address before that trusted suffix for client rate limits. With no trust configured, clients behind a proxy share its budget. api.PeerIP remains the transport peer; api.ClientIP exposes the accepted client address. Neither value establishes an authenticated principal. Both administrative dashboards require HTTPS and a preserved external Host. Business CORS never grants access to /admin; cross-origin reads, preflights and mutations are rejected even when the browser has cached valid Basic Auth."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Retention for version 0.1","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#retention-for-version-01","Text":"Version 0.1 retains notification delivery records and media tombstones until the application owner performs an explicit data-retention migration. There is no automatic age-based purge. Delivery records retain occurrence-key deduplication; media tombstones retain repeated-delete behavior. Subscription pruning disables stale destinations without deleting delivery history. File logs keep their separately configured rotation policy, and the viewer's live buffer is bounded. Monitor retained table size and include these records in backup/restore tests. Before introducing a finite retention window, define the longest supported business retry/replay window and privacy requirements. Preserve deduplication keys and necessary tombstone identifiers for that window, and scrub sensitive payloads separately only after terminal delivery and receipt processing. A purge must exclude pending/in-flight work and document how older replays and repeated deletes behave. Account-erasure requirements need an owner-specific migration; they are not satisfied by the generic maintenance schedule."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Build identity","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#build-identity","Text":"Source runs report dev; OpenAPI uses the same application version as the CLI and startup logs. go run ./cmd/cli --version also reports the VCS revision when Go records it. CLI version inspection follows normal application construction. Release images receive VERSION and REVISION build arguments from scripts/build-release.sh; build from a clean, tagged commit. The API contract header stays X-API-Version: v1, independently of the application release."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Environment loading","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#environment-loading","Text":".env.example is the full local feature profile. Copy it to the ignored .env and fill its credential fields before running the app. app-config.yaml is the corresponding Kubernetes profile, with cluster endpoints and no private credentials. Both use Go defaults for routine tuning; see the complete configuration reference. Tilt loads the ignored .env.mail.yaml as the app-mail Secret. This existing file holds local application credentials, including settings added beyond SMTP: apiVersion: v1 kind: Secret metadata: name: app-mail namespace: go-modular-dev type: Opaque stringData: MAIL_HOST: sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io MAIL_PORT: \"2525\" MAIL_USERNAME: \"\" MAIL_PASSWORD: \"\" AUTH_VERIFICATION_KEY: \"\" AUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN_KEY: \"\" AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID: \"\" QUEUE_DASHBOARD_USERNAME: operator QUEUE_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD: \"\" PUSH_EXPO_PROJECT_ID: \"\" PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN: \"\" Fill the blank values with your own credentials. Generate each Identity key independently with openssl rand -base64 32, and retain the same keys across replicas and restarts. Feature switches belong in app-config.yaml, so the Secret does not silently override them. The checked-in app-secrets contains only the development database/Redis connections and MinIO fixtures. Keep real credentials out of tracked files. CLI help and migrations also validate the full profile. Jobs use Asynq and Redis through the native Asynq integration. Local events use Watermill GoChannel. See the queue guide for configuration, registration, startup, delivery guarantees, retries and observability."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Optional cache","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#optional-cache","Text":"CACHE_DRIVER defaults to disabled. Select memory, database or redis independently of the queue and pubsub drivers. Database cache shares the configured Bun connection and uses its own central cache_entries migration. CACHE_REDIS_URL is provided through the development app Secret; CACHE_NAMESPACE and limits use Go defaults unless overridden. Defaults and operational details are in configuration. Memory cache uses CACHE_MEMORY_MAX_BYTES=67108864 (64 MiB) by default in addition to entry count and per-entry limits. This replaces the previous effective budget of entry count multiplied by entry size. Raise the byte budget deliberately for existing larger caches and leave process/container headroom for metadata and in-flight copies. Full caches reject writes rather than evict live entries."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Working directory for binary and IDE launches","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#working-directory-for-binary-and-ide-launches","Text":"Use the application root as the working directory, regardless of where the binary lives. Logging fixes relative file paths against that directory at construction; there is no go.mod discovery or executable-path fallback. For an installed binary, run cd /opt/myapp followed by ./bin/app. A systemd unit makes the same contract explicit: [Service] WorkingDirectory=/opt/myapp ExecStart=/opt/myapp/bin/app In an IDE, set its launch working directory to the repository root. For VS Code's Go launch configuration, use \"cwd\": \"${workspaceFolder}\". Tilt uses the Docker image's /app directory. Use stdout/stderr or direct OTLP for read-only container deployments; file logging requires writable /app/storage/logs, and local file storage requires writable /app/storage/app (or FILESYSTEM_LOCAL_ROOT)."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Persistent application storage","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#persistent-application-storage","Text":"Use one Docker Desktop cluster with the kubeadm provisioner. In Docker Desktop, open Kubernetes → Edit cluster → Kubeadm → Save changes. Switching provisioners resets all Kubernetes workloads and volume data, including other projects. Do this only when you intend to discard that data. Keep the checkout available in Docker Desktop's Resources → File Sharing settings. tilt up --context docker-desktop tail -f \"storage/logs/app-$(date -u +%F).log\" Tilt creates storage/logs and storage/app, resolves the absolute storage path, and mounts it at /app/storage in both the app and migration containers. Writes go to the same local daily file, app-YYYY-MM-DD.log in UTC; no copying service or polling interval is involved. Rerun the tail command after the UTC date changes. Filesystem buffering and editor reload behavior can affect when a reader displays new records. Tilt uses your host UID/GID so the app and your editor can both write the files. The container root filesystem remains read-only; the shared storage directory contains logs and local uploaded objects. Each project uses its own checkout folder and namespace on the same cluster. Database and broker data still use their own PVCs. Local logs and uploaded objects survive Pod and cluster deletion; deleting the local files removes them. The storage mount has no quota. The application file channel rotates daily and retains 14 days by default; that limits age, not total disk usage. Runtime logs and default local uploads are ignored by Git and Docker builds. This is a local development setup; production should use deployment-owned persistent storage and a deliberate logging policy such as standard output or direct OTLP. Apply the app through Tilt: it replaces the manifest's placeholder host path and default container identity. Docker Desktop's Kind provisioner currently does not expose the required Mac host paths. A missing host folder fails the mount instead of silently creating an unrelated node directory. No separate cluster or NFS server is needed with kubeadm. Re-run Tilt if you move the checkout."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Run the application with Docker Compose","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#run-the-application-with-docker-compose","Text":"The single deployments/compose.yaml contains the app, migrations, infrastructure, and persistent volumes. Run the complete stack: docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml up --build -d Use this instead of Tilt or the host app because their default ports overlap. The app waits for PostgreSQL migrations and Redis health. With the optional storage profile it also waits for MinIO bucket setup. All application containers share the named app-storage volume mounted at /app/storage, use /app as their working directory, and log to stderr and file. Fresh volumes inherit the image's UID/GID 10001 ownership. Existing volumes must already permit that user to write; the image does not recursively change existing data ownership at startup. The named volume is stored by Docker and survives container replacement and ordinary down. It is not your local project's storage directory. Export a snapshot: mkdir -p storage/logs docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml cp api:/app/storage/logs/. storage/logs/ To stop while retaining volumes: docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml down Adding --volumes destroys these volumes, including database data. These are local development manifests with sample credentials; production needs deployment-specific credentials, storage backups, and log retention. Rotation belongs to the configured logging channel; adding a volume alone does not configure it."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Optional observability stack","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#optional-observability-stack","Text":"Tilt starts the stack by default with tilt up. Disable it with tilt up -- --observability=false; this also disables app tracing and metrics and the direct OTLP observability log channel. Alloy receives logs and spans over HTTP; it does not mount or collect application log files. Existing explicit Tilt arguments still take precedence. Compose continues to use its observability environment profile. Both share backend configs and provision Grafana at localhost:3000. Tilt also forwards Alloy at localhost:4318 for a host application's trace and log exporters. See setup and operational limits."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Authentication and API rate limits","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#authentication-and-api-rate-limits","Text":"Identity uses Redis admission limits whenever AUTH_ENABLED is enabled. Set RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL (default redis://127.0.0.1:6379/2) and RATE_LIMIT_NAMESPACE consistently across replicas. This is separate from cache and queue databases. Redis unavailability returns 503 instead of bypassing limits. API_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true adds broad admission to /api/v1; common health routes are exempt. Defaults: API_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=120, API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=120, API_RATE_LIMIT_PERIOD=1m. Authentication applies stricter service-level policies. Budgets replenish gradually and survive application restarts while Redis retains them. Do not flush limiter keys with cache data; budget loss resets abuse protection. Identity uses a pre-release PostgreSQL baseline. Older development schemas may need a deliberate forward migration or a disposable reset; startup never rewrites them. Do not reset a database containing required data. Clients use direct JSON Google ID-token exchange. See Identity behavior and rollout for details. The full development profiles enable HTTP_READINESS_DEPENDENCIES=true. Code defaults and the quickstart keep it disabled. HTTP_READINESS_TIMEOUT=1s is the shared deadline for configured database, queue Redis and rate-limit Redis checks. Set the deployment readiness probe timeout above this budget. /healthz never probes dependencies. Cache, mail and telemetry backends do not gate readiness. A shared dependency outage may remove every replica from traffic; enable this policy only when that behavior matches your deployment."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Experimental brokers","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#experimental-brokers","Text":"RabbitMQ and NATS are not used by the current app. Compose starts them only with --profile extra-brokers; Tilt starts them only with -- --extra-brokers=true. They are not app startup prerequisites. These switches provide infrastructure for experiments, not supported application messaging drivers. Existing volumes remain unmodified when the profile is disabled."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Optional object storage","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#optional-object-storage","Text":"Tilt enables MinIO and its disk by default; use -- --storage=false to omit both. For Compose, enable the storage profile and disk together: FILESYSTEM_DISK=minio docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml --profile storage up --build -d Compose waits for bucket initialization before starting the API when the storage profile is selected. Tilt includes the MinIO resource and bucket initialization unless the storage option is explicitly false. The full application configuration, including provider credentials, must be valid before its migration command runs. Compose respects an explicitly configured FILESYSTEM_DISK, including values in an existing .env. For a minimal stack regardless of that file, run: FILESYSTEM_DISK=disabled docker compose -f deployments/compose.yaml up --build -d Disabling storage does not delete existing objects or volumes. These commands configure local infrastructure; they do not add upload processing to Media."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Optional push","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#optional-push","Text":"Push uses Expo Push Service externally; no local push container is required. Supply PUSH_EXPO_PROJECT_ID and PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN (when enhanced push security is enabled). Compose forwards these settings to application processes; no Firebase service-account credential mount is needed. Worker processes dispatch notification intents every second without requiring the cron scheduler. The development notification baseline schema now includes subscriptions and new channel/destination fields. Existing databases created from the previous baseline need an explicit schema migration or a deliberate disposable-development reset; application startup does not rewrite or reset existing tables."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Recovery and retained state","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#recovery-and-retained-state","Text":"Deployment backup policy must cover each source of retained state: State Recovery requirement PostgreSQL Preserve accounts, sessions, notification intent/read state, media metadata and Goose history Queue Redis Configure persistence and backup for the accepted task backlog; ordinary jobs and queued mail have no SQL outbox Rate-limit Redis Preserve budgets separately from cache and queue administration; resetting them resets abuse controls Object storage or local asset volume Restore compatible object versions alongside media metadata; metadata alone cannot restore bytes Secrets and configuration Restore the matching keys, stable disk names and connection settings through protected deployment configuration Cache and telemetry Treat cache as reconstructible state; manage logs/traces under their own retention policy Rehearse recovery in an isolated deployment before claiming a recovery target: Record the source revision, migration version, queue configuration and backup timestamps. Quiesce producers/consumers when required by the chosen consistent backup procedure; a database and Redis snapshot are not one atomic snapshot. Restore to isolated databases, Redis and storage. Keep workers, schedulers, SMTP and push stopped or directed to test destinations so replay cannot send real messages during validation. Every CLI invocation still opens its configured resources, so use a dedicated recovery profile. Inspect migrate status and representative account, notification and asset records. Validate object contents as well as metadata; run ordinary schema upgrades only according to the source revision's migration plan. Inspect pending/retry/archived tasks and the notification ledger. SQL intent can be redispatched if Redis was lost; an ordinary task lost from Redis cannot be reconstructed automatically. Restoring an older ledger can repeat a provider effect that happened after the backup. Exercise authorized reads, a new write, rollback, an idempotent task and asset download in that isolated environment. Media recovery cleans known interrupted rows; it does not reconcile unknown bucket objects after mismatched backups. Record actual data loss and recovery time, then plan the cutover and controlled backlog replay. Start only one scheduler per schedule set and monitor the drain. These are recovery requirements, not a completed deployment rehearsal. Native backup commands, retention and restoration guarantees depend on the selected deployment. Record a new rehearsal for the source and target you will publish. For current failures, start with troubleshooting."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Plan rollout and termination","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#plan-rollout-and-termination","Text":"A release deployment should make the sequence explicit: Build an immutable image from the intended source revision. Apply compatible migrations through one controlled migration execution. Start application processes with the matching configuration, keys, and task handlers. Wait for startup/readiness before routing traffic. On replacement, send termination to the application process and allow its drain and cleanup budgets before forcibly stopping the container. APP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT defaults to 45 seconds and cleanup has a separate 10-second budget. Choose a platform termination grace period with room for both and its own signal/routing overhead. Queue shutdown must fit inside the application drain budget. Increasing a timeout does not fix work that ignores cancellation. For rolling changes, account for old and new workers sharing queues and old/new processes using one schema. Preserve task names and payload compatibility until accepted work is drained or deliberately migrated. Do the same for public event contracts and stable storage disk names. Do not use migrate refresh during rollout."},{"Title":"Deployment","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Verify the deployment you operate","Parent":"","URL":"deployment.html#verify-the-deployment-you-operate","Text":"Exercise Observe Fresh install Registered migrations, an authorized read/write, and required capability outputs Dependency outage Expected readiness and safe operation failures, then recovery Active-work shutdown Admission stops, work exits within bounds, dependencies close afterward Backup restore Representative rows, pending work, and asset bytes agree in an isolated target Optional provider Actual credential and delivery behavior for your provider/account Telemetry outage Business operations remain independent; export loss is visible The release readiness page distinguishes historical checks from evidence still needed for a release. The supplied local manifests do not establish production availability, recovery targets, or provider support."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html","Text":"Use the logger attached to the execution context. Keep messages short and put useful context in structured fields so the same record works in the console, files, and Grafana. Logs describe observations; they are not a transactional audit database."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Write your first application log","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#write-your-first-application-log","Text":"Inside a service, handler, command, or task, use the supplied operation context: // Import github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/logging. logging.FromContext(ctx).InfoContext(ctx, \"profile updated\", \"user_id\", userID) The app supplies the logger and request/task fields; InfoContext also makes the current trace context available. Choose fields that explain the outcome without copying the request body. Logging does not require importing application resources. Select outputs in the environment: LOG_CHANNELS=stderr,observability That keeps a readable console and exports structured logs directly to the collector. Add file if you also want local daily files. Formats, levels, paths, and rotation are defined once in internal/app/config/observability.go: Channel Output Default level Retention/transport stderr / stdout Colored readable console INFO Process output file Readable file without color WARN UTC daily files, 14 days observability OTLP/HTTP records INFO Bounded export to Alloy/Loki The application's default selection is only stderr; full local profiles select more channels. An INFO request may appear in the console and Loki but not the WARN file. That is a channel-level choice, not a missing log."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Local log viewer","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#local-log-viewer","Text":"Enable the embedded viewer when you want to inspect logs without a collector or Grafana. It shares the application HTTP listener at /admin/logs/ and has its own Basic Auth credentials, independent of Identity and the queue dashboard: LOG_CHANNELS=stderr,file LOG_VIEWER_ENABLED=true LOG_VIEWER_USERNAME=operator LOG_VIEWER_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-long-random-password Serve an entry point with app.Processes{HTTP: true} and open http://localhost:8080/admin/logs/. The browser prompts for the credentials. Set LOG_VIEWER_ENABLED=false to omit the routes and live capture entirely. Tracing, metrics and the observability log channel can all remain disabled. Keep production access behind HTTPS and an operator-restricted ingress. The viewer provides: Live refresh every three seconds, pause/resume, and manual refresh. Channel/file selection, severity filtering and case-insensitive text search, including structured fields and request IDs. Newest-first paging, expandable records, copy, and JSONL export of visible entries. JSON and readable Tint file previews, with light/dark appearance and keyboard controls. Live records are copies of entries accepted by the application's named stdout, stderr and file channels. Their existing minimum levels still apply. A record sent to two selected channels appears once for each channel; filter by channel when inspecting one output. Context fields and groups are preserved. Successful viewer access logs still go to normal outputs but are excluded from live capture to avoid polling noise; failed access remains visible. Live capture is process-local and starts with application construction. It cannot recover earlier stdout/stderr output, intercept unrelated fmt.Print or global loggers, or read another process's stdout. Worker/scheduler history is visible through configured files mounted into the web process; use separate paths for each writing process. Use centralized observability for multi-instance aggregation. File history uses only file channels defined in config/observability.go, including their existing single-file or daily names. It does not enable a channel, change retention, create directories, or accept arbitrary paths. Missing directories have no files to list. Refresh the sources after daily rotation or removal. Text files retain their original timestamps in the message; JSON timestamps also appear in the time column. Only complete newline-terminated records are shown. The default live buffer retains at most 2,000 entries and 8 MiB of record strings, plus bounded entry/index overhead. Oldest entries expire when either limit is reached; the view reports the number expired. LOG_VIEWER_BUFFER_ENTRIES and LOG_VIEWER_BUFFER_BYTES adjust these bounds. Each record preview is limited to 16 KiB; exports contain those same visible previews, not complete original files. Each file query reads at most 1 MiB and returns at most 500 entries. The page requests 200 at a time and displays up to 1,000; load older or narrow the filters to continue. The file directory is limited to fewer than 4,096 entries. Concurrent viewer requests are bounded; busy clients receive a retry response. File browsing is a best-effort view of changing files, not a retained snapshot or audit archive. The UI and every data/asset route require Basic Auth. Responses are non-cacheable, cross-origin data reads are rejected, and log text is never rendered as HTML. Searches use POST bodies and stay out of page URLs and browser storage. Logs may still contain sensitive application data: the viewer does not redact arbitrary fields, so keep secrets out of logs and restrict access appropriately."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Preserve error identity","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#preserve-error-identity","Text":"Return errors with %w and inspect them with errors.Is/errors.As. The canonical error handling guide explains classification, owned sentinels, validation messages, and cancellation. Avoid logging the same returned failure at every layer."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Translate at the boundary","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#translate-at-the-boundary","Text":"HTTP handlers use response.Error(ctx, err) from internal/platform/api/response. The boundary hides raw operational details and records safe failure categories. See boundary translation and response bodies."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"HTTP request logs","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#http-request-logs","Text":"The app automatically records each completed HTTP request using the context logger. Records include the server name, method, route template, status, and duration. Health probes and unmatched routes are included. Successful and redirect responses use INFO, client errors WARN, and server errors ERROR. The same JSON or tint settings apply to these records. There is one built-in access logger. See Middleware for fields and customization."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Retrieve the context logger","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#retrieve-the-context-logger","Text":"Application runners, commands, and HTTP requests receive a logger through their contexts: logger := logging.FromContext(ctx) logger.InfoContext(ctx, \"operation completed\", \"user_id\", userID, \"event_id\", eventID, ) The returned value is a standard *slog.Logger. Use structured key-value fields. FromContext falls back to slog.Default() if no logger was attached, which also makes ordinary standalone contexts usable. Choose fields appropriate to your data policy. Do not log passwords, connection URLs containing credentials, authorization headers, full configuration structs, or complete request payloads."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Add fields for one operation","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#add-fields-for-one-operation","Text":"Derive a logger and pass it through the operation's context: logger := logging.FromContext(ctx).With(\"component\", \"identity\", \"user_id\", userID) ctx = logging.WithLogger(ctx, logger) logger.InfoContext(ctx, \"starting operation\") Pass this derived ctx to downstream calls so they can retrieve the same fields. WithLogger does not mutate the parent context. It requires a non-nil logger. For HTTP middleware, call next(huma.WithContext(request, ctx)), where request is the Huma context. See the middleware example."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Choose the output format","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#choose-the-output-format","Text":"Edit each channel's Options in internal/app/config/observability.go. stderr and stdout use tint with colors; file uses tint without colors. observability uses structured OTLP/HTTP delivery. Console and OTLP channels default to INFO; the readable file channel defaults to WARN. These settings are hardcoded per channel, not loaded from environment variables. The Watermill adapter maps routine library info messages to DEBUG, including handler registration, routing and pub/sub cleanup. This keeps ordinary CLI help and completion free of infrastructure chatter at INFO. Watermill failures still use ERROR; application logs retain their chosen severity. Set a channel's level to slog.LevelDebug in Go when those library diagnostics are needed. Select a console channel with LOG_CHANNELS=stderr or LOG_CHANNELS=stdout. To use JSON, change that channel's format to logging.FormatJSON. Rebuild after changing Go channel definitions and restart after changing the selected defaults. Tint affects application output, not Tilt's own build or Kubernetes event messages."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Configure named channels","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#configure-named-channels","Text":"Channel definitions live in internal/app/config/observability.go. A channel is a named output with its own driver, format and minimum level. The built-in drivers are stdout, stderr, file and otlp. Select one or more default channels with a comma-separated list: LOG_CHANNELS=stdout,file Every normal context log now goes to both outputs. The current file channel uses storage/logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log with 14-day retention, as defined in Go. Unknown or repeated channel names fail configuration validation. Channels are defined in Go; LOG_CHANNELS selects them and OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT configures direct log delivery. Add another channel inside LoggingConfig.Channels: channels[\"audit\"] = logging.Channel{ Driver: \"file\", Path: \"storage/logs/audit.log\", Options: logging.Options{ Format: logging.FormatJSON, Level: slog.LevelInfo, NoColor: true, }, } The configuration file already imports logging and log/slog. You can set different options on each channel, including a readable console and JSON files. Keep paths, formats, levels and color settings in these channel definitions."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Single or daily files","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#single-or-daily-files","Text":"The file driver supports two modes, configured per channel in internal/app/config/observability.go: channels[\"file\"] = logging.Channel{ Driver: \"file\", Path: \"storage/logs/app.log\", FileMode: logging.FileDaily, KeepDays: 7, Options: logging.Options{Format: logging.FormatJSON, Level: slog.LevelInfo}, } FileSingle (also the zero-value default) appends to app.log. KeepDays must be zero; records inside a single file cannot be expired individually. FileDaily appends to app-YYYY-MM-DD.log, using the UTC date at write time. With no extension, app becomes app-YYYY-MM-DD. It reuses the day's file after restart and rotates on the first write of a new day. KeepDays: 7 keeps today and the previous six UTC dates. 0 keeps all dated files; negative values are invalid. Retention is based on filename dates, not modification times or the number of files. Cleanup runs when the channel opens and on daily rotation. No background goroutine or scheduler is needed; idle or stopped applications do not perform cleanup. Only regular files with that channel's exact dated naming pattern are eligible; undated files, unrelated names, directories and symlinks are left alone. Use a distinct path per channel and per application instance, and do not combine this writer with external rotation of its files. Writes and rotation are serialized within a channel; there is no cross-process rotation coordination. A failure to open today's file fails channel opening. During rotation, it is reported through OnError and the next write retries; the failed record is not redirected into yesterday's file. Retention failures are also reported; after a successful rotation the current record is still written. Cleanup is retried on the next rotation or reopen. Daily retention limits age, not total disk usage."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Log to selected channels","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#log-to-selected-channels","Text":"Select outputs from the context logger: logger, err := logging.Channels(ctx, \"audit\") if err != nil { return err } logger.InfoContext(ctx, \"order approved\", \"user_id\", userID) This record goes only to audit. To select several outputs: logger, err := logging.Channels(ctx, \"audit\", \"stdout\") if err != nil { return err } logger.InfoContext(ctx, \"manual adjustment\", \"user_id\", userID) Selection preserves fields and groups added through With and WithGroup, including request and job fields. It does not change the default logger. To use the selection throughout an operation, pass logging.WithLogger(ctx, logger) to that operation. During dependency assembly, use logging.Select(deps.Logger, \"audit\") and inject the resulting *slog.Logger into the service that needs it. No application dependency container is added to context. Selection requires a logger created by the channel manager; a standalone logging.New logger or an unrelated handler has no named channels and returns an error."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Add a logging driver","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#add-a-logging-driver","Text":"pkg/logging accepts explicit driver functions that return a slog.Handler and an optional owned closer. Add a driver to the configuration returned by LoggingConfig.Channels: return logging.Config{ Default: names, Channels: channels, Drivers: map[string]logging.Driver{ \"discard\": func(ch logging.Channel) (slog.Handler, io.Closer, error) { logger, err := logging.New(io.Discard, ch.Options) if err != nil { return nil, nil, err } return logger.Handler(), nil, nil }, }, } Import io for this example and declare a channel with Driver: \"discard\" to use it. A real integration can return its own concurrency-safe handler and closer. A driver must clean up partial construction before returning an error; return a nil closer for borrowed resources. Built-in driver names cannot be overridden."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Ownership and output failures","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#ownership-and-output-failures","Text":""},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Open channels when needed","Parent":"Ownership and output failures","URL":"logging.html#open-channels-when-needed","Text":"Action Resource behavior App construction Opens selected default channels before other dependencies First Channels, Select, or Manager.Logger for another channel Opens that channel once; concurrent selections share it Derive fields with With / WithGroup Does not open unused channels Default output cannot open Startup fails Non-default output cannot open Selection returns an error and may be retried Select after manager Close Rejected Successfully opened outputs remain manager-owned, including when a later channel in the same selection fails. App closes them after other dependencies finish. Table-publication commands follow the same rules: stderr/stdout need no writable log directory, but a selected file channel does."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Resolve paths and permissions","Parent":"Ownership and output failures","URL":"logging.html#resolve-paths-and-permissions","Text":"File paths are converted to absolute paths when the manager opens, using the process working directory. Later channel selection keeps that location even if the working directory changes. There is no source-tree search or executable-path fallback. File operation Behavior Open an existing file Append; preserve existing permissions Create missing directories Mode 0750, subject to process umask Create a new file Mode 0600, subject to process umask Single-file mode No rotation or reopen after external rotation Daily mode Open the new UTC date's file on its first write Neither mode syncs every record. Use stdout/stderr or direct OTLP in containers, or provide a writable volume for files. See daily retention for cleanup timing and failure behavior."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Understand slow or failed outputs","Parent":"Ownership and output failures","URL":"logging.html#understand-slow-or-failed-outputs","Text":"Outputs run in selection order and apply their own minimum level: Output Logging call behavior Console or file Synchronous write; a slow writer slows the call OTLP Enqueue into a bounded asynchronous batch processor Synchronous write failure Report to stderr and continue to remaining outputs slog.Logger does not return write errors to business code. These logs are not a transactional audit store. OTLP export failures and queue drops have separate asynchronous reporting described below. For custom synchronous failure reporting, set logging.Config.OnError. It must be concurrency-safe and must not log back through these channels, which would recurse."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Stop producers before cleanup","Parent":"Ownership and output failures","URL":"logging.html#stop-producers-before-cleanup","Text":"Standalone callers of logging.Open own the returned manager. Stop logging before calling Close or Shutdown(ctx); the latter propagates a cleanup deadline to OTLP flushing. The package neither changes slog.Default() nor closes stdout/stderr."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Set the application working directory","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#set-the-application-working-directory","Text":"Launch from the application root. The configured base path storage/logs/app.log then has a predictable location. With the current daily mode, its output includes the UTC date: Environment Working directory Log file Local go run ./cmd/app Repository root \u003crepository\u003e/storage/logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log Installed binary /opt/myapp /opt/myapp/storage/logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log Docker / Tilt /app from the Dockerfile /app/storage/logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log Kubernetes /app on both application and migration containers /app/storage/logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log For local development, run commands from the repository root. Set your IDE's working directory to that same directory; for VS Code's Go launch configuration use \"cwd\": \"${workspaceFolder}\". Running from a nested directory deliberately uses that directory, not the repository root. For an installed binary: cd /opt/myapp ./bin/app For systemd, set both paths explicitly: [Service] WorkingDirectory=/opt/myapp ExecStart=/opt/myapp/bin/app Docker keeps WORKDIR /app. Kubernetes sets workingDir: /app on both the api and migrate containers. File output requires writable storage at /app/storage/logs; stdout/stderr need no writable log directory. The development containers use a read-only root filesystem and mount storage at /app/storage. Tilt on Docker Desktop kubeadm mounts the local storage/logs directory directly at /app/storage/logs, using your host UID/GID. Writes appear in the same local file without copying. Compose uses a named volume, which is not the local checkout. The full development profile selects stderr,file,observability; the application default remains stderr. See Local development for storage details."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Create a standalone logger","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#create-a-standalone-logger","Text":"Outside the application lifecycle, construct one explicitly. This complete example writes a readable message without changing the global logger: package main import ( \"context\" \"fmt\" \"log/slog\" \"os\" \"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/logging\" ) func main() { logger, err := logging.New(os.Stderr, logging.Options{ Format: logging.FormatTint, Level: slog.LevelInfo, }) if err != nil { fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, err) os.Exit(1) } ctx := logging.WithLogger(context.Background(), logger) logging.FromContext(ctx).InfoContext(ctx, \"example started\", \"component\", \"docs\") } The logger does not own its writer. If you supply an opened file, its creator must close it after logging has finished."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Panics and cancellation","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#panics-and-cancellation","Text":"The API boundary records safe panic diagnostics and preserves transport aborts. Cancellation is not proof that an effect never happened. Follow the error-handling guarantees, and pass the same context through dependent work so logs retain correlation."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Connect logs to traces","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#connect-logs-to-traces","Text":"When an operation has a valid OpenTelemetry span, context-aware logging calls add trace_id, span_id, and trace_sampled automatically. This works with named channels, including channels selected later. Use InfoContext(ctx, ...) rather than Info(...); the context determines the current span. See observability for examples, sampling and grouped loggers."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Send logs to Loki","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#send-logs-to-loki","Text":"The observability channel uses the otlp driver, not a file. Select it alongside readable console and daily file output: LOG_CHANNELS=stderr,file,observability OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/logs For standalone use, define an OTLP channel with the same public API: channels[\"telemetry\"] = logging.Channel{ Driver: \"otlp\", Options: logging.Options{Level: slog.LevelInfo}, OTLP: logging.OTLPOptions{ Endpoint: \"http://localhost:4318/v1/logs\", ServiceName: \"orders\", Environment: \"development\", }, } OTLP preserves structured attributes, groups, severity and active trace context. It does not use tint or JSON text formatting; its Options.Level still filters records. It opens only when selected, does not replace global OTel providers, and needs no log directory. The app flushes it after other dependencies close. The supplied Alloy configuration forwards OTLP logs to Loki. Request IDs and trace IDs are queryable metadata; the message remains the log body. Grafana uses the metadata for View trace and trace-to-log navigation. No file collector or observability.log is needed. Local readable files remain independent. Exporter failures are reported through an independent stderr logger, rate-limited to once per minute with a cumulative failed-record count. The SDK also reports queue drops to stderr. These asynchronous failures do not go through OnError, which handles synchronous handler failures. Export has bounded memory and time budgets and is best-effort, not a durable delivery guarantee. See observability for limits and deployment settings."},{"Title":"Logging","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Troubleshoot a missing or duplicated record","Parent":"","URL":"logging.html#troubleshoot-a-missing-or-duplicated-record","Text":"Symptom Check INFO appears in Loki but not the file The built-in file channel defaults to WARN Selected channel fails at startup Its configuration, path permissions, or driver validation Custom channel missing Define it in Go and select it explicitly; rebuild after definition changes Log lacks a trace ID Use the context-aware method and the active operation context Duplicate records in Loki Ensure no second console/file collector forwards the same records request_id_extracted appears beside request_id An extra JSON parser extracted a field already present as metadata; remove the unnecessary parser for new OTLP logs Yesterday's file stops growing Daily mode writes the current UTC date's file Old daily files remain Retention runs on open/rotation and only targets that channel's filename pattern File logging is independent of OTLP export. An exporter outage does not cause local files to be replayed later. See observability for request-ID filtering and troubleshooting for operation failures beyond logging."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html","Text":"Logs describe individual outcomes. Metrics show rates, latency and capacity over time. Traces connect operations across HTTP requests, dependencies, tasks and events. The app supplies boundary instrumentation; add business signals only where useful."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Choose where to start","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#choose-where-to-start","Text":"Question Start with Continue to Why did this request fail? Its request_id in Loki The linked Tempo trace and safe error code Which query made it slow? The request trace Database child spans and sanitized db.query.text Is latency increasing for everyone? Prometheus route latency/rate Representative traces and pool/backlog signals Why is background work delayed? Queue backlog and task state Worker attempts and the notification ledger Is the process healthy? Liveness, readiness, scrape health The specific dependency or lifecycle signal You do not need to add custom spans before investigating normal HTTP, database, cache, task, and event boundaries. First use the instrumentation already installed."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Start the local stack","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#start-the-local-stack","Text":"Grafana is the UI; Prometheus stores metrics, Tempo stores traces, Loki stores logs, and Alloy receives logs and spans over OTLP/HTTP. Application log files are not collected. flowchart TB accTitle: Three signals, one place to investigate accDescr: Prometheus scrapes metrics. Alloy forwards OTLP spans to Tempo and OTLP logs to Loki. Grafana queries all three stores. Metrics[App /metrics] --\u003e Prometheus[(Prometheus)] Spans[App spans] --\u003e Traces[Alloy: receive traces] Traces --\u003e Tempo[(Tempo)] Logs[App OTLP logs] --\u003e LogReceiver[Alloy: receive logs] LogReceiver --\u003e Loki[(Loki)] Prometheus --\u003e Grafana[Grafana] Tempo --\u003e Grafana Loki --\u003e Grafana With the existing Tilt development environment: tilt up Tilt starts observability by default; use -- --observability=false to disable it. For an already-running session with explicit arguments, use tilt args -- --observability=true to enable it again (retain any other desired flags). Alternatively, start the complete Compose profile: docker compose --env-file deployments/observability/compose.env -f deployments/compose.yaml up --build -d Choose one setup because their host ports overlap. The Compose environment file starts backends and enables app signals; --profile observability alone does not configure the app. Shell environment overrides still apply. Open Labena · Overview. Data sources and the dashboard are provisioned. Local Grafana permits anonymous Viewer access, so a login form is not required to view data. Both full profiles select stderr,file,observability log channels: readable console, readable daily files, and direct OTLP log export. The application's current root sampling default is 100%; the Compose observability profile also explicitly selects 100%. TRACING_SAMPLE_RATIO can reduce the volume outside local exploration. Tilt forwards Alloy's OTLP endpoint to localhost:4318 for host applications. Prometheus's supplied app target is the Kubernetes api:9090 service; scraping a separate host process requires a corresponding scrape target."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Follow your first request","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#follow-your-first-request","Text":"With the local app and observability stack running: curl -i http://localhost:8080/healthz curl -i http://localhost:8080/readyz The response includes X-Request-ID and X-API-Version. Copy the request ID, open Grafana Explore, select Loki, and filter on it using the steps below. Health probes show the HTTP boundary; make an authenticated GET /api/v1/users/me request when you want to inspect database work too: curl -i http://localhost:8080/api/v1/users/me \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN\" Set ACCESS_TOKEN to a current application access token. A successful account response includes the same request ID in its JSON envelope. Do not share the token when reporting a failure. Allow a scrape/export interval, then check application scrape health, request rate and duration. Expand an HTTP log in Grafana and follow its trace link. Generate task and event work to see their signals; unused capabilities have no measurements. A log's trace ID does not guarantee a stored trace. Sampling, dropped exports and retention can all explain a missing trace. Scrape health proves the endpoint is reachable, not that every application dependency is healthy."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Filter logs by request metadata","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#filter-logs-by-request-metadata","Text":"The observability channel sends log attributes such as request_id directly over OTLP. Loki stores these attributes as structured metadata, alongside native trace_id, span_id and severity_text fields. It is available to Loki queries in Explore and dashboard log panels without adding a JSON parser. It is not an indexed stream label, so it does not appear in the initial stream-label selector. In Explore's Builder, select service_name = labena, then add a Label filter operation for request_id, choose =, and enter the request ID. The equivalent query is: {service_name=\"labena\"} | request_id=\"YOUR_REQUEST_ID\" For new OTLP records, filter severity with severity_text=\"WARN\" or severity_text=\"ERROR\". The environment resource field is deployment_environment_name. These replace the old file pipeline's level and environment fields; request_id keeps its name. Log lines contain the message, while their attributes are available as metadata. This applies to newly exported OTLP logs after the app and collector restart. Older JSON log records still require JSON parsing to filter their request ID. Logs without a request ID do not match a specific ID filter. No dashboard textbox is required; each query chooses its own filter. Prometheus metrics remain aggregate measurements, and Tempo traces are reached through a matching log's View trace link."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Understand metadata and labels","Parent":"Filter logs by request metadata","URL":"observability.html#understand-metadata-and-labels","Text":"Field Where to use it Why service_name Loki stream selector Groups a bounded application identity request_id, trace_id, span_id Structured metadata filter Identifies one execution without creating an indexed stream per request severity_text Metadata filter Native OTLP severity such as WARN deployment_environment_name Resource metadata filter Environment reported by the process HTTP route template Metrics labels and spans Groups a bounded operation rather than raw user paths Grafana's Builder can filter metadata using a Label filter operation even though it is not an indexed stream label. Suggestions depend on the selected data and time range. A field may be entered by name when it is absent from suggestions. Do not add | json to parse a new OTLP log's message as though it were the former JSON file record. Applying a parser to records with existing fields can create *_extracted duplicates. Older retained file records may still use level and need JSON parsing; choose the query for the records' actual format."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Configure log export","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#configure-log-export","Text":"Include observability in LOG_CHANNELS to enable the OTLP driver. To avoid local files completely, select stderr,observability; to retain readable daily files, select stderr,file,observability. LOG_CHANNELS=stderr,file,observability OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/logs Use http://alloy:4318/v1/logs inside the supplied containers. Tilt and Compose configure that address; host applications use localhost. The endpoint is a full OTLP/HTTP URL. Authentication belongs at the collector; URL credentials, queries and fragments are rejected. APP_NAME and APP_ENV identify the source. Log export works independently of tracing, trace sampling, metrics and local file output. Console and daily file formats stay readable. The observability channel creates no observability.log, and Alloy has no access to application log files. Existing files are left untouched and are not replayed into Loki. The application queues at most 2,048 records and exports batches of up to 512, with a one-second interval and five-second request timeout. Queue saturation drops oldest records; failed batches are discarded and reported to stderr without raw payloads or endpoint details. Alloy retries its own forwarding with bounded queues. These queues are in memory: outages or process crashes can lose logs. Daily files are an independent local copy, not an automatic export retry buffer. At shutdown, app stops producers, closes dependencies, then flushes log export within three seconds or the remaining cleanup deadline, whichever is shorter. OTLPOptions in the channel definition allows different timeout budgets. Collector availability is not required at startup or for readiness."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Configure tracing","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#configure-tracing","Text":"Without the supplied local profile, configure OTLP HTTP explicitly: TRACING_ENABLED=true TRACING_TRUST_INCOMING=false APP_NAME=labena OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/traces TRACING_SAMPLE_RATIO=1 TRACING_EXPORT_TIMEOUT=5s TRACING_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=3s Tracing defaults to disabled and the default root sampling ratio is 1. Use a reachable collector hostname inside containers, not host loopback. The endpoint is the full traces URL; credentials, queries and fragments are rejected. Incoming HTTP trace context is ignored unless TRACING_TRUST_INCOMING=true. Enable trust only behind controlled ingress: parent-based sampling follows the trusted parent's sampling decision. Only W3C trace context is imported, not baggage. Trace headers are correlation metadata, not identity or authorization. Invalid enabled settings fail validation; collector outages do not block startup. Disabled tracing uses a no-op provider and starts no exporter. App instances own providers explicitly without replacing OpenTelemetry globals. Independent callers use tracing.Open(ctx, cfg, logger) and own provider.Shutdown(ctx). Opening enabled tracing starts its bounded export worker, so cleanup is required even if no spans were recorded. Stop producers before shutting it down."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Enable metrics","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#enable-metrics","Text":"METRICS_ENABLED=true METRICS_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:9090 curl http://127.0.0.1:9090/metrics Metrics work independently of tracing and use an app-owned registry. Disabled mode opens no listener. Selected app/worker/scheduler processes and cli serve expose the listener; one-off commands do not. Give separate host processes different ports. For private scraping across containers, bind :9090. This listener has no auth or TLS and should not be publicly exposed. It stores no history itself. It remains available during business-service drain and stops before provider cleanup."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Available metrics","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#available-metrics","Text":"Metric family Meaning http_server_request_duration_seconds Completed HTTP latency histogram and request count http_server_active_requests Active ordinary requests db_client_operation_duration_seconds Bun query latency by operation and outcome db_pool_connections, db_pool_max_connections Local pool occupancy and capacity db_pool_waits_total, db_pool_wait_duration_seconds_total Pool contention cache_operation_duration_seconds Cache operation latency and hit/miss/error outcomes queue_job_duration_seconds Task attempts by registered task pattern, queue and outcome notification_delivery_attempts_total Persisted channel delivery attempts by channel and outcome notification_pending_age_seconds Dispatcher-sampled age of the oldest unsettled notification intent asynq_queue_size, asynq_queue_latency_seconds Redis backlog and oldest pending latency asynq_tasks_enqueued_total Native state gauge despite its name watermill_events_* Official Watermill publication, subscription and handler metrics go_*, process_*, target_info Runtime, process and service metadata The existing queue_job_* and job_name names remain compatibility surfaces even though guides use task terminology. Route templates and registered task patterns keep labels bounded. IDs, URLs, keys and payloads are not metric labels. HTTP request rate: sum by (http_route) (rate(http_server_request_duration_seconds_count[5m])) HTTP p95 latency: histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le, http_route) (rate(http_server_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) ) Add Prometheus job or instance selectors to isolate a deployment. Counts reset on restart; use rates rather than interpreting process counters as business records."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"HTTP tracing","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#http-tracing","Text":"Standard HTTP middleware records method, registered route and final status. Unknown methods become _OTHER; unmatched paths are not copied into span names. 5xx responses, recovered panics and observed cancellation mark failures; 4xx remains a status attribute. Bodies, raw paths, queries, credentials and client IPs are omitted. Health requests are included. Streaming spans last until the handler returns. WebSocket upgrades are excluded from HTTP spans and metrics; their request logging and shutdown still apply. Redirects pass through the same observability boundary."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Correlate logs with the current span","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#correlate-logs-with-the-current-span","Text":"Use the context-aware slog API supplied by pkg/logging: logging.FromContext(ctx).InfoContext(ctx, \"order accepted\", \"order_id\", orderID) The logger adds trace_id, span_id and trace_sampled from the current context. Named channels preserve correlation. Info(...) has no operation context; use InfoContext. An unrelated logger or fallback slog.Default() is not automatically wrapped. Unsampled contexts still have IDs, but no recorded span. Alloy forwards OTLP logs to Loki without collecting duplicate console/file output. Request and trace IDs remain metadata, not indexed stream labels. The service label comes from APP_NAME; update dashboard selectors when changing it. Grafana's View trace uses the trace_id metadata field. Tempo's logs link also filters on metadata, so it does not depend on a trace ID appearing in the message."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Create a span","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#create-a-span","Text":"In app assembly, create a native tracer and pass it to the service that needs it: tracer := a.resources.TracerProvider.Tracer(\"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/catalog\") Inside that service operation: ctx, span := tracer.Start(ctx, \"catalog.import\") defer span.End() if err := importProducts(ctx); err != nil { span.SetStatus(codes.Error, \"import failed\") return err } return nil This is a workflow fragment: implement importProducts and import OTel codes. Pass the derived context onward. Use stable names and sanitized status descriptions; do not record raw provider errors or payloads as span attributes."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Add a custom metric","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#add-a-custom-metric","Text":"Create instruments once during app assembly and inject them into the consumer: meter := a.resources.MeterProvider.Meter(\"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/catalog\") created, err := meter.Int64Counter(\"catalog.products.created\", metric.WithDescription(\"Products successfully created\"), ) if err != nil { return err } Import go.opentelemetry.io/otel/metric. Record created.Add(ctx, 1) after success. Do not attach a product ID as a label. No-op providers permit the same calls when metrics are disabled. The SDK bounds attribute sets; that is a safety limit, not a substitute for low-cardinality labels."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Database queries and pool metrics","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#database-queries-and-pool-metrics","Text":"The app instruments its Bun pool once. Queries using the operation context and transaction executor retain logging and telemetry. Spans record bounded operation names. Recorded spans also include sanitized SQL in db.query.text: literal values and parameter placeholders become ?, and comments are removed. DSNs, parameter values and raw driver errors are never attached. Missing rows are not failures."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Inspect a request's SQL in Grafana","Parent":"Database queries and pool metrics","URL":"observability.html#inspect-a-requests-sql-in-grafana","Text":"After restarting the app with this version, make a request that queries the database and copy its X-Request-ID response header. Then: Open Grafana Explore, select Loki, and find the request's log using its request ID. For example: {service_name=\"labena\"} | request_id=\"YOUR_REQUEST_ID\". Expand the log and follow View trace, or copy its trace_id into a Tempo trace-ID lookup. Select a child database span (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE) and expand its attributes. db.query.text contains the query structure, for example SELECT \"user\".\"id\" FROM \"users\" AS \"user\" WHERE email = ?. The span shows the query's duration and status. SQL is available only for newly recorded traces, not historical traces or unsampled requests. Existing tracing settings control capture; SQL does not require DEBUG logging. Database logs remain metadata-only, and SQL is never used as a metric label. Capture supports single read/write statements, including CTEs, for PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite. Table, column and alias names remain visible, so keep application data out of identifiers. SQLite double-quoted tokens are redacted because they can also represent string values. For safety, text is omitted for administrative or multiple statements, unsupported/malformed tokens, nested block comments, ambiguous backslash escapes (quoting depends on connection settings), and SQLite bracket/backtick identifiers. Input over 64 KiB or sanitized text over 8 KiB is also omitted. PostgreSQL hex literals used by Bun for binary values, such as verification digests, are supported and replaced with ?. These omissions do not affect execution or the span's timing/status. This follows OpenTelemetry's sanitized SQL guidance."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Instrument another pool","Parent":"Database queries and pool metrics","URL":"observability.html#instrument-another-pool","Text":"Pool measurements read local database/sql statistics without querying the server. Direct db.DB calls, connection probes and SQL-only migration paths bypass Bun query hooks. Query duration is not necessarily server-only execution time. For another app-owned Bun connection, install before concurrent use: unregister, err := database.Instrument(db, \"reporting\", a.resources.TracerProvider, a.resources.MeterProvider, ) if err != nil { return err } Register cleanup immediately. Stop users, call unregister(), then close the pool. Unregister removes pool observation; Bun hooks cannot be detached. Do not instrument the primary pool twice."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Redis and cache operations","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#redis-and-cache-operations","Text":"Configured cache drivers get logical operation tracing/metrics. Misses differ from failures; keys and values are omitted. App's cache Redis client can also carry the native tracing hook. The Asynq connection is instrumented through task middleware and its collector; it does not currently install that Redis command hook. For a separately constructed go-redis client, attach once before use: client.AddHook(redis.NewTracingHook( a.resources.TracerProvider.Tracer(\"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/redis\"), )) Here redis imports github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/redis. The hook records allowlisted commands and pipelines without keys, arguments or raw errors. It does not add retries. cache.WithTelemetry(store, tracerProvider, meterProvider) similarly wraps an independent store without taking ownership of its clients."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Trace queues and events","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#trace-queues-and-events","Text":"Global Asynq client middleware traces EnqueueContext and injects W3C headers into initialized task header maps. Native worker middleware links each consumer attempt to its producer, records duration and adds contextual logs. Schedules bypass the client chain, so executions have worker spans without a stale startup producer. Watermill's publisher copies propagation metadata; handler middleware preserves subscriber cancellation and links each attempt to the producer. Linked consumer spans are independently sampled; do not expect one continuous parent/child tree. The app uses official Asynq and Watermill Prometheus integrations. Small native middleware adapters provide the app's tracing and sanitization policy; business services use native tasks and messages. Asynq scrapes query Redis and can fail when Redis is unavailable. Handler metrics alone do not describe backlog or durability."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Trace outgoing HTTP requests","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#trace-outgoing-http-requests","Text":"Construct and reuse a client in app assembly for an integration allowed to receive trace metadata: transport := http.DefaultTransport.(*http.Transport).Clone() client := \u0026http.Client{ Transport: tracing.NewHTTPTransport(transport, a.resources.TracerProvider.Tracer(\"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/payments\")), Timeout: 10*time.Second, CheckRedirect: func(req *http.Request, via []*http.Request) error { return http.ErrUseLastResponse }, } Use net/http, time and github.com/wisalgroup/labena/pkg/tracing. Pass request contexts with http.NewRequestWithContext; close response bodies and bound their size. App cleanup should call client.CloseIdleConnections() after users stop. The wrapper clones headers, injects W3C context and removes baggage. Spans measure through response headers, not body consumption. It adds no retry or global client changes. If redirects are enabled, authorize each destination for propagation."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Trace mail sends","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#trace-mail-sends","Text":"The app always constructs mail. Its assembly wraps SMTP sending for tracing; use the same wrapper for an independently constructed mailer: mailer := mail.WithTracing(smtpMailer, a.resources.TracerProvider.Tracer(\"github.com/wisalgroup/labena/mail\"), ) mail.send measures submission without recipients, bodies or raw provider errors. It changes neither ownership nor retries. Success means submission, not inbox delivery."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Console commands","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#console-commands","Text":"With tracing enabled, command execution has a console.execute span carrying its registered command path, not arguments or flag values. Commands pass cmd.Context() to services. One-off commands flush on cleanup but start no metrics listener. All CLI commands use application construction, including help and table publication; construction can open exporters but does not start application runners."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Export and shutdown behavior","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#export-and-shutdown-behavior","Text":"Spans use a bounded in-memory batch queue. Full queues drop spans; exporters have timeouts and no app-side retries. Alloy owns bounded backend retry policy. Failed exports are reported through redacted, rate-limited logging. Telemetry is best-effort, not a durable audit log. App shutdown stops work before flushing tracing and closing logging. Flush deadlines and process termination can lose diagnostics. A timed-out exporter can still be finishing its bounded network operation. Successful cleanup does not prove storage at the backend."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Retention, resources and failures","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#retention-resources-and-failures","Text":"Local Prometheus, Tempo and Loki target 24-hour retention. Deletion is asynchronous; WALs and in-flight data add overhead. Docker volumes and PVC requests are not reliable hard disk limits. Monitor local disk use and consult deployments/observability for component memory limits and pinned backend configuration. The observability channel sends logs directly; no source file needs rotation. Readable daily app files have their own retention policy, independent of Loki retention. Alloy does not read or backfill those files. Backends are not app readiness dependencies. During an outage, requests continue, exports may be lost; local file output follows its configured rotation and retention policy. Local volumes retain data across ordinary restarts, but provide neither high availability nor backups."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Troubleshooting","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#troubleshooting","Text":"Symptom Check Scrape health is 0 Metrics enabled, private :9090 binding, Prometheus target Logs missing Observability channel selected, OTLP endpoint reachable, Alloy export status, Loki structured metadata enabled Traces missing Sampling, reachable OTLP endpoint, Alloy and Tempo readiness Trace link missing Context-aware logging and valid span context Trace ID exists but Tempo has no trace Sampling, export loss, retention, time range, and the correct deployment Database span has no query text Supported single statement and sanitization/size limits; timing still records SQL is absent from Loki Expected: logs contain metadata; inspect db.query.text in Tempo Request ID missing in a metric filter Expected: request IDs are not metric labels Grafana “Failed to fetch” Backend readiness and logs, browser connection, exhausted container resources TraceQL reports an empty ring Tempo generator/local-block configuration and generator readiness Tempo connection refused Tempo is listening on the configured OTLP port and has finished startup Check the relevant Tilt resource or Compose service logs before changing application instrumentation. These messages identify a failing boundary, not one guaranteed cause."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Production boundary","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#production-boundary","Text":"The supplied stack is local: anonymous Grafana viewing, no ingestion authentication, local storage and no high availability. Wider deployment needs explicit access control, TLS, backups, retention, alert ownership and per-replica OTLP delivery. No dashboard or metric alone establishes exactly-once processing or data integrity. Service identity belongs to config.App.AppName, loaded from APP_NAME. Metrics, tracing and log export share it when enabled; the application name is exported using the standard service.name resource attribute."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Rate limiting","Parent":"Production boundary","URL":"observability.html#rate-limiting","Text":"The shared limiter emits ratelimit.decisions and ratelimit.duration when metrics are enabled, plus ratelimit allow spans when tracing is enabled. The bounded outcome label separates normal admissions (allowed), policy rejections (denied), unclassified failures (error), cancellation (canceled) and deadline expiry (timeout). An error is not permission to proceed; callers retain their existing failure behavior. API and Identity limits share these signals. Use rising denied counts to investigate rejected traffic; rising error or timeout counts can indicate limiter infrastructure trouble. These aggregate signals do not identify a user or policy. Keys, destinations, policy values and backend error messages are excluded to avoid exposing personal data or secrets."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Safe failure diagnostics","Parent":"Production boundary","URL":"observability.html#safe-failure-diagnostics","Text":"HTTP operation, task-attempt and local-event failure logs include error_category: canceled, timeout, network, or other. Wrapped causes are recognized without exporting error text. Categories describe failures; they do not decide retries. Existing error_type fields remain available where already emitted. The local-event message processing failed record sits outside the configured retry middleware: it reports a failed processing cycle after retries, or early cancellation/timeout. It is not proof of durable delivery or a dead-letter queue. Use its handler, topic and message ID to investigate; GoChannel events can be lost. Attempt spans retain their error status but no longer emit a per-attempt retry_budget_exhausted warning. Update queries that depended on that field. HTTP failures expose a safe error_code alongside the category, never raw causes. application shutdown timed out reports that the drain budget expired. Inspect active handlers for cancellation support. The app does not claim to have stopped uncooperative goroutines or safely closed their dependencies. Queue backlog uses the native Asynq Prometheus collector. Scheduler publication uses the native post-enqueue callback; worker traces describe execution separately. The notification delivery ledger has a separate lifecycle from Asynq. Inspect notifications deliveries --status failed and monitor counts/age in notification_deliveries; see Notifications. The notifier also records delivery attempts and the age of the oldest unsettled intent when metrics are enabled. The dispatcher samples pending age for pending, sending and awaiting_receipt rows; an empty ledger records zero. These signals complement Asynq backlog because an intent can be waiting in SQL before a task reaches Redis. Use the native tracer/meter contracts for extensions. The framework adapters supply correlation, sanitization, and lifecycle behavior at shared boundaries."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Operational signal coverage","Parent":"Production boundary","URL":"observability.html#operational-signal-coverage","Text":"Situation Signal Limit HTTP failures and latency Route-based request histogram and access logs No URL/query values in metric labels Limiter rejection or failure Decision counter, duration and outcome No per-user or per-destination breakdown Queue backlog Native Asynq collector on the metrics listener Requires queue Redis to answer scrapes Task attempt failure Attempt histogram, trace, task ID and safe error category A retryable failure is not terminal completion Local event processing failure Watermill handler metrics and outer failure log Retries are in memory; no durable recovery guarantee Scheduled publication failure scheduled task enqueue failed with safe category Publication success does not prove task completion Notification delivery backlog Delivery attempts and oldest unsettled intent age Age is sampled by a running dispatcher; no per-recipient labels Shutdown budget exceeded application shutdown timed out Does not forcibly stop handlers Trace collector failure Rate-limited trace export failed; batch discarded Exported spans may be lost; buffer overflow also drops spans Dependency unavailability Optional /readyz probes Connectivity only; not schema or workflow validation Tracing uses an asynchronous bounded buffer. A regression test blocks a real OTLP HTTP endpoint, fills the production batch buffer, and completes 4,096 additional instrumented HTTP operations while export remains blocked. Separate tests cover collector error-response redaction and bounded shutdown. This verifies isolation for those paths, not a throughput benchmark or a promise of lossless telemetry."},{"Title":"Observability","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Verify a custom signal","Parent":"","URL":"observability.html#verify-a-custom-signal","Text":"When adding a business span or metric: Create the instrument once during construction and inject it into its consumer. Exercise one successful and one failed operation with real operation contexts. Check the signal name, attributes, units, and correlation in the intended backend. Disable the corresponding exporter and verify business behavior still works. Check that IDs, credentials, payloads, and raw provider errors are absent from metric labels and routine span/error fields. Use stable attributes whose possible values you can enumerate. A telemetry timeout or missing export is not evidence that the business action failed. Continue with logging, application lifecycle, and deployment recovery."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html","Text":"Locate the stage that failed before retrying the whole operation. Keep the request ID, safe error code, process role and task/delivery ID when available. Do not include tokens, OTPs, recipient payloads, environment dumps or connection credentials in a report. Configuration and transport errors deliberately omit sensitive values."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Pick the failing boundary","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#pick-the-failing-boundary","Text":"What you observe Start here Process cannot construct or CLI help fails Configuration and startup 401, 422, missing sign-in route Authentication and HTTP Accepted task never executes Queue state Notification intent exists but nothing arrives Delivery ledger Reaction missing after an event Local event guarantees Wrong asset URL or missing bytes Media and storage Missing logs, trace, or SQL text Observability Pod stays unready or termination times out Lifecycle"},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Configuration or startup fails","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#configuration-or-startup-fails","Text":"Use the same profile in each terminal. Existing shell values override dotenv; changing .env does not change a running process. APP_ENV_FILE='' disables file loading, while an explicit path must exist. Defaults and supported keys are in the configuration reference. Symptom Check and next action A dependency opens unexpectedly Check the selected profile and overrides for that capability. The full defaults select MinIO; quickstart disables it. DATABASE_URL is required Installed modules need PostgreSQL. The component-only Asynq profile cannot serve them without a database. Database or Redis connection refused Match the host/port to where the process runs. Container service names work inside their network; host processes need published ports. Missing tables or columns Run migrate status against the intended database. Apply pending migrations with migrate up. Rewritten development baselines do not upgrade an older schema. Conflicting migration versions or dialects Check all registered sources and timestamp versions. Installed modules use PostgreSQL. Discard failed startup; fix source registration before retrying. CLI help fails Help uses full application construction. Fix settings or resource access first; it is not an offline configuration checker. Address already in use Stop the other local process or change its listener. Give separate host processes different metrics ports too. Invalid Redis isolation Queue, cache and limiter cannot use the same address/database pair. Namespaces alone do not satisfy this isolation check. Read-only filesystem or permission denied Select stdout/stderr or supply a writable log/storage mount with the process UID/GID. Relative paths start at the process working directory. Read-only diagnostics, after the application can construct: go run ./cmd/cli migrate status go run ./cmd/cli route list curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz Run those commands with the same selected profile as the failing process. The quickstart disables authentication, push, and storage; mail is always constructed and connects on send. A quickstart check does not diagnose an enabled full profile. Do not use migrate refresh to repair a database containing data you need: it deletes the configured schema's objects/data. Choose a forward migration or an explicitly disposable database instead."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Authentication fails or a route is missing","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#authentication-fails-or-a-route-is-missing","Text":"Symptom Meaning and next action OTP routes return 404 Enable AUTH_ENABLED, configure its required dependencies and restart. The unified OTP flow has no separate registration/email-verification endpoint. Protected route returns 401 Use an application access token and an active session. Disabled auth fails closed; an X-User-ID, Google token or refresh token is not an app bearer token. Code is rejected Use the challenge ID returned by the latest sign-in-code request and its matching six-digit code, including leading zeroes. Check expiry, resend, attempt limits and prior consumption. OTP send returns 503 Inspect mail and Redis availability. Identity fails closed when its shared admission limiter is unavailable. A failed send can still be subject to cooldown and budgets. Request returns 429 Respect retry metadata and cooldown. Do not flush limiter keys or rotate the verification key to bypass the budget. Google returns 409 The email already belongs to an account. Sign in through its existing method; automatic linking and a linking endpoint are not implemented. New Google account returns 422 Check the safe validation response. The current provider policy can require email OTP for addresses whose ownership Google is not authoritative for. Refresh returns 401 The token can be expired, revoked, malformed or already rotated. Concurrent refresh/retrying a used token can revoke the entire session. JSON returns 400, 413 or 422 Follow the endpoint schema: one object, known fields, valid values and bounded body. Do not retry an unchanged invalid payload. Serialize refresh calls per session. Persist a successful replacement before the next refresh; never keep retrying an old refresh token after an uncertain result. Recover with a new sign-in when the session is invalid. Access tokens expire after seven days by default, while refresh tokens and sessions have no absolute time limit by default. Revocation and account state still apply. See Identity and the API contract. OTP send returns an accepted challenge; account creation occurs when OTP or social proof succeeds. Verification returns 201 for a new account and 200 for an existing account. After either status, inspect data.user.profile_complete and use PATCH /users/me for profile completion. There is no second registration request or verification-unavailable signup warning. Neither sign-in response confirms a background welcome reaction completed."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Queued work does not run","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#queued-work-does-not-run","Text":"Use the always-available queue commands to inspect the actual queue and task ID returned at enqueue: go run ./cmd/cli queue list go run ./cmd/cli queue inspect default TASK_ID Inspection can expose saved payloads; keep output private. Check in this order: Producer and worker use the same Redis address and database number; confirm both process profiles. The worker consumes the task's queue; check QUEUE_DEFAULT, QUEUE_ROUTES and the named priorities. A per-task queue option can override routing. A worker process is running, and the queue is not paused. HTTP-only processes enqueue work but do not execute it. The task is due and its type is registered. Every consumer of app.command must register the requested command factory; every consumer of mail.send.v1 needs valid SMTP settings. Inspect retry/archived state and the safe error. Fix dependencies or invalid payloads before choosing replay. Successful tasks normally disappear unless retained. queue replay QUEUE ID moves eligible work to pending and can repeat effects. It does not reset retry history. First establish whether the previous attempt committed or sent anything. Do not delete tasks or flush Redis as a diagnostic step. See failures and retries. For recurring work, run one cmd/scheduler process. cmd/worker never starts cron scheduling. Central schedules also have feature conditions; command handlers are registered independently of scheduler execution. Calendar expressions use UTC, and missed ticks are not replayed. See scheduling."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Notifications are pending, failed or duplicated","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#notifications-are-pending-failed-or-duplicated","Text":"Notification intent is stored in PostgreSQL before a dispatcher submits delivery IDs to Redis. A returned receipt inside a transaction still depends on commit. First inspect the ledger rather than assuming the notification is an ordinary task: go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status pending go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status awaiting_receipt go run ./cmd/cli notifications deliveries --status failed go run ./cmd/cli notifications delivery DELIVERY_ID Pending work needs a dispatcher and worker using the same database and queue. Workers run the dispatcher without cron. notifications dispatch is a manual enqueue operation; it does not deliver synchronously. Sending rows are leased. Let an expired lease recover; do not manually overwrite state while a worker may still own it. Pending rows can be waiting for their next automatic attempt; inspect the due time. Failed rows need operator review. Use notifications retry DELIVERY_ID after correcting the cause and accepting possible duplicate provider effects. An omitted key creates a new occurrence on every Enqueue call. Use the same business occurrence key across retries to reuse saved channel intents. A repeated key retains the first snapshot; editing a template does not rewrite existing delivery content. Database, email and push channels progress independently. Email success means SMTP accepted the operation, not inbox placement. A network failure after acceptance leaves uncertainty. Ordinary Mailer.Enqueue is tracked in Asynq, not the notification ledger; use queue inspection for those sends. Expo awaiting_receipt is normal after ticket acceptance. The dispatcher starts receipt checks after 15 minutes and polls missing receipts without resending. An ok receipt confirms provider handoff, not device display. After 24 hours an unconfirmed result becomes a failed uncertain delivery; manual retry may duplicate. Check the current subscription, project configuration and client permissions using the push guide. Explicit logout detaches session-bound subscriptions; already admitted sends cannot be retracted."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"A local event disappeared","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#a-local-event-disappeared","Text":"GoChannel delivers only within the publishing process. A CLI process cannot notify subscribers in an API/worker process. Register local handlers before producers start, and keep subscribers with each producer role. Router retry middleware bounds one processing cycle; a subsequent Nack can trigger GoChannel redelivery while the process is alive. It is not a global attempt cap. There is no configured failed.events dead-letter route or durable replay store. Restart and process failure can lose in-memory events. For the existing welcome example, an operator can enqueue the stable welcome task with identity welcome USER_ID. For business work that must survive failure, persist notification intent in the business transaction or design an explicit outbox. Adding a broker container alone changes none of these guarantees. See events and transactional notifications."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Media upload, download or cleanup fails","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#media-upload-download-or-cleanup-fails","Text":"Check both PostgreSQL metadata and the disk selected by the asset. A configured definition alone is not an opened disk: use the default or additional-disk list. Keep disk names stable for existing records; changing the default does not move previous objects. Authentication supplies user ownership; other model types need business-authorized handlers. The default HTTP upload accepts exactly one file and optional visibility, with a 20 MiB file limit and bounded multipart overhead. Authenticated downloads require a ready asset belonging to the caller; the content route allows public assets or valid temporary links. A ready metadata row with a missing object points to backing-storage or restore consistency, not a reason to reassign ownership. Interrupted uploads and deletions retain recoverable metadata. Run media recover or the central recovery schedule, with storage available. Recovery leaves recent uploads alone and processes bounded batches; it is not a bucket inventory cleaner. Back up metadata and objects together. See Media."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"The returned asset URL does not work","Parent":"Media upload, download or cleanup fails","URL":"troubleshooting.html#the-returned-asset-url-does-not-work","Text":"Symptom Check Direct URL contains an internal container hostname Configure a client-reachable storage endpoint; signatures cannot be repaired by rewriting the host afterward Public provider URL returns access denied Public-read policy must cover the asset's public prefix; the app does not change bucket policy Private link worked earlier Its expiry; request a fresh authorized temporary link Internal media URL points to the wrong origin MEDIA_BASE_URL for this deployment Changing the default disk did not move old assets Each record keeps its disk; migration is an explicit operation Old public asset still uses an internal URL Legacy object prefixes retain the internal fallback See Media for direct versus internal URLs and visibility. A direct provider URL bypasses application response headers and may have provider/CDN cache behavior independent of metadata deletion."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Request diagnostics are missing","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#request-diagnostics-are-missing","Text":"Start with the request's response header or body ID: {service_name=\"labena\"} | request_id=\"YOUR_REQUEST_ID\" Use your APP_NAME as service_name if it differs. In Grafana Explore's Builder, add a Label filter for request_id; it is structured metadata, not an indexed stream label. New OTLP records need no JSON parser. Symptom Next check No matching log Time range, application identity, selected observability channel, collector reachability request_id_extracted or level_extracted Remove an unnecessary JSON parser; old file records and new OTLP metadata use different shapes Trace link exists but trace does not Sampling, export loss, retention, and selected Tempo instance INSERT span has duration but no text SQL sanitization/size limits; use current code and a newly recorded trace Query text missing from log records Expected: sanitized SQL lives in Tempo's db.query.text, not Loki INFO missing only from local file Built-in file channel defaults to WARN Follow request → log → trace → SQL. Do not enable raw payload or SQL-value logging to diagnose these boundaries."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Readiness or shutdown does not finish","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#readiness-or-shutdown-does-not-finish","Text":"Readiness is a startup signal unless dependency probing is enabled. A green ready response does not establish schema correctness, SMTP delivery or cloud health. During shutdown it becomes unavailable before admission stops. Worker-only processes have no business HTTP readiness endpoint. Shutdown stops admission, drains workers then local subscribers, stops metrics and closes owned resources. A handler that ignores cancellation can exhaust the shared drain budget. The app then reports failure and refuses to close dependencies still in use; it cannot forcibly stop a goroutine. Give the deployment enough termination time for both shutdown and cleanup, and ensure the executable receives the signal. Fix unbounded work or an unreachable dependency before increasing timeouts. Check application lifecycle and observability for correlation and bounded diagnostics. Recovery after forced termination must still tolerate incomplete operations and duplicate task delivery."},{"Title":"Troubleshooting","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Prepare a useful issue report","Parent":"","URL":"troubleshooting.html#prepare-a-useful-issue-report","Text":"Include the operation and expected result, source revision, environment/role, relevant non-secret feature switches, HTTP status and safe error type, request ID, and task/delivery ID where applicable. State whether the problem reproduces after restart and which backend check failed. Attach a minimal sanitized request or task shape only when needed. Remove access and refresh tokens, OTPs, connection URLs with credentials, provider payloads, and full configuration dumps. Report uncertain effects explicitly—for example, “SMTP acceptance is unknown”—so recovery does not accidentally duplicate them."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html","Text":"This repository is distributed as a modular Go application template. The support policy defines latest-release maintenance, compatibility and verification scope. The adoption guide explains module renaming and updates for customized applications. The following gates distinguish verified behavior from remaining release work; a passing working-copy check is not a published release."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Verification matrix","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#verification-matrix","Text":"Capability Required evidence / current gate Go behavior and lifecycle Fast CI race tests and vet with the go.mod toolchain Commands and examples CI builds cmd and examples packages PostgreSQL module queries and migrations Required disposable PostgreSQL integration job Asynq, Redis cache and rate limits Required disposable Redis integration job GoChannel Local integration tests; explicitly ephemeral, not durable delivery HTTP contract and docs Live OpenAPI contract tests, compiled guide examples, and regenerated documentation site Reachable dependency vulnerabilities Pinned govulncheck tool; advisory database remains current Generic MySQL, Redis Cluster, cloud storage Not required core CI; validate separately before claiming support Live Google sign-in, SMTP providers Local/unit coverage does not establish live provider compatibility"},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Build a local release bundle","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#build-a-local-release-bundle","Text":"The same deployments/Dockerfile serves Tilt, Compose and release verification. Its Go builder is pinned by digest, refuses automatic toolchain downloads, verifies module checksums and builds with readonly dependencies. The non-root runtime contains the four application commands (app, cli, worker and scheduler), CA certificates and the project and Go licenses. Time-zone data is embedded; there is no shell or package manager. Keep /tmp writable and mount writable storage when using file logs or local media. Select configuration explicitly, including APP_ENV_FILE='' in managed deployments. Use HTTP probes rather than shell probes; run /app/cli directly for operator commands. From a stable checkout, with Docker, Git, Go and shasum available: ./scripts/build-release.sh build/release-candidate linux/amd64 # Use linux/arm64 for an ARM deployment. The output directory must be new. The build command requires a clean checkout with a version tag pointing at HEAD (for example v0.1.0) and does not publish anything. It injects that version and commit into all four binaries. Local builds without those arguments report dev. It saves: app, cli, worker, scheduler and binaries.sha256; image identity/configuration and each binary's embedded build/module information; source-file checksums, Git revision, version.txt and working-tree status; the full selected module graph, target-specific license report and copied notices. The pinned license tool runs on the host while inspecting the selected Linux target. Keep notices with any binary/image distribution; the application image itself only contains project and Go notices. Review the report and its warnings, including files the scanner cannot inspect. The module path is github.com/wisalgroup/labena; while the upstream repository is private, unauthenticated tools cannot resolve its source/license URL. Record the upstream repository/tag and retain the local project license in the release evidence. Independently publishing importable Go packages would require accessible source and a separate API support declaration. License generation does not establish provider compatibility or expand the support policy. Tools and evidence belong to this local bundle, not the app's runtime or go.mod. Use the immutable identity in image.id for subsequent tagging/export. Keep the bundle with the final published digest, test results and release notes. A local image ID is not a registry publication or signature. Repeat the build from the same source and toolchain, then compare binaries.sha256. Matching binaries do not imply byte-identical OCI archives; image metadata and exporter settings also affect archive identity. Before publication, require a clean tagged checkout. A dirty checkout's Git revision alone cannot identify what was tested; source.sha256 records the actual build inputs."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"How to read the recorded evidence","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#how-to-read-the-recorded-evidence","Text":"The dated sections below preserve historical verification. They describe the source and fixtures tested at that time, including behavior that may since have changed. They are not certification of the current working tree, current provider compatibility, or current vulnerability status. For a new release candidate, create a new evidence record rather than replacing old checksums or silently carrying forward earlier passes. Record the commit/tag, source checksums, toolchain, architecture, backend versions, commands, results, skips, and operational limitations. The testing guide gives the repeatable core gate; deployment-specific recovery and provider checks remain separate."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Version 0.2 verification on 2026-10-19","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#version-02-verification-on-2026-10-19","Text":"The v0.2.0 source adds the reversible push-subscription table rename. Local verification used Go 1.27.2 on macOS ARM64 with disposable PostgreSQL 17 and standalone Redis 7. The release's exact source commit, tagged build evidence and publication checks belong to the GitHub release. Check Result Required integration gate scripts/verify-integration.sh passed the complete race-enabled suite with all four PostgreSQL/Redis test settings supplied Retained-state migration Original schema populated with active and disabled installations and pending push deliveries; upgrade, downgrade and re-upgrade preserved every stored column; queued delivery selected the original eligible device; refresh retained ID/version and active-token uniqueness held Fresh schema Existing notification and Identity suites applied the full history, including the rename, before exercising registration, delivery, logout and pruning Go checks Vet, command/example builds and go mod tidy -diff passed Installer distribution A locally packaged module version installed outside the checkout and generated MyApp with .env.example, all example profiles, private .env, provenance, rebuilt docs and passing build/configuration/architecture checks Documentation Builder tests, all 58 generated pages and browser navigation/search/mobile/fallback checks passed using local Chrome Vulnerabilities Pinned govulncheck v1.8.0 found no reachable or imported-package findings; one module-only advisory affects unused code The original notification migration is unchanged. This upgrade requires all old application processes to stop before migration; mixed-version rolling deployment is not supported. See the upgrade and rollback procedure. These tests do not establish live Expo/SMTP/Google/cloud compatibility, MySQL or Redis Cluster support, production load capacity, or deployment-specific recovery. No application service is deployed by documentation publication."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Candidate hardening verification on 2026-10-18","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#candidate-hardening-verification-on-2026-10-18","Text":"The local v0.1.0 candidate was checked with Go 1.27.2 and disposable PostgreSQL 17 and standalone Redis 7. The release bundle records its exact source revision, version and artifact identities; this source record does not announce publication. Check Result Full required integration gate scripts/verify-integration.sh passed, including go test -race -count=1 ./... Queue dashboard regression Authenticated cross-origin mutations return 403; same-origin mutations still work. A Chrome HTML-form reproduction also returned 403 without pausing the queue Subscription maintenance Concurrent refresh remains active; cleanup skips locked rows and preserves the 1,000-row batch limit and idempotency Trusted ingress Untrusted forwarding claims cannot alter budgets; valid trusted chains separate clients; malformed, duplicate and excessive trusted chains fail closed Static and build checks go vet ./..., go mod tidy -diff, command/example builds and documentation tests passed Documentation All 56 pages regenerated with local link validation Vulnerability scan No reachable vulnerabilities or affected imported packages; one module-only advisory concerns unused OpenPGP code Build metadata A Linux ARM64 image reported injected version/revision through the CLI; source runs report dev No schema or task-payload migration is required for these hardening fixes. The adoption changes and rollout considerations are in the update guide. pkg/mapper, the reserved phone-auth switch and the app's infrastructure file remain in place. Notification records and media tombstones retain their existing state until an explicit owner-defined retention migration. Live provider compatibility, production ingress configuration, sustained load, disaster recovery and the GitHub reporting channel still require deployment- or publication-specific evidence. This local gate does not establish those outcomes."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Local verification on 2026-10-11","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#local-verification-on-2026-10-11","Text":"This is development-template evidence, not a published release. Go 1.27.2 and golang.org/x/net v0.60.0 replace versions flagged by the reachable-vulnerability scan. See the Go patch notes and networking advisory. Exercise Observed result Required core integration Full suite passed with race detection against disposable PostgreSQL 17 and Redis 7; all four required test endpoints configured Static verification Vet and command/example builds passed on Go 1.27.2 Release bundle Script completed for Linux ARM64; a second compilation with go build -a in the pinned builder produced identical app, cli and worker binaries Vulnerability scan No reachable vulnerabilities and no affected imported packages; four module-only findings remain in unused OpenPGP and Chi middleware packages Dependency inventory 490 selected modules including this application; Linux ARM64 license report has 171 entries: 69 Apache-2.0, 48 MIT, 41 BSD-3-Clause, 12 BSD-2-Clause, one MPL-2.0 (MySQL driver) Fresh installation All seven migrations applied; HTTP registration returned 201; CLI account read and local media upload/list succeeded; welcome event → job → database notification completed Database outage Pausing the isolated PostgreSQL container produced readiness 503 in about one second, while liveness remained 200; unpausing restored readiness 200 Redis outage The same readiness/liveness/recovery behavior was observed with the isolated Redis container paused Kubernetes termination Deleting the test pod invoked graceful shutdown and exited 0 in 0.414 seconds within a 70-second grace period; no active long-running request/job at deletion Database and media restore A PostgreSQL custom dump restored into a separate empty database; the account, notification and media record survived; copied local object bytes matched their SHA-256 Queue restore and replay A quiesced Redis RDB containing one pending welcome job restored into a separate Redis container; workers drained it and the existing welcome notification remained a single record The deployment exercise used Linux ARM64, Docker Desktop 29.6.1 and Kubernetes 1.36.1. The temporary namespace ran as UID/GID 10001 with a read-only root filesystem, no service-account token, dropped capabilities, writable temporary/local-storage volumes and separate startup, liveness and dependency-readiness probes. Authentication, mail, push, scheduling and external providers were disabled for that fixture. Existing integration checks cover authentication independently. The recorded ARM64 binary checksums are: Command SHA-256 app b896fb1876cb84894e17611476fdd6a95b9011e5b5c32f3e91cf1a4fa2fb8cf0 cli 4fa69dbcb90d1888c6eeebaaf3a090801eb19186fecf9f63a70dbff582276fea worker bc36fb23e1123768aaca30a6149dd01a3f9712c824e10493ddc4f2ab6498ceda These identify this working-copy verification only. Regenerate all evidence after changing the module path or any build input. The source scan and compiled Linux application scan both passed; that result is dated, not a permanent vulnerability guarantee. The shared image build is now a CI gate; local runtime evidence is ARM64, and other architectures still require their own runtime checks. The cluster's kubectl exec transport still fails with an HTTP/HTTPS mismatch. HTTP exercises used the Kubernetes API proxy; operator commands used Docker against only the test container. No cluster settings were changed. This does not certify kubectl exec or port forwarding as healthy. Restore used stopped producers/workers and a local object snapshot with no concurrent writers. Redis used RDB for this exercise; AOF, Redis Cluster, point-in-time database recovery and cloud object restore need separate rehearsals. The restore target used a separate database on the disposable PostgreSQL instance and a new Redis container. It was a Docker process, not a Kubernetes rollout. No production RTO/RPO, sustained-load drain, live provider delivery or historical-release upgrade guarantee was measured. Run scripts/verify-integration.sh with all four test connection settings from .github/workflows/verify.yml. Missing or empty settings fail before tests run. Credentials must point to disposable services. PostgreSQL and Redis are the required matrix; unrelated optional suites can still report skips. A successful fast job alone is not sufficient for release."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Platform harmonization verification — 2026-10-11","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#platform-harmonization-verification--2026-10-11","Text":"After the release-bundle exercise above, the harmonization changes passed the full race-enabled PostgreSQL/Redis integration gate, vet, command/example builds, focused regression checks, the shared Linux AMD64 Docker image build and the documentation builder. A second site build produced identical output. The image was built locally; no registry publication or Linux AMD64 runtime/load check was performed in this pass. The 51 published URLs, API schema, migrations, dependency versions and configuration profiles are retained. The pinned vulnerability scan again found no reachable or imported-package findings; four module-only advisories remain. No external provider delivery was attempted. Regression coverage now includes safe HTTP diagnostics, transaction-effect guards, bounded SMTP attachment reads and cleanup, selected configuration validation, central subscription maintenance and retry-cycle diagnostics. Existing tests cover active HTTP, queue and subscriber drain; no new Kubernetes termination, sustained production load or disaster-recovery measurement was made for this patch. Existing installations must deploy pruning-capable workers before enabling the new hourly schedule; see scheduling. Mail's aggregate attachment limit defaults to 20 MiB; see mail for ownership and retry semantics. Standalone named storage uses configuration selection plus storage.Open. The previous binary hashes and release bundle above identify the earlier source, not this patch. Regenerate the bundle before publication."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Before publication","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#before-publication","Text":"Record the upstream repository URL and publish the selected template adoption model, module-renaming instructions and support policy. Enable GitHub private vulnerability reporting, verify the reporting entry point and assign report monitoring to the repository owner or a delegated maintainer; update the pending setup status in SECURITY.md only after verification. Publish release notes covering fixes, compatibility changes and concrete update steps according to the release-note contract. Record a tagged source revision, toolchain, dependency inventory and final license review, including non-Go runtime inputs such as the CA certificate bundle. Retain release bundles, notices and checksums; verify each published architecture. Run fresh installation tests. For later releases, state and test the supported upgrade starting points, including retained schema and queued work. This evidence does not promise automatic merging of customized application source. Rehearse database/Redis backup and restore and document measured recovery outcomes. Exercise process termination and dependency outages in the actual deployment target. Measure performance before advertising throughput, latency or resource claims. The support policy applies to future published releases; no historical release upgrade evidence is implied by the dated checks above. Do not rewrite applied migrations in a deployed application; publish versioned upgrade notes for schema, configuration, task names, event contracts and public API changes. Adopters port fixes into their own source, following the update workflow. CI validates code and configured test backends. It does not prove disaster recovery, cloud behavior, deployment security, exactly-once execution or lossless telemetry. Maintain this matrix when adding a driver or capability; an interface alone is not evidence that a replacement has the same guarantees."},{"Title":"Release readiness","Section":"Operations","Heading":"Assemble the next release evidence","Parent":"","URL":"release-readiness.html#assemble-the-next-release-evidence","Text":"Evidence Re-run or record for the candidate Source identity Upstream repository URL, clean tagged revision, github.com/wisalgroup/labena module path, exact build inputs Core behavior Race suite with all required PostgreSQL/Redis endpoints; inspect skips Build and static checks Vet, commands/examples, target image, documentation checks Public contract Live OpenAPI and schema/task/event/configuration compatibility notes Operational behavior Active-work drain, dependency outage, restart/recovery on the target Retained data Backup/restore rehearsal with measured loss/time and matched asset bytes Dependencies Current reachable-vulnerability and license review Distribution Checksums, notices, release notes, support/security contacts A failure or an unperformed check remains explicit in the release record. “The interface supports a driver” and “this deployment was verified with that driver” are different statements. Publish only the support scope backed by the candidate's evidence. Continue with deployment and testing."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html","Text":"The tables list the environment settings decoded by internal/app/config. Defaults come from config.Defaults() without reading files, opening connections or starting services. Tests check this reference against those defaults. The configuration guide explains loading and overrides. Unset APP_ENV_FILE loads an optional .env; a nonempty process value selects a required file; an explicitly empty value disables dotenv loading. Set this selector outside the file being selected. Existing process variables win, even when empty; empty decoded fields with an envDefault still use that default. unset below means an empty value, not a generated credential. Lists use commas. Durations use Go units such as 250ms, 15m or 168h, not 7d. Callbacks, native clients and definitions of named queues, disks and logging channels remain Go values. Unknown environment keys are ignored: obsolete names are not aliases. Update settings and restart processes; there is no live reload. The quickstart overrides several defaults, including disabling storage. Full .env.example is a reference, not a ready production profile. Container addresses, secrets and enabled features must match the deployment. These tables describe the application adapter; independent pkg clients may expose additional native options."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Find the setting you need","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#find-the-setting-you-need","Text":"Task Reference Usage guide Tune process budgets Application and logging below Lifecycle Connect SQL or tune its pool Database Connections Configure listener, CORS, or docs HTTP and CORS Routing Enable cache Optional cache Cache Run workers and recurring work Queue and event settings Queues, scheduling Send mail or push Mail and push settings Mail, push Choose asset visibility and URLs Filesystems and media settings Media Investigate a request Tracing/metrics plus log channels Observability The tables below are the complete environment reference. Usage guides explain when to change a value; keeping the Go default is usually enough. Avoid copying every row into .env: publish connection information, credentials, intentional feature choices, and the overrides your deployment actually needs."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Application and logging","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#application-and-logging","Text":"Key Default APP_NAME labena APP_ENV development APP_STARTUP_TIMEOUT 30s APP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT 45s APP_CLEANUP_TIMEOUT 10s MIGRATION_TIMEOUT 5m LOG_CHANNELS stderr OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT http://localhost:4318/v1/logs LOG_VIEWER_ENABLED false LOG_VIEWER_USERNAME unset LOG_VIEWER_PASSWORD unset LOG_VIEWER_BUFFER_ENTRIES 2000 LOG_VIEWER_BUFFER_BYTES 8388608 Process selection lives in each main.go as explicit app.Processes flags. HTTP and workers automatically include their local event consumers; a separate cmd/scheduler runs recurring schedules. See entry points. Startup and cleanup have separate budgets; the shutdown groups share the drain budget. Queue shutdown must be shorter than app shutdown. Migration timeout includes lock acquisition; migrate --timeout overrides it. Set APP_ENV=production in production; migrate refresh is then rejected, including with --force. LOG_CHANNELS selects named definitions in config/observability.go: stderr, stdout, file and observability (OTLP/HTTP). Formats, paths and levels belong to those definitions. The observability channel exports directly to the logs endpoint; it does not write a file. Its service identity comes from APP_NAME and APP_ENV. The optional /admin/logs/ viewer needs both credentials and the HTTP process. It works without OTLP, tracing or metrics. Buffer entries must be 1–100000; the record-byte budget must be 65536–67108864. Keep it behind HTTPS and restrict ingress to operators. See the local viewer for scope and retention details. Relative paths resolve against the process working directory. See lifecycle and logging."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Database","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#database","Text":"Key Default DATABASE_NAME unset DATABASE_DRIVER postgres DATABASE_URL unset DATABASE_MAX_OPEN_CONNS 20 DATABASE_MAX_IDLE_CONNS 5 DATABASE_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME 30m DATABASE_CONN_MAX_IDLE_TIME 5m DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT 5s DATABASE_PING_TIMEOUT 5s DATABASE_SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD 500ms A nonempty DATABASE_URL opens a connection; serving the installed modules requires PostgreSQL and their applied migrations. DATABASE_NAME is an optional diagnostic name, not a replacement for the database selected by the URL. The reusable database package supports MySQL, but the installed Identity, Media and Notification schemas are PostgreSQL. Switching the driver does not translate their SQL. Budget pool limits across every API, worker and operator process. Startup validates and pings configured connections; migrations only run on explicit command. See database and migrations."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"HTTP and CORS","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#http-and-cors","Text":"Key Default HTTP_READINESS_DEPENDENCIES false HTTP_READINESS_TIMEOUT 1s HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES unset HTTP_DOCS_ENABLED true HTTP_ADDRESS :8080 HTTP_READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT 5s HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT 15s HTTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT 15s HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT 60s HTTP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT 10s HTTP_MAX_HEADER_BYTES 1048576 HTTP_CORS_ENABLED true HTTP_CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS * HTTP_CORS_ALLOW_METHODS GET,POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE,HEAD,OPTIONS HTTP_CORS_ALLOW_HEADERS Origin,Content-Type,Authorization HTTP_CORS_EXPOSE_HEADERS X-Request-ID,X-API-Version HTTP_CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS false HTTP_CORS_MAX_AGE 12h HTTP_DOCS_ENABLED=true serves /docs and /openapi.json from the registered operations. Disable it to hide both endpoints. No file generation is required. There is one application listener. HTTP_READINESS_DEPENDENCIES=true probes the configured database, queue Redis and limiter Redis under one shared deadline. Cache, SMTP and telemetry are excluded; /healthz never probes dependencies. A worker-only process has no HTTP health listener. See readiness. Origins, methods and header lists are comma-separated. Wildcard origins cannot be combined with credentialed CORS. For a browser that needs to read request IDs, X-Request-ID and X-API-Version are exposed by default through HTTP_CORS_EXPOSE_HEADERS. CORS is browser policy, not access control; bearer authorization still applies. See middleware. Administrative paths under /admin are excluded from business CORS. Both built-in dashboards require their separate Basic Auth credentials, reject cross-origin reads and mutations, disable caching and framing, and must be served over HTTPS outside local development. Preserve the external Host through your ingress. HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES is a comma-separated list of explicit IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs for controlled ingress proxies. It is empty by default; /0, hostnames and wildcard trust are rejected. Only a trusted immediate peer can supply X-Forwarded-For. The server walks the chain from right to left through trusted hops and selects the first untrusted address. It ignores Forwarded and X-Real-IP, never changes Host or scheme from forwarding headers, and rejects malformed or duplicate X-Forwarded-For fields from a trusted peer (maximum 32 addresses / 4096 bytes). A missing header falls back to the immediate peer. Secure the proxy-to-backend network and prevent untrusted workloads from using trusted source addresses. The public ingress must discard incoming forwarding claims and rebuild the chain; later trusted proxies append their observed peer. Use exact proxy ranges, not an entire shared cluster network. Client IP affects HTTP/OTP rate limits and session client metadata, never user authorization. See ingress and readiness."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Queues and local events","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#queues-and-local-events","Text":"Key Default QUEUE_DASHBOARD_ENABLED false QUEUE_DASHBOARD_USERNAME unset QUEUE_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD unset QUEUE_ROUTES unset QUEUE_REDIS_URL redis://127.0.0.1:6379/1 QUEUE_DEFAULT default QUEUE_CONCURRENCY 4 QUEUE_STRICT_PRIORITY false QUEUE_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT 15s QUEUE_DEFAULT_PRIORITY 1 QUEUE_HIGH_PRIORITY 3 EVENTS_DRIVER gochannel EVENTS_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT 10s QUEUE_ROUTES maps exact task names to configured queues, for example identity.welcome.v1=high,mail.send.v1=default. Unlisted tasks use QUEUE_DEFAULT. The queue names are defined in config/queues.go; QUEUE_\u003cNAME\u003e_PRIORITY overrides each weight. Priorities must be positive. Concurrency is total active tasks per process, not per queue. Strict priority can starve lower-priority queues. Retries and per-attempt timeouts belong to task options, not global environment settings. Native retry/classification callbacks can be supplied in Go. QUEUE_WEIGHTS, QUEUE_NAME and database-queue settings are not supported. Command scheduling enables the app.command handler and allowlisted factories; the scheduler switch separately starts recurring enqueue. Enable command handling on every consuming worker. The notification dispatcher runs with workers without the cron scheduler. Administrative CLI commands and the authenticated Asynq HTTP dashboard are separately opt-in. Dashboard credentials are required when enabled. EVENTS_DRIVER accepts only gochannel. Events and poison messages are process-local and ephemeral. NATS/RabbitMQ development containers do not create application drivers. See tasks, scheduling and events."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Optional cache","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#optional-cache","Text":"Key Default CACHE_DRIVER disabled CACHE_NAMESPACE default CACHE_MAX_ENTRY_BYTES 1048576 CACHE_MEMORY_MAX_BYTES 67108864 CACHE_MEMORY_CAPACITY 10000 CACHE_REDIS_URL redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0 CACHE_DATABASE_TABLE cache_entries CACHE_OPERATION_TIMEOUT 5s Only the selected cache driver's settings are validated. Redis/database zero limits and timeouts resolve to their owner defaults; memory requires positive capacity and entry size, while zero memory byte budget selects 64 MiB. Disabled cache and unselected driver settings do not require operational configuration. Selected namespace/table mistakes fail before connections open. Drivers are disabled, memory, database and redis. Memory budgets apply per process. Database cache uses the configured Bun pool; publish its table with go run ./cmd/cli cache tables, then apply it with migrate up. Changing CACHE_DATABASE_TABLE does not rename an existing table. Expired SQL rows need explicit PruneExpired work; no cleaner starts automatically. Use an explicit namespace per app/environment. Queue, cache and authentication limits must not share a Redis database: configuration rejects matching configured address/database pairs. Different DB numbers do not isolate resource pressure or outages. Do not flush Redis to resolve task or authentication failures. See cache."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Mail and mail views","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#mail-and-mail-views","Text":"Key Default MAIL_HOST sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io MAIL_PORT 2525 MAIL_USERNAME unset MAIL_PASSWORD unset MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS no-reply@example.com MAIL_FROM_NAME unset MAIL_BRAND_NAME unset MAIL_ACCENT_COLOR #1D4ED8 MAIL_LOGO_URL unset MAIL_WEBSITE_URL https://example.com MAIL_SUPPORT_EMAIL support@example.com MAIL_FOOTER Account updates, thoughtfully delivered. Unset or blank MAIL_FROM_NAME and MAIL_BRAND_NAME inherit APP_NAME. MAIL_LOGO_URL is optional; without it, mail uses text branding. Immediate SMTP attachments default to 20 MiB total raw bytes. Override config.MailConfig.MaxAttachmentBytes in Go if needed; zero uses the default. Queued mail still rejects attachment readers and limits its encoded message to 64 KiB. SMTP options use the same pure validator as standalone mail. The sender address is required by app; standalone SMTP may receive From per message. Mail is always wired into the app. Startup constructs a verified STARTTLS SMTP sender, wraps it with the existing optional Asynq enqueue capability, and registers mail.send.v1. Sending opens a connection; startup does not prove SMTP delivery. Set username and password together. SMTP and brand settings always validate; rendering and previews do not open a connection. Send is immediate; Enqueue stores a rendered snapshot in Redis. Both reject caller SQL transactions. OTP mail stays immediate. For transactional multi-channel intent, use Notifier.Enqueue. Replace example addresses, artwork and branding before real delivery. See mail and templates."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Identity authentication","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#identity-authentication","Text":"Key Default AUTH_GOOGLE_ENABLED false AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID unset AUTH_GOOGLE_TRUSTED_PRESENTER_IDS unset AUTH_ENABLED false AUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN_KEY unset AUTH_TOKEN_ISSUER go-modular AUTH_TOKEN_AUDIENCE go-modular-api AUTH_ACCESS_TTL 168h AUTH_REFRESH_TTL 0 AUTH_SESSION_TTL 0 AUTH_PHONE_VERIFICATION_ENABLED false AUTH_VERIFICATION_KEY unset AUTH_ENABLED enables the single email OTP signup/sign-in flow, refresh and session endpoints. It requires PostgreSQL, configured mail, both keys and the shared Redis limiter. Sending a code returns 202; confirming it returns 201 for a new account or 200 for an existing account, with the user and profile-completion state. Phone verification is not implemented; enabling it fails validation. The two keys must be independently generated, different base64-encoded 32-byte values. Store them as secrets and share them across replicas. Access signing-key replacement invalidates existing access tokens; overlapping keys are not supported. Verification-key replacement invalidates outstanding challenges and changes abuse budget keys. Never use example placeholders as credentials. Access lifetime must be 1 minute through 7 days (default: 168h). Zero refresh/session lifetime means no time-based expiry for that limit; rotation, logout and revocation still apply. Positive refresh/session bounds are 30/90 days respectively, with access no longer than either finite bound and refresh no longer than a finite session. Use 168h refresh and 720h session for seven-day inactivity and a 30-day absolute limit. Identity's SessionPolicy owns validation. Concurrent sessions are controlled by domain.AllowConcurrentSessions in Go. Google also requires authentication enabled and the configured client ID as token audience. Additional trusted presenter IDs authorize azp, not additional audiences. Provider linking is not implemented; matching an existing email returns a conflict. See Identity for exact routes and account behavior."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Rate limits","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#rate-limits","Text":"Key Default API_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED false RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL redis://127.0.0.1:6379/2 RATE_LIMIT_NAMESPACE go-modular API_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS 120 API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST 120 API_RATE_LIMIT_PERIOD 1m The broad /api/v1 limiter is opt-in. Identity still requires its narrower service limits whenever auth or email verification is enabled. Redis failures fail closed; limits are not bypassed. Namespace must match across replicas. Counts describe a burst and gradual replenishment over the period. Health routes are exempt. See Identity rate limits."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Notifications and push","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#notifications-and-push","Text":"Key Default NOTIFICATION_DISPATCH_INTERVAL 1s PUSH_ENABLED false PUSH_EXPO_PROJECT_ID unset PUSH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN unset Notification dispatch runs in worker processes; zero interval selects one second, and a positive interval must be at least 100ms. Persistent delivery requires PostgreSQL, Redis and a worker; adding email or push requires the corresponding sender. Dispatch is independent of the cron scheduler. Push is opt-in and uses Expo. A project UUID is required when enabled; the access token is required by Expo when enhanced push security is enabled. Keep provider credentials only on the server. Receipt polling uses the existing dispatcher and worker, not a separate container. See notifications and push for subscriptions, receipts and limits."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Optional tracing and metrics","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#optional-tracing-and-metrics","Text":"Key Default TRACING_TRUST_INCOMING false TRACING_ENABLED false OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT http://localhost:4318/v1/traces TRACING_SAMPLE_RATIO 1 TRACING_EXPORT_TIMEOUT 5s TRACING_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT 3s METRICS_ENABLED false METRICS_ADDRESS 127.0.0.1:9090 Tracing and metrics are independent. The trace endpoint is a complete OTLP HTTP traces URL. In containers, localhost means that container; select a reachable collector. Trust incoming propagation only at an intentional trusted boundary. APP_NAME supplies both telemetry identities. Metrics use a separate listener. Give host processes distinct addresses and keep metrics private. Tracing export failures do not fail business operations; bounded buffers may lose spans. See observability."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Filesystems","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#filesystems","Text":"Key Default FILESYSTEM_ADDITIONAL_DISKS unset FILESYSTEM_DISK minio FILESYSTEM_DISK selects a named definition from config/filesystems.go, not a driver. disabled omits the default disk. FILESYSTEM_ADDITIONAL_DISKS explicitly opens other configured names even when the default is disabled. Unselected disks stay unopened. Definitions are local, s3, minio, spaces, gcs and azure; MinIO and Spaces use the S3 driver. Adding a named disk in Go exposes its prefixed fields. For each definition, substitute its uppercase name for \u003cNAME\u003e below. The selected driver uses only relevant fields: local uses ROOT; S3 uses BUCKET, REGION, ENDPOINT, USE_PATH_STYLE and its key fields; GCS uses BUCKET and CREDENTIALS_FILE; Azure uses BUCKET, ENDPOINT, ACCOUNT_NAME and ACCOUNT_KEY. Other fields are ignored. Key Default FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_VISIBILITY public FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_URL_MODE internal FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_PUBLIC_URL unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_ROOT storage/app FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_BUCKET unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_REGION us-east-1 FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_ENDPOINT unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_USE_PATH_STYLE false FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_ACCESS_KEY_ID unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_SESSION_TOKEN unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_CREDENTIALS_FILE unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_ACCOUNT_NAME unset FILESYSTEM_\u003cNAME\u003e_ACCOUNT_KEY unset MinIO overrides the generic defaults with these development fixtures: Key Default FILESYSTEM_MINIO_BUCKET uploads FILESYSTEM_MINIO_ENDPOINT http://127.0.0.1:9000 FILESYSTEM_MINIO_USE_PATH_STYLE true FILESYSTEM_MINIO_ACCESS_KEY_ID demo FILESYSTEM_MINIO_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY local-demo-minio S3, GCS and Azure support their native credential chains when explicit credentials are absent. Mount credential files read-only. Cloud buckets/containers must already exist; Compose/Tilt storage setup creates the development MinIO bucket. Host MinIO uses the address above; containers use http://minio:9000. Local storage needs a writable root and deliberate shared storage for multiple replicas. Changing a disk name or location does not migrate existing assets. See filesystems and Media."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Diagnose configuration failures","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#diagnose-configuration-failures","Text":"Start with the selected file, existing process overrides and the relevant section above. Avoid dumping an entire environment or connection URL into a bug report. CLI help also constructs the application and can fail on unavailable resources. Use the troubleshooting guide to locate the failing stage and choose a recovery action."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Media URLs","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#media-urls","Text":"Variable Default MEDIA_BASE_URL http://localhost:8080/api/v1/media MEDIA_SIGNING_KEY unset MEDIA_BASE_URL is the browser-reachable media route prefix, not a bucket URL. Set it to your HTTPS API origin and /api/v1/media in production. An absolute path is also supported for same-origin clients. Never derive it from request headers. MEDIA_SIGNING_KEY is an optional independent base64-encoded 32-byte secret shared across replicas. It enables temporary links; without it, public URLs and authenticated private downloads still work. Rotation invalidates existing temporary links."},{"Title":"Configuration reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Check a configuration change","Parent":"","URL":"configuration-reference.html#check-a-configuration-change","Text":"After editing a profile, restart the affected process and verify the capability's public boundary: a readiness probe, representative request, queue inspection, or provider test appropriate to the change. Environment values do not hot-reload. Do not print a full decoded config to diagnose it. DSNs, provider tokens, signing keys, and SMTP credentials require private handling even when some structs omit them from JSON. CLI help constructs resources too; it is not a connection-free validation command. When adding or renaming a setting in code, update this reference and its focused check: go test ./internal/app/config -run TestConfigurationReferenceMatchesDefaults Then verify the examples, Compose interpolation, and Kubernetes ConfigMap/Secret wiring that consume it. An environment key listed in a document but no longer read by the code is ignored; there is no automatic alias or migration layer."},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html","Text":"Run the application and open the live API docs. OpenAPI JSON comes from the same registered Huma operations and Go types that handle requests. Use your application's host and port when they differ from localhost:8080. There is no generation command or checked-in specification to maintain. After editing a route or request/response type, rebuild/restart the application and refresh the page. The document reflects that instance's enabled routes. HTTP_DOCS_ENABLED=false disables both URLs. The JSON endpoint is self-contained; the interactive page loads a pinned Scalar viewer from a CDN and needs network access."},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Find and try an operation","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html#find-and-try-an-operation","Text":"Start the application with the capabilities and dependencies you need. Open /docs on that same instance and choose a tagged operation. Inspect required fields, accepted values, successful statuses, and problem responses. For a protected operation, supply your access token through the viewer's bearer authentication control. Send the request, then copy its X-Request-ID response header when investigating the result. Use a local or intended development instance when trying operations that write data. The viewer sends real requests; it is not a simulator. A docs page does not bypass authentication or enable a disabled capability. To inspect the document without the interactive viewer: curl --fail http://localhost:8080/openapi.json"},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Enable capabilities","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html#enable-capabilities","Text":"Business paths include the /api/v1 prefix. Endpoints Availability /auth/otp/*, refresh, sessions, logout AUTH_ENABLED=true /auth/social/{provider} Authentication and AUTH_GOOGLE_ENABLED=true User reads, Media and Notifications Protected; fail closed when authentication is disabled Authentication also requires valid keys, mail and rate-limiter dependencies. Configuration validation reports missing requirements. Phone verification, Apple sign-in and SMS delivery are not implemented. Expo push is optional. Use Authorization: Bearer \u003caccess_token\u003e on protected operations. Send refresh tokens in the refresh body. User reads are self-service, notifications are recipient-scoped, and Media management authorizes the owner. Content reads require public visibility or a valid temporary link. Uploads default to public visibility. A successful registration does not guarantee completion of best-effort local welcome-event reactions. Email OTP sends return 202 for all addresses. OTP and social verification return 201 for a newly created account, or 200 for an existing account, with tokens and data.user. After either status, use data.user.profile_complete to decide whether to prompt for the name. Update it through PATCH /users/me; GET /users/me exposes the same completion state."},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Change an endpoint","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html#change-an-endpoint","Text":"Edit the Go input/output types and their Huma schema tags. Register the typed handler once with huma.Register and huma.Operation. Return response.OK, Created or Accepted with a typed result, or response.Error(ctx, err) for a service failure. Declare non-200 success statuses on the operation too. Rebuild/restart and refresh /docs. Method, path, security, validation and schemas come from those registrations. Use explicit stable operation IDs when clients depend on them. Tests in this package exercise the live document, authentication, response schemas and concurrent first requests without application configuration or infrastructure. Application success bodies contain typed data and optional operation-specific meta. Problems keep Huma's top-level error fields, errors list and optional meta. Use apperror.InvalidFields for multiple application validation messages. The generated body schemas contain neither request_id nor api_version; read X-Request-ID and X-API-Version response headers instead. Both headers also accompany 204 responses and file streams, which retain their native semantics. For a deployment-specific snapshot, download /openapi.json with your preferred HTTP client. Health, metrics and the optional queue dashboard are standard HTTP infrastructure handlers outside the business OpenAPI document. See handlers, requests and responses for the common patterns."},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Keep runtime and OpenAPI aligned","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html#keep-runtime-and-openapi-aligned","Text":"The usual route change has one source of truth: Go request/response types + huma.Operation → route + validation + live OpenAPI A few explicit declarations still matter: Change Update A new accepted field or constraint The input type and its Huma tags A new public output field The response type and conversion Fixed 201/202 result Both the status constructor and Operation.DefaultStatus Conditional 200/201 result The operation's documented responses for both statuses Protected route Register on the authenticated API so enforcement and security match File upload/download The multipart or binary media-type declaration Operation rename Preserve its public OperationID unless intentionally changing the client contract A runtime response constructor cannot change the startup-time specification. Test both sides when adding status variants, streams, or custom headers. The response guide shows each shape."},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Export a deployment snapshot","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html#export-a-deployment-snapshot","Text":"For client generation or a reviewed release artifact: curl --fail http://localhost:8080/openapi.json -o /tmp/go-modular-openapi.json That file describes the instance you queried, including its enabled routes. It is an export, not a new hand-maintained source. Generating a client from it is a separate tool choice; the framework does not ship an automatic client pipeline."},{"Title":"API reference","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Troubleshoot the reference","Parent":"","URL":"api-reference.html#troubleshoot-the-reference","Text":"Symptom Check /docs and /openapi.json both return 404 HTTP_DOCS_ENABLED on the running process JSON works but interactive page does not Network access to the pinned Scalar CDN and deployment content policies Missing endpoint Feature settings, module registration, and whether the process was rebuilt/restarted Unexpected response schema The concrete output type, response helper, and operation status declarations Docs under a deployment prefix cannot load JSON Proxy path rewriting; preserve the page's relative openapi.json request The developer documentation website and /docs are different surfaces: this site explains how to use the framework; the live viewer describes HTTP operations registered by your running application. Continue with routing and HTTP tests."},{"Title":"Support and compatibility","Section":"Reference","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"support.html","Text":"Labena is distributed as a modular Go application template. Start from a published release, rename the module, and maintain the resulting application in your own repository. The adoption and update guide explains how to record your starting point and apply later fixes."},{"Title":"Support and compatibility","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Maintenance policy","Parent":"","URL":"support.html#maintenance-policy","Text":"Only the latest published non-prerelease version receives bug and security fixes. Older versions receive no backports. Before the first such release, development checkouts and prereleases are evaluation snapshots with no stable compatibility promise. Maintenance is best effort. There is no guaranteed response time, resolution time, or long-term support window. Wisal Group, the upstream repository owner, is responsible for release maintenance and private security-report triage, and may delegate those duties to repository maintainers. Application owners are responsible for their customizations, deployments and applying upstream fixes. Use upstream GitHub issues for reproducible, non-sensitive bugs and feature requests. Include the template tag or commit, Go version, relevant configuration with secrets removed, and reproduction steps. Report vulnerabilities through the private route in the security policy."},{"Title":"Support and compatibility","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Verified scope","Parent":"","URL":"support.html#verified-scope","Text":"Area Scope Go toolchain The version declared in the release's go.mod Core application backends PostgreSQL and standalone Redis; core CI currently tests PostgreSQL 17 and Redis 7 Local events Process-local, best-effort delivery with the documented capacity and retry limits MySQL and Redis Cluster Optional driver paths; outside the required core integration matrix Cloud storage, Google sign-in, SMTP and Expo Local or fake-provider coverage where present; live compatibility requires provider-specific verification Operating systems and architectures Only the build/runtime evidence recorded for the particular release; an image build alone does not verify runtime behavior See release readiness for candidate evidence and its limitations. Support does not imply lossless events, exactly-once external effects, automatic disaster recovery, or a throughput guarantee."},{"Title":"Support and compatibility","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Reusable packages","Parent":"","URL":"support.html#reusable-packages","Text":"All pkg packages are maintained with the template and remain independent of internal. None is separately released or supported as an external Go dependency at present. The template's module path is github.com/wisalgroup/labena; adopting an application includes replacing it with that application's own module path. Reusable implementation and examples are available for application owners to retain or extract. Extraction requires maintaining the copied code and its dependencies. Any future independently published package must declare its public import path, API scope, versioning and verification separately before being advertised for go get consumption."},{"Title":"Support and compatibility","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Compatibility and release notes","Parent":"","URL":"support.html#compatibility-and-release-notes","Text":"Version tags identify template snapshots. During v0, documented breaking changes may accompany minor releases. From v1, breaking changes to documented contracts require a new major version. Patch releases preserve those contracts except where a security correction requires rejecting previously accepted unsafe behavior; release notes must explain that change and its migration impact. The documented contracts include exported pkg APIs, HTTP operations and error codes, configuration keys, CLI commands, persisted schemas, task names and payloads, and event topics and payloads. Internal source layout and assembly helpers are customization points; changes to them must be called out when adopters need to adjust their applications. This policy does not promise automatic merges of modified template code. Each release must state: Its source tag and commit, required toolchain, and verified backends/targets. Bug and security fixes, with affected and fixed versions when known. Breaking changes, including defaults and operational behavior, and concrete migration steps for every affected contract. Required schema migrations, worker/scheduler rollout order, and handling of already queued tasks or stored event/notification payloads. Verification performed, skipped checks and known limitations. Existing deployed migrations are immutable. Changes use new migrations and explicit deployment instructions. A release's compatibility policy does not authorize rewriting an adopter's schema or replacing their application files."},{"Title":"Support and compatibility","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Updating a customized application","Parent":"","URL":"support.html#updating-a-customized-application","Text":"Fixes to this repository do not automatically reach applications created from it. Record the upstream tag and commit when adopting the template, follow upstream releases and security advisories, then review and port applicable changes into your application. Track the last incorporated release and any changes deferred. When skipping releases, read every intervening release note and apply required migrations in order. Validate the resulting application against its own database, queue, storage and provider contracts before deploying. Older applications can receive a manually ported fix, but upstream does not maintain their release branches. See the update workflow."},{"Title":"Security reporting","Section":"Reference","Heading":"","Parent":"","URL":"security.html","Text":""},{"Title":"Security reporting","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Supported versions","Parent":"","URL":"security.html#supported-versions","Text":"Bug and security fixes target only the latest published non-prerelease version; older versions receive no backports. Maintenance is best effort, with no guaranteed response or resolution time. Before the first release, development snapshots have no stable support commitment. See support and compatibility."},{"Title":"Security reporting","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Report a vulnerability privately","Parent":"","URL":"security.html#report-a-vulnerability-privately","Text":"Report vulnerabilities through GitHub private vulnerability reporting. You can also open Security → Advisories → Report a vulnerability in the repository. A customized application may have a different owner and reporting channel; report application-specific issues to that owner. Include the affected tag or commit, impact, reproduction steps and a minimal proof of concept with synthetic data. Do not post credentials, personal data or exploitable production details in public issues. If the private reporting button is unavailable, do not substitute a public issue containing vulnerability details. Wisal Group's repository maintainers are responsible for monitoring reports, assessing impact, coordinating a fix and publishing an advisory with affected/fixed versions and adoption instructions. Customized applications must incorporate those fixes themselves. Private vulnerability reporting is enabled for the public repository. Its setting and public reporting entry point were verified on 2026-10-18. Repository maintainers must keep report notifications enabled and monitor incoming reports. Notification delivery has not been tested by submitting a report. Follow GitHub's setup instructions."},{"Title":"Security reporting","Section":"Reference","Heading":"Application security","Parent":"","URL":"security.html#application-security","Text":"Use placeholder credentials in examples. Rotate accidentally exposed credentials. Enable authentication deliberately, restrict administrative endpoints, and review deployment secrets and network policies before exposing an application. Run govulncheck ./... with the declared Go toolchain when updating dependencies. A passing scan does not replace authorization, integration and concurrency tests."}];